<node id="Immunohistochemistry">
  <data key="d0">Immunohistochemistry</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry is a widely applied laboratory technique used to detect, localize, and examine the expression of specific proteins within tissue sections. In the context of pancreatic research, it serves as a primary method for investigating protein distribution and cellular localization across various tissue types and developmental stages.

In studies focused on pancreatic islet biology, immunohistochemistry has been employed to reveal the nuclear localization of transcription factors MafA and MafB specifically within islet β cells, while confirming their absence in pancreatic acinar cells. The technique is also used to determine the spatiotemporal distribution of MafA and MafB within the β-cell population across various developmental and perinatal stages in mice, providing insight into transcription factor dynamics during pancreatic maturation.

The method encompasses both standard immunohistochemistry and immunofluorescence labeling approaches, typically performed on fixed, paraffin-embedded tissue sections using specific primary antibodies. This allows visualization of multiple proteins simultaneously, including MafA, MafB, insulin, glucagon, Pdx1, Arx, Ki67, and cleaved caspase 3, in pancreatic tissue sections derived from wild-type (WT), A0B2, and A0B0 mouse models.

Beyond developmental studies, immunohistochemistry is utilized to examine protein expression within specific islet cell types in adult pancreatic sections and serves as a pathological assessment tool to evaluate MAFA expression in both familial and sporadic insulinomatosis samples compared to controls. Together, these applications underscore immunohistochemistry's versatility as both an experimental and diagnostic method for characterizing protein expression patterns in pancreatic tissue under normal, developmental, and pathological conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Tissue staining method using HRP-conjugated antibodies and DAB reagents for histopathological analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a laboratory technique used to detect and localize specific proteins within tissue sections by employing antibody-based staining methods. It serves as a fundamental tool in both research and diagnostic settings, enabling the visualization of protein expression and distribution at the cellular and tissue level.

In the context of cellular senescence research, immunohistochemistry is widely applied to detect and localize key senescence-associated markers, including p21Cip1, p16Ink4a, LAMINB1, and HMGB1, in tissue specimens such as pancreatic sections. By staining for these markers, researchers can assess the presence, distribution, and extent of cellular senescence within biological tissues.

Immunohistochemistry has also been employed to evaluate the expression and expansion of C-MAF protein in heterozygous versus wild-type mice, demonstrating its utility in comparative studies of gene expression across different genetic backgrounds.

Despite its broad applicability, immunohistochemistry presents notable technical challenges, particularly regarding antibody specificity. Detection of p16Ink4a protein is especially problematic in mouse tissue, where the specificity of available p16Ink4a antibodies is known to be limited. These specificity issues can complicate the interpretation of results, particularly when attempting to quantify absolute levels of protein expression. Consequently, researchers must exercise caution when drawing conclusions from IHC-based quantification of p16Ink4a in murine models, as antibody cross-reactivity or non-specific binding may affect the reliability of the data obtained.&lt;SEP&gt;Method used to confirm higher levels of ARNTL/BMAL and PDX1 in CR beta cells in situ.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is used to measure protein levels of 53BP1 and LMNB1 in pancreatic sections from AL and CR mice.&lt;SEP&gt;IHC method used to detect proteins in formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded tissue sections from mouse pancreas, liver, and adipose tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Technique used for staining pancreatic sections with insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin for morphology analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Microscopy-based method for which statistical analysis is performed using Student's t-test or one-way ANOVA in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Method used to detect expression of p16, p21, and NPM in pancreatic islets of normal and HFHC diet mice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bf89ad08d3f81ffe84c9bf7f208c604b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9693157a2d458ecc8255c49ec77d95f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f82db25373e25b34bbbcfebc1c0f861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff8c5dc48dde6c3ccfdc01c9e025403d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9ed09558012a9cb8eba4b1efe943943&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207f97a443acec0faaf8f9f2d208e61f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e531f038e6fcc1cbb89c2336af984719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5afa9fdb0e4f653f1c7825c0b258326d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b377c464e48f2efecc4b62133585c2f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0be98cbe29b4c692e555154146fad640&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c0cbc7de7a8a1060e8f1608271a28c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7d4c1829099c6037d8b8b7a83c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac258601b299e0c883e8e1c4a3030d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56d5ffe02105b1bd6d77faab9c812433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dcd3bdc472cb74376c18c72cf9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cadc21c6ca916dd06510987c1b7dbf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f39153d9f864070e17a74724a6c8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e33cd1d99c96a7f6ffdd0fbea902670</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;Members of the large Maf transcription family regulate insulin gene transcription in islet beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;MafB Is Important for Pancreatic β-Cell Maintenance under a MafA-Deficient Condition.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA missense mutation causes familial insulinomatosis and diabetes mellitus.&lt;SEP&gt;Nucleolar stress facilitates islet β cell senescence via hijacking the DNA damage response pathways.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">3</data>
