<node id="Pancreatic Islets">
  <data key="d0">Pancreatic Islets</data>
  <data key="d1">Tissue</data>
  <data key="d2">Pancreatic islets are clusters of endocrine (hormone-producing) cells located within the pancreas. These structures contain multiple distinct cell types, most notably insulin-expressing beta (β) cells, glucagon-expressing alpha (α) cells, and somatostatin-expressing delta (δ) cells. Key genes such as MafA, MafB, Slc30a8, and G6pc2 are expressed within these islet cell populations, where they play important roles in regulating gene expression and overall cell function.

Structurally, pancreatic islets can be classified by size into small (less than 100 μm), medium (100–200 μm), and large (more than 200 μm) categories, a classification scheme applied particularly in studies involving high-fat diet (HFD)-treated mice. Histological analyses of islet morphology, size, and cellular composition have been conducted across multiple mouse genotypes, including wild-type (WT), A0B2, and A0B0 mice. Such analyses have revealed that deletion of MafA and/or MafB significantly alters islet morphology and the numbers of insulin- and glucagon-positive cells. In contrast, MafA knockout mice display normal islet morphology at birth, a finding potentially attributable to functional redundancy among large Maf transcription factors.

Pancreatic islets serve as a primary tissue source in research settings for both gene expression profiling and functional studies. For example, islets have been isolated from 9-month-old female WT, A0B2, and A0B0 mice for RNA extraction and downstream gene expression analyses. Collectively, pancreatic islets represent a critical endocrine compartment of the pancreas, and their structural integrity and cellular composition are tightly regulated by transcription factors and metabolic conditions, making them a central focus of diabetes-related research.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets are clusters of hormone-secreting cells, including β cells, studied in male and female MafAS64F/+ mice for gene expression dysregulation, Ca2+ responses, and insulin secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;Clusters of cells in the pancreas containing β- and α-cells, used as the primary biological material in hormone secretion, perifusion, RNA sequencing, and calcium imaging studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Clusters of cells in the pancreas containing β-cells; site of insulitis and M2 macrophage recruitment in T1D progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets are clusters of cells within the pancreas that contain β-cells and are the site of insulitis, immune infiltration, and insulin production studied in this research.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets are the sites where increased M2 macrophage infiltration was observed in UPR-deficient mice, co-stained for Arginase1 and insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets are the endocrine clusters of the pancreas isolated via collagenase/protease digestion and used in various downstream analyses including C12FDG staining, apoptosis, cell cycle, and gene expression assays.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5d5e51c0312e54ad3757625ff1041f76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7255d0eadb6b7f3a3a51c10b8ea742&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb7aaa2476f3163b5c3de684504f42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94526413b6fbcf0ec99212789cec9eea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45b0e9a843120dd461f5ba7ec823e41f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7568462935971fd17472cf21ed7b8a2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5afa9fdb0e4f653f1c7825c0b258326d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb31cc9ec659a6d29b2ec1e3870fb57b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d01061347ba219119efa4109d89e71f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aad668b81389b88364235018ee9c389&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5afc163e9261bd9f96c6156bfdb27585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e08f93585dc5d50ccfbac2feb8d206&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2caaffb8c37fe000f2af35e115fc27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c625d74e06abff522b8eb3d76df6ae32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3838ae3de5236c272eda0072cc83f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18ba6ed2ada6acd593e0a5d2f036f947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e72d8d21ebb3fc252c498fcbfeed6c1d</data>
