<node id="Glucose Homeostasis">
  <data key="d0">Glucose Homeostasis</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucose homeostasis refers to the regulation of blood glucose levels, which is disrupted by β-cell loss or dysfunction, leading to diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose homeostasis is the physiological process of regulating blood glucose levels, critically dependent on the function of pancreatic islet cells, particularly β-cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose homeostasis is the maintenance of blood glucose levels, regulated by insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells and influenced by transcription factors and transporters.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose homeostasis refers to the regulation of blood glucose levels in the body, which is impaired in diabetes mellitus conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose homeostasis refers to the regulation of blood glucose levels, which is differentially controlled in male and female MafAS64F/+ mice due to differences in β cell activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose homeostasis refers to the regulation of blood glucose levels, which can be restored by removal of senescent β cells in mouse models of T1D and T2D.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose homeostasis is a biological process linked to many genes enriched in the GAST+ MAFBKO β-like cell population, as identified by gene ontology pathway analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose homeostasis refers to the physiological regulation and maintenance of normal blood glucose levels within the body, a process governed by a complex interplay of cellular, hormonal, genetic, and tissue-level mechanisms.

A central requirement for proper glucose homeostasis is the accumulation of sufficient beta cell mass in the pancreas. Beta cells maintain normoglycemia primarily through insulin secretion, and any disruption to their mass or function — including early onset cellular senescence or broader beta cell dysfunction — impairs the body's ability to sustain proper glycemic control. A healthy reserve of insulin-producing beta cells is therefore considered essential to optimal glucose regulation.

Genetic factors also play a significant role in glucose homeostasis. Loss-of-function mutations in the CDKN2A gene in humans have been shown to affect glucose homeostasis, underscoring the importance of this gene in pancreatic beta cell biology and broader metabolic regulation.

Glucose homeostasis is also notably influenced by dietary interventions, particularly caloric restriction (CR). CR has been shown to improve glucose homeostasis and is considered a contributing factor to extended lifespan and healthspan. However, these effects are sex-dependent: CR improves glucose homeostasis in male mice but not in female mice. Furthermore, glucose homeostasis can be impaired in CR-conditioned mice subsequently fed a high-fat diet (HFD), highlighting the sensitivity of glycemic regulation to dietary challenges. Glucose homeostasis is commonly assessed experimentally through meal tolerance and insulin tolerance tests in mice undergoing dietary interventions.

Additionally, glucose homeostasis exhibits notable sexual dimorphism, being subject to sex and gender differences that manifest across multiple metabolic tissues, including adipose tissue, the liver, and the pancreas. These differences are modulated by sex hormones as well as sex-specific biological factors, contributing to variability in blood glucose regulation among individuals.

Taken together, glucose homeostasis is a multifaceted physiological process influenced by beta cell mass and insulin secretion, genetic determinants such as CDKN2A, dietary factors including caloric restriction, and sex-dependent regulatory mechanisms spanning key metabolic tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance of blood glucose levels, regulated by pancreatic beta cells through insulin secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation of blood glucose levels; can be restored through interventions that reduce secretory demand on beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation of blood glucose levels maintained by coordinated actions of pancreatic beta and alpha cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9693157a2d458ecc8255c49ec77d95f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a87d40ff620e7f930f155b5dea286c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ecc1e3f157d04d78962c82537c6b7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-968f694e317648c82a07ee150e63adbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63a4c12087ceb7cec876e14e7fc45727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b39885796d53f3f69a4452a58c07c2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbe530a83d027293c37b530e3c376973&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8304b17dc893b8c9bfb7bfdb03703396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7bb3e4a69884a1b79485b95f6f502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdd484417981e099a8f308ba0994bcaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b67e26d82684726785f1910ba6919cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51601485f0924871a988aae2e5ce30bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80318e0997c6182c0124775c5f01f6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002f23b1d29df711e839459b4d6f0711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce68225f2288adf7d07962282496e8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-363f3a19905f833d29a7d1242acc6641&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d286ee457150be65228790e800b5207b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b61717ec6764bc92fa7c3c5067d345&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddac43d21e0426c8d61d02d10c0cb772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43761b34ecc024bd90bfa8711c45a233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28a44d52c17c29e9741a935aa76e8afe</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;MafB Is Important for Pancreatic β-Cell Maintenance under a MafA-Deficient Condition.</data>
