<node id="Insulin Secretion">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Secretion</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin secretion is a key function of islet β-cells regulated by transcription factors such as MafA and MafB, and is central to glucose metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin secretion is a key function of adult β-cells regulated by MafA target genes, disrupted in MafA mutant mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin secretion is a key functional output of β cells, markedly reduced at baseline and upon stimulation in male compared to female MafAS64F/+ islets, with mildly elevated levels observed in 10- to 12-week-old male MafAS64F/+ islets.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin secretion is a biological process associated with many genes enriched in the GAST+ MAFBKO β-like cell population, as identified by gene ontology pathway analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of insulin release from beta cells; enhanced by p16(Ink4a)-induced senescence and studied in INS-1-derived cell lines and human pancreatic beta cell lines.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin secretion is the biological process by which pancreatic beta cells release insulin into the bloodstream in response to glucose and other secretagogues, including pharmacological stimulants such as incretins, IBMX, epinephrine, and potassium chloride (KCl). It is a critical secretory function of beta cells and serves as a key indicator of beta cell health and functionality, commonly assessed through glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (GSIS) assays, dynamic perifusion experiments, islet perfusion assays, and meal tolerance tests conducted in both human and murine islets. The process is characterized by dynamic and pulsatile components, with basal pulsatile secretion driven by glycolytic oscillations. Early abnormalities in insulin secretion play a fundamental role in type 2 diabetes (T2D) pathogenesis, and functional defects in this process are considered to outweigh beta cell mass loss in driving T2D.

The process is regulated by multiple molecular, genetic, and hormonal factors. The transcription factor MAFA plays an important role in stimulating insulin release in response to glucose, with insulin secretion improved in C57 MafaS64F/+ males but impaired in mixed-background MafaS64F/+ males. Expression levels of CDKN2A/p16Ink4a, p21Cip1, TFB1M variants, androgen receptor actions, DNA methylation patterns, microRNAs, and various ion channels have all been identified as modulators of insulin release. SLC30A8 protective loss-of-function variants enhance insulin secretion, while CALCOCO2 loss and pancreatic elastase reduce it. Insulin secretion is studied across multiple contexts including incretin mimetics, bariatric surgery outcomes, and microRNA regulation.

Sex-based and racial factors also modulate insulin secretion. Women demonstrate increased insulin secretion following a glucose load and enhanced GSIS from isolated islets compared to males, with sex hormones including testosterone and estrogen influencing beta cell function. Insulin secretion is notably suppressed in male beta cells during the transition to T2D. Racial differences have been observed, with cAMP-stimulated insulin secretion decreased in non-diabetic black male islets compared to white male islets, while dynamic insulin secretion shows no significant difference across sex and race in non-diabetic donors more broadly.

Dietary and pathological conditions significantly affect insulin secretion. Under caloric restriction, beta cells secrete approximately 50% less insulin than those in ad libitum-fed animals in vivo. Insulin secretion is broadly impaired in aging, in T2D models, and in individuals with impaired glucose tolerance, including decreased responses to high glucose, incretin, and KCl stimulation. Individuals carrying CDKN2A/B polymorphisms similarly show reduced insulin release, reinforcing connections between genetic factors, beta cell senescence, and functional insulin secretory capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;A key function of pancreatic beta cells that is impaired during beta cell senescence and diabetes progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Biological process used to authenticate beta-TC-6 and INS-1 cell lines.&lt;SEP&gt;Process of insulin release from beta cells that is impaired in diabetes due to beta-cell dysfunction and death.&lt;SEP&gt;Functional output of beta cells impaired in diabetes; influenced by DNA methylation and candidate genes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9693157a2d458ecc8255c49ec77d95f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff8c5dc48dde6c3ccfdc01c9e025403d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2caaffb8c37fe000f2af35e115fc27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbe530a83d027293c37b530e3c376973&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18ba6ed2ada6acd593e0a5d2f036f947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02381256ec5732a712b97ced237c2acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8304b17dc893b8c9bfb7bfdb03703396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7d4c1829099c6037d8b8b7a83c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7bb3e4a69884a1b79485b95f6f502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac258601b299e0c883e8e1c4a3030d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdd484417981e099a8f308ba0994bcaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b67e26d82684726785f1910ba6919cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f42efe8d71d6b6b69c377d2a7c44b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe5b7d8c678cb311e300af3b74f19ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3982c3cb7b00162818de82027a853e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7873cb15c71b20e5fc369a6f0ab52a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794cb97944fe847e61831fceed4d92ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51601485f0924871a988aae2e5ce30bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a7d380dafd31120f8a1af317cfb0ab1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238834380bfb40677324883af3e3004&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3f8e2c47cb96d65689fa504202cd0f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0269f34de8f0928a9614438917079ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76049b85888e8e904e7c01de9740d34f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de697a7536ea955d878a95580bf8aa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cadc21c6ca916dd06510987c1b7dbf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce68225f2288adf7d07962282496e8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d286ee457150be65228790e800b5207b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b61717ec6764bc92fa7c3c5067d345&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c41ef398df926f1c888f284aff2a98e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d0bf8cd8c8cb69199fb8f9bf6c37e70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d41db2658ec2709309c04b39b1894e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd4f6cfb9180aade52c8cb84446ced85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2496631e25b79859522b657da346b58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cd7041acf7539c8db67413bcd98f41b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804ddf6c13fee09d00987476aa3c478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efc110efa119e343b95df1abbd0a327&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0386e6a8316aca2a67c3466442e7e1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba0d7fafdd66d2ffb133e04939d15f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddac43d21e0426c8d61d02d10c0cb772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e8cbb766d17c90c84d2a9452a51a41e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43761b34ecc024bd90bfa8711c45a233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-044fb2d3ececa99a53eb87d2ee6bf18f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10bdc47e4d027606476b48a401061a27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5596e00050d9f0135f87252a0093e9cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f09e5d1418f8ae04b43a24b97c1d52d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e181574d19e6f17c0fd64b0feecf8f9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af17b436ff0aa3dd562bbc7e986cc909&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28a44d52c17c29e9741a935aa76e8afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86</data>
  <data key="d4">MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.</data>
