<node id="Pancreatic Islet">
  <data key="d0">Pancreatic Islet</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Pancreatic islets are clusters of endocrine cells located within the pancreas, responsible for hormone production and regulation. These structures contain multiple distinct cell types, including α-cells (alpha cells), β-cells (beta cells), δ-cells (delta cells), ε-cells (epsilon cells), and pancreatic polypeptide-producing cells, each contributing to the overall endocrine function of the organ.

The architecture and cellular composition of pancreatic islets are subject to regulation by key transcription factors, most notably MafA and MafB. The expression dynamics of these transcription factors have been studied extensively during both developmental stages and postnatal life, revealing their critical roles in islet cell differentiation and mature function. Notably, species-specific differences in MAFA and MAFB expression patterns have been observed, highlighting interspecies variation in islet architecture and composition.

Pancreatic islets also serve as a primary context for studying the effects of genetic modifications and signaling pathway disruptions. In MafaΔpanc mice — animals with pancreas-specific deletion of Mafa — islet architecture, cellular composition, and gene expression profiles are significantly altered, underscoring the importance of MafA in maintaining normal islet structure and β-cell and α-cell function.

Furthermore, pancreatic islets, particularly β-cells and δ-cells, represent the site of MafA expression and islet cell development. Research involving individuals carrying the MAFAS64F variant has identified the islet as the context in which accelerated aging and cellular senescence occur, linking specific genetic variants of transcription factors to pathological changes in islet biology.

Overall, pancreatic islets are central to endocrine regulation and serve as a critical model system for investigating transcription factor activity, islet development, interspecies variation, and disease-related cellular processes such as senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;The endocrine tissue within the pancreas composed of distinct cell types including β cells, α cells, and δ cells, each expressing distinct peptide hormones.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets are endocrine cell clusters in the pancreas containing β cells, α cells, δ cells, and other cell types, and are the site of MAFA and MAFB activity and hormone expression studied in this research.&lt;SEP&gt;The microanatomical structures in the pancreas containing β cells, α cells, δ cells, and PP cells, studied in the context of MafA loss and Gast+ cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets are cellular structures containing α and β cells where MAFA and MAFB play important regulatory roles in hormone production.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets are the tissue of interest in the study, processed for RNA isolation, histology, and immunophenotyping.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets are clusters of endocrine cells located within the pancreas, responsible for hormone secretion and metabolic regulation. They are composed of multiple cell types, most notably insulin-producing beta cells and glucagon-producing alpha cells, along with other endocrine cell populations. The transcription factors MAFA, MAFB, and c-MAF play key regulatory roles in controlling gene expression within these cell types, and heterogeneous patterns of transcription factor expression have been observed specifically in human islets.

Pancreatic islets are a major focus of diabetes research, particularly in the context of type 2 diabetes (T2D). Studies have investigated sex-specific differences in chromatin accessibility and gene expression within islet beta, alpha, and other cell types, examining donors both with and without T2D. These analyses have provided insights into the sex-specific regulatory architecture of islet tissue and its relationship to diabetes risk.

Autophagy is recognized as an important process for maintaining islet homeostasis, particularly in beta cells. Disruption of autophagic pathways can compromise islet function and contribute to disease states.

From an experimental standpoint, pancreatic islets are commonly isolated from mouse models for research purposes. Isolation is typically performed via collagenase digestion of the pancreas, and the resulting islets are used in a variety of experimental applications, including perfusion assays and RNA/DNA extraction. Specific experimental models include islets isolated from C57/B6 male mice aged 4–8 weeks, representing a standard preclinical research preparation.

Overall, pancreatic islets serve as a critical site of endocrine regulation, and their study encompasses transcription factor biology, epigenetic regulation, sex-based differences in gene expression, autophagic homeostasis, and experimental modeling of metabolic disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Clusters of pancreatic cells including beta and alpha cells that maintain glucose homeostasis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9693157a2d458ecc8255c49ec77d95f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f7f8610c162035d52f2edbe846e8a97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41e9c4875e9ee2b378c620275d81fe7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f67b6052ac2da6848aaabbd71f9d4d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ee443fb4c37f27d6895254d3f95c708&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75261aabfd0eeb269665c62f36dfeb2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ecc1e3f157d04d78962c82537c6b7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f38c4d8fe95ae0a2b5cc05069361348&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce67306159c306b2c966577061cee6bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6c6371cdef301723817998e0efc9ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b8d7d9156faa43670557fc5e83791e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b5b330edc0a8ae22148338c6d94f154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40bbefacfceda2b6335fdb6310e2f7ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02381256ec5732a712b97ced237c2acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce68225f2288adf7d07962282496e8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b61717ec6764bc92fa7c3c5067d345&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4fecd9e508da1daa4a0d2d27a7e759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae638e9cabe91a68fc062c9981b0e86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c41ef398df926f1c888f284aff2a98e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d41db2658ec2709309c04b39b1894e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af17b436ff0aa3dd562bbc7e986cc909&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28a44d52c17c29e9741a935aa76e8afe</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Defining unique structural features in the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors that control Insulin gene activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778564612</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">16</data>
