  <data key="d9">0.00131605</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00407698</data>
  <data key="d11">0.0335687</data>
</node>
<node id="Human Embryonic Stem Cells">
  <data key="d0">Human Embryonic Stem Cells</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Human embryonic stem cells are pluripotent cells used in stepwise differentiation protocols to produce β-like cells, expressing transcription factors in a manner that reinforces their significance in β-cell development.&lt;SEP&gt;Human embryonic stem cells are pluripotent cells from which pancreatic hormone-expressing endocrine cells and glucose-responsive insulin-secreting cells can be produced in vitro and in vivo.&lt;SEP&gt;Human embryonic stem cells are pluripotent cells used to generate pancreatic endoderm and hormone-expressing endocrine cells, including glucose-responsive insulin-secreting cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Human embryonic stem cells (hESCs) are pluripotent cells used in β cell differentiation protocols; MAFB-deficient hESCs fail to produce insulin-positive cells despite undergoing standard differentiation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9693157a2d458ecc8255c49ec77d95f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9ed09558012a9cb8eba4b1efe943943&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ecc1e3f157d04d78962c82537c6b7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6c6371cdef301723817998e0efc9ec6</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562887</data>
