<node id="G6pc2">
  <data key="d0">G6pc2</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">G6pc2, also known as IGRP, is a glucose-6-phosphatase catalytic subunit-2 protein that is a major autoantigen in type 1 diabetes and may regulate fasting plasma glucose levels in humans. Its expression is regulated by both MafA and MafB.&lt;SEP&gt;G6pc2 is a gene whose mRNA expression is significantly downregulated in adult MafAΔPanc islets, as revealed by quantitative RT-PCR and immunostaining analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;G6pc2 is a candidate gene whose mRNA changes were independently verified in E18.5 MafB−/− and MafAΔPanc;MafB−/− pancreata by quantitative RT-PCR.&lt;SEP&gt;G6pc2 is a gene involved in glucose metabolism with reduced mRNA expression in 3-week-old MafaΔpanc islets.&lt;SEP&gt;A gene involved in glucose metabolism in β-cells, regulated by MafA in adult islets.&lt;SEP&gt;G6pc2 is a key gluconeogenic enzyme gene whose expression is decreased in MafaΔpanc islet β-cells, contributing to impaired glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;G6pc2 is a gene whose mRNA expression is significantly altered in MafAΔPanc and other mutant pancreata relative to wild-type.&lt;SEP&gt;G6PC2 (IGRP, glucose-6-phosphatase catalytic subunit-related protein) is a gene whose polymorphism is associated with fasting plasma glucose levels, and its islet-specific expression is regulated by Foxa2 and MafA.&lt;SEP&gt;G6PC2 is a protein target for which rabbit α-G6PC2 antibody was provided as a gift by Dr. John Hutton, used in immunolabeling studies of pancreatic tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;G6PC2 is a gene containing a polymorphism associated with fasting plasma glucose levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta cell-specific gene whose deletion reduces fasting blood glucose in pancreatic islets.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose-6 phosphatase enzyme gene upregulated in CR beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;G6PC2 encodes glucose-6-phosphatase catalytic subunit 2 and has increased expression in CR state 2 beta cells, potentially suggesting increased glucose excursion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f82db25373e25b34bbbcfebc1c0f861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e517ac1160d78d9b935ab9b9913bf46a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7255d0eadb6b7f3a3a51c10b8ea742&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5af2ab88dac4e2b983a277be1a9c536&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f67b6052ac2da6848aaabbd71f9d4d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ee443fb4c37f27d6895254d3f95c708&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff8c5dc48dde6c3ccfdc01c9e025403d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9ed09558012a9cb8eba4b1efe943943&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41e9c4875e9ee2b378c620275d81fe7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ecc1e3f157d04d78962c82537c6b7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9ed09558012a9cb8eba4b1efe943943&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41e9c4875e9ee2b378c620275d81fe7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ecc1e3f157d04d78962c82537c6b7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561511</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00077816</data>
