<node id="Type 2 Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Type 2 Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 2 diabetes (T2DM) is a metabolic disease and form of diabetes mellitus characterized by the body's cells having difficulty responding properly to insulin, which impairs glucose homeostasis. This insulin resistance is a hallmark feature of the condition and underlies many of its downstream complications.

At the molecular and genetic level, Type 2 diabetes is associated with several genome-wide identified risk loci, including variants in the *Slc30a8* locus in humans, which encodes ZnT8, a zinc transporter implicated in the disease's pathogenesis. The condition is also linked to alterations in triglyceride levels and fasting plasma glucose levels, reflecting its broad metabolic dysregulation.

Type 2 diabetes has notable connections to retinol-binding protein 4 (RBP4/Rbp4), which contributes to the disease by causing systemic insulin resistance when released from adipocytes. This mechanism links obesity — a well-established risk factor for T2DM — to the insulin resistance central to the disorder's progression.

At the level of pancreatic islet cell biology, Type 2 diabetes is associated with the selective loss of islet MafA expression, which may in turn induce islet Rbp4 expression, further compounding insulin resistance. Additionally, MafB expression is acutely decreased in individuals affected by Type 2 diabetes, with potential consequences including impaired β-cell proliferation and stimulation of apoptosis, thereby reducing the functional β-cell mass necessary for adequate insulin secretion.

Collectively, Type 2 diabetes represents a complex interplay of genetic susceptibility, adipose tissue signaling, and islet cell dysfunction, all converging to disrupt normal glucose regulation and metabolic homeostasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 2 diabetes (T2D) is a metabolic condition in which MAFA and MAFB levels are reduced in islets, implicating these transcription factors in disease pathology.&lt;SEP&gt;A metabolic disease associated with inactivation of specific β cell transcription factors and dysfunction of pancreatic islet cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 2 diabetes is a metabolic disease associated with loss of β-cell identity, dedifferentiation, transcription factor inactivation, and compromised β-cell function.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 2 diabetes (T2D) is a metabolic disease associated with loss of β cell identity and activity postnatally, in which non–β cell hormone production, SST+ and GAST+ cells, are observed in β cells, and MAFA/MAFB protein levels are reduced.&lt;SEP&gt;A metabolic disease associated with beta cell fragility and insulin resistance, discussed in relation to genetic predisposition and hormesis.&lt;SEP&gt;A metabolic disease involving beta cell failure and insulin resistance, studied alongside type 1 diabetes in the context of beta cell fragility and hormesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 2 diabetes (T2D) is a complex, heterogeneous, and chronic metabolic disease representing a major form of diabetes mellitus, affecting approximately 460 million people worldwide. It is fundamentally characterized by insulin resistance, impaired insulin secretion, hyperglycemia, and progressive pancreatic beta-cell dysfunction and loss. T2D is broadly classified within the spectrum of diabetes mellitus and is frequently studied alongside type 1 diabetes in the context of shared molecular programs and beta-cell biology.

A central mechanism in T2D pathogenesis is pancreatic beta-cell senescence, with research demonstrating that up to 50–60% of human beta cells in T2D contexts are senescent, exhibiting senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) signatures. Senescent beta cells contribute to insulin resistance and glucose intolerance, and their removal has been shown to help restore beta-cell function. T2D is also associated with beta-cell DNA damage, DNA double-strand breaks, DNA damage response (DDR) activation, p53 activity, and impaired DNA repair. Notably, DDR processes typically enriched in non-diabetic beta cells are downregulated in T2D, indicating compromised repair capacity, while metabolic stress further impairs DNA repair mechanisms. Additionally, DNA methylation patterns associated with beta-cell identity, function, and survival are erased in human islets under T2D conditions. Nucleolar stress in pancreatic beta cells is also implicated in T2D-associated beta-cell mass decline and diabetic progression.

Anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family upregulation, depletion of YY1, and increased GLIS3 expression have been identified in beta cells under T2D conditions. Expression and mutations of MAFA and MAFB transcription factors — including the MAFA_S64F point mutation, more frequent in males — are clinically pathogenic. T2D is influenced by genetic, epigenetic, and environmental factors, with sex-stratified GWAS studies such as DIAMANTE identifying risk loci and cell type-specific genomic enrichment. Noncoding genetic signals at the CDKN2A/B locus are associated with T2D susceptibility, and the disease demonstrates sex-specific transcriptional dysregulation in both beta and alpha cells.

Beyond the pancreas, T2D is associated with accelerated senescence in multiple tissues, including the kidneys — where SERPINE1 drives glomerulosclerosis in diabetic nephropathy — and the skeleton, where increased CDKN2A, CDKN1A, and SASP markers characterize accelerated osteocyte senescence and skeletal fragility. T2D has been modeled experimentally using high-fat, high-cholesterol diet interventions, db/db mice, and HFD-fed mice. Overnutrition, elevated insulin demand, and beta-cell hypersecretion and adaptive expansion are also implicated in disease progression. Importantly, T2D can achieve remission through extreme caloric restriction in recently diagnosed patients, as well as through weight loss and incretin-based therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 2 diabetes, a cardio-metabolic disease proposed to be combated by caloric restriction via reduced metabolic demand for insulin.</data>
