  <data key="d11">0.20502494</data>
</node>
<node id="Gene Ontology Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Gene Ontology Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">Gene ontology analysis is a method used to categorize differentially expressed genes by biological function, revealing involvement in ion binding, transport, signal transduction, and hormone secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene Ontology analysis is a bioinformatics method used to identify functional categories of differentially expressed genes, revealing 391 DEGs similarly regulated between sexes with p &lt; 0.05.&lt;SEP&gt;Analytical method used to evaluate gene sets for enrichment of biological process terms in cell type-specific and sex-associated analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene ontology database analysis was used to identify upregulated cell type-specific pathways across sex and race.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene ontology (GO) analysis using the clusterProfiler hypergeometric statistical test was used to identify upregulated cell type-specific pathways (FDR &lt; 0.2).&lt;SEP&gt;GO analysis used to identify enriched biological pathways in beta and alpha cell differentially expressed genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene ontology analysis is used to evaluate enrichment of GO terms in gene sets near cell type-specific cCREs, revealing cell type-specific biological processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f82db25373e25b34bbbcfebc1c0f861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df94a97c87154646f3ffd59dd4b312d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7873cb15c71b20e5fc369a6f0ab52a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a24341c9b63bd3b6313252fe6ed4b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87d48756321bdf8ef533359c0655a3d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da10e6374cdbd3d6e4a07d773cd172d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e42c3b77d3170e854edbbc691159609d</data>
  <data key="d4">MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562653</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00042527</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00198586</data>
  <data key="d11">0.0203636</data>
</node>
<node id="YO-PRO-1">
  <data key="d0">YO-PRO-1</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">YO-PRO-1 is a nuclear stain (blue) used in immunostaining experiments to visualize cell nuclei.&lt;SEP&gt;YO-PRO-1 is a nuclear stain used in blue to label cell nuclei in pancreatic sections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f82db25373e25b34bbbcfebc1c0f861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e517ac1160d78d9b935ab9b9913bf46a</data>
  <data key="d4">MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561055</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">24</data>
  <data key="d8">YO-PRO-1</data>
  <data key="d9">3.644e-05</data>
  <data key="d10">0.0</data>
  <data key="d11">0.0</data>
</node>
<node id="E18.5">
  <data key="d0">E18.5</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">E18.5 refers to embryonic day 18.5, a developmental time point at which Slc30a8 expression was lost in α-cells of MafB−/− mutants.&lt;SEP&gt;E18.5 refers to embryonic day 18.5, a developmental time point at which Nnat and Rbp4 expression patterns are analyzed in wild-type and mutant pancreata.&lt;SEP&gt;E18.5 refers to embryonic day 18.5, the developmental stage at which microarray screens were performed on MafB−/−, MafAΔPanc;MafB−/−, and MafAΔPanc pancreata.&lt;SEP&gt;E18.5 refers to embryonic day 18.5, a developmental time point at which MafB mRNA expression and gene expression profiles were analyzed in wild-type and mutant mice.&lt;SEP&gt;E18.5 refers to embryonic day 18.5, the developmental stage at which Mafb−/− pancreata were analyzed using the PancChip 6 microarray.&lt;SEP&gt;Embryonic developmental stage at which Ki67 signaling is observable in insulin-positive cells, indicating high cell proliferative activity during embryonic development.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f82db25373e25b34bbbcfebc1c0f861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e517ac1160d78d9b935ab9b9913bf46a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7255d0eadb6b7f3a3a51c10b8ea742&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb7aaa2476f3163b5c3de684504f42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75261aabfd0eeb269665c62f36dfeb2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7568462935971fd17472cf21ed7b8a2b</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;MafB Is Important for Pancreatic β-Cell Maintenance under a MafA-Deficient Condition.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562074</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00012457</data>
  <data key="d10">5.359e-05</data>
  <data key="d11">0.00094554</data>
</node>
<node id="Secondary Transition">
  <data key="d0">Secondary Transition</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">The secondary transition refers to the massive expansion of pancreatic insulin- and glucagon-positive cells starting at E13.5 that populates the adult islet during embryogenesis.&lt;SEP&gt;The secondary transition is a developmental stage beginning at E13.5 during which residual insulin+ cell production is delayed in MafB−/− mice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f82db25373e25b34bbbcfebc1c0f861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41e9c4875e9ee2b378c620275d81fe7e</data>
  <data key="d4">MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561055</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
  <data key="d8">Insulin / Pancreatic Islets / A0B0 Mice</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00018489</data>
  <data key="d10">1.344e-05</data>
  <data key="d11">0.01626329</data>
</node>
<node id="MafA−/− Mice">
  <data key="d0">MafA−/− Mice</data>
  <data key="d1">Organism</data>
  <data key="d2">MafA−/− is a mutant mouse model lacking MafA, similar to MafAΔPanc in showing changes in β-cell function and islet morphology observed postnatally, with unaffected insulin mRNA levels at E18.5.&lt;SEP&gt;Mafa−/− mice are MafA knockout mice in which islet cell development is unchanged, but glucose-regulated insulin secretion and islet cell architecture are compromised in adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Mafa−/− mice are knockout mouse models lacking MafA, used to demonstrate MafA's primary role in adult β-cell activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f82db25373e25b34bbbcfebc1c0f861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a87d40ff620e7f930f155b5dea286c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ecc1e3f157d04d78962c82537c6b7d9</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00010946</data>
  <data key="d10">0.0</data>
  <data key="d11">0.00842166</data>
</node>
<node id="Zinc">
  <data key="d0">Zinc</data>
  <data key="d1">Tissue</data>
  <data key="d2">Zinc (Zn2+) is a cation whose intracellular transport and regulation in pancreatic α-cells is mediated by transporters such as ZnT8.&lt;SEP&gt;Zinc is a metal ion transported by Slc30a8 from the cytoplasm into intracellular vesicles, essential for insulin maturation and storage.&lt;SEP&gt;Zinc is a mineral accumulated from the cytoplasm into intracellular vesicles by the ZnT8 transporter, essential for insulin maturation and storage in β-cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f82db25373e25b34bbbcfebc1c0f861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9ed09558012a9cb8eba4b1efe943943&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7255d0eadb6b7f3a3a51c10b8ea742</data>
  <data key="d4">MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561056</data>
