  <data key="d8">SLC30A8 / RBP4 / acinar cell</data>
  <data key="d9">7.712e-05</data>
  <data key="d10">0.0</data>
  <data key="d11">0.0015489</data>
</node>
<node id="FoxA1">
  <data key="d0">FoxA1</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">FoxA1 is a transcription factor with an early expression pattern in the forming pancreas, similar to Nkx6.1/6.2, but plays a subordinate role compared to FoxA2 in islet cell formation and function.&lt;SEP&gt;FoxA1 is a winged-helix/forkhead-containing transcription factor expressed in pancreatic islet cells and involved in β-cell formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Foxa1 is a transcription factor that dynamically regulates Pdx1 enhancers together with Foxa2, playing an essential role in pancreas development.&lt;SEP&gt;Foxa1 is a transcription factor involved in the dynamic regulation of Pdx1 enhancers essential for pancreas development.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f7f8610c162035d52f2edbe846e8a97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a87d40ff620e7f930f155b5dea286c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9ed09558012a9cb8eba4b1efe943943&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ecc1e3f157d04d78962c82537c6b7d9</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561515</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00016619</data>
  <data key="d10">0.0</data>
  <data key="d11">0.00374779</data>
</node>
<node id="FoxA2">
  <data key="d0">FoxA2</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">FoxA2 is a transcription factor that plays the dominant and general role in islet cell formation and function among the FoxA family.&lt;SEP&gt;FoxA2 is a winged-helix/forkhead-containing transcription factor expressed broadly in early pancreatic epithelial cells and confined to all islet cell types.&lt;SEP&gt;A transcription factor that, along with Nkx2.2 and PDX-1, regulates islet beta-cell-specific mafA expression through conserved sequences located between base pairs -8118 and -7750 upstream from the transcription start site.&lt;SEP&gt;Foxa2 is a transcription factor that controls vesicle docking and insulin secretion in mature β cells, regulates islet-specific glucose-6-phosphatase catalytic subunit-related protein gene expression, and whose tissue-specific deletion in pancreatic β cells results in hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia.&lt;SEP&gt;FOXA2 is an islet-enriched transcription factor with binding sites near the GAST gene, potentially acting with MAFB to regulate GAST expression.&lt;SEP&gt;FOXA2 is a beta cell identity transcription factor identified among 242 TFs enriched in beta cells via SCENIC analysis, with positive correlation in TF activation.&lt;SEP&gt;A transcription factor positively correlated with PDX1 and MAFA in beta cell activation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f7f8610c162035d52f2edbe846e8a97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a87d40ff620e7f930f155b5dea286c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f38c4d8fe95ae0a2b5cc05069361348&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9ed09558012a9cb8eba4b1efe943943&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c914d24696d0f6e2087f40c21ca5649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c914d24696d0f6e2087f40c21ca5649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08e345f1e7ff9529c2ab94760dcb50f5</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562675</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00031178</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00014882</data>
  <data key="d11">0.03665041</data>
</node>
<node id="Myf-5">
  <data key="d0">Myf-5</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Myf-5 is a basic helix-loop-helix myogenic factor that acts upstream and is critical to myoblast formation, analogous to MafB in islet development.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f7f8610c162035d52f2edbe846e8a97</data>
  <data key="d4">MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">25</data>
  <data key="d8">MYOGENIN</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00019901</data>
  <data key="d10">9.268e-05</data>
  <data key="d11">0.00760057</data>
</node>
<node id="MyoD">
  <data key="d0">MyoD</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">MyoD is a transcription factor in the myoD gene family, serving as a nodal point during specification of the muscle cell lineage; MyoD or Myf-5 is required for the formation of skeletal muscle.&lt;SEP&gt;MyoD is a basic helix-loop-helix myogenic factor that acts upstream and is critical to myoblast formation, analogous to MafB in islet development.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f7f8610c162035d52f2edbe846e8a97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9ed09558012a9cb8eba4b1efe943943</data>
  <data key="d4">MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">25</data>
  <data key="d8">MYOGENIN</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00028792</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00031104</data>
  <data key="d11">0.0077767</data>
</node>
<node id="Skeletal Muscle Formation">
  <data key="d0">Skeletal Muscle Formation</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Skeletal muscle formation is a biological process regulated by myogenic factors Myf-5, MyoD, and Myogenin, used as an analogy for islet cell development regulated by MafA and MafB.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f7f8610c162035d52f2edbe846e8a97</data>
  <data key="d4">MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">26</data>
  <data key="d8">Skeletal Muscle Formation</data>
  <data key="d9">3.644e-05</data>
  <data key="d10">0.0</data>
  <data key="d11">0.0</data>
</node>
<node id="P. Fueger">
  <data key="d0">P. Fueger</data>
  <data key="d1">Person</data>
  <data key="d2">P. Fueger is a researcher who conducted unpublished studies on Rbp4's role in repressing glucose-stimulated insulin secretion in isolated rat islets and in vivo.&lt;SEP&gt;P. Fueger is a researcher involved in unpublished studies showing that Rbp4 produced from rat islet cells inhibits glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f7f8610c162035d52f2edbe846e8a97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e517ac1160d78d9b935ab9b9913bf46a</data>
