<node id="Agilent 2100 Bioanalyzer">
  <data key="d0">Agilent 2100 Bioanalyzer</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Agilent 2100 Bioanalyzer is a laboratory instrument widely used for quality control and analysis of nucleic acid samples, including RNA and DNA, in genomics and molecular biology workflows.

In the context of RNA analysis, the Agilent 2100 Bioanalyzer is used to assess the quality of isolated RNA samples prior to downstream applications such as amplification, microarray analysis, and library preparation. It is employed at facilities such as the Vanderbilt Functional Genomics Shared Resource Core Laboratory for this purpose. Quality thresholds vary by application: in some workflows, only RNA samples with an RNA Integrity Number (RIN) of 7 or greater are accepted, while more stringent protocols require a RIN greater than 8.0 before proceeding to library preparation.

The Agilent 2100 Bioanalyzer is also used in conjunction with the Agilent High Sensitive DNA kit for quality control of DNA-based libraries. Specific applications include quality control assessment of single-cell ATAC (Assay for Transposase-Accessible Chromatin) sequencing libraries and single-cell RNA sequencing libraries. In these contexts, the instrument and kit combination provides sensitive and accurate evaluation of library integrity and size distribution, ensuring that only high-quality libraries advance to sequencing workflows.

Overall, the Agilent 2100 Bioanalyzer serves as a versatile quality control platform across multiple next-generation sequencing and genomics applications, supporting both RNA integrity assessment and DNA library evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Agilent instrument used for quality assessment of nucleic acid libraries.&lt;SEP&gt;Instrument used with Agilent High Sensitive DNA kit for quality control of single-cell RNA sequencing libraries.&lt;SEP&gt;Instrument used for quality control assessment of single-cell ATAC libraries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ff8c5dc48dde6c3ccfdc01c9e025403d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207f97a443acec0faaf8f9f2d208e61f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e08f93585dc5d50ccfbac2feb8d206&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46772cbd15b1e36b4aedeac0a06fd4de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78c6090d91c84168c42d7ba3a2808633&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bcdcae8f65f7440848eed5a2448750b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6615f49a664b0e6bfe3dc5ebc25a278f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a7d380dafd31120f8a1af317cfb0ab1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7913917fca6b789f10e2948623ce222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d0bf8cd8c8cb69199fb8f9bf6c37e70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804ddf6c13fee09d00987476aa3c478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-835fc8c5c19bf30c115aefbeec789479</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778570247</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
