<node id="Invitrogen">
  <data key="d0">Invitrogen</data>
  <data key="d1">Organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Invitrogen is a biotechnology company recognized as a prominent supplier and manufacturer of a wide range of research reagents, tools, and kits used across numerous molecular and cellular biology applications.

In the area of RNA and cDNA research, Invitrogen manufactures TRIzol reagent, widely used for RNA isolation, as well as the Turbo DNase Kit for genomic DNA removal. The company also produces Superscript III and SuperScript II Reverse Transcriptase for cDNA synthesis, and the Invitrogen Qubit HS cDNA Kit for RNA-seq library preparation and quality assessment.

Invitrogen is also known for its genomic and DNA quantification tools, including the BioPrime Array CGH Genomic Labeling System and the Quant-iT PicoGreen reagent, the latter of which is used for DNA quantification in islet hormone content measurements.

In the field of cell biology and imaging, Invitrogen supplies several fluorescent reagents and dyes. These include FluoZin-3 fluorescent dye, nuclear counterstaining reagents such as DAPI and YoPro1, and secondary antibodies including Alexa Fluor 488 (#A11073) and Alexa Fluor 568 (#A11036) used for immunostaining. Additionally, Invitrogen supplies mouse α-insulin primary antibody used in immunohistochemistry experiments.

For cell transfection applications, Invitrogen provides Lipofectamine 2000 (#11668027), used for transfection of INS1 832/3 cells, and its Lipofectamine protocol has also been applied for transfecting GAST-driven luciferase constructs into EndoC-βH1 cells.

Overall, Invitrogen serves as a comprehensive supplier to the life sciences research community, offering critical reagents spanning RNA isolation, genomic labeling, DNA quantification, fluorescence imaging, immunostaining, and cell transfection workflows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ff8c5dc48dde6c3ccfdc01c9e025403d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41e9c4875e9ee2b378c620275d81fe7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5af2ab88dac4e2b983a277be1a9c536&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207f97a443acec0faaf8f9f2d208e61f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e08f93585dc5d50ccfbac2feb8d206&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b39e1736c730a00c8edfab90d7dc64da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-667c57ab63de668da1132b51f1e9f411&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74e3fadd5f43dbb51005345f56dec5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02c41952a3f79a76f3315858bb8a14bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40bbefacfceda2b6335fdb6310e2f7ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e72d8d21ebb3fc252c498fcbfeed6c1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7dd7d21dd9a5aa2ba54defd650b8ebd</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778564575</data>
