<node id="β-Cells">
  <data key="d0">β-Cells</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">β-cells are insulin-producing pancreatic islet cells in which Nnat synthesis is predominantly localized by E18.5 and where MafA and MafB regulate gene expression.&lt;SEP&gt;β-cells are insulin-producing cells in pancreatic islets whose development and function are regulated by MafA and MafB.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic insulin-producing cells that are the focus of study; UPR-deficient β-cells exhibit features of cellular senescence including p21 upregulation, γ-H2AX accumulation, and cell cycle arrest.&lt;SEP&gt;β-cells are insulin-producing pancreatic cells whose survival depends on p21 levels and whose UPR-deficiency leads to cellular senescence features.&lt;SEP&gt;β-cells are pancreatic insulin-producing cells whose survival upon UPR deficiency is studied in the context of p21-mediated signaling and macrophage recruitment.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin-producing cells in the pancreatic islets, central to the study of T1D and UPR-related senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic beta cells sorted from dispersed islets of 6-week-old mice for RNA sequencing analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;β-cells are insulin-producing cells of the pancreatic islets. MafA and MafB regulate gene expression in these cells, including Slc30a8 and G6pc2.&lt;SEP&gt;β-cells are insulin-producing cells in the pancreatic islets that are the target of Atf6 deletion and are central to diabetes progression and protection in this study.</data>
