<node id="Glucose-Stimulated Insulin Secretion">
  <data key="d0">Glucose-Stimulated Insulin Secretion</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucose-Stimulated Insulin Secretion (GSIS) is a fundamental physiological process in which pancreatic β-cells release insulin in response to elevated blood glucose levels. It is a critical mechanism for maintaining glucose homeostasis and represents a key functional property of islet β-cells.

Several molecular regulators have been identified as central to this process. The transcription factor MAFA plays a prominent role in governing GSIS within pancreatic β-cells. MAFA's regulatory function is glucose-responsive, and disruption of this regulation — such as through the p.Ser64Phe mutation — leads to impairment of GSIS. Studies in MafaΔpanc β-cells and MafaΔpanc mice have demonstrated that MAFA deficiency particularly compromises the first phase of insulin secretion, attributed to deficiencies in key downstream effectors. Additionally, MAFA:MAFB heterodimer activity has been proposed as a potential explanation for observed species-specific differences in GSIS between rodents and humans.

Beyond MAFA, other molecular factors also modulate GSIS. Rbp4 (Retinol Binding Protein 4) has been identified as a repressor of this process, with evidence from isolated rat islets and in vivo studies demonstrating its inhibitory effect on β-cell insulin release in response to glucose. Conversely, overexpression of Slc30a8 — a zinc transporter — in INS1 β-cells has been shown to enhance GSIS, suggesting a positive regulatory role for zinc transport in this secretory process.

Collectively, GSIS is a tightly regulated and multifactorial biological process, with transcription factors such as MAFA, secreted proteins such as Rbp4, and ion transporters such as Slc30a8 all contributing to the precise control of insulin release from pancreatic β-cells in response to glucose stimulation.&lt;SEP&gt;The process by which β cells secrete insulin in response to elevated blood glucose levels; impaired by MafA deletion due to reduced transcription of insulin and related genes.&lt;SEP&gt;A measure of β-cell function assessing insulin release in response to elevated blood glucose levels, showing no significant differences between A0B2 and A0B0 mice under normal diet but comparable levels under HFD conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (GSIS) is the biological process by which pancreatic beta cells release insulin in response to elevated glucose levels. It represents a central mechanism of glycemic regulation and has been studied across multiple experimental contexts involving hormonal, genetic, and dietary factors.

In male subjects, GSIS is enhanced by extranuclear androgen receptor actions, suggesting a sex-specific modulatory role of androgens in potentiating insulin release triggered by elevated glucose concentrations.

At the cellular level, GSIS can be impaired by specific molecular perturbations. Overexpression of the miR-21 precursor in beta cells has been shown to impair GSIS, as has the targeting of M6PR and MTFP1 by miR-125b-5p, indicating that microRNA-mediated regulation plays a significant role in controlling the efficiency of this secretory process.

GSIS has also been evaluated in the context of dietary interventions, including ad libitum (AL) feeding, caloric restriction (CR), and high-fat diet (HFD) conditions. When assessed in vitro using isolated islets, no significant differences in basal or glucose-stimulated insulin secretion were observed among AL, CR, and HFD groups, suggesting that dietary manipulation alone may not substantially alter the acute secretory response under certain experimental conditions. However, CR beta cells have been shown to exhibit impaired GSIS under specific in vitro conditions, despite displaying lower glucokinase (GCK) expression and higher glucose-6-phosphatase catalytic subunit 2 (G6PC2) expression. Notably, glucose-stimulated insulin release dynamics in CR beta cells remain broadly appropriate, indicating a degree of functional compensation despite these molecular alterations.

Overall, glucose-stimulated insulin secretion is a multifactorial process subject to regulation by hormonal signaling, microRNA activity, and metabolic context, with impairments arising from distinct genetic and physiological perturbations.</data>
