<node id="Type 1 Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Type 1 Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune disease in which ZnT8 is a major autoantigen and IGRP epitopes are targeted by the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune disorder for which both Slc30a8 and G6pc2 serve as autoantigens in humans.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 1 diabetes is a form of diabetes mellitus in which the pancreas no longer produces insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 1 diabetes (T1DM) is a condition in which the pancreas no longer produces insulin, impairing glucose homeostasis.&lt;SEP&gt;An autoimmune form of diabetes that can be prevented by targeted elimination of senescent beta cells, as described in related research.&lt;SEP&gt;An autoimmune disease involving β-cell destruction, with discussion of potential interventions.&lt;SEP&gt;An autoimmune disease characterized by immune-mediated destruction of pancreatic beta cells, associated with ER stress, beta cell senescence, and dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 1 diabetes (T1D) is a chronic autoimmune disease characterized by the immune-mediated destruction of pancreatic beta cells, resulting in insulin deficiency, hyperglycemia, and progressive beta cell failure. The disease follows a staged progression through phases of autoimmunity, glucose intolerance, and full clinical diabetes, accompanied by the presence of islet autoantibodies.

At the cellular level, T1D involves significant beta cell pathology, including DNA double-strand breaks, persistent DNA damage, and activation of the DNA damage response (DDR). Islets proximal to immune cell infiltrate display particularly prominent beta cell DDR activity. Replication-associated vulnerability to DNA damage may help explain the observed peaks in autoimmunity during infancy and puberty. Beta cell senescence is closely associated with disease pathogenesis; senescent beta cell subpopulations expressing p21Cip1 are elevated in T1D compared to healthy controls and exhibit distinct senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) signatures that contribute to immune activation, islet dysfunction, and broader beta cell loss. Senescent beta cell-derived extracellular vesicles (EVs) may further propagate disease progression through intercellular communication. Targeted elimination of senescent beta cells has been shown to help restore beta cell function and glucose homeostasis, and even prevent disease onset, with senolytic compounds demonstrating preclinical promise.

At the molecular level, T1D donor pancreas tissue exhibits upregulation of CDKN1A and p21Cip1 without a corresponding increase in CDKN2A or p16Ink4a. Expression of MAFA and MAFB proteins is affected in T1D, though beta cell MAFA protein levels remain unchanged in donor tissue. Senescence markers have been observed in both human pancreatic islets and NOD (non-obese diabetic) mouse models, where a minority of beta cells develop senescence prior to immune-mediated destruction. Approximately 80% of female NOD/ShiLtJ mice develop diabetes, making this a widely used preclinical model. Disease-specific marker genes associated with T1D have also been evaluated across human islet cell types in translational research settings.

Alpha cell dysfunction is additionally implicated in T1D, with compromised alpha cell function and gene expression observed alongside beta cell pathology. Importantly, this alpha cell dysfunction occurs independently of a senescence program, mechanistically distinguishing it from beta cell senescence-related pathology.

Regarding sex differences, T1D demonstrates equivocal or male-predominant incidence in humans, with males showing distinct patterns in residual beta cell function, immune modulation, and IL-18 expression at diagnosis. In contrast, female NOD mice exhibit higher disease penetrance than male counterparts, highlighting a notable divergence between human epidemiology and animal models. Human T1D donor pancreas tissue has served as a critical resource for translational research into beta cell senescence and disease pathogenesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 1 diabetes, in which beta cell "rest" is proposed as a strategy to recover failing beta cell function.</data>
