<node id="Immunofluorescence">
  <data key="d0">Immunofluorescence</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">Immunofluorescence is a technique used to visualize protein expression in pancreatic sections, employing primary and secondary antibodies conjugated with fluorescent dyes.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunofluorescence is a technique used to detect Slc30a8 expression in islet β-cells and α-cells of wild-type and MafAΔPanc mice.&lt;SEP&gt;A histological staining method used to visualize proteins in pancreatic sections using fluorescent antibodies.&lt;SEP&gt;Method using fluorescently labeled antibodies to visualize proteins in fixed cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunofluorescence is a microscopy-based imaging and detection method widely used in the study of beta-cell biology, particularly in the context of cellular senescence and nucleolar stress responses.

As an imaging technique, immunofluorescence is employed to detect protein localization and expression in senescent beta cells, serving as a key tool for identifying protein-level markers associated with beta-cell senescence. In this capacity, it enables researchers to visualize and quantify specific molecular signals within cellular compartments.

Immunofluorescence is also utilized to detect and quantify phosphorylated forms of DNA damage response proteins, including phosphorylated ATM, ATR, and γ-H2AX foci, in beta cells. This makes it particularly valuable for assessing DNA damage signaling pathways. Specifically, the method has been applied to analyze γ-H2AX foci formation in β-TC-6 and INS-1 cell lines following exposure to compounds such as CX-5461 and Actinomycin D (ActD), providing insight into the cellular response to nucleolar stress-inducing agents.

In studies involving mouse primary islets, immunofluorescence has been used to stain and quantify multiple senescence- and DNA damage-related markers, including γ-H2AX, p53, and p16, in cells treated with CX-5461. This highlights its versatility in simultaneously assessing several molecular markers within the same experimental system.

As an experimental technique, immunofluorescence is frequently performed alongside complementary methods such as Western blot analysis in cells subjected to nucleolar stress exposure, reinforcing its role as part of a broader multi-method approach to characterizing stress responses and senescence in beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Technique performed on paraffin-embedded pancreatic sections to detect protein markers in endocrine cells</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dc7255d0eadb6b7f3a3a51c10b8ea742&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41e9c4875e9ee2b378c620275d81fe7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40bbefacfceda2b6335fdb6310e2f7ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b377c464e48f2efecc4b62133585c2f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f008854d459401e11c35e6bd2541318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a0590fd9be1db550e8a84c0781e8955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-787de2a35f76e08d5b7cddadbcc01954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af17b436ff0aa3dd562bbc7e986cc909&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e953ac093714735fa9b5b27463bf5dff</data>
