<node id="DAPI">
  <data key="d0">DAPI</data>
  <data key="d1">Molecule</data>
  <data key="d2">DAPI, short for 4′,6-diamidino-2-phenylindole (also referred to as 4′,6-diamidino-2-phenylindole dihydrochloride), is a widely used fluorescent nuclear stain and DNA-binding dye employed across a variety of biological imaging and analytical applications.

As a nuclear counterstaining reagent, DAPI binds selectively to DNA, making it a standard tool for labeling and visualizing cell nuclei. It is utilized in immunofluorescence imaging, including the staining of pancreatic sections, where it is commonly applied alongside primary antibodies such as anti-insulin and anti-glucagon antibodies to provide nuclear context within tissue architecture. In immunohistochemistry protocols, DAPI is applied at a dilution of 1:400. It is also incorporated into mounting media, such as Vector Labs H-1800, enabling simultaneous nuclear staining during slide preparation for immunofluorescence experiments.

Beyond histological applications, DAPI serves as a fluorescent DNA-binding dye in cell cycle analysis, where it stains DNA to enable determination of cell cycle phase based on DNA content. In confocal imaging workflows, the DAPI signal is used as an input channel for nuclei segmentation algorithms. Additionally, DAPI is employed in RNAscope assays to label cell nuclei during immunofluorescence imaging of pancreas sections, providing a nuclear reference alongside RNA detection.

DAPI is commercially available from multiple suppliers, including Invitrogen, Southern Biotech, and BioLegend (catalog #422801), reflecting its broad adoption as a reliable and versatile nuclear staining reagent across diverse experimental contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorescent dye used for nuclear counterstaining in EdU and immunofluorescence experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Nuclear stain used to label nuclei in immunofluorescence experiments via antifade medium</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-41e9c4875e9ee2b378c620275d81fe7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207f97a443acec0faaf8f9f2d208e61f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-667c57ab63de668da1132b51f1e9f411&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c625d74e06abff522b8eb3d76df6ae32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40bbefacfceda2b6335fdb6310e2f7ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e72d8d21ebb3fc252c498fcbfeed6c1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56d5ffe02105b1bd6d77faab9c812433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dcd3bdc472cb74376c18c72cf9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cadc21c6ca916dd06510987c1b7dbf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b377c464e48f2efecc4b62133585c2f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e953ac093714735fa9b5b27463bf5dff</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Nucleolar stress facilitates islet β cell senescence via hijacking the DNA damage response pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571462</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">16</data>
  <data key="d8">Pancreatic Islet / Bulk RNA-Seq / Islet Isolation</data>
