<node id="Glucose Intolerance">
  <data key="d0">Glucose Intolerance</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucose intolerance is a metabolic condition characterized by impaired regulation and clearance of blood glucose levels, and has been studied extensively in the context of pancreatic β cell dysfunction and diabetes-related phenotypes.

In mouse model research, glucose intolerance has been prominently observed in MafAS64F/+ mice, particularly in males. In male MafAS64F/+ mice, the condition develops persistently beginning as early as 4 to 5 weeks of age, preceding the full detection of senescence markers and manifesting alongside β cell dysfunction. At 5 weeks of age, the condition is associated with Ca2+ non-responsive β cell populations, poor islet function, and a reduced glucose-stimulated β cell Ca2+ response. Notably, glucose intolerance levels in male MafAS64F/+ mice remain temporally stable across different ages. The phenotype observed in these male mice is considered to mimic expected outcomes in human male carriers of the MAFAS64F variant, suggesting translational relevance to human disease.

Glucose intolerance has also been detected in female mice under specific experimental conditions. While MafAS64F/+ males develop the condition spontaneously, glucose intolerance can be induced in wild-type (WT) females through ovariectomy, highlighting a hormonal component in its regulation and sex-specific differences in susceptibility.

Additionally, glucose intolerance is noted as a metabolic condition that is more severe in MafaΔpanc;Mafb+/− compound mutants compared to MafaΔpanc single mutants of the same age, indicating that the combined loss of Mafa and one allele of Mafb in the pancreas exacerbates the condition beyond what either mutation causes individually.

Beyond animal models, glucose intolerance has been detected in humans, particularly in the context of impaired blood glucose regulation, further underscoring its clinical significance as a diabetes-related phenotype.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose intolerance refers to an impaired ability to properly process and maintain glucose homeostasis. In the context of human prediabetes, glucose intolerance displays notable sex-based differences: women with prediabetes tend to manifest primarily with glucose intolerance, whereas men more commonly present with impaired fasting glucose.

Several animal models have been used to study the condition. In Sei1-deficient mice, glucose intolerance has been observed as a phenotype associated with increased levels of p21Cip1 and impaired insulin secretion, suggesting a link between cell cycle regulation and beta-cell function. Similarly, ERCC1 hypomorphic mutant mice exhibit impaired glucose homeostasis characterized by reduced beta-cell mass and cellular senescence, implicating DNA repair mechanisms in the maintenance of normal glucose regulation.

Research has also highlighted the role of senescent beta-cells in driving glucose intolerance. In mouse models subjected to a high-fat diet (HFD) or treated with the insulin receptor antagonist S961, glucose intolerance has been shown to be mitigated upon the removal of senescent beta-cells. This finding underscores the contribution of beta-cell senescence to the deterioration of glucose homeostasis and suggests that targeting senescent cells may represent a potential therapeutic strategy for improving glucose tolerance.&lt;SEP&gt;Characteristic of Stage 2 T1D, present before overt diabetes symptoms emerge.&lt;SEP&gt;Characteristic of stage 2 T1D where the patient is asymptomatic but shows impaired glucose regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic phenotype associated with aging and obesity, protected against by BMAL1 induction and caloric restriction.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenotype observed in HFD mice, characterized by impaired glucose tolerance during meal tolerance tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Impaired ability to clear glucose; observed in CR-HF mice similar to AL-HF control mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Impaired glucose regulation caused by inhibition of pancreatic beta-cell insulin secretion, associated with miR-27a-5p from visceral adipocytes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b158cd1c233246631a3b8f719a275f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df94a97c87154646f3ffd59dd4b312d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-427bdb72b4ec16ac96774e76f03ada77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b3deb83588a816faad5340f542e1f43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63a4c12087ceb7cec876e14e7fc45727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9977f67772ceb665bdd71b221a2d4234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2caaffb8c37fe000f2af35e115fc27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b39885796d53f3f69a4452a58c07c2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8304b17dc893b8c9bfb7bfdb03703396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b861cee193cad9e0f142051047e83aae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7d4c1829099c6037d8b8b7a83c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdd484417981e099a8f308ba0994bcaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-732255e9544dde6a7dbc319c61cd8e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f3e602fed4b4ebfff436b75194d507&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2e4a544176811f5b43952b185e1ed3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d286ee457150be65228790e800b5207b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e181574d19e6f17c0fd64b0feecf8f9e</data>
