<node id="qPCR">
  <data key="d0">qPCR</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">qPCR, or quantitative polymerase chain reaction, is a widely utilized molecular biology technique capable of both amplifying and quantifying specific nucleic acid sequences. It serves multiple analytical purposes across gene expression studies and genomic research.

As a quantitative gene expression measurement method, qPCR is employed to measure mRNA levels of key regulators involved in the insulin secretion pathway within islets. It is also extensively used as a gene expression validation tool, confirming results obtained from microarray studies — particularly in contexts such as 3-week-old MafaΔpanc islet RNAs and gene expression regulation identified in MafaΔpanc islet microarray analyses. Similarly, qPCR is applied to confirm candidate differentially expressed genes (DEGs) identified in MafAS64F/+ female islets and other experimental conditions, providing a reliable secondary verification of high-throughput data.

Beyond mRNA quantification and microarray validation, qPCR is also used in chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP) workflows, where it amplifies and quantifies specific target DNA regions derived from ChIP samples, enabling the assessment of protein-DNA interactions at defined genomic loci.

Additionally, qPCR is utilized to detect and quantify mRNA expression levels of senescence markers and to monitor gene expression changes in senescent beta cells. This application underscores its utility in cellular aging research, where precise measurement of senescence-associated transcriptional changes is critical for understanding beta cell biology and related pathological processes.

Overall, qPCR functions as a versatile and sensitive quantitative method applicable across diverse biological contexts, including metabolic regulation, transcriptomic validation, epigenomic analysis, and cellular senescence studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantitative PCR assay used to quantify CDKN2A mRNA levels in peripheral T cells from fresh blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantitative PCR method used to measure expression of senescence markers in bone marrow cells of mouse models and human needle biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantitative PCR method used to measure expression of senescence markers in sorted skeletal cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b158cd1c233246631a3b8f719a275f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f67b6052ac2da6848aaabbd71f9d4d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94526413b6fbcf0ec99212789cec9eea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9977f67772ceb665bdd71b221a2d4234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02c41952a3f79a76f3315858bb8a14bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d3b1de44d6b73a10793a43351e18e6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a471400d67fa8643230222fb3576486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic β-cell senescence in diabetes: mechanisms, markers and therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;SenNet recommendations for detecting senescent cells in different tissues.</data>
