<node id="Diabetes Mellitus">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Mellitus</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes Mellitus is a serious chronic metabolic disease characterized by impaired blood glucose homeostasis and elevated blood glucose levels (hyperglycemia). It arises either when the pancreas fails to produce sufficient insulin, as in Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (T1DM), or when cells fail to respond to insulin properly, as in Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (T2DM). The condition is life-threatening and affects millions of people worldwide, representing a major global health burden.

At its core, Diabetes Mellitus involves the loss or dysfunction of pancreatic β-cells, dysregulation of insulin secretion or action, and disruption of normal blood glucose homeostasis. Evidence from animal models, specifically A0B0 double-knockout mice under high-fat diet (HFD) feeding conditions, has demonstrated that the disease can be associated with reduced MafB expression in human islets, impaired β-cell proliferation, and increased apoptosis, underscoring the critical role of β-cell integrity in disease development.

Diabetes Mellitus is also closely linked to genetic factors, particularly the p.Ser64Phe variant in the MAFA gene. In affected families carrying this mutation, Diabetes Mellitus presents as one of two distinct phenotypes, with a clinical presentation resembling maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY). Notably, this MAFA-associated form of the disease occurs more frequently in male carriers within affected families, suggesting a sex-influenced expressivity of the mutation.

Overall, Diabetes Mellitus encompasses a spectrum of etiologies — ranging from autoimmune and metabolic dysfunction to genetic mutations and dietary-induced β-cell failure — all converging on the common pathological outcome of impaired glucose regulation and its associated systemic consequences.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes mellitus is a metabolic disease associated with the MAFA S64F mutation, developing in individuals carrying the heterozygous mutation in the MAFA transactivation domain.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes mellitus is a metabolic disease associated with MAFA S64F carriers, predominantly occurring in male carriers at a 3:1 ratio, with a mean age of diagnosis of 38 years.&lt;SEP&gt;A metabolic disease to which pathologically accelerated senescence contributes, including through pancreatic beta cell dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;A collection of conditions including T1D, T2D, and MODY commonly marked by hyperglycemia and beta cell malfunction or loss.&lt;SEP&gt;A metabolic disease to which pathologically accelerated senescence contributes, including through pancreatic beta cell dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;A collection of conditions including T1D, T2D, and MODY commonly marked by hyperglycemia and beta cell malfunction or loss.&lt;SEP&gt;A chronic metabolic syndrome characterized by persistent hyperglycemia and insufficient insulin secretion, comprising type 1 and type 2 DM.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a87d40ff620e7f930f155b5dea286c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fffe587d9d8a94d6179115102ef90a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06849490a7a3c085d30a2a392584bd67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-348e687c811009b30863b17f466ac662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e531f038e6fcc1cbb89c2336af984719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5afa9fdb0e4f653f1c7825c0b258326d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d01061347ba219119efa4109d89e71f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e0ae079210c3ef99872d0747c5a9b88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74fc4f55c8c6d83ebd1d8dba07d90692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-427bdb72b4ec16ac96774e76f03ada77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298</data>
  <data key="d4">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafB Is Important for Pancreatic β-Cell Maintenance under a MafA-Deficient Condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA missense mutation causes familial insulinomatosis and diabetes mellitus.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562662</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
