<node id="C57BL/6J">
  <data key="d0">C57BL/6J</data>
  <data key="d1">Organism</data>
  <data key="d2">C57BL/6J is a well-established inbred mouse genetic strain widely utilized across a broad range of biomedical research contexts, obtained from Jackson Laboratory and recognized as one of the most commonly employed mouse models in experimental biology.

In metabolic research, C57BL/6J serves as a key model for studying sex differences in metabolism and diabetes, high-fat diet-induced insulin resistance, and caloric restriction experiments, including studies on circadian alignment of caloric restriction and longevity. The strain is frequently used alongside other mouse strains such as FVB mice in caloric restriction paradigms. It is also employed in studies examining sex chromosome effects on adiposity and other metabolic phenotypes, as well as investigations into SLC30A8 gene deletion and zinc transporter-8 function.

C57BL/6J is extensively employed in aging and cellular senescence research. It serves as a model in aging paradigms focused on beta-cell senescence, where senescence-associated beta-galactosidase-positive (SA-βgal+) cells derived from 7–8 month old male C57BL/6J mice have been shown to be sensitive to ABT-263, a senolytic compound. Primary islets isolated from C57BL/6J mice have also been used to validate nucleolar stress-induced senescence markers, further underscoring the strain's utility in senescence-related investigations.

In diabetes and islet biology research, C57BL/6J is a common background strain for studying pancreatic beta cell function. It is used as the genetic background for MafA knockout mice, where MafA deficiency alone was found insufficient to reveal the role of MafB in maintaining adult β-cell function, highlighting strain-specific influences on pancreatic biology. Additionally, backcrossing onto the C57BL/6J background has been shown to prevent diabetic pathology in MafaS64F/+ males, demonstrating the strain's modulatory role in disease expression.

In studies involving transcription factor knockouts, C57BL/6J is associated with sustained normal fasting blood glucose levels, in contrast to the ICR strain. The strain has also been employed in lens biology research, where c-MAF deletion in adult C57BL/6J mice induces cataract formation and abnormal lens fiber cell differentiation, underscoring its utility in studying transcription factor roles in ocular development.

Overall, C57BL/6J is a versatile and widely adopted inbred mouse strain spanning metabolic disease, islet and beta cell biology, transcription factor biology, ocular development, aging, senescence, and caloric restriction research.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard inbred mouse strain used in animal experiments in this study.&lt;SEP&gt;Inbred mouse strain (JAX 000662; BKS) used as control for db/db mice in metabolic studies&lt;SEP&gt;Mouse strain used to study phenotypic sexual dimorphism in response to dietary fat manipulation.&lt;SEP&gt;C57BL/6J is the mouse strain used to study DNA damage and replication in neonatal islet cells at postnatal days 7 and 21.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouse strain used in experiments to study islet genomic stability, DNA damage, and the effects of high fat diet on beta cells.</data>
