<node id="Ki67">
  <data key="d0">Ki67</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">Ki67 is a proliferation marker used to identify and quantify proliferating insulin-positive cells in pancreatic islets.&lt;SEP&gt;Ki67 is a proliferative-cell marker used to stain pancreatic islets to assess cell proliferation in HFD-treated mice.&lt;SEP&gt;A proliferative-cell marker used to stain islets to assess cell proliferation; no detectable Ki67-positive cells were found in islets of any mouse group after 5-month HFD treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Ki67 is a cell proliferation marker used in immunohistochemistry experiments to identify proliferating cells in pancreatic islets.&lt;SEP&gt;Ki67 is a marker of cell proliferation used to quantify β-cell proliferation in Atf6β−/− mice and control animals.&lt;SEP&gt;Ki67 is a proliferation marker used to measure cell proliferation; its positive cells were significantly reduced in Ceapin-A7-treated NIT1 cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Marker of cell replication used to quantify beta- and alpha-cell proliferation in HFD, db/db, and NOD mouse models.&lt;SEP&gt;Proliferation marker used in combination with CDKN2A and BCL2 to define senescent cells in bone; senescent cells are Ki67–.&lt;SEP&gt;Proliferation marker used to identify replicating beta cells in immunostaining experiments&lt;SEP&gt;KI67 is a marker of cell replication used to quantify replication rates in alpha and beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Proliferation marker; Ki67+ cells in neonatal islets were enriched in DDR pathways including base excision and mismatch repair.</data>
