<node id="MAFA">
  <data key="d0">MAFA</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">MAFA (V-Maf avian musculoaponeurotic fibrosarcoma oncogene homolog A), also known as MafA or RIPE3b1 in rodents, is a basic leucine zipper-containing transcription factor and a member of the large MAF transcription factor family, typically ranging from approximately 240 to 340 amino acids in length. It is closely related to MAFB and functions as a critical regulator of insulin gene expression, glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (GSIS), and pancreatic islet β-cell function and identity. MAFA operates through an N-terminal transactivation domain and can function as a homodimer or heterodimer, including with MAFB, to regulate insulin gene transcription. It is the only transcription factor capable of independently activating insulin transcription both in vitro and in vivo, and cooperates with other islet-enriched transcription factors to regulate insulin enhancer-driven transcription and DNA binding.

MAFA is predominantly enriched in pancreatic islet β-cells, found exclusively in insulin-positive cells, with expression first detectable at embryonic day 13.5 during the second wave of pancreatic development. Beyond the pancreas, it is also expressed in skeletal muscle, testes, and the developing lens. It is consistently identified as a β-cell marker in single-cell RNA sequencing analyses of human pancreatic islets, expressed independently of sex and race/ethnicity, and exhibits high centrality in β-cell gene regulatory networks. Notable species-specific differences exist: in rodents, MafA expression declines postnatally, while in humans, MAFA mRNA expression does not peak until approximately 9–10 years of age. In adult humans and nonhuman primates, MAFA is coexpressed with MAFB in β-cells. MAFA regulates insulin content, insulin granule density, and broad β-cell gene expression, including activation of Slc30a8 and G6pc2, while maintaining monohormonal β-cell identity by repressing non-β-cell transcriptional programs. Its expression is progressively lost during β-cell dedifferentiation in type 2 diabetes, where it is considered functionally inactivated, though protein expression appears largely unchanged in type 1 diabetes donors.

At the molecular level, MAFA contains multiple phosphorylation sites, including Ser64 and Ser65, and its stability, transactivation capacity, and cellular localization are regulated by post-translational modifications sensitive to glucose concentrations, oxidative stress, and glucotoxic conditions. Structural and functional analyses, including studies employing AlphaFold 2 and mutant investigations, have further characterized its biology.

Clinically, MAFA is recognized as a causative gene for maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY), a form of monogenic diabetes, and mutations can also predispose carriers to hypoglycemia. The missense variant p.Ser64Phe (S64F; c.191C&gt;T) is a well-characterized pathogenic variant that promotes protein stability, induces premature aging and senescence in β-cells, and predisposes carriers to familial insulinomatosis, adult-onset diabetes mellitus, and sex-biased β-cell dysfunction, with β-cell loss and disease severity occurring principally in males. Mutations causing adult-onset β-cell dysfunction are more frequently observed in men than in women. Additional point mutations in MAFA are associated with rare conditions including congenital cataracts and Aymé–Gripp syndrome. MAFA has also been implicated in multiple myeloma and insulinoma due to its oncogenic transformation potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor expressed in pancreatic beta cells; the S64F variant causes premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor gene associated with beta-cell maturation; MODY variant S64F causes premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
