<node id="PAX6">
  <data key="d0">PAX6</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">PAX6 is a paired box homeodomain transcription factor that plays a central and multifaceted role in pancreatic islet development, function, and cell identity. It is required for the normal transcription of pancreatic hormone genes and is essential for proper islet development, including the differentiation of glucagon-producing α-cells in the mouse pancreas.

During development, PAX6 is expressed broadly across all islet cell types, and its expression becomes progressively confined to all islet cells as development advances. It contributes to both islet cell formation and β-cell differentiation and is involved in a developmental pathway with MafB that contributes to the generation of β cells in the pancreas. PAX6 also maintains β-cell identity by repressing genes associated with alternative islet cell types; however, its downregulation does not produce Gast+ cells, indicating that the regulation of β-cell identity involves distinct mechanisms.

Conditional inactivation of PAX6 in the pancreas leads to early onset of diabetes, underscoring its functional indispensability in maintaining islet integrity. PAX6 deficiency has also been associated with α-cell infiltration into the islet core and compromised MafA expression, further highlighting its role in preserving normal islet architecture. PAX6 is closely related to the transcription factor PAX4; however, it does not act as specifically or in as coordinated a manner as MafA and MafB in regulating pancreatic cell identity. Notably, PAX6 expression levels remain unaffected in MafB−/− mutants and are similarly unaltered in MafAΔPanc islets during development, suggesting that PAX6 expression is largely independent of MafA and MafB regulation.

In addition to its developmental roles, PAX6 exhibits high centrality in alpha cell gene regulatory networks, and this characteristic holds irrespective of sex, underscoring its broad regulatory importance in alpha cell biology. In research contexts, PAX6 has been detected using a rabbit α-PAX6 antibody sourced from Covance Research Products, employed in immunolabeling studies of pancreatic tissue. Overall, PAX6 represents a pivotal regulatory factor in pancreatic islet biology with indispensable roles in hormone gene transcription, islet development, and the maintenance of normal α- and β-cell populations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ecc1e3f157d04d78962c82537c6b7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b13f08985b48653da8be9b63098bbb2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7873cb15c71b20e5fc369a6f0ab52a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0269f34de8f0928a9614438917079ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd4f6cfb9180aade52c8cb84446ced85</data>
  <data key="d4">MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafB Is Important for Pancreatic β-Cell Maintenance under a MafA-Deficient Condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778570277</data>
