  <data key="d11">0.00139437</data>
</node>
<node id="Nonhuman Primate (NHP)">
  <data key="d0">Nonhuman Primate (NHP)</data>
  <data key="d1">Organism</data>
  <data key="d2">Nonhuman primates (NHPs) are primate species whose islet α- and β-cells produce MAFB, suggesting MAFB is a unique signature and regulator of primate islet β-cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69365499ad956be649b962c79d46f483</data>
  <data key="d4">The MAFB transcription factor impacts islet α-cell function in rodents and represents a unique signature of primate islet β-cells.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561629</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00016386</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00143149</data>
  <data key="d11">0.00691555</data>
</node>
<node id="Hyperglycemia">
  <data key="d0">Hyperglycemia</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperglycemia is an abnormally high blood glucose level; MafABΔpanc mice died soon after birth from hyperglycemia, highlighting the essential role of MafA and MafB in β-cell function.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperglycemia is a pathophysiologic condition that suppresses MAFA expression in islet β cells and induces non–β cell hormone production, including gastrin-positive cells, in mouse islets.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperglycemia is a condition characterized by elevated blood glucose levels and serves as a common clinical marker of diabetes mellitus, encompassing Type 1 Diabetes (T1D), Type 2 Diabetes (T2D), and Maturity-Onset Diabetes of the Young (MODY).

In the context of pancreatic beta-cell biology, mild hyperglycemia has been shown to induce significant beta-cell identity changes with broad implications for cellular function, growth, and vulnerability. This is further evidenced in animal models: Mafa homo-deficient mice display mild fasting hyperglycemia, indicating impaired glucose regulation, while S961-treated mice exhibit hyperglycemia in conjunction with hyperinsulinemia, beta-cell proliferation, and beta-cell senescence, highlighting the complex interplay between insulin resistance and pancreatic beta-cell responses.

Beyond pancreatic effects, hyperglycemia exerts substantial downstream consequences on multiple organ systems. In the kidney, hyperglycemia promotes cellular senescence through an SGLT2- and p21-dependent pathway specifically within proximal tubule cells, representing a key mechanism in the early stages of diabetic nephropathy. Additionally, hyperglycemia fosters the development of a senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) in both endothelial cells and macrophages, suggesting a broader role in driving chronic inflammation and vascular dysfunction associated with diabetic complications.

Collectively, hyperglycemia acts as both a diagnostic indicator and a pathophysiological driver, contributing to beta-cell dysfunction, renal tubular senescence, and systemic inflammatory responses across multiple cell types and tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Elevated blood glucose that acts as an extrinsic stressor inducing beta-cell DNA damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Elevated blood glucose levels observed in CR-HF mice after high-fat diet exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Elevated blood glucose observed in islet tissues of HFHC diet-fed mice after 12 weeks.&lt;SEP&gt;Persistent high blood glucose characteristic of diabetes mellitus, an unfavorable stimulus for pancreatic beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Elevated blood glucose resulting from impaired insulin secretion due to beta-cell failure in diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Elevated blood glucose state preceding diabetes onset in db/db mice, associated with beta-cell DNA damage accumulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69365499ad956be649b962c79d46f483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6c6371cdef301723817998e0efc9ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1a171c046b7e074fd282a07d58cc39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7bb3e4a69884a1b79485b95f6f502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac258601b299e0c883e8e1c4a3030d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71a6a7a3a4894dbd7d979b7e5766d5a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58f0193e389064a60d513f13c5cad52b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2e4a544176811f5b43952b185e1ed3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e33cd1d99c96a7f6ffdd0fbea902670&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28a44d52c17c29e9741a935aa76e8afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad82642ce24967122d02819b52a6f45</data>
  <data key="d4">The MAFB transcription factor impacts islet α-cell function in rodents and represents a unique signature of primate islet β-cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562887</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00066241</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00161795</data>
  <data key="d11">0.04044884</data>
</node>
