<node id="Insulinomatosis">
  <data key="d0">Insulinomatosis</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulinomatosis is an adult-onset condition characterized by multicentric, insulin-producing neuroendocrine tumors of the pancreas that cause recurrent hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia. It is classified as a non-syndromic disease, meaning it presents without additional syndromic features, and can occur both sporadically and in familial settings.

A defining molecular feature of insulinomatosis is its strong association with the p.Ser64Phe (S64F) missense mutation in the MAFA gene. This mutation has been identified in individuals from at least two unrelated families, supporting a genetic basis for the condition in familial cases. Insulinomatosis is considered one of several adult-onset β-cell dysfunctions linked to the MAFA S64F variant, and its pathophysiology has been further studied using the MafAS64F/+ mouse model, which recapitulates key features observed in human carriers.

The condition is defined pathologically by the presence of multiple insulin-secreting neuroendocrine tumors (insulinomas) throughout the pancreas, reflecting a process of abnormal insulin-producing cell proliferation. These multicentric tumors collectively drive the hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia that is the hallmark clinical manifestation of the disease.

Epidemiologically, insulinomatosis demonstrates a notable sex predilection, occurring more frequently in females, with a reported female-to-male ratio of approximately 4:1 among MAFA S64F carriers. The mean age of diagnosis is approximately 38 years, consistent with its adult-onset nature. Early and recurrent episodes of hypoglycemia are frequently observed, often prompting clinical investigation that leads to diagnosis. Due to the multicentric nature of the tumors, patients may experience recurrent disease following surgical intervention, making management clinically challenging.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulinomatosis is a disease characterized by the presence of multiple insulin-secreting beta-cell tumors known as insulinomas. It is classified as a non-syndromic condition, meaning it presents with insulin-producing beta-cell tumors without the broader syndromic features typically observed in other endocrine tumor syndromes. As a familial condition, insulinomatosis is closely associated with beta-cell dysfunction and has been referenced extensively in the context of pancreatic beta-cell biology and islet research.

A defining genetic feature of insulinomatosis is its association with the MAFA_S64F missense mutation, a pathogenic variant in the large MAF transcription factor gene MAFA. This substitution prevents priming serine 65 phosphorylation, thereby disrupting normal beta-cell function and contributing to aberrant beta-cell proliferation and neoplastic transformation. Insulinomatosis is additionally associated with diabetes mellitus, highlighting the dual role of MAFA dysfunction in both tumor formation and impaired glucose regulation, including hyperinsulinemia and dysregulated basal insulin secretion.

From an epidemiological standpoint, insulinomatosis exhibits a pronounced sex bias, with female carriers of the MAFA_S64F variant affected at a significantly higher rate than male carriers, at an approximate female-to-male ratio of 4:1. This female predominance is a consistent and distinguishing feature of the condition.

Pathophysiologically, insulinomatosis has been studied in the context of beta-cell proliferative heterogeneity during islet growth, beta-cell senescence and aging, and transcription factor variant-driven proliferation. The condition has also been linked to released exosomes or microparticles from insulinoma cells that may activate autoreactive T cells, suggesting a potential immunological dimension. Additionally, some insulinoma cases have been associated with recurrent T372R mutations in the transcription factor YY1, pointing to molecular heterogeneity in the broader insulinoma disease context.

From a research perspective, insulinomatosis serves as an important model for understanding how MAFA variants contribute to neoplastic transformation of pancreatic beta cells, metabolic dysregulation, and the development of diabetes mellitus in affected individuals and families. It remains a key subject for studying beta-cell proliferation, insulin secretion, and the pathological roles of large MAF transcription factors.

Note: One description in the input references a form of type 2 diabetes with acute beta-cell failure and severe insulin deficiency predominantly observed in Black men. This description does not align with the established clinical and genetic profile of insulinomatosis and likely refers to a distinct condition; it has been noted here but not incorporated into the primary summary of insulinomatosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Disease mentioned in the context of pancreatic beta cell dysfunction and senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Disease mentioned in the broader context of novel differentially methylated regions in type 2 diabetes pathogenesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Referenced in context of beta-cell proliferative disorders related to DNA damage and beta-cell dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Not mentioned in this text.</data>
