<node id="EndoC-βH1">
  <data key="d0">EndoC-βH1</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">EndoC-βH1 is a human pancreatic β-cell line that serves as a widely used in vitro model for studying β-cell biology and related molecular mechanisms. The cell line was provided by R. Scharfmann and is cultured in Dulbecco's modified Eagle medium on Matrigel-fibronectin–coated plates.

EndoC-βH1 expresses both MAFA and MAFB transcription factors and retains functional β-cell characteristics, including the ability to secrete insulin in response to high glucose concentrations or other secretagogues. This makes it a physiologically relevant model for investigating pancreatic hormone regulation and β-cell function.

The cell line has been extensively employed in studies involving the transcription factor MAFA. It has been used to examine the stability of wild-type and the p.Ser64Phe (MAFAS64F) mutant forms of MAFA under varying glucose concentrations, with findings demonstrating that the MAFAS64F variant dramatically increases MafA protein stability when expressed in this line. EndoC-βH1 has also been utilized in MAFA knockdown experiments to assess downstream effects on hormone gene expression, including insulin (INS), peptide YY (PYY), gastrin (GAST), and somatostatin (SST). Additionally, knockdown experiments targeting both MAFA and MAFB have been conducted in this cell line to dissect the individual and combined roles of these transcription factors in β-cell function.

EndoC-βH1 has further been applied in transfection experiments involving GAST-driven firefly luciferase constructs and reporter assays, enabling the investigation of transcriptional regulation of the gastrin gene. The cell line has also been employed to examine whether loss of the unfolded protein response (UPR) impacts human β-cells in a manner analogous to its effects observed in NOD mice, thereby bridging findings between animal models and human cellular contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;EndoC-βH1 is a human β cell line in which both MAFA and MAFB are required for glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (GSIS).&lt;SEP&gt;Human beta cell line; knockdown of CDKN2A in this line increased insulin secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;Human beta cell line; knockdown of CDKN2A in this line increased insulin secretion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61ea436af2c14f0df238edb6a5db2bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63a4c12087ceb7cec876e14e7fc45727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbe530a83d027293c37b530e3c376973&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b8d7d9156faa43670557fc5e83791e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b39e1736c730a00c8edfab90d7dc64da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-667c57ab63de668da1132b51f1e9f411&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9e53f09e3b38fb66b0f093b0768d32a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f797167cb0737019ee587d4f66f13530&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac258601b299e0c883e8e1c4a3030d6</data>
  <data key="d4">Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA missense mutation causes familial insulinomatosis and diabetes mellitus.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic β-cell senescence in diabetes: mechanisms, markers and therapies.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778566602</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
