<node id="MODY">
  <data key="d0">MODY</data>
  <data key="d1">Disease</data>
  <data key="d2">Maturity-Onset Diabetes of the Young (MODY) is a monogenic form of noninsulin-dependent diabetes characterized by mutations in transcription factors that regulate pancreatic beta cell function. It represents a clinically and genetically distinct group of diabetes disorders, differing from both type 1 and type 2 diabetes in its hereditary, single-gene basis, and is directly relevant to pancreatic beta cell biology and insulin secretion.

MODY encompasses multiple known subtypes in humans, caused by pathogenic variants in a range of transcription factors and genes. These include HNF1A, TCF4, NEUROD1, HNF4A, MAFA, MAFB, ONECUT1, and other related genes affecting insulin secretion and beta cell identity. Among the at least seven known human MODY transcription factor subtypes, only one has demonstrated a heterozygous mouse phenotype comparable to that observed in human carriers, underscoring the complexity of translating animal models to human disease understanding.

One well-characterized genetic variant associated with MODY is the MAFA S64F (p.Ser64Phe) mutation, which causes a distinct MODY subtype. This variant has been identified in affected individuals from at least two unrelated families, and its phenotype closely resembles the classical MODY presentation of noninsulin-dependent diabetes. The MafAS64F/+ mouse model has been employed to study the pathophysiological outcomes observed in human carriers, providing mechanistic insight into how this mutation contributes to disease. A key feature of MODY associated with the MAFA S64F variant is sex-biased islet beta cell dysfunction, with senescence of subpopulations of pancreatic beta cells observed predominantly in males. This beta cell senescence is considered a contributing pathophysiological mechanism underlying impaired insulin secretion, and the overall predisposition to MODY conferred by the MAFA S64F mutation occurs in a sex-dependent manner.

More broadly, the transcription factors MAFA and MAFB play critical roles in beta cell identity and function, and mutations in these factors disrupt normal beta cell physiology, ultimately leading to the diabetic phenotype observed in MODY patients. Pathogenic variants in ONECUT1 and other genes affecting insulin secretion have also been linked to MODY, further highlighting the genetic heterogeneity of this condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Maturity-onset diabetes of the young, associated with MAFA S64F variant causing beta cell senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Maturity-onset diabetes of the young; associated with MAFA S64F variant causing sex-biased beta-cell dysfunction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61ea436af2c14f0df238edb6a5db2bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6854465a5ab40a3dbe223209e2c5cd66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b3deb83588a816faad5340f542e1f43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b39885796d53f3f69a4452a58c07c2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02381256ec5732a712b97ced237c2acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b67e26d82684726785f1910ba6919cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea13fe99fbc01cb6036ecddcda0aae8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51601485f0924871a988aae2e5ce30bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f6e43723dd8061d1a0532981e4a43b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-735ea17eaeb5e3524d0871e16116e986&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb32288d5dedac1b23370508bf2f7b2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3384cef9855a1b408c68f7d1ea3153c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c41ef398df926f1c888f284aff2a98e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d41db2658ec2709309c04b39b1894e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0386e6a8316aca2a67c3466442e7e1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba0d7fafdd66d2ffb133e04939d15f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-044fb2d3ececa99a53eb87d2ee6bf18f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10bdc47e4d027606476b48a401061a27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e181574d19e6f17c0fd64b0feecf8f9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ab370ab273e97005ced113e59cc48a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86</data>
  <data key="d4">Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA missense mutation causes familial insulinomatosis and diabetes mellitus.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploring Large MAF Transcription Factors: Functions, Pathology, and Mouse Models with Point Mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic β-cell senescence in diabetes: mechanisms, markers and therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;SenNet recommendations for detecting senescent cells in different tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploring senescence as a modifier of β cell extracellular vesicles in type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Safeguarding genomic integrity in beta-cells: implications for beta-cell differentiation, growth, and dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Defining unique structural features in the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors that control Insulin gene activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic background influences the phenotypic penetrance by MAFA (S64F) MODY in male mice.&lt;SEP&gt;The role of the beta cell in type 2 diabetes: new findings from the last 5 years.&lt;SEP&gt;Nucleolar stress facilitates islet β cell senescence via hijacking the DNA damage response pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
