</node>
<node id="N-Terminal Transactivation Domain">
  <data key="d0">N-Terminal Transactivation Domain</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">The N-terminal transactivation domain is a functional region of the MAFA protein where Ser64 and Ser65 reside, and where GSK3-mediated sequential phosphorylation occurs.&lt;SEP&gt;The N-terminal transactivation domain of MAFA is the region where sequential phosphorylation events occur at residues Ser49, Thr53, Thr57, Ser61, and Ser65, regulating both transactivation capacity and protein degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Domain of MAFA and MAFB with 51% sequence identity; contains phosphorylation sites that regulate MAFA DNA-binding ability.&lt;SEP&gt;Underdefined domain of MAFA and MAFB analyzed using AlphaFold 2 to understand structural differences contributing to unique functional properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-71db066aa18a18c64b2b33ad26f7bfab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6854465a5ab40a3dbe223209e2c5cd66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb32288d5dedac1b23370508bf2f7b2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4fecd9e508da1daa4a0d2d27a7e759</data>
  <data key="d4">MAFA missense mutation causes familial insulinomatosis and diabetes mellitus.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778561806</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00029227</data>
  <data key="d10">1.608e-05</data>
  <data key="d11">0.02033197</data>
</node>
<node id="Exome Sequencing">
  <data key="d0">Exome Sequencing</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">Exome sequencing is a genomic method used on subjects III/1, III/2, III/8, and IV/4 from Family 1 to identify heterozygous variants, leading to the discovery of the MAFA p.Ser64Phe variant.&lt;SEP&gt;Exome sequencing is a genomic technique used to identify the p.Ser64Phe MAFA missense mutation segregating with insulinomatosis and diabetes in the index family.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic sequencing technique performed using the SureSelect Human All Exon Kit (v5) on an HiSeq2500 system to identify the MAFA missense variant in affected individuals from Family 1.&lt;SEP&gt;Method used to identify MAP3K15 as a gene involved in cellular stress response pathways.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-06849490a7a3c085d30a2a392584bd67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-348e687c811009b30863b17f466ac662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e531f038e6fcc1cbb89c2336af984719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-044fb2d3ececa99a53eb87d2ee6bf18f</data>
