<node id="Glucose Tolerance">
  <data key="d0">Glucose Tolerance</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucose tolerance is the ability to regulate blood glucose levels; it is severely impaired in A0B0 mice, particularly after HFD treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A measure of metabolic function assessing the body's ability to regulate blood glucose; more severely impaired in A0B0 mice than in A0B2 mice, especially under HFD conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The ability to regulate blood glucose levels, which is impaired in A0B0 mice and used as a key metabolic phenotype measurement in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose tolerance is the ability to maintain normal blood glucose levels; female MafAS64F/+ mice maintain glucose tolerance due to compensation by responder islet populations, while male MafAS64F/+ mice display glucose intolerance.&lt;SEP&gt;Improved glucose tolerance observed in mice with reduced p21Cip1 in the setting of constitutively active Akt signaling.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic phenotype measuring the ability to clear glucose from blood, assessed in senolytic treatment studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Improved glucose tolerance observed in mice with reduced p21Cip1 in the setting of constitutively active Akt signaling.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic phenotype measuring the ability to clear glucose from blood, assessed in senolytic treatment studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Improved phenotype observed in CR mice compared to AL mice during meal tolerance tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Ability to clear glucose from blood; improved by at least 6 weeks of caloric restriction in mice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-45b0e9a843120dd461f5ba7ec823e41f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7568462935971fd17472cf21ed7b8a2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d01061347ba219119efa4109d89e71f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df94a97c87154646f3ffd59dd4b312d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7d4c1829099c6037d8b8b7a83c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2e4a544176811f5b43952b185e1ed3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d286ee457150be65228790e800b5207b</data>
  <data key="d4">MafB Is Important for Pancreatic β-Cell Maintenance under a MafA-Deficient Condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562655</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">14</data>
  <data key="d8">caloric restriction / autophagy / beta cell state 2</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00059993</data>
