<node id="Sex Differences">
  <data key="d0">Sex Differences</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Biological differences between males and females that underlie pancreatic islet biology and its dysfunction, including sex-biased effects of the MAFA S64F variant.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex differences refer to the biological and regulatory distinctions between males and females that manifest across a wide range of physiological systems and disease contexts.

In the domain of cardiovascular disease, sex differences are recognized as relevant biological factors influencing the underlying mechanisms of disease development and progression, though specific mechanistic details vary across studies.

A substantial body of descriptions pertains to the role of sex differences in metabolic and pancreatic biology. These differences exert significant influence over pancreatic islet biology, beta cell resilience, glucose homeostasis, and senescent responses. Notably, islet stress responses differ between the sexes, with evidence supporting greater female beta cell resilience compared to males. Sex differences also affect metabolic homeostasis more broadly, as well as diabetes risk, gene expression patterns, and overall islet function. Biological and regulatory distinctions between males and females have been studied extensively across these interconnected domains, underscoring the multifactorial nature of sex-based variation in metabolic health.

At the molecular and epigenetic level, sex differences in chromatin accessibility have been observed in islet cells derived from non-diabetic donors. These differences predominantly affect genes located on sex chromosomes, suggesting that sex-specific gene regulation in islets is driven, at least in part, by chromosomal rather than purely hormonal mechanisms.

Collectively, sex differences represent a critical biological variable with implications spanning cardiovascular disease mechanisms, pancreatic islet function, beta cell stress resilience, epigenetic regulation, and metabolic disease risk, highlighting the importance of considering sex as a fundamental dimension in biomedical research and clinical investigation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets and sex differences in islet stress responses supporting female beta cell resilience.&lt;SEP&gt;Influence of sex on disease mechanisms in type 2 diabetes, noted as underexplored in current research.</data>
