<node id="Islet Α Cell">
  <data key="d0">Islet Α Cell</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Islet α cells are glucagon-secreting cells in the pancreatic islets, where MafB is expressed postnatally in rodents. Glucagon secretion from α cells is compromised in the absence of MafB.&lt;SEP&gt;Islet α cells are pancreatic cells responsible for glucagon secretion, whose area was found to be unchanged in MafAS64F/+ mice of both sexes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-74fc4f55c8c6d83ebd1d8dba07d90692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63a4c12087ceb7cec876e14e7fc45727</data>
  <data key="d4">Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562661</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00010712</data>
  <data key="d10">0.0</data>
  <data key="d11">0.01098156</data>
</node>
<node id="GSIS">
  <data key="d0">GSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (GSIS) is the process by which β cells secrete insulin in response to glucose. MafA and MafB are required for GSIS in human β cells.&lt;SEP&gt;GSIS (glucose-stimulated insulin secretion) is a key functional measure of β cell activity that is compromised upon MAFB depletion in human EndoC-βH1 cells and islets.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion; enhanced in isolated islets from women compared to men.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion, a key functional measure of beta cells affected by senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion; enhanced in isolated islets from women compared to men.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion, a key functional measure of beta cells affected by senescence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-74fc4f55c8c6d83ebd1d8dba07d90692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea3320816339f9f109dec0e1621420bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8304b17dc893b8c9bfb7bfdb03703396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242</data>
  <data key="d4">Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic β-cell senescence in diabetes: mechanisms, markers and therapies.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778566537</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00023861</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00017857</data>
  <data key="d11">0.02452856</data>
</node>
<node id="Human Embryonic Stem Cell-Derived Β Cells">
  <data key="d0">Human Embryonic Stem Cell-Derived Β Cells</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Human embryonic stem cell-derived β cells are laboratory-generated β cells in which MAFB is essential for insulin production, contrasting with its dispensable role in rodents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-74fc4f55c8c6d83ebd1d8dba07d90692</data>
  <data key="d4">Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562662</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">7.271e-05</data>
  <data key="d10">0.0</data>
  <data key="d11">0.0069073</data>
</node>
<node id="Juvenile Β Cell Population">
  <data key="d0">Juvenile Β Cell Population</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">The juvenile β cell population refers to human β cells in individuals under 10 years of age, characterized by MAFALow:MAFB+ expression, representing one of at least two postnatal β cell populations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-74fc4f55c8c6d83ebd1d8dba07d90692</data>
  <data key="d4">Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562663</data>
