<node id="EndoC-βH2">
  <data key="d0">EndoC-βH2</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">EndoC-βH2 is a human pancreatic β cell line derived from male human tissue, widely used in research to study β cell biology, transcription factor function, and cellular senescence.

A notable characteristic of EndoC-βH2 is that it produces only MAFB (not MAFA) and is not glucose responsive in its baseline immortalized state. The cell line requires conditional Cre recombinase–mediated excision of immortalizing transgenes — specifically hTERT and SV40 — to achieve glucose responsiveness, making it a conditionally functional model of human β cells.

EndoC-βH2 has been extensively employed in knockdown experiments involving the transcription factors MAFA and MAFB. Notably, knockdown of MAFB, but not MAFA, results in the inappropriate expression of somatostatin (SST), pancreatic polypeptide (PPY), and gastrin (GAST), highlighting MAFB's critical role in maintaining proper β cell identity and suppressing non-β cell hormone gene expression in this line.

EndoC-βH2 has also been used as a model system for studying β cell senescence. Expression of the MAFA S64F variant in this cell line leads to increased markers of senescent gene expression, upregulation of BCL-2, and the secretion of human-specific senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) factors. These findings position EndoC-βH2 as a valuable human-relevant platform for investigating how MAFA mutations and transcriptional dysregulation contribute to β cell senescence and dysfunction, with potential implications for understanding diabetes pathophysiology.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-427bdb72b4ec16ac96774e76f03ada77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbe530a83d027293c37b530e3c376973&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6c6371cdef301723817998e0efc9ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b8d7d9156faa43670557fc5e83791e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b39e1736c730a00c8edfab90d7dc64da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6</data>
