<node id="DNA Damage">
  <data key="d0">DNA Damage</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Cellular damage to DNA; sex-dependent changes to genes involved in DNA damage are observed in MafAS64F/+ males.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA damage is a cellular marker observed in male MafAS64F/+ islet β cells at 5 weeks of age, accompanying functional deficiencies and consistent with accelerated cellular aging and senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Genotoxic stress occurring in beta cells that recapitulates features of senescent beta cells accumulating in type 1 diabetes, marked by gammaH2AX.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA damage is a broad and multifaceted category of genomic and cellular stress that encompasses a wide range of insults to the genome, including single-strand breaks, double-strand breaks, replication stress, damage to non-coding regions, telomere-associated foci, and photodamage markers. It represents endogenous or exogenous lesions that threaten genomic integrity and is a well-established central driver of cellular senescence, inflammation, and aging-related pathology across diverse biological systems.

In beta cell biology, DNA damage plays a particularly critical upstream role in driving beta cell pathology. It arises from metabolic activity, secretory burden, replication stress, or programmed breaks, and serves as a key early driver of beta cell defects in diabetes. DNA damage accumulates preferentially in beta cells under metabolic stress, obesity-induced type 2 diabetes (T2D), and autoimmune type 1 diabetes (T1D) conditions, and is selectively and sustainably increased in beta cells of 3aPKO mice from neonatal growth through adulthood. Replication-associated DNA damage is also selectively elevated in beta cells under stress or following Dnmt3a loss. In neonatal alpha and beta cells, DNA damage — quantified by H2AX staining — declines from postnatal day 7 (P7) to P21, with alpha cells showing higher levels during neonatal growth, while beta cells exhibit selectively increased damage under high-fat diet (HFD) metabolic stress. DNA damage in beta cells is also experimentally induced by agents such as CX-5461 or Etoposide, detectable via the comet assay in beta-TC-6 cells.

Persistent DNA damage is associated with upregulation of cell cycle inhibitors p21Cip1 and p16Ink4a, induction of senescent-like growth arrest, and secretion of senescence-associated inflammatory cytokines, linking genomic stress to inflammatory outcomes in the pancreatic microenvironment. The cellular outcome — senescence or apoptosis — is modulated by factors such as genomic location of damage and sex hormones. Caloric restriction reduces age-dependent accumulation of 53BP1 foci in beta cells, potentially through elevated ADP-ribose levels, and poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase is recognized as necessary for DNA damage recovery.

In vascular biology, DNA damage accumulates with aging in endothelial and vascular smooth muscle cells and is exacerbated in atheroprone regions. In mammary gland tissue, DNA damage has been associated with TNFRSF11A upregulation in aged mammary glands, contributing to senescence induction.

DNA damage is detectable through established markers including γH2AX foci, telomere-associated foci (TAF), TP53BP1, 53BP1-positive foci, and H2AX pan-nuclear staining indicative of severe pre-apoptotic damage. An additional cellular mechanism involves DNA damage excretion via exosomes, proposed to maintain cellular homeostasis and delay senescence. DNA damage has also been identified as a therapeutic target, as demonstrated by its alleviation through senotherapy in cisplatin-induced ovarian injury models.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-427bdb72b4ec16ac96774e76f03ada77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f38c4d8fe95ae0a2b5cc05069361348&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d13aa78bf917b6b3ed2e772b6f010e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8304b17dc893b8c9bfb7bfdb03703396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7d4c1829099c6037d8b8b7a83c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdd484417981e099a8f308ba0994bcaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5bb73f6c7980a9f906f42f6e0e9ce27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a471400d67fa8643230222fb3576486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c5a8121875c37f2659ae345688f6afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-682da4208f0eb800bc5523bd70a84eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf5aeca434044079720fd4ae2d32c9b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f25c34949789593e96b7a57ead73f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f6e43723dd8061d1a0532981e4a43b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b0125512b4fa44926ba8a80a62b0a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68a87a3a1f3eb5d8b0e53b41df492512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dcd3bdc472cb74376c18c72cf9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002f23b1d29df711e839459b4d6f0711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f39153d9f864070e17a74724a6c8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b61717ec6764bc92fa7c3c5067d345&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b377c464e48f2efecc4b62133585c2f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28a44d52c17c29e9741a935aa76e8afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c63b5ad461844f8e5d9a90f5dfc52f1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e953ac093714735fa9b5b27463bf5dff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7af94fcbbc3d167377ece712218fa36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c461d96db4bed849ef22472fc2b0cd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35d7a41162ea60151fe17b4e60e06d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad82642ce24967122d02819b52a6f45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6204b07313b33d741c243f18ce86735</data>
  <data key="d4">Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
