<node id="53BP1">
  <data key="d0">53BP1</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">53BP1 (also known as p53 Binding Protein 1, TP53-binding protein 1, or Trp53 binding protein 1) is a well-established protein involved in the cellular response to DNA damage, particularly DNA double-strand breaks (DSBs).

As an early participant in the DNA damage response (DDR), 53BP1 recognizes and localizes to sites of double-strand DNA breaks, where it forms characteristic enriched punctate nuclear foci detectable by immunofluorescence and immunostaining techniques. These nuclear foci serve as a reliable indicator of DNA damage events and are widely used as a molecular marker in research settings.

53BP1 plays a notable role in cellular senescence, particularly in pancreatic beta cells, where it is upregulated and detected as DNA damage foci in senescent cells. It has been identified as a DNA damage marker showing increased expression in MafA S64F/+ male islets and in human EndoC-βH2 cells expressing MAFA S64F, highlighting its relevance in beta cell biology and diabetes-related research. In this context, it is used as an immunostaining marker in EndoC-βH2 cells to assess DNA damage levels.

Beyond beta cell biology, 53BP1 also accumulates in UV-damaged cells, functioning as a photodamage marker, and is referred to as TP53BP1 in the context of chromatin aberration marking during skin senescence. Collectively, 53BP1 serves as a broadly applicable and sensitive marker of DNA double-strand breaks, genomic instability, and cellular senescence across multiple tissue types and experimental models.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA damage response marker whose accumulation in beta cells marks DNA double-strand breaks in type 1 and type 2 diabetes models.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA damage marker that accumulates in aging beta cells; used as an aging hallmark in pancreatic sections.&lt;SEP&gt;53BP1 is a DNA damage marker used to quantify DNA damage in beta cells via immunohistochemistry in pancreatic sections from AL and CR mice.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA damage marker whose accumulation is reduced under CR, potentially linked to reduced cell proliferation and replication stress.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA damage response protein detected by immunohistochemistry using rabbit anti-53bp1 antibody.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA damage marker protein whose puncta frequency in beta cell nuclei was quantified by double-blind manual analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-82e08f93585dc5d50ccfbac2feb8d206&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f38c4d8fe95ae0a2b5cc05069361348&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9977f67772ceb665bdd71b221a2d4234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c0cbc7de7a8a1060e8f1608271a28c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f25c34949789593e96b7a57ead73f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58f0193e389064a60d513f13c5cad52b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68a87a3a1f3eb5d8b0e53b41df492512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-364a12c675ebfd67c468ab6fc47e6696&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dcd3bdc472cb74376c18c72cf9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cadc21c6ca916dd06510987c1b7dbf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f39153d9f864070e17a74724a6c8c6</data>
