<node id="BCL2">
  <data key="d0">BCL2</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">BCL2 is an anti-apoptotic protein and member of the BCL-2 family that plays a central role in regulating programmed cell death (apoptosis) and is prominently associated with cellular senescence across multiple tissue types and biological contexts.

In the context of pancreatic beta cells, BCL2 is notably upregulated in senescent beta cells, particularly in those expressing the MAFA S64F mutation (MAFA_S64F/+ beta cells), where it serves as a recognized senescence marker. Elevated BCL2 levels have also been observed in islets from male MafAS64F/+ mice. Its upregulation in these senescent beta cells contributes to a prosurvival phenotype, helping cells resist apoptosis despite undergoing senescence. As a result, BCL2 family members have become key targets for senolytic agents designed to selectively clear senescent beta cells, thereby restoring normal tissue homeostasis.

Beyond beta cells, BCL2 has been identified as a senescence marker and anti-apoptotic factor in at least four distinct tissue types. In idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF), BCL2 shows enhanced expression in senescent fibroblasts, implicating it in the persistence and prosurvival behavior of these pathological cells. In the bone microenvironment, BCL2 is used as part of combinatorial marker panels—such as CDKN2A+Ki67–BCL2+ and CDKN1A+Ki67–BCL2+—to define and identify senescent cells. Additionally, BCL2 expression is increased in age-associated and neurodegeneration-associated neuronal and glial cells, suggesting a broader role in protecting damaged or aging cells in the central nervous system from apoptotic clearance.

Collectively, BCL2 functions as both a pro-survival regulator and a reliable biomarker of cellular senescence across diverse tissue contexts, making it a significant target in therapeutic strategies aimed at eliminating senescent cells in aging and disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Anti-apoptotic protein gene targeted by miR-21 to increase apoptosis in beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Antiapoptotic protein whose mRNA is targeted by MIR21, leading to increased apoptosis in rat and human beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Anti-apoptotic gene whose mRNA levels are downregulated by miR-21 in beta cells, increasing susceptibility to apoptosis; also a target of SASP-related miRNAs.&lt;SEP&gt;Anti-apoptotic protein involved in the Bcl-2/Bax ratio pathway by which diazoxide prevents beta cell apoptosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b3deb83588a816faad5340f542e1f43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdd484417981e099a8f308ba0994bcaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d3b1de44d6b73a10793a43351e18e6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca3e5da7a4fe15854dde6289fd15667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b773584b36e5b4845bedffea9a1b6add&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f6e43723dd8061d1a0532981e4a43b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6215695e8bd809fd02cb8b9b7b4123ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-732255e9544dde6a7dbc319c61cd8e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10bdc47e4d027606476b48a401061a27</data>
