<node id="LMNB1">
  <data key="d0">LMNB1</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">LMNB1 is the gene encoding Lamin B1, a nuclear integrity protein that plays a critical role in maintaining nuclear lamina structure and genomic organization. It is widely recognized as a established marker of cellular senescence, with its decreased expression documented across multiple biological contexts and tissue types.

As a senescence marker, loss of nuclear LMNB1 expression has been documented in at least five distinct tissue types. These include pancreatic β cells, bone marrow, neuronal and glial cells, as well as skin-derived cells such as human keratinocytes and mouse epidermal cells. In the context of skin biology, LMNB1 is specifically lost following repeated UVB exposure in human keratinocytes and mouse epidermis, and is also downregulated in skin chemical carcinogenesis models, highlighting its role in stress-induced and oncogene-associated senescence.

In pancreatic biology, LMNB1 downregulation has been specifically identified as a senescence marker in MAFAS64F-expressing human β cells and in male MafAS64F/+ mouse islets, linking its reduced expression to a diabetes-associated mutation in the MAFA transcription factor. This positions LMNB1 as a molecular indicator of pathological β cell aging in the context of MAFA dysfunction.

At the mechanistic level, depletion of LMNB1/Lamin B1 leads to loss of lamina-associated domains (LADs), which are regions of chromatin tethered to the nuclear periphery. This structural disruption results in broad changes in gene expression during senescence, suggesting that LMNB1 loss is not merely a passive marker but an active contributor to the transcriptional and epigenetic reprogramming that characterizes the senescent state.

Overall, LMNB1 serves as a broadly applicable, multi-tissue biomarker of cellular senescence, with functional implications for nuclear architecture and gene regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene encoding Lamin B1, a nuclear lamina protein whose loss is associated with beta cell aging.&lt;SEP&gt;LMNB1 (Lamin B1) is a nuclear envelope protein whose levels are measured in beta cells; CR sustains higher LMNB1 levels compared to AL conditions.</data>
