<node id="X Chromosome">
  <data key="d0">X Chromosome</data>
  <data key="d1">Tissue</data>
  <data key="d2">The X chromosome is proposed as a source of sex chromosome-linked candidate genes potentially influenced by MAFAS64F, found differentially expressed in 5-week-old MafAS64F/+ male islets.&lt;SEP&gt;The X chromosome is a sex chromosome proposed as a focus for identifying sex chromosome-linked candidate genes differentially expressed in MafAS64F/+ male islets.&lt;SEP&gt;The X chromosome is a sex chromosome found in humans and other mammals, playing a central role in sex determination and contributing to a wide range of biological differences between males and females.

In the context of disease research, the X chromosome is recognized as highly relevant to studies of sex differences in disease mechanisms, given that its gene content and regulatory features differ between sexes. In females, who carry two copies of the X chromosome, one copy undergoes X-chromosome inactivation; however, certain genes escape this inactivation process. Notably, KDM6A is one such gene that escapes X inactivation, thereby contributing to sex-specific gene expression patterns in islet cells and potentially influencing sex-biased disease susceptibility.

Gene expression analyses further underscore the functional significance of the X chromosome in sex differences. GTEx-based analysis of the human transcriptome has revealed higher overall gene expression from the X chromosome in females compared to males. This pattern is also observed specifically in human pancreatic islets, where females exhibit both higher X-linked gene expression and higher DNA methylation on the X chromosome relative to males, suggesting distinct epigenetic regulation between the sexes at this locus.

Beyond gene expression, the complement of X chromosomes has been shown to influence adiposity and metabolism in mice, indicating that the number and dosage of X-linked genes directly affect metabolic traits independent of hormonal factors. This finding highlights the X chromosome's broader physiological importance beyond its role in sex determination, pointing to its contribution to metabolic regulation and potentially to sex differences in conditions such as obesity and metabolic disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex chromosome subject to X inactivation in mammals, with escape from inactivation studied in mice and humans; number affects adiposity.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex chromosome from which KDM6A is expressed, contributing to sex differences in T cell biology and potentially islet function.</data>
