<node id="Testosterone">
  <data key="d0">Testosterone</data>
  <data key="d1">Tissue</data>
  <data key="d2">Testosterone is a sex hormone that affects islet cell function; its levels were not markedly altered in MafAS64F/+ males and were similar to age-matched females during the phenotypic window.&lt;SEP&gt;Testosterone is a sex hormone proposed to not have an impact on divergent MAFAS64F-related disease processes in humans.&lt;SEP&gt;Testosterone is a male sex hormone (gonadal hormone) with a broad range of physiological roles spanning reproductive biology, cardiovascular health, and metabolic function.

In the context of pancreatic biology, testosterone plays an emerging and significant role in pancreatic beta cell function and insulin secretion. It produces measurable differences in islet function in vivo, contributing to sex-specific islet biology. When administered exogenously, testosterone has been shown to improve beta-cell function, suggesting therapeutic potential in metabolic disorders.

Testosterone also plays a notable role in developmental epigenetic programming. Testicular testosterone during male development is implicated in establishing sex-specific epigenetic patterns that influence islet biology, contributing to long-term differences in pancreatic function between males and females.

In the cardiovascular domain, testosterone demonstrates protective effects against cellular aging. Specifically, it ameliorates vascular senescence and antagonizes DNA-damage-induced senescence in cardiomyocytes, including that induced by doxorubicin, a chemotherapeutic agent known to cause cardiotoxicity. These findings highlight testosterone's potential role in mitigating vascular aging and protecting cardiac muscle cells from senescence-related damage.

Collectively, testosterone's functions extend well beyond its classical role as a male reproductive hormone, encompassing regulatory influences over pancreatic endocrine function, developmental epigenetic programming, and cardiovascular cellular aging, making it a hormone of broad physiological and clinical significance.&lt;SEP&gt;Male gonadal hormone with sex-specific blood concentrations that influence islet function in vivo; testicular testosterone during development may cause epigenetic programming in males.</data>
