<node id="β Cell Proliferation">
  <data key="d0">β Cell Proliferation</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">β cell proliferation rates were lower in MafAS64F/+ mice relative to WT littermates, potentially contributing to the decrease in β cell area observed in both sexes.&lt;SEP&gt;β cell proliferation refers to the replication of pancreatic beta cells, which ceases substantially in humans before MAFA expression and is decreased in male and female MafAS64F/+ mice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-63a4c12087ceb7cec876e14e7fc45727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b39885796d53f3f69a4452a58c07c2d</data>
  <data key="d4">Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778562682</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">0</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell / MAFA / MAFB</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00010621</data>
  <data key="d10">0.0</data>
  <data key="d11">0.01182989</data>
</node>
<node id="MafA Binding Site">
  <data key="d0">MafA Binding Site</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">The MafA binding site is a conserved sequence within the 5′-flanking sequences of the Region 3 transcription control domain where the MafAS64F protein likely binds in an autoregulatory manner.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-63a4c12087ceb7cec876e14e7fc45727</data>
  <data key="d4">Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
