  <data key="d9">0.00066764</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00230863</data>
  <data key="d11">0.01539323</data>
</node>
<node id="DNA Damage Response (DDR)">
  <data key="d0">DNA Damage Response (DDR)</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">The DNA damage response (DDR) is a cellular mechanism to address DNA damage, characterized by markers such as phosphorylated histone H2AX (gH2AX) and 53BP1 recruitment to chromatin.&lt;SEP&gt;A cellular stress response pathway; several DDR genes are differentially expressed in β-cells of Atf6β−/− mice and are regulated in part by p53/p21 signaling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9977f67772ceb665bdd71b221a2d4234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40e720fe9c9efb2c2c2dedf6638ae8f4</data>
  <data key="d4">Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778564588</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">3</data>
  <data key="d8">cellular senescence / CDKN2A / CDKN1A</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00024168</data>
