<node id="Chromatin Remodeling">
  <data key="d0">Chromatin Remodeling</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Chromatin remodeling is a process involved in senescence progression that influences gene expression, metabolism, autophagy, and SASP in senescent cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromatin remodeling refers to the dynamic alteration of chromatin structure and organization, serving as a fundamental epigenetic process that regulates transcription factor access to DNA, thereby controlling gene expression across a wide range of biological contexts.

At the cellular level, chromatin remodeling plays a critical role in safeguarding genomic stability and is essential for successful DNA replication and transcription during different phases of the cell cycle. By modulating chromatin organization, this process ensures proper coordination of cell cycle progression and maintenance of genome integrity.

Chromatin remodeling is also closely associated with cellular senescence. In senescent cells, changes in chromatin architecture characterize the senescent state and enforce cell cycle arrest, particularly at E2F target gene loci. As part of the broader senescence program, senescent cells progressively acquire remodeling of gene regulatory networks, contributing to the establishment and maintenance of the senescent phenotype. These chromatin-level changes are furthermore relevant to the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) and to beta-cell aging, highlighting its significance in tissue-specific aging processes.

Additionally, chromatin remodeling exhibits sex-specific differences in islet cells. This includes the involvement of specific histone demethylases, KDM6A and KDM5D, which mediate sex-specific chromatin remodeling activity in pancreatic islet cells, underscoring the role of this process in sex-based differences in metabolic and endocrine tissue function.

Overall, chromatin remodeling represents a multifaceted regulatory mechanism that integrates cell cycle control, genomic stability, senescence programming, and sex-specific gene regulation, with particular relevance to aging and endocrine biology.&lt;SEP&gt;Epigenetic process linked to genes downregulated in CR-HF mice including ATRX and BRD4/9.&lt;SEP&gt;Process by which chromatin remodelers like KDM6A and KDM5D influence sex-specific chromatin access to transcription factors in islet cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Process during beta-cell differentiation that may create regions of genomic instability requiring DNA methylation for protection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fa2caaffb8c37fe000f2af35e115fc27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7bb3e4a69884a1b79485b95f6f502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3982c3cb7b00162818de82027a853e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794cb97944fe847e61831fceed4d92ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25d34b513b15180c8a2d7b57ae829067&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e7d877b531b99ded5aba6df6f1635f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-822d03d11fbf61e2a25591e2663ef1ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35d7a41162ea60151fe17b4e60e06d1b</data>
