<node id="S961">
  <data key="d0">S961</data>
  <data key="d1">Drug</data>
  <data key="d2">S961 is an insulin receptor antagonist used extensively in research to model insulin resistance, hyperinsulinism, and hyperglycemia in mice.

In experimental settings, S961 is administered to male mice to induce hyperinsulinism and marked hyperglycemia, and has been shown to reduce islet β cell MAFA protein and mRNA levels. Treatment with S961 induces a sequential cascade of cellular senescence markers, specifically triggering Cdkn1a expression followed by Cdkn2a expression, alongside the development of hyperinsulinemia and hyperglycemia. The compound is also associated with the induction of acute insulin resistance and beta-cell senescence, particularly in male mice.

In terms of experimental methodology, S961 has been loaded into ALZET osmotic pumps and implanted in ICR (CD-1) mice to achieve sustained delivery. The compound was provided as a gift from Novo Nordisk, indicating its origins in pharmaceutical research.

S961 has also been employed in combination with high-fat diet (HFD) protocols to study the removal of senescent beta cells via the INK-ATTAC system, highlighting its utility in investigating beta-cell biology and senescence-related mechanisms in metabolic disease contexts.

A notable finding from studies using S961 is a sex-dependent difference in disease susceptibility: female mice demonstrate relative protection from S961-induced diabetes compared to male mice, suggesting sex-specific differences in the response to insulin receptor antagonism.

Overall, S961 serves as a well-established pharmacological tool for generating mouse models of insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes-like conditions, enabling the study of beta-cell dysfunction, senescence, and potential therapeutic interventions targeting these pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;An insulin receptor antagonist used in mouse models to induce insulin resistance and study cellular senescence in the pancreas.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin receptor antagonist used in mouse models to induce insulin resistance and senescence markers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d1c90235486a0fe738831a720e1f94ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6c6371cdef301723817998e0efc9ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b8d7d9156faa43670557fc5e83791e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8304b17dc893b8c9bfb7bfdb03703396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b861cee193cad9e0f142051047e83aae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac258601b299e0c883e8e1c4a3030d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b773584b36e5b4845bedffea9a1b6add&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6</data>
