<node id="INS">
  <data key="d0">INS</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">INS is the gene encoding insulin, a critical hormone produced by pancreatic beta cells that plays a fundamental role in glucose homeostasis and the maintenance of euglycemia. As a well-established beta cell marker gene, INS is specifically upregulated in beta cells and expressed independently of sex and race/ethnicity. It is widely used to validate and identify beta cell clusters in single-cell analyses, including scRNA-seq and snATAC-seq studies of human pancreatic islets, as well as in islet mapping studies to mark insulin-positive cells. INS expression has also been used to distinguish INS-high and INS-low beta cell subpopulations identified in human studies. Additional experimental applications include immunostaining, confocal imaging, colocalization studies, and RNAscope image analysis.

The transcriptional regulation of INS is governed by multiple factors. Key regulators include MAFA and MAFB, members of the large MAF transcription factor family, whose regulatory relationship with INS is conserved across species in pancreatic islets. The INS gene's enhancer and control element DNA sequences are employed in reporter constructs, luciferase assays, and EMSA experiments to study transcription factor binding. Knockdown of either MAFA or MAFB in human beta cell lines, including EndoC-βH1 cells, leads to measurable decreases in INS mRNA levels. INS transcription is also regulated by NEUROD1 under estrogen receptor alpha stimulation, reflecting an intersection between nuclear hormone receptor signaling and beta cell transcriptional networks. Additionally, insulin production is regulated by autophagy, and estrogen-mediated degradation of misfolded proinsulin serves as a quality control mechanism protecting functional INS output.

Due to its notably high expression in pancreatic cells, INS can contribute to ambient RNA contamination concerns in sequencing studies. Insulin protein serves as a marker for mature insulin granules; colocalization studies have noted that its association with LAMP1 is unchanged by caloric restriction. Insulin levels are measured in functional assays such as meal tolerance tests and glucose-stimulated insulin secretion assays across sex, race, and disease state.

In the context of disease, INS is central to both type 1 and type 2 diabetes pathogenesis. In type 2 diabetes (T2D), INS expression and insulin secretion are suppressed, particularly in male beta cells, with decreased secretion in response to high glucose, incretin, and KCl stimulation. In type 1 diabetes (T1D), insulin is a key autoantigen detected during stage 1 autoimmunity, and its production becomes deficient due to progressive beta cell loss. Notably, INS expression is selectively maintained in senescent beta cells, which do not acquire expression of other islet hormones, indicating that INS transcription is preserved even as broader hormonal cell identity is altered during senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin protein used as marker to identify beta cells in immunostaining experiments&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin gene expressed in beta cells; used in lineage tracing of adult insulin-producing cells.</data>
