<node id="PPY">
  <data key="d0">PPY</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">PPY, the gene encoding pancreatic polypeptide (PP), is a well-established marker gene associated with pancreatic gamma cells (also known as PP cells) within the islets of Langerhans. It is widely used across multiple single-cell and single-nucleus genomic approaches to identify and validate gamma cell populations.

In the context of single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analyses of human pancreatic islets, PPY serves as a canonical marker gene for gamma cell cluster annotation and identification. Similarly, in single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq) and chromatin accessibility analyses, PPY functions as a cell type-specific chromatin accessibility marker, used to validate and confirm the identity of gamma cell clusters.

Beyond its role as a marker, PPY encodes pancreatic polypeptide, a hormone that under normal physiological conditions is expressed exclusively in non-β cells. However, research has demonstrated that PPY can be inappropriately or ectopically expressed in β-like cells under certain conditions of transcription factor deficiency. Specifically, PPY is misexpressed in MAFBKO β-like cells (i.e., cells lacking the transcription factor MAFB) and is also upregulated upon MAFB knockdown in human EndoC-βH2 cells. This aberrant expression reflects a broader phenomenon of intraislet cell plasticity, wherein the loss of key islet transcription factors can lead to the misexpression of non-β cell hormones in β cells or β-like cells, underscoring the critical role of transcription factors such as MAFB in maintaining proper β cell identity and suppressing the expression of gamma cell markers.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic polypeptide gene used as a marker for gamma cell cluster identification in snATAC-seq.&lt;SEP&gt;PPY is a marker gene specifically upregulated in gamma cells, expressed independently of sex and race/ethnicity.&lt;SEP&gt;PPY is a marker gene for gamma cells in pancreatic islets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e6c6371cdef301723817998e0efc9ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e607d2cca86a6257f1db73bacae2c96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c0d05211cf3080af14ec050e5cc97ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7873cb15c71b20e5fc369a6f0ab52a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7bd4bfe81f828dceb60b520d85bcf2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a24341c9b63bd3b6313252fe6ed4b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87d48756321bdf8ef533359c0655a3d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da10e6374cdbd3d6e4a07d773cd172d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e42c3b77d3170e854edbbc691159609d</data>
  <data key="d4">Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778570286</data>
