<node id="Human Pancreas Analysis Program">
  <data key="d0">Human Pancreas Analysis Program</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Human Pancreas Analysis Program (HPAP) is an NIH initiative designed to improve understanding of the pancreas, islets, and autoimmunity, with a particular focus on type 1 diabetes. It functions as a multidimensional pancreatic phenotyping program, also aimed at elucidating islet dysfunction in the context of type 2 diabetes.

As a research program, HPAP has made significant contributions to the scientific community by serving as a source of primary human islets for pseudoislet generation studies. It has also contributed donor data to investigations of sex and race differences in human pancreatic islets, with at least 37 donors contributed to such studies. Through the analysis of donor islets, HPAP has revealed altered expression of genes involved in insulin granule docking and exocytosis in donors with impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) and type 2 diabetes, providing critical insights into the molecular mechanisms underlying islet dysfunction.

Overall, HPAP represents a comprehensive, multi-faceted research effort that integrates donor tissue analysis, multidimensional phenotyping, and molecular profiling to advance the understanding of pancreatic biology across a spectrum of metabolic and autoimmune conditions, including both type 1 and type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b39e1736c730a00c8edfab90d7dc64da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b67e26d82684726785f1910ba6919cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3982c3cb7b00162818de82027a853e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51601485f0924871a988aae2e5ce30bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c41ef398df926f1c888f284aff2a98e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e8cbb766d17c90c84d2a9452a51a41e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e181574d19e6f17c0fd64b0feecf8f9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86</data>
  <data key="d4">Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;The role of the beta cell in type 2 diabetes: new findings from the last 5 years.&lt;SEP&gt;Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571462</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">31</data>
  <data key="d8">islet / pancreas / snATAC-seq</data>
