<node id="ELISA">
  <data key="d0">ELISA</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">ELISA (Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay) is a widely applied biochemical technique used for the quantitative measurement of specific proteins, hormones, and cytokines across a variety of experimental contexts.

In the context of hormonal and metabolic research, ELISA has been employed to quantify serum insulin levels as well as mouse CXCL14 levels, following standard manufacturer's instructions. It has also been extensively used to measure insulin and glucagon concentrations in perifusion samples, with some studies specifically utilizing Mercodia kits for this purpose. Additionally, ELISA has been applied to measure insulin levels in isolated perifusion fractions, underscoring its utility in islet biology and pancreatic function studies.

ELISA has further been utilized to measure human GAST (gastrin) protein levels from cell lysates, with high-sensitivity formats employed per manufacturer recommendations to ensure accurate detection of low-abundance proteins.

In the field of immunology and inflammation research, ELISA has been used to quantify secreted cytokine levels, specifically IL-1β (IL-1 beta) and IL-6, in beta cells following nucleolar stress induction. This includes experimental conditions involving treatment with CX-5461 and KU60019, where ELISA served as the primary method for assessing cytokine secretion profiles.

Overall, ELISA represents a versatile and highly sensitive detection method applied across multiple biological disciplines, including metabolic research, protein quantification from cell lysates, and cytokine profiling in stress-induced cellular models. Its consistent use in adherence to manufacturer protocols highlights its reliability and standardization across diverse experimental settings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-667c57ab63de668da1132b51f1e9f411&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e72d8d21ebb3fc252c498fcbfeed6c1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f42efe8d71d6b6b69c377d2a7c44b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a7d380dafd31120f8a1af317cfb0ab1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cadc21c6ca916dd06510987c1b7dbf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d0bf8cd8c8cb69199fb8f9bf6c37e70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-787de2a35f76e08d5b7cddadbcc01954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b377c464e48f2efecc4b62133585c2f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f082082b0871749ee637b1571aa95c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e5c8949dfa6aa24ddf3e3b92d956e4</data>
  <data key="d4">Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Nucleolar stress facilitates islet β cell senescence via hijacking the DNA damage response pathways.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571186</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
