<node id="XBP1">
  <data key="d0">XBP1</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">A transcription factor mRNA that undergoes a highly specific splicing reaction upon IRE1α activation as part of the UPR.&lt;SEP&gt;XBP1 is a transcription factor generated by IRE1α-mediated splicing of XBP1 mRNA; it regulates expression of UPR target genes including ER chaperones, ERAD components, and lipid biosynthetic enzymes.&lt;SEP&gt;A transcription factor whose mRNA is induced by ATF6 and spliced by IRE1 in response to ER stress, producing a highly active transcription factor involved in UPR.&lt;SEP&gt;A transcription factor downstream of IRE1α in the UPR pathway, targeted along with IRE1α by the inhibitor 4μ8C in human donor islet experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress-dependent transcription factor upregulated in both beta cell subtypes in type 2 diabetes as part of a stress-response program.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea9634b81ecdb467a2b26648246e5b5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d13aa78bf917b6b3ed2e772b6f010e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18ba6ed2ada6acd593e0a5d2f036f947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f797167cb0737019ee587d4f66f13530&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba0d7fafdd66d2ffb133e04939d15f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;The role of the beta cell in type 2 diabetes: new findings from the last 5 years.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778570767</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">20</data>
  <data key="d8">ATF6 / β-Cells / Chromatin Immunoprecipitation</data>
  <data key="d9">0.0005361</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00089251</data>
  <data key="d11">0.01025007</data>
</node>
<node id="Unfolded Protein Response (UPR)">
  <data key="d0">Unfolded Protein Response (UPR)</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">A cellular stress response triggered by accumulation of unfolded proteins in the ER, mediated by IRE1, PERK, and ATF6, aimed at restoring cellular homeostasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The UPR is a cellular stress response mediated by IRE1, PERK, and ATF6 that initially attempts to promote cellular adaptation to ER stress but can induce a pro-apoptotic response under prolonged or severe stress.&lt;SEP&gt;The unfolded protein response is a cellular stress response pathway that is compromised in Atf6β−/− mice and is linked to induction of senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;A cellular stress response pathway with multiple sensor branches including ATF6, IRE1α, and PERK; deficiency in ATF6 and IRE1α branches leads to β-cell senescence features.&lt;SEP&gt;An intracellular signaling pathway activated during ER stress that regulates protein homeostasis and is involved in beta cell survival and diabetes pathogenesis.&lt;SEP&gt;A cellular stress response pathway activated by ER stress, involving sensors such as ATF6 and IRE1α, whose deletion in NOD mice leads to reduced diabetes incidence and altered β-cell senescence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea9634b81ecdb467a2b26648246e5b5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40e720fe9c9efb2c2c2dedf6638ae8f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d13aa78bf917b6b3ed2e772b6f010e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c625d74e06abff522b8eb3d76df6ae32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18ba6ed2ada6acd593e0a5d2f036f947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f797167cb0737019ee587d4f66f13530</data>
  <data key="d4">Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778564575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">20</data>
  <data key="d8">ATF6 / β-Cells / Chromatin Immunoprecipitation</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00076957</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00143495</data>
  <data key="d11">0.03367775</data>
</node>
<node id="ER Stress">
  <data key="d0">ER Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">ER stress is a cellular condition that activates UPR sensors and has been implicated in β-cell dysfunction and T1D disease progression.&lt;SEP&gt;ER stress, or endoplasmic reticulum stress, is a well-established hallmark of aging beta cells that increases progressively with age and is closely associated with impaired beta cell function, senescence, and the pathogenesis of diabetes.

In aging beta cells, ER stress accumulates and contributes to decreased transcription factor activity and the gradual loss of beta cell identity. This stress response is mechanistically linked to impaired autophagic flux observed in aging beta cells. Notably, caloric restriction (CR) has been shown to reduce ER stress accumulation in these cells, with CR-enhanced autophagy potentially helping to normalize beta cell function. The regulation of ER stress under caloric restriction conditions involves the ATF6 transcriptional modulator, which plays a key role in mediating the ER stress response pathway in CR beta cells.

ER stress is also subject to sex-based differences in resilience. Studies in mouse islets have demonstrated that female mouse islets exhibit improved resilience to the ER stress response compared to male islets, attributed to a larger capacity for protein production and protein folding. This sex-dependent disparity suggests that intrinsic differences in proteostatic capacity may influence susceptibility to ER stress-related beta cell dysfunction.

Overall, ER stress represents a critical intersection of aging, metabolic dysfunction, and beta cell biology, with its accumulation in aging beta cells serving as a driver of functional decline. Interventions such as caloric restriction that reduce ER stress and enhance autophagic activity are considered potential strategies to preserve beta cell identity and function during aging.&lt;SEP&gt;ER stress is a cellular stress process in beta cells that female islets resist by upregulating the UPR pathway.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea9634b81ecdb467a2b26648246e5b5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8304b17dc893b8c9bfb7bfdb03703396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68a87a3a1f3eb5d8b0e53b41df492512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002f23b1d29df711e839459b4d6f0711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f39153d9f864070e17a74724a6c8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-363f3a19905f833d29a7d1242acc6641&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f09e5d1418f8ae04b43a24b97c1d52d</data>
