<node id="M2 Macrophages">
  <data key="d0">M2 Macrophages</data>
  <data key="d1">Organism</data>
  <data key="d2">Anti-inflammatory macrophages recruited to islets by the altered β-cell secretome; promote immune surveillance, removal of terminally senesced β-cells, and protection against T1D.&lt;SEP&gt;M2 macrophages are anti-inflammatory, immunosuppressive immune cells recruited to islets by the unique secretome of senescent β-cells lacking Atf6α or Ire1α; they mediate immune surveillance resulting in reduced β-cell apoptosis and increased β-cell survival.&lt;SEP&gt;M2 macrophages are anti-inflammatory "alternatively activated" macrophages; their recruitment to islets was increased in UPR-deficient mice, associated with protection from diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Immune cells proposed to mediate immune surveillance and removal of acutely senescent β-cells, protecting non-senescent counterparts from autoimmune destruction.&lt;SEP&gt;M2 macrophages are a polarization state of macrophages with immunosuppressive functions; their adoptive transfer to prediabetic NOD mice was shown to prevent T1D, and they are implicated in the clearance of senescent β-cells.</data>
