<node id="Insulitis">
  <data key="d0">Insulitis</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Inflammatory infiltration of pancreatic islets by immune cells, occurring during the progression of type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulitis is the inflammatory infiltration of pancreatic islets that occurs during T1D progression; tamoxifen was administered to mouse pups prior to its onset to achieve β-cell-specific Atf6 deletion.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulitis refers to the infiltration of pancreatic islets by immune cells and is a hallmark of type 1 diabetes, markedly reduced in Atf6β−/− mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulitis refers to immune cell infiltration into pancreatic islets, which is inhibited by early senescence in UPR-deficient mice.&lt;SEP&gt;An inflammatory infiltration of pancreatic islets by immune cells, representing an early stage of T1D during which loss of UPR sensors may influence β-cell survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulitis is the immune cell infiltration of pancreatic islets, scored in the study as peri-insulitis, non-aggressive insulitis, or aggressive insulitis based on extent of infiltration and β-cell damage.</data>
