<node id="NOD Mice">
  <data key="d0">NOD Mice</data>
  <data key="d1">Organism</data>
  <data key="d2">NOD (Non-Obese Diabetic) mice are a well-established preclinical animal model widely used in the study of type 1 diabetes (T1D) and related pancreatic β-cell biology. As a mouse model, they are particularly valued for recapitulating key aspects of T1D progression, including insulitis and immune cell infiltration of the islets.

A central area of research involving NOD mice concerns β-cell senescence. In these animals, senescent β-cells are known to accumulate over time, and these cells exhibit characteristic markers of senescence, including upregulation of p21 at both the mRNA and protein levels, as well as γ-H2AX marking, which indicates DNA damage. NOD mice are therefore used as a reference model for studying β-cell terminal and late-stage senescence, particularly in the context of unfolded protein response (UPR) deficiency.

Importantly, β-cell-specific deletion of key UPR components — namely ATF6 (Atf6α) and IRE1α (Ire1α) — in NOD mice has been shown to confer protection against T1D. UPR-deficient NOD mice also display high levels of islet infiltration by M2 macrophages and reduced terminal or late senescence in β-cells, suggesting a mechanistic link between UPR signaling, β-cell senescence, and the immune microenvironment in disease progression.

In addition to their use in genetic and molecular studies, NOD mice (typically 4- to 6-week-old males) serve as a source of peritoneal immune cells for functional assays such as transwell migration experiments, enabling investigation of immune cell behavior relevant to T1D pathogenesis. Overall, NOD mice represent a versatile and informative model for dissecting the interplay between β-cell stress responses, senescence, and autoimmune destruction in type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-obese diabetic mouse model used to study type 1 diabetes and beta-cell senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-obese diabetic mouse model used to study type 1 diabetes and beta-cell senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-obese diabetic mice used as a model for type 1 diabetes; suppression of SASP from senescent beta cells in these mice protects against diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-obese diabetic mice used as a preclinical model for studying T1D and senescent beta cell depletion.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-obese diabetic mouse model of autoimmune T1D used to study cell-type-specific DDR differences in prediabetic stages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02c41952a3f79a76f3315858bb8a14bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9634b81ecdb467a2b26648246e5b5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23c7b10bcd96e1a6a6ea025a643b2b01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40e720fe9c9efb2c2c2dedf6638ae8f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c625d74e06abff522b8eb3d76df6ae32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18ba6ed2ada6acd593e0a5d2f036f947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f797167cb0737019ee587d4f66f13530&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-353703794bc6a501b5c11f84a2064293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6215695e8bd809fd02cb8b9b7b4123ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1628eba511291c52eab3e72ef0fd0239&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad82642ce24967122d02819b52a6f45</data>
