<node id="ATF6">
  <data key="d0">ATF6</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">ATF6 (activating transcription factor-6) is an endoplasmic reticulum (ER)-membrane localized protein and a key sensor branch of the unfolded protein response (UPR). Upon proteolytic cleavage in the Golgi apparatus, ATF6 functions as an active transcription factor, upregulating genes encoding ER chaperones, ER-associated degradation (ERAD) components, and XBP1, thereby maintaining ER proteostasis under conditions of cellular stress.

As a UPR sensor, ATF6 plays several regulatory roles beyond canonical ER stress signaling. It suppresses CREB-dependent transcription of Cdkn1a and inhibits CREB-dependent transcription of gluconeogenic enzymes, indicating its involvement in cell cycle regulation and metabolic control. Additionally, ATF6 inhibition has been shown to increase LIF expression in NIT1 cells, suggesting broader signaling interactions within the UPR network.

ATF6 is particularly significant in pancreatic β-cell biology, where it exerts prosurvival homeostatic functions related to ER proteostasis, β-cell survival, and proliferation. The specific loss of ATF6 in β-cells has been associated with the development of cellular senescence features. Notably, ATF6 protein levels are significantly diminished in β-cells of individuals with type 1 diabetes (T1D), as demonstrated by immunofluorescence assay and single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis, implicating ATF6 in T1D progression.

ATF6 is the protein product of the Atf6 gene and can be detected using anti-ATF6 antibodies in pancreatic tissue sections, a method employed to confirm β-cell-specific deletion. In mouse models, ATF6 has been targeted for deletion using tamoxifen-induced Cre recombinase activity, enabling conditional, cell-type-specific studies of its function in vivo.&lt;SEP&gt;Atf6 is a gene involved in the unfolded protein response (UPR), whose deletion in β-cells was studied for its effects on diabetes progression and insulitis in NOD mice.&lt;SEP&gt;ATF6 is a protein folding and ER stress response gene with higher expression in CR state 2 beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A transcription factor associated with ER homeostasis and stress response, linked to increased gene expression in CR beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptional modulator involved in regulation of ER stress, cell homeostasis, and autophagy in CR beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress-dependent transcription factor upregulated in both beta cell subtypes in type 2 diabetes as part of a stress-response program.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea9634b81ecdb467a2b26648246e5b5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23c7b10bcd96e1a6a6ea025a643b2b01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9e53f09e3b38fb66b0f093b0768d32a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40e720fe9c9efb2c2c2dedf6638ae8f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8dfde3b4ec67624c5a1e8f788a0f0311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c625d74e06abff522b8eb3d76df6ae32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f797167cb0737019ee587d4f66f13530&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-353703794bc6a501b5c11f84a2064293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08e345f1e7ff9529c2ab94760dcb50f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002f23b1d29df711e839459b4d6f0711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba0d7fafdd66d2ffb133e04939d15f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;The role of the beta cell in type 2 diabetes: new findings from the last 5 years.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778570767</data>
