<node id="γ-H2AX">
  <data key="d0">γ-H2AX</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorylated histone H2A member X; a key marker of the DNA damage response and cellular senescence, found gradually increased in β-cells of Atf6β−/− mice and elevated in 5-week-old Ire1αβ−/− mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorylated histone H2AX; forms foci as part of pseudo-DNA damage response (pseudo-DDR) during nucleolar stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorylated histone H2AX, a marker of DNA damage foci formation; its foci are induced in beta cells following CX-5461 and ActD exposure, implicating DNA damage response activation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40e720fe9c9efb2c2c2dedf6638ae8f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f008854d459401e11c35e6bd2541318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e33cd1d99c96a7f6ffdd0fbea902670</data>
  <data key="d4">Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Nucleolar stress facilitates islet β cell senescence via hijacking the DNA damage response pathways.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571122</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">76</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell senescence / DNA Damage / DNA damage response</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00027488</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00012346</data>
  <data key="d11">0.00935032</data>
</node>
<node id="Cell Cycle Arrest">
  <data key="d0">Cell Cycle Arrest</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">A feature of senescent cells; both Atf6β−/− and Ire1αβ−/− islet cells show decreased G0/G1 phase percentage and increased G2/M phase percentage at 6 weeks of age.&lt;SEP&gt;Cell cycle arrest is a fundamental biological process characterized by the cessation of cell division and the halting of cell cycle progression. It is most widely recognized as a defining hallmark of cellular senescence — the state in which cells permanently exit the cell cycle and cease to proliferate. In the context of senescence, cell cycle arrest represents a persistent and often irreversible inhibition of cell proliferation, triggered by a variety of cellular stresses, genomic damage, or oncogenic signals.

The molecular mechanisms underlying cell cycle arrest are primarily mediated by cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK) inhibitors, most notably p21 (P21CIP1/p21Cip1), p16 (P16INK4A/p16Ink4a), and P57KIP2. These inhibitors suppress cell proliferation by blocking the activity of cyclin-dependent kinases that would otherwise drive the cell through successive stages of the cell cycle. The p16INK4a–Rb pathway plays a particularly important role in enforcing the stable arrest associated with senescence, and ribosome biogenesis defects have also been implicated in maintaining this halted state. Experimentally, cell cycle arrest can be indicated by reduced BrdU incorporation alongside upregulation of CDK inhibitors.

Cell cycle arrest is closely linked to the DNA damage response, wherein cellular stress or genomic damage activates signaling cascades that enforce a stable and often irreversible arrest. This mechanistic connection highlights cell cycle arrest not only as a response to genotoxic stress but also as a protective mechanism preventing the propagation of damaged or dysfunctional cells.

Beyond classic senescence, cell cycle arrest has been observed in additional biological contexts. P57KIP2-driven cell cycle arrest plays a role in inducing trophoblast fusion, and cell cycle arrest is associated with BRAF-V600E mutation in naevi senescence. It is also observed in dedifferentiated acinar cells during acinar-to-ductal metaplasia, underscoring its broader relevance across diverse physiological and pathological processes.

Overall, cell cycle arrest serves as a central and defining feature of the senescent state, coordinating with other senescence-associated mechanisms to establish and maintain permanent growth suppression.&lt;SEP&gt;Irreversible cell cycle arrest is a hallmark phenotype of cellular senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanism of cellular senescence involving irreversible arrest of cell division.&lt;SEP&gt;Temporary halt of cell cycle progression induced by DNA damage to allow time for repair.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells with modest DNA damage may temporarily pause the cell cycle to repair damage before resuming cell cycle or differentiating.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40e720fe9c9efb2c2c2dedf6638ae8f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c0cbc7de7a8a1060e8f1608271a28c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7bb3e4a69884a1b79485b95f6f502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25d34b513b15180c8a2d7b57ae829067&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca3e5da7a4fe15854dde6289fd15667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a471400d67fa8643230222fb3576486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b02375738a9ebacd53446fec2077ee76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ff588c6efe73ecb07c6b95acb392e92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf5aeca434044079720fd4ae2d32c9b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32fd907a01d621388ff41b9b47bfa34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de697a7536ea955d878a95580bf8aa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58f0193e389064a60d513f13c5cad52b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408</data>
  <data key="d4">Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778564588</data>
