<node id="Mdm2">
  <data key="d0">Mdm2</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">A gene in the p53 signaling pathway substantially upregulated in β-cells of Atf6β−/− mice, involved in regulation of cell cycle and cell survival.&lt;SEP&gt;MDM2 is a multifunctional protein that serves as a critical regulator of the tumor suppressor protein p53. It functions as an E3 ubiquitin ligase, a enzymatic role that enables it to target p53 for ubiquitin-mediated degradation, thereby controlling p53 protein levels and activity within the cell.

A key aspect of MDM2's biological significance lies in its regulatory axis with p53, which can be modulated by the tumor suppressor proteins p19Arf (in mice) and p14Arf (in humans). Alterations to the MDM2-p53 axis by these ARF proteins play an important role in cell cycle inhibition, suggesting that MDM2 acts as a pivotal node in the control of cell proliferation and the response to oncogenic stress.

MDM2 has also been identified as a significant component in pathways related to pancreatic beta-cell health and senescence. Specifically, MDM2 is involved in the pathway through which palmitic acid hydroxy stearic acids (PAHSAs) exert their protective effects on pancreatic beta cells under conditions of metabolic stress. PAHSAs have been shown to target the MDM2-p53 pathway to prevent or reverse beta-cell senescence, including in non-obese diabetic (NOD) mice, a widely used animal model for type 1 diabetes research.

Collectively, MDM2 occupies a central role at the intersection of cell cycle regulation, tumor suppression, and metabolic stress response, making it a protein of considerable interest in both oncology and metabolic disease research.&lt;SEP&gt;MDM2 forms a dysfunctional axis with p53 in adipocytes, contributing to aging-related metabolic complications by induction of lipodystrophy.&lt;SEP&gt;Mdm2 is involved in the Mdm2/p53 regulatory axis through which PAHSAs reduce cellular senescence and protect pancreatic beta cells from metabolic stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Reported as a negative regulator of cellular senescence in adipose tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Negative regulator of p53; involved in the PAHSAs-mediated protection of beta cells from metabolic stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40e720fe9c9efb2c2c2dedf6638ae8f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b861cee193cad9e0f142051047e83aae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7d4c1829099c6037d8b8b7a83c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71a6a7a3a4894dbd7d979b7e5766d5a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce1a7b385e0f22ca823c850de9bf8a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea13fe99fbc01cb6036ecddcda0aae8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408</data>
  <data key="d4">Stress-induced β cell early senescence confers protection against type 1 diabetes.</data>
