<node id="NOD Mouse">
  <data key="d0">NOD Mouse</data>
  <data key="d1">Organism</data>
  <data key="d2">NOD (non-obese diabetic) mouse is a mouse model used to study immune cell migration and macrophage behavior in the context of T1D.&lt;SEP&gt;The NOD (Non-Obese Diabetic) mouse is a well-established animal model widely used in autoimmune diabetes research, particularly for studying type 1 diabetes (T1D). It serves as a key experimental system for investigating the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying autoimmune-mediated destruction of pancreatic beta cells.

One notable characteristic of the NOD mouse model is its sex-biased disease manifestation: type 1 diabetes develops at a higher penetrance in female mice compared to males, making it a notable exception to the general pattern of female protection observed in other diabetes models.

A significant area of research using the NOD mouse involves the study of beta cell senescence. Within this model, a minority subpopulation of beta cells undergoes cellular senescence, and these senescent beta cells have been characterized by their expression of established senescence markers, including p21Cip1 and p16Ink4a. These senescent beta cell subpopulations have been identified and profiled in the context of autoimmune diabetes progression.

Mechanistically, senescent beta cells in the NOD mouse preferentially upregulate the prosurvival protein BCL-2, and studies using this model have been instrumental in establishing the requirement of BCL-2 for maintaining the prosurvival state of these senescent cells. As a result, the NOD mouse has been used to evaluate the efficacy of senolytic agents (which selectively eliminate senescent cells) and senomorphic agents (which modulate the senescent phenotype without eliminating the cells) as potential therapeutic strategies targeting senescent beta cells in type 1 diabetes.

Overall, the NOD mouse represents a critical preclinical tool for understanding the interplay between autoimmunity, beta cell senescence, and survival signaling pathways in type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-obese diabetic mouse model used to study type 1 diabetes and cellular senescence in islets.&lt;SEP&gt;NOD (Non-Obese Diabetic) mouse is an autoimmune diabetes mouse model in which senescent beta cells accumulate during T1D development.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-obese diabetic mouse model used in preclinical studies of T1D, in which senolytic compounds ABT-199 and ABT-737 depleted senescent beta cells and protected against T1D.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-obese diabetic mouse model used to study autoimmune Type 1 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3838ae3de5236c272eda0072cc83f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8304b17dc893b8c9bfb7bfdb03703396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-add59972a262992e4bde6910520c0cdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b861cee193cad9e0f142051047e83aae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdd484417981e099a8f308ba0994bcaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-184abd4f45a78238743890eb9d4f8c56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32fd907a01d621388ff41b9b47bfa34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86</data>
