<node id="P53">
  <data key="d0">P53</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">P53 is a well-characterized tumor suppressor protein with diverse and critical roles in cellular biology, particularly in regulating cell cycle progression, senescence, apoptosis, and DNA damage responses. It functions as a multifaceted integrator of signals arising from DNA damage, oncogenic stress, nucleolar stress, and metabolic perturbations, coordinating cell fate decisions across a wide range of tissues and disease contexts.

As a cell-cycle inhibitor and senescence marker, P53 is involved in the early initiation of cellular arrest during senescence. It is activated during cellular senescence in contexts such as MAFAS64F-induced senescence in pancreatic β cells, accumulates during oncogene-induced senescence, and is upregulated following TIF-IA depletion in beta cells. P53 has also been implicated in mediating regenerative senescence in the injured heart and has been identified as a senescence marker in peripheral mononuclear blood cells and in thoracic and abdominal aortic aneurysm models. Its activity is notably increased in beta cells in type 2 diabetes and congenital hyperinsulinism.

P53 plays a central role in DNA damage-repair (DDR) processes and serves as a critical DDR-related signaling protein upregulated during nucleolar stress-induced beta cell senescence. It is a substrate of ATM kinase and is activated in response to both DNA damage and nucleolar stress. P53 also mediates the release of HMGB1, underscoring its involvement in the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) and cell-nonautonomous signaling. It regulates the balance between senescence and apoptosis in beta cells, and its selective ablation in pancreatic beta cells has been studied across genetic, dietary, and pharmacological models of diabetes mellitus.

In terms of molecular interactions, P53 is repressed by DNMT1 to maintain progenitor cell survival during pancreatic organogenesis, and is targeted by miRNAs as a key cell cycle arrest regulator. It regulates c-MAF together with Prox-1, influencing the expression of Crystallin genes. Its binding to FOXO4 can be disrupted by senolytic peptides, representing a therapeutic avenue for selectively eliminating senescent cells. P53 is also targeted through the MDM2/P53 pathway by PAHSAs (palmitic acid hydroxystearic acids) in the context of beta-cell senescence, highlighting its relevance as a pharmacological target in metabolic disease. In experimental research, P53 has been targeted using a mouse monoclonal antibody (1C12).&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor suppressor protein with activity in beta cells following DNA double-strand breaks caused by type 2 diabetes and congenital hyperinsulinism.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor suppressor protein involved in mediating apoptotic DNA damage response and cell-cycle arrest in islet cells under metabolic stress.&lt;SEP&gt;p53 is a well-established tumor suppressor protein encoded by the *TP53* gene in humans and the *Trp53* gene in mice. It plays a central role in numerous cellular processes, most notably the regulation of cell cycle arrest, senescence, and the suppression of oncogenic activity across a wide range of tissue types.

One of p53's prominent functions is its involvement in cellular senescence and the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP). Its activation is a hallmark of senescence and other forms of cell cycle arrest, and it serves as a recognized senescence marker protein. In this context, p53 expression can be induced by CX-5461 exposure and suppressed by ATM inhibitor KU60019 in beta cells. Similarly, p53 is upregulated in primary islet cells upon nucleolar stress induced by CX-5461, contributing to a pseudo-DNA damage response (pseudo-DDR).

In metabolic and endocrine tissues, p53 has a crucial role in the regulation of insulin resistance in adipose tissue and is subject to Mdm2/p53 regulatory mechanisms in beta cells, where its activity is also linked to oncogenic RAS signaling. In the context of beta-cell biology, p53 hypomorphism combined with LIG4 loss leads to DNA damage accumulation, accelerated senescence, and reduced beta-cell mass, underscoring its protective role in maintaining pancreatic cell integrity.

p53 is also upregulated in mouse and human lung tissue upon senescence and is associated with cell cycle arrest in the setting of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). In the skin, p53 is expressed in melanocytes within solar lentigines alongside CDKN2A and CDKN1A, suggesting a coordinated senescence response in sun-exposed tissue. Additionally, p53 expression is increased in trophoblasts as a hallmark of developmental senescence, highlighting its role beyond pathological contexts and into normal developmental programs.

Collectively, p53 functions as a critical integrator of stress signals across diverse tissue types, mediating responses to DNA damage, nucleolar stress, oncogenic activation, and developmental cues through the regulation of senescence and cell cycle arrest.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-18ba6ed2ada6acd593e0a5d2f036f947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01e46f30e47340f5c5b95376196543ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b861cee193cad9e0f142051047e83aae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdd484417981e099a8f308ba0994bcaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a471400d67fa8643230222fb3576486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf5aeca434044079720fd4ae2d32c9b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1628eba511291c52eab3e72ef0fd0239&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-735ea17eaeb5e3524d0871e16116e986&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9977f67772ceb665bdd71b221a2d4234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2caaffb8c37fe000f2af35e115fc27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f008854d459401e11c35e6bd2541318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bb74bfaf0604b4b9f891e16fc9f0ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ab370ab273e97005ced113e59cc48a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e5c8949dfa6aa24ddf3e3b92d956e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6204b07313b33d741c243f18ce86735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9977f67772ceb665bdd71b221a2d4234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2caaffb8c37fe000f2af35e115fc27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d13aa78bf917b6b3ed2e772b6f010e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25d34b513b15180c8a2d7b57ae829067&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b773584b36e5b4845bedffea9a1b6add&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea13fe99fbc01cb6036ecddcda0aae8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5bb73f6c7980a9f906f42f6e0e9ce27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ff588c6efe73ecb07c6b95acb392e92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f25c34949789593e96b7a57ead73f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db03a7d3c861658f2beac422f86b21ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a0590fd9be1db550e8a84c0781e8955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-787de2a35f76e08d5b7cddadbcc01954</data>
