<node id="scRNA-seq">
  <data key="d0">scRNA-seq</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) is a high-resolution genomic method designed to analyze gene expression at the level of individual cells, enabling researchers to capture transcriptional heterogeneity that would otherwise be masked in bulk sequencing approaches. By resolving gene expression at single-cell resolution, scRNA-seq provides detailed insights into the diversity of cellular states within complex tissues.

In the context of pancreatic and diabetes research, scRNA-seq has been applied extensively to study human and murine pancreatic islet cells. It has been used to profile gene expression across multiple dimensions, including sex, race, and disease status, revealing transcriptional states specific to distinct human islet cell types. Notably, scRNA-seq has been employed to examine sex and race differences in human pancreatic islets, providing insights into how these biological and demographic variables influence islet cell transcriptomes. The method has also been used to profile gene expression in human pancreatic islet cells from donors with and without Type 2 Diabetes (T2D), as well as to measure gene expression profiles of islet cells and validate chromatin accessibility findings derived from complementary techniques such as snATAC-seq. In studies of Type 1 Diabetes (T1D), scRNA-seq has revealed reduced unfolded protein response (UPR) transcriptome expression in human T1D β-cells and diminished ATF6 mRNA levels, highlighting disease-associated transcriptional alterations.

scRNA-seq has also been instrumental in investigating beta cell senescence, detecting gene expression changes in senescent beta cells and revealing cell-to-cell variability in the expression of senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) factors. Additionally, the method has been applied to analyze DNA damage response (DDR) programs in alpha and beta cells under various conditions, as well as to assess DDR dynamics and cell-cycle progression in neonatal P7 C57BL/6J islets.

Beyond cell-state profiling, scRNA-seq has been utilized to assess cell type-specific transcription factor expression, to generate transcriptional profiles of islet cell types alongside complementary sequencing modalities, and to map gene regulatory networks governing islet cell identity and function. Collectively, scRNA-seq serves as a versatile and powerful tool in pancreatic and diabetes research, enabling multidimensional transcriptional analyses across diverse biological and disease contexts.</data>
