<node id="PAX4">
  <data key="d0">PAX4</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">PAX4 is a paired box homeodomain transcription factor that plays a critical role in pancreatic development, particularly in the differentiation and lineage commitment of insulin-producing β cells in the mammalian pancreas.

During pancreatic development, PAX4 is expressed in pancreatic epithelial cells, where it contributes to the formation of islet cells and the specification of the β-cell lineage. Its expression is transient, disappearing late in development and ceasing after birth, suggesting it serves a primarily developmental rather than a maintenance role. PAX4 is closely related to PAX6, another paired box transcription factor, though PAX4 does not act as specifically or in as coordinated a manner as MafA and MafB in pancreatic contexts.

PAX4 functions within a regulatory network that governs endocrine pancreas development. It acts in concert with Nkx2.2 to promote β-cell differentiation and operates in opposition to ARX, a transcription factor that drives alternative endocrine cell fates. This antagonistic relationship between PAX4 and ARX is considered essential for the proper initiation of pancreatic β-cell differentiation. Notably, PAX4 expression levels are unaffected in MafB-deficient mutants, indicating that its regulation is independent of MafB signaling.

PAX4 has also been identified as a transcription factor motif that is highly enriched in β cells, with a reported fold enrichment greater than 1.5 and a -log10 false discovery rate (FDR) exceeding 50, underscoring its strong association with the β-cell transcriptional identity.

Overall, PAX4 is recognized as an essential transcriptional regulator in the early stages of pancreatic β-cell development, integrating signals from multiple pathways to ensure proper endocrine cell fate determination.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor involved in combinatorial profiles predicting mature and functional human islet alpha and beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;PAX4 is a transcription factor motif highly enriched in beta cells (fold enrichment &gt; 1.5, −log10 FDR &gt; 50).</data>
