<node id="SLC30A8">
  <data key="d0">SLC30A8</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">SLC30A8, also known as Slc30a8 or ZnT8, is an islet-specific zinc transporter gene that plays a critical role in pancreatic function, particularly in β-cell biology. It encodes Zinc Transporter 8 (ZnT8), a protein responsible for facilitating zinc (Zn2+) accumulation from the cytoplasm into intracellular vesicles, specifically insulin granules. Within these granules, zinc is essential for insulin crystallization, dense-core granule formation, and overall insulin maturation and storage. The gene is expressed in both α- and β-cells of the pancreatic islets and has been implicated in the pathophysiology of both type 1 diabetes (T1D) and type 2 diabetes (T2D).

In the context of T1D, ZnT8 serves as a recognized autoantigen found in beta cell extracellular vesicles (EVs) and is associated with T1D autoimmunity seropositivity, making it a clinically relevant marker of autoimmune diabetes.

Regarding transcriptional regulation, SLC30A8 is a mutual target gene of the transcription factors MafA and MafB. During pancreatic development, its expression is regulated by MafB, and is compromised in MafB mutant glucagon-positive and insulin-positive cells. In adult β-cells, regulation shifts primarily to MafA; SLC30A8 is notably the first gene found to be significantly reduced as early as 2 weeks in MafAΔPanc (MafaΔpanc) islets, with both mRNA and protein expression markedly diminished. Its expression is further reduced in MafAΔPanc;MafB−/− mutant pancreata compared to wild-type, indicating an additive or cooperative regulatory relationship between MafA and MafB.

Functional studies using Slc30a8 knockout models (Slc30a8−/−) have demonstrated deficient mature insulin granule formation, a phenotype resembling that observed in MafaΔpanc mice, underscoring its importance to β-cell vesicle maturation.

In the context of T2D, SLC30A8 is located at a T2D risk locus, with the index variant rs80244329 identified in males. Regulatory variants associated with T2D overlap with differentially accessible chromatin regions in male acinar cells, suggesting a sex-specific regulatory mechanism in acinar cell chromatin accessibility that may influence SLC30A8 expression and T2D susceptibility.

From a methodological standpoint, SLC30A8 is utilized as a gene probe in in situ hybridization assays and as an antibody target in immunolabeling and immunostaining studies of pancreatic tissue sections, with detection facilitated by a rabbit α-Slc30a8 antibody supplied by Mellitech.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene encoding zinc transporter-8; loss-of-function mutations protect against type 2 diabetes by enhancing insulin secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene encoding the zinc transporter ZnT8; protective loss-of-function mutations associated with reduced type 2 diabetes risk and enhanced insulin secretion.</data>
