<node id="ISL1">
  <data key="d0">ISL1</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">ISL1 is a transcription factor with important roles in pancreatic endocrine biology. It is broadly characterized as a pan-endocrine and islet-enriched transcription factor associated with islet cell lineage commitment, and its motifs are enriched in endocrine cells as identified through chromatin accessibility profiling.

At the molecular level, ISL1 is capable of binding to the Insulin gene and plays a functional role in regulating insulin gene expression. In cell culture assays involving cotransfection with MAFA and MAFB, ISL1 demonstrates differential cooperative behavior: it provides enhanced activation of an Insulin reporter construct when paired with MAFB, but notably does not exhibit the same cooperative activation with MAFA. Specifically, ISL1 cooperates with MAFB, but not with MAFA/B or MAFB/A chimeras, to activate the Insulin -238 WT reporter. Furthermore, the ability of MAFB to enhance ISL1-driven activation of an INS-driven reporter is abolished in the MAFB/A chimera, highlighting that ISL1 is differentially recruited by MAFB compared to MAFA. These findings underscore a selective functional interaction between ISL1 and MAFB in the regulation of insulin gene transcription.

With respect to in vivo expression, ISL1 protein and mRNA levels remain relatively unaffected across several mutant mouse models. At embryonic day E18.5, ISL1 expression is unchanged in MafAΔPanc, MafB−/−, and MafAΔPanc;MafB−/− mutant pancreata compared to wild-type controls, indicating that ISL1 levels are not dependent on MafA or MafB during this developmental stage.

For experimental purposes, ISL1 protein is detected in immunolabeling studies of pancreatic tissue using a mouse α-Isl1 antibody sourced from the Developmental Studies Hybridoma Bank.&lt;SEP&gt;ISL1 is a transcription factor with enriched motifs in endocrine cells of pancreatic islets.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor upregulated in alpha cells irrespective of sex.</data>
