<node id="ARX">
  <data key="d0">ARX</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">ARX is an islet-enriched transcription factor that plays a central role in the development and adult function of pancreatic islet cells. It is most prominently recognized as a key regulator in endocrine pancreas development, where it functionally opposes Pax4, another transcription factor critical to islet cell fate determination. This antagonistic relationship between ARX and Pax4 is fundamental to the lineage commitment of distinct islet cell types.

ARX is closely associated with alpha cell identity and is widely used as a marker gene for alpha cells, as identified through single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analyses of human pancreatic islets. Its altered expression has been shown to influence the presence and/or activity of specific islet cell types, underscoring its importance in both developmental and adult islet biology. Notably, ARX's role in islet cell lineage commitment appears to be independent of MafB regulation, suggesting it operates through distinct transcriptional pathways.

In experimental settings, ARX is employed as a marker in immunohistochemistry, often used in combination with Pdx1 staining to confirm the absence of cell conversion in pancreatic islets. The antibody used in such experiments has been provided as a gift from Kunio Kitamura and Kenichirou Morohashi of Kyushu University, Japan.

From a gene regulatory network (GRN) perspective, ARX is upregulated in alpha cells regardless of sex; however, it exhibits higher network centrality in male alpha cell GRNs compared to females. This sex-dependent difference in centrality suggests that while ARX is a universal alpha cell transcription factor, its regulatory influence and connectivity within gene networks may be modulated by biological sex.&lt;SEP&gt;ARX is a marker gene for pancreatic alpha cells identified in scRNA-seq cluster analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor upregulated in alpha cells irrespective of sex; exhibits higher centrality in male alpha cell GRNs.&lt;SEP&gt;ARX is a transcription factor whose DNA methylation-mediated repression maintains pancreatic beta-cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;ARX is a marker gene specifically upregulated in alpha cells, expressed independently of sex and race/ethnicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor upregulated in alpha cells irrespective of sex, exhibiting higher centrality in male alpha cell gene regulatory networks.</data>
