<node id="INS2">
  <data key="d0">INS2</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">INS2 (Insulin 2) is a mouse gene encoding insulin, recognized as a key target in pancreatic β-cell biology and regulation. Its expression and transcriptional control have been studied extensively in the context of MafA and MafB transcription factors, as well as various genetic models.

In terms of developmental and postnatal regulation, INS2 expression is required by MafB during embryogenesis, highlighting its importance in early pancreatic development. In postnatal and adult contexts, INS2 has been analyzed by quantitative real-time PCR as part of time course studies examining MafA's role in β-cell regulation, with specific primer sequences used for precise measurement in pancreatic islets.

INS2 expression is consistently reduced under conditions of MafA deficiency. Specifically, mRNA expression of INS2 is decreased in 3-week-old MafAΔpanc islets, and its expression level is further confirmed to be diminished in MafAΔpanc islet β-cells in adult mice. It has also been identified as a target gene of MAFA through gene expression analysis in pancreatic islets of Mafa-knockout ICR mice. Additionally, INS2 expression is reduced in A0B2 and A0B0 islets compared to wild-type islets, underscoring the combined roles of MafA and MafB in maintaining normal insulin gene expression.

Beyond transcriptional regulation, INS2 plays a functional role in insulin secretion, and its reduced expression contributes to β-cell non-responder dysfunction observed in MafAS64F/+ islets, linking INS2 expression levels to impaired β-cell secretory capacity in disease-relevant genetic contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin 2 gene whose promoter was used to drive overexpression of p16Ink4a in a mouse model studying beta cell function.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin 2 gene whose promoter was used to drive overexpression of p16Ink4a in a mouse model studying beta cell function.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin gene upregulated in CR beta cells as part of beta cell identity gene expression.&lt;SEP&gt;INS2 is a beta cell identity and function marker gene with higher expression in CR state 2 beta cells.</data>
