<node id="GCG">
  <data key="d0">GCG</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">GCG, the glucagon gene, is a well-established marker gene primarily associated with pancreatic alpha cells. It encodes the hormone glucagon, a key endocrine peptide produced in the islets of Langerhans. In multiple single-cell and single-nucleus sequencing studies, GCG is consistently used to identify and validate alpha cell populations. Specifically, it serves as a cell type-specific marker in scRNA-seq cluster analyses of human pancreatic islets, where it reliably annotates alpha cell clusters. Similarly, in snATAC-seq studies examining pancreatic islet chromatin accessibility, GCG expression is employed to validate alpha cell cluster identity based on gene activity signals.

From a regulatory standpoint, the glucagon gene is transcriptionally activated by c-MAF in mature alpha cells and by MAFB during islet development, highlighting the distinct transcription factor networks that govern its expression across different developmental stages. GCG can also be misexpressed in non-alpha cell types upon deficiency of key islet transcription factors, reflecting intraislet cellular plasticity and the potential for lineage conversion within the endocrine pancreas.

In experimental contexts, Gcg expression is quantified by quantitative real-time PCR in pancreatic islets using specified primer sequences, serving as a target gene in transcriptional studies. Notably, Gcg has been identified as an endocrine marker gene significantly enriched in Gast-positive cells compared with Gast-negative β cells in S961-treated male mice, suggesting its relevance in models of insulin resistance and beta-to-alpha cell identity shifts. Collectively, GCG represents a critical molecular marker and functional gene in pancreatic endocrine biology, useful for both cell type annotation and the study of islet cell plasticity.&lt;SEP&gt;GCG is a hormone marker gene used as a chromatin accessibility marker for alpha cells in ATAC-seq analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucagon gene used as a marker for alpha cell cluster identification in snATAC-seq.&lt;SEP&gt;GCG is a marker gene specifically upregulated in alpha cells, expressed independently of sex and race/ethnicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucagon, a hormone secreted by alpha cells, measured in dynamic secretion assays across sex and race.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucagon gene expressed in alpha cells; used in lineage tracing of adult glucagon-producing cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb31cc9ec659a6d29b2ec1e3870fb57b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea3320816339f9f109dec0e1621420bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b8d7d9156faa43670557fc5e83791e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02381256ec5732a712b97ced237c2acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e607d2cca86a6257f1db73bacae2c96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c0d05211cf3080af14ec050e5cc97ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7873cb15c71b20e5fc369a6f0ab52a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7bd4bfe81f828dceb60b520d85bcf2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a24341c9b63bd3b6313252fe6ed4b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87d48756321bdf8ef533359c0655a3d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da10e6374cdbd3d6e4a07d773cd172d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cd7041acf7539c8db67413bcd98f41b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86</data>
