<node id="MAFA_S64F">
  <data key="d0">MAFA_S64F</data>
  <data key="d1">Mutation</data>
  <data key="d2">MAFA_S64F, also referred to as MAFA p.Ser64Phe, MafAS64F, or the S64F mutation, is a pathogenic missense variant in the MAFA transcription factor gene involving a serine-to-phenylalanine substitution at amino acid position 64 (c.191C&gt;T; NM_201589.3). This substitution occurs within the transactivation domain at a highly conserved residue and is classified as a gain-of-function, disease-causing variant associated with adult-onset beta-cell dysfunction.

Clinically, MAFA_S64F is linked to familial insulinomatosis, maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY), or both, depending on context and biological sex. Diabetes is more prevalent in males at approximately a 3:1 ratio, while insulinomatosis is more common in females at a 4:1 ratio. The mutation also represents at least one form of monogenic diabetes associated with DNA damage hallmarks.

At the molecular level, the S64F substitution impairs a critical priming phosphorylation event at serine 65, disrupting the normal phosphorylation cascade within the transactivation domain. This renders MAFA_S64F an underphosphorylated variant, blocking ubiquitin-mediated proteasomal degradation and resulting in increased protein stability across varying glucose concentrations. The mutant retains similar binding capacity to the INS enhancer and MARE elements but loses cooperative enhancement with GLIS3. It demonstrates enhanced transactivation capacity and oncogenic potential in beta-cell lines. Structural analyses using AlphaFold 2 predict conformational differences between the mutant and wildtype MAFA proteins. In partial phosphorylation mimicry models, residues 49, 53, 57, 61, and 65 are excluded from phosphomimetic substitution, further underscoring the mechanistic relevance of this site.

When expressed in human EndoC-βH2 cells via lentivirus, MAFA_S64F displays faster migrating properties on electrophoresis due to phosphorylation impairment, induces markers of cellular senescence, beta-cell dysfunction, and senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) development, along with altered paracrine signaling. Upregulation of BCL-2 is observed, consistent with an absence of apoptosis and a senescence-dominant cellular response. The variant has also been used in chimeric protein studies to evaluate the functional impact of the N-terminal region on ISL1 and PDX1 activation.

A prominent feature of MAFA_S64F is its sex-dependent pathophysiology. In male murine models, both heterozygous (MafAS64F/+) and homozygous (MafAS64F/S64F) mice display elevated fasting blood glucose, glucose intolerance worsening with age, premature beta-cell aging, senescence signatures, dysregulated Ca²⁺ handling, and impaired insulin secretion, without apoptosis. Female mice display divergent phenotypes consistent with sex-biased clinical outcomes observed in humans. This sex-biased islet beta-cell dysfunction is observed across both rodent models and human EndoC-βH2 cell studies, underscoring a species-overlapping mechanism.

Overall, MAFA_S64F represents a gain-of-function pathogenic variant with broad implications for understanding beta-cell senescence, dysfunction, and the etiology of both diabetes and insulinomatosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A missense mutation in the MAFA transcription factor associated with adult-onset beta cell dysfunction, with greater severity in men than women.&lt;SEP&gt;Missense mutation in MAFA associated with adult-onset beta cell dysfunction; causes premature aging and senescence predominantly in males, linked to MODY.&lt;SEP&gt;Naturally occurring mutation in MAFA that predisposes carriers to either MODY or hypoglycemia in a sex-dependent manner.&lt;SEP&gt;A sex-biased MODY-causing mutation in MAFA that induces premature aging and senescence specifically in male beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;MODY-associated variant of MAFA that induces premature aging and senescence in male beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;MODY variant of MAFA that causes sex-biased islet beta-cell dysfunction by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.</data>
