<node id="beta cell">
  <data key="d0">beta cell</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Beta cells are specialized endocrine cells located within the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas, functioning as the body's primary insulin-producing cells. Their core physiological role is to sense blood glucose levels and synthesize and secrete insulin in response, making them central mediators of glucose homeostasis, fuel metabolism, and the maintenance of euglycemia. Dysfunction, senescence, or loss of beta cells directly disrupts glucose balance and contributes to the pathogenesis of both type 1 diabetes (T1D) and type 2 diabetes (T2D), including disease progression, remission, and recovery.

Beta cells are professional secretory cells characterized by high metabolic activity, oxidative metabolism, and a significant secretory burden. They are largely post-mitotic and long-lived, sharing several characteristics with neurons, rendering them particularly vulnerable to cellular stress, DNA damage, genomic instability, aging-associated functional decline, endoplasmic reticulum stress, apoptosis, and dedifferentiation. A prominent feature of beta cell pathology is their susceptibility to senescence, which can be driven by nucleolar stress through ATM pathway activation in metabolic disease contexts. Senescent beta cells are characterized by SA-β-gal activity, elevated senescence markers, loss of LAMINB1 and HMGB1, a senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP), upregulation of anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family members, and release of extracellular vesicles with altered miRNA cargo and autoantigens.

Beta cells exhibit selective vulnerability to DNA damage, particularly during replication, and rely on efficient DNA repair before mitosis rather than high cellular turnover to maintain genomic integrity. They display elevated DNA damage during neonatal growth and lower replication rates compared to alpha cells. Under metabolic stress conditions—including high-fat diet (HFD) and diabetic states such as NOD mice and 3aPKO mice models—beta cells show preferential increases in DNA damage, terminal damage, and apoptosis. Continuous epigenetic reinforcement is required to maintain beta cell identity.

Developmentally, beta cells arise from the second wave of insulin-positive cells during pancreatic embryogenesis, governed by transcription factors including MAFB, PDX1, NKX6.1, and Rbp4. Both MAFA and MAFB are critical regulators of development and maturation; in mice, MAFA is predominantly expressed, whereas in humans both MAFA and MAFB are expressed. Beta cells are ultrastructurally identified by condensed electron-dense insulin crystals surrounded by a large halo. Distinct subtypes—such as beta-high/beta-low and beta1/beta2—have been identified with varying functional and transcriptional properties.

Beta cells exhibit prominent sex-specific differences in gene expression, chromatin accessibility, transcription factor activity, gene regulatory networks, and mitochondrial function. Their structure, function, and aging signatures are also modulated by dietary interventions, including caloric restriction and high-fat diet. Methodologically, beta cells are identifiable by top marker genes including INS, INS-IGF2, and MAFA, and have been studied through single-cell RNA sequencing, snATAC-seq profiling, single-cell multiomics, islet perifusion, immunohistochemistry, confocal imaging, and MIMS-EM. Highly differentiated human beta cell lines serve as relevant models for studying disease mechanisms in T2D. Senolytic and senomorphic therapies targeting senescent beta cells represent an active area of therapeutic investigation.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin-producing cells of the pancreatic islets studied for nucleolar stress-induced senescence.</data>
