<node id="alpha cell">
  <data key="d0">alpha cell</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Alpha cells are glucagon-producing endocrine cells residing within the pancreatic islets of Langerhans, constituting a critical component of the endocrine pancreas. They are functionally and molecularly distinct from insulin-producing beta (β) cells and play a central role in glucose homeostasis through the regulation of glucagon secretion. Alpha cells are canonically identified by the expression of the glucagon gene (GCG) and the transcription factor ARX, and are further characterized by enriched transcription factor motifs for MAFB, FOXD2, and GATA2-5. They are routinely studied using single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq), chromatin accessibility profiling (snATAC-seq), gene regulatory network (GRN) approaches, single-cell multiome sequencing, and perifusion experiments. Whole-organ mapping studies have suggested that over 50% of islets may lack glucagon-positive cells, highlighting spatial heterogeneity in alpha cell distribution across the pancreas.

The development and spatial organization of alpha cells are critically dependent on key transcription factors, most notably MAFB and MAFA. During embryonic and fetal development, MAFB is required for proper terminal maturation of alpha cells, with homozygous Mafb-deficient mice exhibiting defective alpha cell development. In adult pancreas, MafB expression becomes exclusive to alpha cells. In MafaΔpanc mice, alpha cells remain numerically intact but become mislocalized to the islet core rather than their normal peripheral position. Alpha cells have also been studied in relation to precursor cells coexpressing both insulin and glucagon.

Alpha cells exhibit notable sex-specific biology, including differences in gene expression — such as X and Y chromosome-linked chromatin remodelers KDM6A and KDM5D — as well as sex-specific differences in chromatin accessibility and gene regulatory networks, particularly in the context of type 2 diabetes (T2D). Alpha cells contribute pathogenic signals affecting beta cell function in T2D, and beta cells may transdifferentiate into alpha cells in T2D contexts.

Alpha cell function and gene expression are compromised in type 1 diabetes (T1D) and in nondiabetic autoantibody-positive individuals, indicating that dysfunction can precede overt disease. Unlike beta cells, alpha cells in T1D do not undergo senescence, though alpha cells in NOD mice exhibit some senescence markers, including CDKN1A and CDKN2A. Glucagon secretion can be indirectly influenced by senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) factors released by senescent beta cells, suggesting a paracrine relationship between islet cell types. Proinflammatory cytokines and ER stressors have been shown to upregulate genes such as CIB1 in alpha cells.

A distinctive feature of alpha cells is their behavior during neonatal growth and under metabolic stress. Compared to beta cells, alpha cells display higher replication rates and greater DNA damage, with DNA damage persisting through mitosis. They also exhibit limited DNA repair capacity and higher cellular turnover. Despite this elevated DNA damage response (DDR) under homeostatic conditions, alpha cells are notably resistant to terminal DNA damage, senescence, and cell death, compensating through increased cellular turnover. Conversely, alpha cells generally do not show increased DNA damage under metabolic stress or diabetic conditions in most studied contexts. In the mouse pancreas, alpha cells are long-lived, with no changes in alpha cell mass observed after caloric restriction, and no effects of high-fat diet or caloric restriction detected via 15N level measurements.</data>
