<node id="cellular senescence">
  <data key="d0">cellular senescence</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Cellular senescence is a fundamental biological process characterized by a durable, stable state of irreversible cell cycle arrest, in which cells permanently cease to divide and undergo extensive functional, molecular, and structural changes. It represents a highly heterogeneous cell fate triggered by a broad range of stressors, including DNA damage, oncogene activation, unfolded protein response (UPR) deficiency, nucleolar stress, and physiological aging.

The process is mediated by key molecular regulators, including cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors p21Cip1 (CDKN1A) and p16Ink4a (CDKN2A), as well as the tumor suppressor p53. These mediators drive a terminal stress response marked by DNA damage response (DDR) activation — including ATM kinase signaling — chromatin remodeling, mitochondrial dysfunction, metabolic alterations, anti-apoptotic pathway upregulation, and loss of nuclear integrity. Established markers include elevated SA-β-galactosidase (SA-β-gal) activity, upregulation of p16, p21, p53, γ-H2AX foci, BCL2, decreased LMNB1 levels, telomere-associated foci (TAF), increased lysosomal content, and genomic instability. Cellular senescence can be studied experimentally through techniques such as RNAscope in situ hybridization for p16 and p21 expression, SA-β-gal staining, and shRNA-mediated knockdown of target genes.

A hallmark feature of cellular senescence is the Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype (SASP), whereby arrested cells secrete a complex array of pro-inflammatory bioactive factors that influence the surrounding tissue microenvironment, contributing to inflammation, fibrosis, and tissue dysfunction. SASP also dramatically increases small extracellular vesicle (EV) biogenesis and alters EV RNA and protein cargo.

Cellular senescence plays dual biological roles. Transient senescence contributes positively to embryonic development, wound healing, tissue repair, tumor suppression, and tissue remodeling. Conversely, persistent or pathologically accelerated senescence compromises tissue homeostasis and contributes to aging, fibrosis, and a wide spectrum of age-related and metabolic diseases.

In the context of diabetes, cellular senescence is particularly relevant to pancreatic beta cell biology. Senescent beta cells accumulate during the development of both type 1 diabetes (T1D) and type 2 diabetes, contributing to beta cell dysfunction, failure, and disease progression through SASP production. Notably, this pro-inflammatory senescence response occurs in beta cells but not alpha cells in T1D. Accelerated senescence is also observed in UPR-deficient beta cells from Atf6β−/− and Ire1αβ−/− mouse models, as well as in beta cells subjected to nucleolar stress via ATM activation. Importantly, senescent beta cells represent a therapeutic target, as senolysis — the selective elimination of senescent cells — has been shown to improve disease outcomes in diabetes.</data>
