<node id="C-MAF">
  <data key="d0">C-MAF</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">Large MAF transcription factor, cellular homolog of v-MAF, plays essential roles in immune cell function, lens formation, bone formation, and cell differentiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Large musculoaponeurotic fibrosarcoma transcription factor involved in macrophage self-renewal and differentiation; point mutations such as p.Thr58Ile cause rare diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor associated with Aymé–Gripp syndrome; homozygous mutations are embryonic lethal in mice and cause intellectual/developmental disabilities in human patients.&lt;SEP&gt;C-MAF is a transcription factor whose deficiency or mutation is associated with various diseases including cataracts, diabetes, nephropathy, and Aymé–Gripp syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;C-MAF is a MAF transcription factor implicated in renal function changes, multiple myeloma, and potentially other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;MAF transcription factor gene whose point mutations cause diseases including nephropathies and mesangiocapillary glomerulopathy.&lt;SEP&gt;c-Maf is a member of the large Maf transcription factor family expressed in mouse islets, capable of binding to and activating insulin C1 element-mediated expression.&lt;SEP&gt;c-Maf is a proto-oncogene whose overexpression is a frequent oncogenic event in multiple myeloma and T-cell lymphoma, promoting proliferation and pathological interactions with bone marrow stroma.&lt;SEP&gt;C-Maf is a member of the large Maf transcription factor family that activates glucagon gene expression in pancreatic islet alpha cells, differentially expressed alongside MafA.&lt;SEP&gt;c-MAF is a transcription factor playing important roles in lens, bone, liver, and kidney formation; mutations cause congenital cataracts, deafness, skeletal dysplasia, and other disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;Large MAF transcription factor with conserved basic region sequences similar to MAFA and MAFB.&lt;SEP&gt;Large MAF family transcription factor involved in terminal differentiation, including hypertrophic chondrocytes during endochondral bone development.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01e46f30e47340f5c5b95376196543ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0be98cbe29b4c692e555154146fad640&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a91f8726a8e590e31edad7232b84804b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed6515ae53fc7e07b1c44a83258c3224&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1a171c046b7e074fd282a07d58cc39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe670890a826e2e1970f382c46b77260&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf89ad08d3f81ffe84c9bf7f208c604b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fffe587d9d8a94d6179115102ef90a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e0ae079210c3ef99872d0747c5a9b88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-222f634a871d4f5fd5cffac2fce0d698&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3384cef9855a1b408c68f7d1ea3153c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4fecd9e508da1daa4a0d2d27a7e759</data>
