<node id="diabetes">
  <data key="d0">diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">Disease</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes is a complex metabolic disease characterized by progressive dysfunction in glucose regulation, encompassing a broad range of cellular, molecular, and genetic mechanisms that collectively contribute to its pathophysiology.

At the cellular level, diabetes is strongly associated with beta cell pathology, including beta cell dysfunction, senescence, apoptosis, and the progressive depletion of beta cell reserve and mass. Senescent beta cells play a significant role in disease progression through the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP), and unresolved DNA damage has been identified as a causal driver of beta cell failure. Nucleolar stress has also been implicated during disease progression, and the acceleration of beta cell aging is considered a key contributing factor. Given these mechanisms, senotherapeutics — strategies targeting senescent cells — are being actively developed as potential interventions.

From a genetic and molecular standpoint, diabetes has been linked to mutations in large MAF transcription factors, with mouse models harboring such point mutations exhibiting diabetes-like phenotypes. C-MAF deficiency has been shown to confer protection against diabetes through regulation of glucose transporters SGLT2 and GLUT2, which are critical for glucose uptake and renal glucose reabsorption. Additionally, loss of CDK4 and various epigenetic alterations have been associated with beta cell dysfunction and the onset of diabetes.

Diabetes is further characterized by dysregulation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and glycolytic signaling, which are linked to basal hyperinsulinemia, as well as glucose-stimulated beta cell proliferation mediated by MLXIPL/ChREBP under high-fat diet (HFD) conditions. Insulin deficiency or resistance remains a central feature of the disease.

Diabetes is also associated with aging and impaired beta cell function, and caloric restriction (CR) has been suggested as a means to preserve compromised beta cell structure and function during disease progression.

Together, these findings portray diabetes as a multifaceted disease involving transcription factor dysregulation, impaired glucose transport, epigenetic changes, DNA damage, oxidative stress, and beta cell deterioration driven by senescence and apoptosis, all of which continue to inform the development of targeted therapeutic strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes is a complex metabolic disease and a major subject of biomedical research, characterized by the loss or dysfunction of β-cells in the pancreas, leading to imbalanced glucose homeostasis. It is associated with frequent urination, elevated urine glucose levels, and high fasting blood glucose levels. In experimental contexts, diabetes has been operationally defined as a fasting blood glucose level of ≥250 mg/dL measured on two consecutive occasions, serving as a classification criterion in murine studies.

The disease arises from multiple etiological factors, including mutations in key transcription factors observed in type 2 diabetic patients. Notably, the MAFA S64F mutation has been linked to diabetes in human subjects from distinct family cohorts (family 1 and family 2), with a sex-based disparity observed: men carrying this mutation are more prone to developing diabetes than women. In most patients harboring this mutation, insulinomatosis and diabetes appeared to be mutually exclusive diagnoses, suggesting distinct pathological trajectories. Research into β-cell formation and function is actively motivated by the potential to generate insulin-producing cells as a therapeutic strategy for the disease.

Animal model studies have further elucidated diabetes pathogenesis. In A0B0 mice subjected to a high-fat diet (HFD), symptoms consistent with diabetes were observed, with some mice succumbing to severe conditions. In Ire1αβ−/− mice, diabetes incidence was recorded at approximately 25%, but genetic inhibition of p21 dramatically increased this rate to over 75%, highlighting the role of specific molecular pathways in disease progression.

Separately, *Diabetes* is also the name of a peer-reviewed scientific journal published by the American Diabetes Association, serving as a prominent publication venue for research in this field.</data>
