<node id="macrophage">
  <data key="d0">macrophage</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Macrophages are a broadly important and functionally diverse class of immune cells with critical roles in tissue homeostasis, development, aging, and disease. They are found across multiple tissue contexts, including pancreatic islets, the aging ovary, and various other tissues, where they serve as tissue-resident immune cells.

In terms of cellular identity and function, macrophages are professional phagocytes involved in antigen processing and presentation, cell chemotaxis, and immune regulation. Within the pancreas, they represent a non-endocrine cell type characterized by the expression of marker genes SDS and FCER1G, and are associated with antigen processing, cell chemotaxis, and cell chemotaxis pathways. Their presence in pancreatic islets has been confirmed through single-cell multiome sequencing and snATAC-seq clustering, the latter identifying macrophages specifically via SDS gene activity. Notably, macrophage infiltration into islets is reduced upon inhibition of small extracellular vesicle (EV) release from stressed beta cells.

Macrophages also display sex-specific characteristics in pancreatic tissue, including sex-based differences in chromatin accessibility and genomic enrichment exclusively in male type 2 diabetes (T2D) genome-wide association studies (GWAS), suggesting sex-based heterogeneity in immune regulation of T2D risk.

Transcriptional regulation is central to macrophage biology. The transcription factor MAFB governs macrophage development, phagocytic function, F4/80 surface marker expression, and normal actin organization, as confirmed by MAFB p.Leu239Pro mutant mouse models. Simultaneous deficiency of MafB and c-Maf confers differentiated macrophages with an atypical capacity for self-renewal. Additionally, c-MAF is essential for expression of the anti-inflammatory cytokine IL-10 within macrophages.

With respect to aging and senescence, macrophages exhibit complex behavior. p16Ink4a- and β-galactosidase-positive macrophages accumulate progressively during mouse aging, driven in part by the presence of senescent cells. Despite displaying these senescence-associated markers, macrophages are considered a non-senescent cell type, and their p16Ink4a expression does not reflect true cellular senescence. This distinction poses a technical challenge, as p16Ink4a antibodies can produce nonspecific signals in macrophages, potentially confounding experimental interpretations of senescence in tissue studies. Macrophages are also predominant producers of senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) factors and exhibit age-related dysfunction, including inflammasome-driven catecholamine catabolism. In the aging mouse ovary, macrophages show shifted ontogeny and polarization associated with inflammaging. Furthermore, downregulation of GBP1 in macrophages causes mitochondrial dysfunction and promotes cellular senescence, highlighting a molecular mechanism linking macrophage biology to senescence pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Immune cell incorporated into vascularized macrophage-islet organoids to model immune-mediated beta cell pyroptosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Immune cell incorporated into vascularized macrophage-islet organoids to model immune-mediated beta cell pyroptosis upon viral infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Immune cells found infiltrating islets in type 2 diabetes and mediating proinflammatory responses including to bacterial flagellin via TLR5.&lt;SEP&gt;Immune cells present in islets of Langerhans involved in diabetic autoimmunity, inflammatory responses, and arginase-mediated immunosuppression.</data>
