<node id="liver">
  <data key="d0">liver</data>
  <data key="d1">Tissue</data>
  <data key="d2">The liver is a multifunctional organ that has been extensively studied in the context of developmental biology, metabolic regulation, disease pathophysiology, and aging.

From a developmental standpoint, the liver is a site where c-MAF plays a critical regulatory role. In c-Maf homozygous-deficient mice, the liver exhibits abnormal blood island formation in embryos, and sinusoid development within the organ is also regulated by c-MAF, highlighting its importance in hepatic vascular development.

The liver demonstrates notable hepatic sexual dimorphism, influencing both disease risk and disease manifestation. This dimorphism has significant implications for non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) and is closely tied to the regulation of glucose homeostasis, suggesting that biological sex is an important determinant of how liver-related metabolic diseases develop and progress.

Cellular senescence within the liver is implicated in age-dependent dysfunction and contributes to the development of various chronic liver diseases, underscoring the organ's vulnerability to aging-related pathological changes.

In the context of metabolic disease, the liver plays a pivotal role in type 2 diabetes remission, as a reduction in hepatic fat content is considered a prerequisite for achieving remission. The liver is also a site of endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress, which can be attenuated through caloric restriction, suggesting a direct link between dietary interventions and hepatic cellular health.

The liver has additionally been studied as a site of insulin-degrading enzyme (IDE) activity. Experimental studies have measured IDE activity in liver samples from mice subjected to ad libitum (AL), caloric restriction (CR), and high-fat diet (HFD) conditions after two months on their respective diets. Liver tissue from these models was also dissected and processed for immunohistochemistry to further characterize metabolic and enzymatic changes under varying dietary conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ whose triacylglycerol content decrease is associated with type 2 diabetes reversal.&lt;SEP&gt;Tissue that converts trimethylamine to TMAO via FMO3, indirectly impacting beta cell function.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ whose fat content is reduced by weight loss and bariatric surgery, contributing to metabolic improvement.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is an organ that also produces Rbp4 protein, in addition to adipocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver tissue collected from WT and MafAS64F/+ mice was flash frozen and used in the glycogen storage assay.</data>
