<node id="mouse">
  <data key="d0">mouse</data>
  <data key="d1">Organism</data>
  <data key="d2">The mouse (Mus musculus) is a widely utilized model organism across numerous fields of biological and biomedical research, serving as a critical system for investigating gene function, cellular biology, disease mechanisms, and aging.

In the context of transcription factor research, the mouse has been extensively employed to study the functions and mutations of MAF transcription factors, particularly C-MAF. Mouse models — including knockout, transgenic, heterozygous, and homozygous strains generated via CRISPR-Cas9 technology — exhibit phenotypes closely mirroring those observed in human patients carrying MAF mutations, reinforcing the translational relevance of the mouse for understanding human genetic disorders.

The mouse is a central model organism in the study of beta cell biology, senescence, diabetes, and aging. Researchers have used mouse models to investigate cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors p21Cip1 and p16Ink4a in regulating beta cell proliferation and senescence, as well as the roles of DNA repair components such as LIG4, Ku70, ERCC1, and CDK4 in beta cell health. Mouse models of diabetes have revealed that senescent beta cells upregulate anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family members and senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) factors.

A major application of the mouse as a model organism involves dietary intervention studies. Both male and female mice — organized into ad libitum (AL), caloric restriction (CR), and high-fat diet (HFD) groups — have been used extensively to study the effects of caloric restriction, fasting, diet dilution, and high-fat feeding on beta cell function, aging, glucose homeostasis, insulin sensitivity, and longevity. These studies have employed advanced techniques including pancreatic islet isolation, perfusion assays, single-cell and single-nucleus multiomics, stable isotope labeling, multi-isotope imaging mass spectrometry electron microscopy (MIMS-EM), electron tomography (eTomo), and mitochondrial modeling to examine beta cell gene regulatory networks, metabolism, turnover, and longevity under varying dietary conditions.

Beyond beta cell biology, the mouse is employed in studies of cellular senescence across multiple tissue types — including skeletal tissue, skin, mammary gland, ovary, cardiovascular and aortic tissue, brain, and hematopoietic stem cells — as well as in research on epigenetic regulation, sex differences in adiposity, X chromosome effects, and senolytic therapies. The mouse is also one of the species in which the SenNet initiative maps senescent cells across tissues and lifespan, highlighting its indispensable role in understanding age-related diseases, metabolic dysfunction, and tissue homeostasis throughout life.&lt;SEP&gt;Model organism in which only MAFA (not MAFB) is produced in beta cells; used to study pancreatic development and MAFA/MAFB functional differences.&lt;SEP&gt;Model organism used for studying X chromosome effects on adiposity and escape from X inactivation.&lt;SEP&gt;Model organism used in Slc30a8 knockout studies; displays conflicting phenotypes across studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Animal model used for HFHC diet-induced metabolic stress and diabetic studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Murine model organism used in high-fat diet-induced diabetes and beta cell senescence studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouse is the model organism used in in vivo studies of nucleolar stress and beta cell senescence under metabolic stress conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01e46f30e47340f5c5b95376196543ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0be98cbe29b4c692e555154146fad640&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02381256ec5732a712b97ced237c2acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed6515ae53fc7e07b1c44a83258c3224&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c0cbc7de7a8a1060e8f1608271a28c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7d4c1829099c6037d8b8b7a83c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac258601b299e0c883e8e1c4a3030d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b67e26d82684726785f1910ba6919cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25d34b513b15180c8a2d7b57ae829067&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca3e5da7a4fe15854dde6289fd15667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5bb73f6c7980a9f906f42f6e0e9ce27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c5a8121875c37f2659ae345688f6afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b02375738a9ebacd53446fec2077ee76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ff588c6efe73ecb07c6b95acb392e92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f25c34949789593e96b7a57ead73f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db03a7d3c861658f2beac422f86b21ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56d5ffe02105b1bd6d77faab9c812433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68a87a3a1f3eb5d8b0e53b41df492512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2e4a544176811f5b43952b185e1ed3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dcd3bdc472cb74376c18c72cf9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08e345f1e7ff9529c2ab94760dcb50f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002f23b1d29df711e839459b4d6f0711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cadc21c6ca916dd06510987c1b7dbf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f39153d9f864070e17a74724a6c8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce68225f2288adf7d07962282496e8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa01ef8f1657a9890660f864f52123c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d5c817dce40d1df02e7717bb9598632&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d286ee457150be65228790e800b5207b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c85c9569fee140bfd52fcc0bbfbcc9c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b61717ec6764bc92fa7c3c5067d345&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae638e9cabe91a68fc062c9981b0e86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c41ef398df926f1c888f284aff2a98e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-044fb2d3ececa99a53eb87d2ee6bf18f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bb74bfaf0604b4b9f891e16fc9f0ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ab370ab273e97005ced113e59cc48a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e33cd1d99c96a7f6