<node id="kidney">
  <data key="d0">kidney</data>
  <data key="d1">Tissue</data>
  <data key="d2">The kidney is a vital organ that plays a central role in several developmental, functional, and pathological processes, particularly in relation to transcription factors of the MAF family and cellular aging mechanisms.

In terms of development and maintenance, MAFB is essential for the creation and maintenance of podocytes in the kidney at both embryonic and adult stages. MAFB is broadly required for normal renal development, and mutations in MAFB are associated with podocyte defects, glomerular sclerosis, and ultimately renal failure. These findings underscore the kidney's dependence on MAFB for proper glomerular architecture and function throughout life.

The kidney is also a key organ in which C-MAF (also referred to as c-MAF) exerts significant regulatory influence. C-MAF regulates the expression of SGLT2 and GLUT2 within the kidney, and both C-MAF RNA and protein levels have been quantified in mouse models to better understand its role. Additionally, C-MAF regulates genes relevant to diabetic nephropathy, highlighting its importance in the context of metabolic kidney disease. Pathogenic variants of C-MAF have been shown to result in changes in renal function and mesangiocapillary glomerulopathy, further demonstrating the organ's susceptibility to MAF-related genetic disruptions.

Beyond transcription factor regulation, the kidney is an organ in which cellular senescence is prominently observed. Senescence markers increase in multiple cell types during the processes of aging, glomerular disease, and diabetic nephropathy in both humans and mice. Furthermore, iron accumulation within the kidney has been identified as a contributor to fibrosis and the promotion of cellular senescence, linking metabolic dysregulation to progressive kidney damage.

Collectively, the kidney serves as a critical site for MAF-family-driven developmental and disease processes, as well as age- and disease-associated cellular senescence, making it central to understanding conditions such as focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS), diabetic nephropathy, and mesangiocapillary glomerulopathy.</data>
