<node id="TP53">
  <data key="d0">TP53</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">TP53 is a critical tumor suppressor gene that encodes the p53 protein, playing a central and multifaceted role in cell cycle regulation, senescence, apoptosis, and various metabolic and developmental processes.

At its core, TP53 functions as a key tumor suppressor. The p53 protein is stabilized by ATM and ATR kinases in response to DNA damage, subsequently activating CDKN1A to halt cell cycle progression. The Mdm2/p53 axis is a central regulatory mechanism through which p53 activity is modulated, and this axis can be further altered by p19Arf/p14Arf in the context of cell cycle inhibition. In adipocytes, p53 forms a dysfunctional axis with MDM2 that contributes to aging-related metabolic complications and lipodystrophy.

TP53 is notably involved in cellular senescence across multiple tissue types. It is overexpressed in senescent cells of the central nervous system during aging and disease, and p53 induction has been observed during acinar-to-ductal metaplasia and in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma senescence. In pancreatic beta cells, mutations in TP53 affect the balance between beta cell senescence and apoptosis, though selective ablation of TP53 in pancreatic beta cells fails to ameliorate glucose metabolism in diabetes models.

A specific isoform of p53, designated Δ133p53, has been identified as capable of rescuing radiation-induced astrocyte senescence, highlighting the functional diversity encoded within the TP53 locus. Beyond its roles in senescence and the DNA damage response, TP53 also regulates developmental processes; the p53 protein controls lens differentiation by regulating the transcription factors c-Maf and Prox-1.

Overall, TP53 is a pleiotropic gene whose encoded protein and isoforms coordinate tumor suppression, cellular aging, metabolic regulation, and tissue development across a wide range of biological contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor suppressor p53 involved in regulatory pathways with miR-29 and miRNAs/Ccna2 that serve as regulators of cellular senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor suppressor gene involved in senescence/SASP; a target of beta cell EV miRNAs.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor suppressor gene encoding p53 protein; regulates cellular senescence, apoptosis, and beta cell function; active in beta cells under metabolic stress and DNA damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor suppressor p53, phosphorylated by ATM/ATR and Chk1/2 kinases to induce cell cycle inhibitors and DNA repair pathways.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02381256ec5732a712b97ced237c2acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7d4c1829099c6037d8b8b7a83c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7bb3e4a69884a1b79485b95f6f502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdd484417981e099a8f308ba0994bcaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d3b1de44d6b73a10793a43351e18e6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71a6a7a3a4894dbd7d979b7e5766d5a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25d34b513b15180c8a2d7b57ae829067&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca3e5da7a4fe15854dde6289fd15667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c5a8121875c37f2659ae345688f6afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6215695e8bd809fd02cb8b9b7b4123ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-732255e9544dde6a7dbc319c61cd8e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58f0193e389064a60d513f13c5cad52b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408</data>
