<node id="pancreas">
  <data key="d0">pancreas</data>
  <data key="d1">Tissue</data>
  <data key="d2">The pancreas is a vital organ that serves dual endocrine and exocrine functions, playing a central role in glucose homeostasis, metabolic regulation, and the pathogenesis of multiple diseases, including type 1 diabetes (T1D) and type 2 diabetes (T2D).

Structurally, the pancreas contains the islets of Langerhans, which harbor specialized endocrine cell types including alpha cells and beta cells, as well as exocrine components such as acinar cells and ductal cells. Beta cells are a primary focus of diabetes research, as they are targeted in T1D, and senescence hallmarks have been observed in human donor pancreatic beta cells during T1D development. The organ's fat content has been implicated in T2D, with pancreatic fat reduction through weight loss considered necessary for disease remission and reversal.

The pancreas is a critical site of transcriptional and molecular regulation. Transcription factors MAFA and MAFB play important regulatory roles in islet cell development, differentiation, and function, with MAFB particularly important for alpha cell glucagon production and beta cell maintenance during fetal development in mice. CDKN2A transcription in beta cells and islets has been studied in relation to the rs10811661 SNP and T2D genetic risk. Single-cell analyses have revealed transcriptional signatures associated with aging and somatic mutation patterns, reflecting cellular heterogeneity across the organ.

Senescence is an important dimension of pancreatic biology. An age-dependent increase in SA-β-gal-positive cells has been documented in human cadaveric islets, with senescence affecting acinar, beta, and ductal cells across various disease contexts. Studies using caloric restriction (CR) models have shown reduced pancreas and islet beta cell mass, associated with a post-mitotic cell state, and cellular age mosaicism has been observed via MIMS-EM studies.

The pancreas also exhibits sexual dimorphism in gene expression and disease susceptibility. The majority of sex-biased expressed genes are located on sex chromosomes, and most sex-biased autosomal genes are not under direct sex hormone influence, suggesting chromosome-level regulatory mechanisms. Islet cell types have been studied for sex-specific genomic differences in subjects with and without T2D.

Experimentally, the pancreas has been widely used as a tissue source across numerous research modalities, including single-cell RNA sequencing and snATAC-seq studies with samples obtained from Tulane, nPOD, and UPenn, as well as islet isolation via collagenase digestion, MALDI-MS metabolomic profiling, immunohistochemistry, X-ray microscopy, scanning electron microscopy, MIMS imaging of 15N-labeled tissue, and in situ hybridization. The organ has also been identified as a target tissue for engineered extracellular vesicles carrying p88-lactoadherin fusion proteins upon intravenous administration in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ from which islets and tissue sections are collected for experimental analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ from which islets are isolated and sections prepared for immunostaining in multiple mouse models&lt;SEP&gt;Organ containing islets of Langerhans; subject of transcriptomic mapping and multidimensional phenotyping studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ in which early developmental DNA methylation patterns are established that shape postnatal islet homeostasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ in which Dnmt3a-dependent DNA methylation patterns established during early development shape postnatal islet homeostasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The pancreas is a vital organ with both exocrine and endocrine functions, playing a central role in glucose metabolism and hormonal regulation. It is responsible for producing key hormones, most notably insulin and glucagon, which are essential for regulating blood glucose levels. Failure of the pancreas to produce adequate insulin is directly associated with the development of type 1 diabetes.

Structurally, the pancreas contains both exocrine and endocrine components. Its endocrine component is organized into the islets of Langerhans, which are clusters of specialized cells including β-cells (responsible for insulin production), α-cells (responsible for glucagon production), and other endocrine cell types such as GAST+ cells. The pancreas undergoes a well-defined developmental process, progressing through stages of organogenesis from bud to plexus to gland, during which islet formation (islet organogenesis) occurs.

During pancreatic development and in mature tissue, several critical transcription factors are expressed, including Pdx1, MafA, and MafB. These transcription factors regulate cell identity and function within the islets. The pancreas is also the organ in which MafA is deleted pancreas-wide in experimental mouse models such as the Mafa∆panc model, making it a key site for studying transcription factor dynamics and endocrine cell behavior.

The pancreas serves as an important subject of scientific investigation. Islets are isolated from the pancreas for experimental study, and pancreatic sections are routinely harvested for histological and immunofluorescence analyses. Research conducted on the pancreas encompasses the study of islet cell composition, hormone secretion, transcription factor expression, and the developmental origins of endocrine cell populations, contributing broadly to the understanding of metabolic diseases and pancreatic biology.</data>
