<node id="apoptosis">
  <data key="d0">apoptosis</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Apoptosis is a programmed cell death process that plays a central and multifaceted role in tissue homeostasis, cellular stress responses, disease pathology, and developmental regulation across a wide range of biological contexts. It is experimentally assessed using TUNEL staining and the One Step TUNEL Apoptosis Assay Kit, including in pancreatic beta-TC-6 cells and islet cell models exposed to hypoxia and cytokine stress conditions. Apoptosis is also indicated by pan-nuclear H2AX staining in neonatal islet cells, with no significant difference observed between alpha and beta cells.

A major research focus concerns the interplay between apoptosis and cellular senescence. Senescent cells characteristically upregulate anti-apoptotic pathways—including BCL-2 family proteins—to resist apoptosis, but this resistance can be overcome by BH3 mimetic compounds. Targeted apoptosis of senescent cells through senolytic strategies, such as FKBP8-Caspase8 dimerization or FOXO4-inhibiting peptides, has been shown to restore tissue homeostasis in the context of chemotoxicity and aging. Apoptosis is also triggered by miR-107, contributing to downregulation of CXCR2, MMP-2, and MMP-9 and decreased SASP secretion.

In pancreatic beta cells, apoptosis represents an alternative fate to senescence under conditions of stress or DNA damage and is directly relevant to beta cell loss and diabetes pathogenesis. It is associated with unresolved DNA damage, including DNA double-strand breaks, and is regulated by p53 and nitric oxide signaling. Alpha cells show higher rates of apoptotic turnover to eliminate damaged cells, while apoptosis is selectively increased in beta cells of 8-week-old NOD mice and through p53-mediated pathways under high-fat diet conditions. Apoptosis is also increased in 3aPKO beta cells at postnatal day 7, despite reduced replication, indicating heightened genomic instability. The molecular mediator p21 plays a critical protective role in limiting apoptosis in response to DNA damage. Estrogen receptor alpha protects beta cells from apoptosis by preserving mitochondrial function and suppressing ER stress. Diazoxide prevents beta cell apoptosis via modulation of the Bcl-2/Bax ratio and p38-beta MAPK signaling, while CIB1 also confers protection. Conversely, pathophysiological levels of pancreatic elastase increase apoptotic susceptibility, and miR-21 promotes beta cell apoptosis through downregulation of BCL2 translation. Apoptotic signals can additionally be transmitted via exosomal microRNAs, implicating intercellular communication in beta cell depletion and diabetes progression.

Beyond pancreatic biology, apoptosis is regulated by nucleolar stress signaling and plays disease-specific roles in multiple tissues. In atherosclerosis, apoptosis in foam cells is inhibited by the transcription factor MAFB, promoting lesion progression, whereas Mafb knockout mice exhibit tubular apoptosis, underscoring MAFB's protective role. In pulmonary fibrosis, apoptosis is promoted in fibroblasts through histone deacetylase inhibition as a therapeutic strategy. In melanocytes exposed to UVB radiation, apoptosis is inhibited by HMGB1/RAGE signaling, whereas in granulosa cells, apoptosis is induced by CCL5. Furthermore, apoptosis is triggered in podocytes upon SERPINE1 overexpression derived from senescent glomerular endothelial cells, implicating paracrine apoptotic signaling in renal pathology.&lt;SEP&gt;Apoptosis is a form of programmed cell death demonstrated in this paper to be stimulated in β cells when MafB expression is reduced, contributing to diabetes mellitus.&lt;SEP&gt;Apoptosis is a programmed cell death process that was not induced in response to DNA damage in either male or female MafAS64F/+ islets, as determined by absence of TUNEL+ cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Apoptosis is a programmed cell death process that senescent cells become resistant to through upregulation of anti-apoptotic proteins such as those in the BCL2 family.&lt;SEP&gt;Programmed cell death that can result from persistent or excessive stress responses; reduced in β-cells through M2 macrophage-mediated immune surveillance in T1D protection.&lt;SEP&gt;Apoptosis is a pro-apoptotic cell death response induced by the UPR under prolonged or severe ER stress to eliminate terminally stressed cells; β-cell apoptosis is reduced by M2 macrophage-mediated responses in the study model.</data>
