<node id="human">
  <data key="d0">human</data>
  <data key="d1">Organism</data>
  <data key="d2">The human species (Homo sapiens) serves as a central and indispensable subject across a wide range of biomedical research domains, including transcription factor biology, cellular senescence, aging, metabolic disease, and islet biology.

In the context of transcription factor biology, humans are a key species in which mutations in MAF transcription factors are associated with various rare diseases. Notably, both MAFA and MAFB are expressed in human pancreatic islet beta cells, exhibiting heterogeneous coexpression patterns. MAF transcription factors are highly conserved between humans and mice, underscoring the relevance of murine models for understanding human disease mechanisms. Additionally, YY1 mutations in insulinoma and beta cell replication are actively studied in humans.

Humans are extensively studied in research on beta cell biology, senescence, and type 2 diabetes (T2D). Pancreatic islet donors—up to 52 individuals with and without T2D—have been recruited for single-cell epigenomic and transcriptomic analyses, encompassing sex and race differences in islet biology, gene expression, DNA methylation, and diabetes. Human beta cells exhibit differences in their senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) compared to mouse beta cells. The expression of p16INK4a in human beta cells follows a clear age-dependent pattern, rising from approximately 8% in prenatal beta cells to roughly 63% in adult beta cells, suggesting progressive accumulation of senescent cells over the human lifespan. Single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) at the CDKN2A/B locus in humans have been associated with T2D risk and beta cell function. Humans are also the subject of studies on postnatal beta-cell mass expansion, in vitro islet maturation, beta cell turnover, aging effects on islets, and caloric restriction effects on islet function.

More broadly, humans are a primary organism of study for cellular senescence across multiple tissues and physiological contexts. Senescent CD8+ T cells, T cell p16INK4a expression, vascular senescence, and brain senescence have all been studied in human subjects. Senescence markers have been investigated in bone biopsies, skin, mammary gland, mammary epithelial cells, post-menopausal ovaries, and placental tissue. Humans are also one of the species in which the SenNet initiative maps senescent cells across tissues and the lifespan.

Beyond metabolic and senescence research, humans are the species in which calorie restriction has been shown to increase muscle mitochondrial biogenesis in healthy individuals, and neuronal DNA modifications have been examined. Collectively, the human species represents an essential reference and subject for understanding the interplay between aging, cellular senescence, beta cell dysfunction, metabolic disease, and broader physiological and pathological processes.</data>
