<node id="islet">
  <data key="d0">islet</data>
  <data key="d1">Tissue</data>
  <data key="d2">Pancreatic islets, specifically the islets of Langerhans, are discrete clusters of endocrine and non-endocrine cells distributed throughout the pancreas. These functional microenvironments contain multiple cell types, most notably beta cells, alpha cells, and delta cells, alongside resident immune, vascular, and neuronal cells, as well as acinar cells. Beta cells and alpha cells cooperate to regulate key metabolic processes, including glucose homeostasis, insulin secretion, and glucagon secretion. Pancreatic islets exhibit notable heterogeneity in size and cellular composition, and intra-islet connectivity has been observed to be enhanced following bariatric surgery.

Pancreatic islets are a unique tissue in which islet-enriched transcription factors — including MAFA, MAFB, PDX1, ISL1, and GLIS3 — are coexpressed and collectively regulate insulin gene transcription. Human pancreatic islets exhibit heterogeneous beta-cell expression of MAFA and MAFB, with both factors playing important roles in hormone production across alpha and beta cell populations. Postnatal islet homeostasis is further shaped by DNA methylation patterns established during early pancreas development, and islets display high DNA damage response (DDR) activity during neonatal stages, with genomic integrity being a focus of study during neonatal growth.

Pancreatic islets are extensively studied in the context of cellular senescence, diabetes, aging, and dietary interventions. They serve as the primary site of senescent beta-cell accumulation in diabetes models, with age-dependent increases in SA-β-gal-positive cells and co-occurring senescence and apoptosis. Islets also exhibit nucleolar aberrations and senescence markers under metabolic stress, and primary islets isolated from C57/B6 mice have been exposed to CX-5461 to validate nucleolar stress-induced senescence markers. In type 1 diabetes, islets proximal to immune infiltrates display prominent DNA damage responses, and islet-derived extracellular vesicles carrying autoantigens are implicated in autoimmune pathology. DNA damage and immune infiltration have also been studied in NOD mouse models. Pancreatic islets exhibit compensatory expansion in obesity-induced diabetes models and display sex- and race/ethnicity-specific differences in gene expression, chromatin accessibility, hormone secretion, and bioenergetics.

As a critical experimental tissue, pancreatic islets — including those from non-diabetic and diabetic cadaveric donors, as well as murine models — serve as the source for numerous methodological approaches. These include isolation protocols, primary islet cultures, in vitro insulin secretion and perifusion assays, bulk RNA-seq, single-cell RNA sequencing, single-nucleus multiome sequencing, snATAC-seq chromatin accessibility profiling, bioenergetics assays, confocal and electron microscopy, metabolomic profiling via imaging mass spectrometry, immunostaining, and gene regulatory network inference. In one large-scale study, pancreatic islets served as the tissue source for 141,739 single cells analyzed across 52 datasets, with chromatin accessibility profiled across 52,613 single cells from 15 donors. Endogenous islets are also identified and mapped using X-ray microscopy for downstream electron microscopy and MIMS imaging.

Overall, the pancreatic islet represents a multifaceted and critical microenvironment central to understanding metabolic diseases such as diabetes, age-related endocrine dysfunction, and broader questions of developmental epigenetics and genomic integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;Islets are pancreatic cell clusters containing β-cells and other cell types; adult islets from 12-week-old mice were used in quantitative RT-PCR analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Islets are clusters of endocrine cells in the pancreas, including β-cells and α-cells, whose architecture and function are regulated by large Maf proteins.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets are clusters of endocrine cells including β, α, and δ cells; they are the primary tissue studied in the context of diabetes, MafA/MAFB function, and GAST+ cell states.</data>
