<node id="IL6">
  <data key="d0">IL6</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">IL6, encoding the pleiotropic cytokine Interleukin-6 (IL-6), is a well-established component of the Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype (SASP) with broad relevance across multiple biological contexts and disease models.

As a SASP factor, IL6 is consistently upregulated in senescent cells across a diverse range of tissues and conditions. In the context of metabolic disease, IL6 serves as both a SASP component and a senescence marker in senescent beta cells, with upregulation documented in both human and mouse models. Its expression in these settings has been detected and validated through quantitative PCR (qPCR) and immunofluorescence techniques, confirming its utility as a reliable molecular marker of cellular senescence.

Beyond pancreatic beta cells, IL6 upregulation has been reported in senescent hepatic stellate cells, where the secreted IL-6 cytokine plays a functional role in promoting liver regeneration, highlighting a context-dependent, potentially beneficial role of this SASP component. IL6 is also upregulated in senescent-like cell populations derived from aged mouse adipose tissue-derived mesenchymal stem cells, further underscoring its association with aging-related cellular changes.

IL6 expression has additionally been identified as a SASP factor in senescent cells across multiple organ systems and pathological contexts, including bone marrow, the central nervous system, adipose tissue, as well as in the settings of Alzheimer's disease and Parkinson's disease. Furthermore, IL6 is elevated in mammary glands of aged mice and in ovaries subjected to chemotherapeutic treatment, reinforcing its role as a key SASP mediator in both age-driven and therapy-induced senescence.

Collectively, IL6 represents a central, multi-tissue biomarker and effector of cellular senescence, with implications spanning diabetes, neurodegeneration, liver biology, reproductive aging, and cancer therapy-related contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Interleukin-6; a gene involved in senescence/SASP and a target of beta cell EV miRNAs; induced by miR-29 in splenocytes of NOD mice.</data>
