<node id="IL-6">
  <data key="d0">IL-6</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">IL-6 (Interleukin-6) is a pro-inflammatory cytokine widely recognized as a key component of the Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype (SASP), secreted by senescent cells across a broad range of biological contexts.

As a SASP factor, IL-6 has been reported in more than ten tissues as a senescence marker and is considered a common constituent of the classical SASP. It is expressed and secreted by a variety of senescent cell types, including fibroblasts, keratinocytes, and melanocytes. Notably, IL-6 levels are elevated in UVB-exposed melanocytes, highlighting its relevance in stress-induced senescence. IL-6 is also involved in SASP signaling pathways more broadly, functioning as both a biomarker and a mediator of the senescence program.

In the context of beta cell senescence, IL-6 plays a particularly notable role. Its expression has been characterized in both mouse and human models, underscoring cross-species relevance. Specifically, IL-6 has been identified as a SASP factor expressed during late-stage beta cell senescence in euglycemic NOD (Non-Obese Diabetic) mice, suggesting its upregulation is temporally associated with advanced stages of the senescence program. Its detection has been confirmed through multiple methodological approaches, including quantitative PCR (qPCR), Luminex assays, and immunofluorescence.

An important consideration in IL-6 research is its dual cellular origin. While IL-6 is secreted by senescent cells, it is also produced predominantly by immune cells, including monocytes and macrophages. In senescence models involving the immune system, careful experimental design is therefore required to accurately distinguish IL-6 originating from senescent cells versus immune cells.

Beyond senescence biology, IL-6 has also been implicated in other pathological contexts. Upregulation of IL-6 in plasma has been associated with increased susceptibility to interstitial lung abnormalities, indicating its broader relevance as a circulating inflammatory mediator in disease.&lt;SEP&gt;IL-6 is a cytokine component of the SASP secreted by senescent beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Interleukin-6; a SASP-associated cytokine suppressed by miR-204 in diabetic retinopathy.&lt;SEP&gt;Interleukin-6, a hallmark component of SASP, markedly elevated in beta cells under nucleolar stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Pro-inflammatory cytokine secreted at elevated levels in CX-5461-treated cells; attenuated following KU60019 treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouse IL-6 is a pro-inflammatory cytokine measured by ELISA kit in the study, associated with senescence-associated secretory phenotype.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouse interleukin-6 cytokine measured by ELISA following CX-5461 exposure of beta-TC-6 cells.</data>
