<node id="CXCL8">
  <data key="d0">CXCL8</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">CXCL8 is a chemokine gene that encodes Interleukin-8 (IL-8), a well-characterized component of the Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype (SASP). It is consistently identified as a key SASP factor across multiple human tissue types and disease contexts.

In the context of cellular senescence, CXCL8/IL-8 is upregulated in senescent human beta cells, where its expression and secretion have been detected and quantified using both qPCR and Luminex-based assays. Similarly, CXCL8 is expressed and elevated in senescent adipose tissue, with increased protein secretion observed specifically in senescent mature human adipocytes under conditions of obesity and hyperinsulinemia.

Beyond metabolic contexts, CXCL8 has been implicated in several disease states. It is elevated in tissues affected by Alzheimer's disease and Parkinson's disease, suggesting a potential role in neurodegeneration linked to senescence-associated inflammation. Additionally, CXCL8 is associated with increased cellular senescence in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), further highlighting its relevance across diverse pathological conditions.

CXCL8 is also elevated in the SASP of primary human melanocytes following exposure to UVB radiation, indicating its role in skin cell senescence triggered by genotoxic stress. Collectively, CXCL8/IL-8 represents a broadly upregulated SASP mediator across multiple human cell types and senescence-inducing conditions, including metabolic stress, neurodegeneration, pulmonary disease, and UV-induced damage, making it a significant biomarker and potential therapeutic target in senescence-related pathologies.&lt;SEP&gt;CXCL8 is a chemokine component of the SASP secreted by senescent beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammation-related gene upregulated in islets under glucolipotoxic stress.</data>
