<node id="BCL-2">
  <data key="d0">BCL-2</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">BCL-2 is an anti-apoptotic protein and a well-characterized member of the BCL-2 family, with over 30 years of research that has contributed to the development of novel cancer therapies and senolytic treatments.

BCL-2 plays a central role in promoting cell survival by inhibiting apoptosis. It is notably upregulated in senescent beta cells across multiple contexts, including mouse models of diabetes and specifically in euglycemic non-obese diabetic (NOD) mice. In these models, BCL-2 expression is preferentially elevated in late-stage senescent beta cells, where it serves both as a marker of cellular senescence and as a key driver of a prosurvival phenotype. This upregulation activates a prosurvival pathway that allows senescent beta cells to resist programmed cell death, effectively maintaining their persistence within the pancreatic environment.

BCL-2 has also been shown to be upregulated in human EndoC-βH2 cells expressing the MAFA S64F mutation, further linking its activity to beta cell senescence in disease-relevant human cellular models. The preferential upregulation of BCL-2 in senescent beta cells renders these cells particularly sensitive to BCL-2 family senolytic agents — compounds designed to selectively eliminate senescent cells by targeting anti-apoptotic proteins.

Given its dual relevance in cancer biology and cellular senescence, BCL-2 is an important therapeutic target. Its inhibition is being actively explored in both oncology and diabetes research, with senolytic strategies aimed at clearing senescent beta cells representing a promising avenue for the treatment or prevention of type 1 diabetes and related metabolic disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;BCL-2 family includes BCL-W and BCL-XL, which are inhibited to eliminate senescent cells; BCL-extra large inhibitors are used to target senescent cholangiocytes and fibroblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;BCL-2 is a prosurvival protein upregulated in senescent beta cells in NOD mice, rendering them sensitive to BCL-2 inhibitor senolytics.&lt;SEP&gt;A protein family targeted by senolytic inhibitors such as ABT-263 and ABT-737 to eliminate senescent cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Anti-apoptotic protein upregulated by miR-204, contributing to suppression of IL-6, IL-18, and TNF-α.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-add59972a262992e4bde6910520c0cdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b861cee193cad9e0f142051047e83aae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c0cbc7de7a8a1060e8f1608271a28c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea13fe99fbc01cb6036ecddcda0aae8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-184abd4f45a78238743890eb9d4f8c56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1628eba511291c52eab3e72ef0fd0239&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32fd907a01d621388ff41b9b47bfa34</data>
