<node id="BCL-XL">
  <data key="d0">BCL-XL</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">BCL-XL is an anti-apoptotic member of the BCL-2 protein family that plays a significant role in cell survival, particularly in the context of cellular senescence.

BCL-XL is notably upregulated in senescent beta cells, where its increased expression contributes to a prosurvival phenotype. This upregulation has been observed at the mRNA level during DNA damage-induced senescence in both EndoC-βH5 cells and human islets, indicating its relevance in human pancreatic biology. Furthermore, BCL-XL upregulation in senescent beta cells has also been documented in mouse models of diabetes, reinforcing its conserved role across species in promoting the survival of senescent cells under diabetic conditions.

From a therapeutic standpoint, BCL-XL has been identified as a target for the elimination of senescent cells. Specifically, combined inhibition of BCL-XL and BCL-W — another anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family member — has been shown to direct the selective elimination of senescent cells, suggesting a cooperative survival mechanism between the two proteins that, when disrupted, renders senescent cells susceptible to apoptosis. This dual-inhibition strategy highlights the potential of targeting BCL-XL in senolytic approaches aimed at clearing senescent cells in age-related or metabolic diseases such as diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;BCL-XL (encoded by BCL2L1) is targeted by inhibitors to ameliorate fibrosis in Mdr2-knockout mice by targeting senescent cholangiocytes and activated fibroblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;BCL-XL is a BCL-2 family anti-apoptotic protein targeted for directed elimination of senescent cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c0cbc7de7a8a1060e8f1608271a28c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71a6a7a3a4894dbd7d979b7e5766d5a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea13fe99fbc01cb6036ecddcda0aae8a</data>
