<node id="HMGB1">
  <data key="d0">HMGB1</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">HMGB1 (High Mobility Group Box 1 protein) is a chromatin-associated nuclear protein with multifaceted roles spanning chromatin biology, cellular senescence, innate immune signaling, and tissue-specific pathophysiology.

Under normal conditions, HMGB1 resides in the nucleus, where it interacts with chromatin to establish and maintain topological domains, playing a critical role in chromatin organization and gene regulation. During cellular senescence, however, HMGB1 undergoes nuclear exclusion — a hallmark feature of the senescent state documented across at least 7 distinct tissues and multiple cell types, including beta cells in mice and cultured human beta cells, alpha cells of NOD mice, melanocytes, and trophoblasts. This nuclear loss is also observed in mature human adipocytes undergoing senescence in obesity and following hyperinsulinemia, as well as in aged mouse ovaries. The conservation of this phenomenon across species and cell types underscores its significance as a robust senescence marker.

The nuclear exclusion of HMGB1 has profound functional consequences. It disrupts chromatin topology and contributes to the regulation of the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP), coordinating SASP-related chromatin folding changes that drive the senescent program. HMGB1 is itself a SASP factor, produced predominantly by immune cells including monocytes and macrophages, and is elevated in the SASP of UVB-exposed melanocytes.

Beyond its intranuclear role, HMGB1 functions as an alarmin — a danger-associated molecular pattern (DAMP) — released from cells in a p53-dependent manner to signal cellular stress to immune cells and amplify inflammatory responses. Extracellular HMGB1 acts as a senescence factor that directly inhibits oligodendrocyte progenitor cell differentiation and impairs CNS remyelination. It is also released by tau oligomers, contributing to cellular senescence and neuropathology in Alzheimer's disease and frontotemporal dementia.

In additional tissue-specific contexts, HMGB1 mediates UVB-induced secretory inflammatory responses and resistance to apoptosis in melanocytes, and is associated with placental membrane aging and parturition. This dual role as both a nuclear architectural protein and an extracellular alarmin positions HMGB1 as a central, multifunctional regulator at the intersection of chromatin biology, cellular senescence, inflammation, and disease pathology.&lt;SEP&gt;High mobility group box 1 protein involved in signaling pathway related to hepatic fibrosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c0cbc7de7a8a1060e8f1608271a28c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca3e5da7a4fe15854dde6289fd15667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca844018665036149579ef01b8f9ec3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce1a7b385e0f22ca823c850de9bf8a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5bb73f6c7980a9f906f42f6e0e9ce27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a471400d67fa8643230222fb3576486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c5a8121875c37f2659ae345688f6afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b02375738a9ebacd53446fec2077ee76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf5aeca434044079720fd4ae2d32c9b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f25c34949789593e96b7a57ead73f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ab370ab273e97005ced113e59cc48a</data>
