<node id="CDKN2A">
  <data key="d0">CDKN2A</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">CDKN2A (Cyclin-Dependent Kinase Inhibitor 2A) is a genomic locus of broad biological significance, encoding two distinct protein products: p16INK4A (also referred to as p16Ink4a or P16INK4A) and p19ARF (also known as p19Arf, or p14ARF in humans). These proteins play critical and non-redundant roles in cell cycle regulation and tumor suppression.

p16INK4A is the primary and most widely studied product of the CDKN2A locus. It functions as a cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK) inhibitor — specifically inhibiting CDK4/6 — and mediates cell cycle arrest in conjunction with the retinoblastoma protein (Rb) through the p16-pRB axis. This arrest is particularly relevant in telomere attrition-induced DNA damage responses (DDR), where p16INK4A supplements the ATR pathway. p16INK4A is recognized as a major driver and marker of cellular senescence, though it is also expressed in some non-senescent cells and can be absent in certain senescent cells, making it a strong but not absolute senescence marker. p16-positive senescent cells are distinct from p21-positive cells. CDKN2A is involved in senescence and the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP).

p16INK4A expression is subject to epigenetic and post-transcriptional regulation. Age-dependent decreased methylation at the CDKN2A locus correlates with increased p16INK4A expression. At the post-transcriptional level, miR-24 suppresses p16INK4A translation by binding to the 3′UTR of its mRNA, thereby hindering replicative senescence. Additionally, CDKN2A is a target of beta cell extracellular vesicle (EV) miRNAs.

p16INK4A serves as a documented senescence marker across multiple tissues, including lung, liver, kidney, adipose tissue, bone marrow, bone, skin, mammary gland, ovaries, nevi, and the central nervous system. It is expressed in specific cell types such as beta cells, alpha cells, acinar cells, skeletal cells, T cells, and tubular epithelial cells. In the context of beta cell biology, CDKN2A (p16) expression is measurable via FISH and detectable by RNAscope multiplex fluorescent assay in FFPE pancreas slices, and its expression is downregulated under caloric restriction (CR). Naturally occurring p16INK4A-positive cells have been shown to shorten healthy lifespan, and senolytic compounds can deplete p16INK4A-expressing senescent cells in aged human donor islets.

Elevated CDKN2A expression has been observed in disease contexts including Alzheimer's disease, Parkinson's disease, and fetal growth restriction. Noncoding genetic signals at the CDKN2A locus are also associated with high risk for type 2 diabetes, impaired beta cell function, and loss of insulin release. CDKN2A-expressing peripheral mononuclear blood cells do not appear to survive cryopreservation.

p19ARF, the second major protein product of the CDKN2A locus, plays a distinct tumor-suppressive role. Selective loss of p16Ink4a with retention of p19Arf in mouse models predisposes mice to tumorigenesis, underscoring the functional non-redundancy of both proteins encoded by the same locus. Collectively, CDKN2A is a gene of broad significance spanning cancer predisposition, cellular aging, tissue homeostasis, and metabolic disease.&lt;SEP&gt;CDKN2A/p16INK4a is a protein targeted by rabbit monoclonal antibody in the study, associated with cell senescence.</data>
