<node id="autophagy">
  <data key="d0">autophagy</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Autophagy is a fundamental cellular degradation and recycling process that plays a central role in maintaining protein homeostasis, organelle turnover, and macromolecular complex degradation. It operates primarily through the ubiquitin and lysosome systems, involving the formation of autophagosomes and their fusion with lysosomes to degrade and recycle cellular components.

In the context of pancreatic beta cells, autophagy is subject to complex regulation and has been extensively studied in relation to caloric restriction (CR) and aging. Caloric restriction has been shown to significantly enhance autophagy in beta cells, with evidence including increased LC3I-II conversion, elevated LAMP1 vesicle density, and higher gene expression of autophagy-related genes such as LAMP1, CLTC, SQSTM1, ULK1, and CTSB, particularly in CR state 2 beta cells. CR-activated autophagy in beta cells preferentially targets poly-ubiquitinated (poly-Ub) substrates via p62-positive vesicles, rather than insulin granules or mitochondria. Additionally, autophagy in this context is linked to MAFA transcription factor targets, including BECLIN1 and LAMP1, and is regulated by transcription factors TFEB and TFE3, which are associated with CR-induced chromatin changes in beta cells.

Autophagy also differentially regulates insulin production and insulin sensitivity and is controlled in part by insulin granules through the autophagy-lysosome pathway. This process is notably preserved by intermittent fasting. In aging beta cells, autophagy becomes defective; however, CR has been shown to rescue autophagy homeostasis in these cells, thereby reducing aging-associated phenotypes.

Beyond beta cell biology, autophagy is increased by NRIP1 deletion, a change associated with delayed senescence and extended longevity. Autophagy is experimentally assessed using established markers such as LC3A/B, LAMP1, and p62, typically visualized via confocal imaging in pancreatic tissue.

Importantly, autophagy can also exhibit elevated senescence-associated beta-galactosidase (SA-βgal) activity, which may confound the detection of cellular senescence in experimental settings.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular self-degradation process regulated by lysosomal calcium signalling through calcineurin and TFEB, studied in pancreatic beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular self-degradation process regulated by nucleolar stress signaling.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathological alteration of pancreatic beta cells under stress; regulated by nucleolar stress signaling pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Biological process that can be facilitated by nucleolar stress signaling events.&lt;SEP&gt;Autophagy is a cellular process influenced by chromatin remodeling during senescence progression in the context of MAFAS64F-induced senescence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71a6a7a3a4894dbd7d979b7e5766d5a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68a87a3a1f3eb5d8b0e53b41df492512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0c13c5a18e2c5037815818d22c48a64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-364a12c675ebfd67c468ab6fc47e6696&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dcd3bdc472cb74376c18c72cf9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08e345f1e7ff9529c2ab94760dcb50f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002f23b1d29df711e839459b4d6f0711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cadc21c6ca916dd06510987c1b7dbf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f39153d9f864070e17a74724a6c8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-363f3a19905f833d29a7d1242acc6641&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa01ef8f1657a9890660f864f52123c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b61717ec6764bc92fa7c3c5067d345&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e181574d19e6f17c0fd64b0feecf8f9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-787de2a35f76e08d5b7cddadbcc01954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e33cd1d99c96a7f6ffdd0fbea902670&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2caaffb8c37fe000f2af35e115fc27</data>
