<node id="EndoC-βH5">
  <data key="d0">EndoC-βH5</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">EndoC-βH5 is a human pancreatic beta cell line that is storable and ready-to-use, characterized by physiological insulin secretion, making it a valuable tool for research purposes. It is derived from human fetal tissue and is of female origin.

EndoC-βH5 has been widely employed in studies investigating DNA damage-induced senescence in beta cells. In these experimental contexts, the cell line serves as a model for direct DNA damage studies related to beta-cell senescence. A notable finding from such research is the upregulation of BCL-XL at the mRNA level following DNA damage-induced senescence, suggesting a role for anti-apoptotic signaling in the senescent state of these cells. Additionally, DNA damage-induced senescence in EndoC-βH5 cells has been shown to impair glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (GSIS), highlighting the functional consequences of senescence on beta-cell physiology.

Overall, EndoC-βH5 represents a physiologically relevant human beta cell model that bridges practical utility — through its storable and ready-to-use nature — with scientific applicability in understanding mechanisms of beta-cell senescence, DNA damage responses, and insulin secretion dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5dc00b5dc127eca5ff2847cc45392453&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43761b34ecc024bd90bfa8711c45a233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10bdc47e4d027606476b48a401061a27</data>
