<node id="aging">
  <data key="d0">aging</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Aging is a multifaceted biological process characterized by the progressive deterioration of cellular, tissue, and organ function across multiple systems. It represents a physiological context in which numerous interconnected mechanisms converge to drive functional decline and age-related disease.

At the cellular and molecular level, aging is strongly associated with the accumulation of senescent cells across tissues. This includes beta-cell senescence in both mouse and human models, where cells with high SA-βgal activity accumulate and display characteristic senescence features. Aging serves as a key physiological trigger for beta-cell senescence and broadly impairs tissue function while reducing overall longevity. The process is also driven by cellular senescence in post-mitotic cells, contributing to endocrine disease and broader aging biology.

Aging exerts particularly well-documented effects on pancreatic islet biology. It is associated with p16INK4a-induced decline in islet regenerative potential, accompanied by epigenetic changes that compromise cellular plasticity and renewal capacity. Aging further compromises human islet beta-cell function and identity by decreasing transcription factor activity and inducing endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress. At the transcriptional level, aging results in lower transcriptional fidelity over time, contributing to progressively worse beta-cell function and reflecting a broader deterioration of cellular quality control mechanisms. DNA breaks also progressively accumulate with aging, contributing to altered cellular identity, function, and survival of mature beta cells, and aging-associated DNA lesions further impact genome integrity.

Beyond pancreatic biology, aging is linked to immune dysfunction, vascular disease, neurodegeneration, and DNA oxidation resulting in genomic and functional mosaicism in human neurons. At the molecular signaling level, aging involves CD38 induction, GSK3β overexpression, BDNF induction in adipocyte progenitors, and impaired stem cell function. Aging is also associated with increased circulating levels of specific microRNAs in extracellular vesicles (EVs), including miR-146a, miR-21, miR-223, let-7a, and upregulation of miR-29 in aged mice, with EVs serving as modulators of beta-cell dysfunction, senescence, and diabetes in the context of aging.

In summary, aging represents the convergence of senescent cell accumulation, DNA damage, telomere dysfunction, epigenetic dysregulation, impaired transcription factor activity, ER stress, reduced transcriptional fidelity, and altered miRNA signaling, collectively driving tissue deterioration, metabolic dysfunction, and systemic age-related decline.&lt;SEP&gt;Progressive loss of normal cell and tissue function linked to degenerative and metabolic diseases; caloric restriction delays beta cell aging hallmarks.&lt;SEP&gt;Biological aging process during which loss of autophagy markers LC3I-II and LAMP1 is prevented by caloric restriction in beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Process of biological aging linked to DNA damage, beta cell senescence, and diabetes progression.&lt;SEP&gt;A biological process associated with sex dimorphism and age-related diseases; sex-dependent changes to genes involved in aging are observed in MafAS64F/+ males.&lt;SEP&gt;The biological process of growing older, associated with cellular senescence, accumulation of senescent cells, and organ dysfunction, studied in the context of immune surveillance and therapeutic opportunities.</data>
