<node id="ATM">
  <data key="d0">ATM</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">ATM (Ataxia Telangiectasia Mutated) is a multifunctional serine/threonine kinase that serves as a central sensor and mediator of the DNA damage response (DDR), with broad roles in cellular senescence, stem cell biology, and tissue-specific stress signaling.

As a primary responder to DNA double-strand breaks (DSBs), ATM initiates and propagates downstream DDR signaling cascades. Upon activation, ATM is phosphorylated at Serine 1981 (Ser1981), and this phosphorylated form (p-ATM) serves as a detectable marker of DDR activation and cellular senescence, commonly identified via techniques such as Western blot analysis and rabbit monoclonal antibody targeting. ATM also mediates the stabilization of p53, a key mechanism triggering cellular senescence, positioning ATM as a pivotal upstream regulator in the DNA damage-to-senescence signaling axis. Additionally, ATM is involved in the initiation and propagation of the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) and paracrine senescence.

Beyond canonical DSB-induced activation, ATM can be activated through non-canonical mechanisms. Notably, ATM is activated during nucleolar stress-induced pseudo-DDR independently of DNA double-strand breaks, driving beta cell senescence through nucleolar chromatin reorganization and oxidative stress pathways. Pharmacological inhibition of ATM using KU60019 has been shown to attenuate this senescent phenotype, underscoring ATM's functional role in nucleolar stress-induced senescence. ATM is also activated by nitric oxide in beta cells, causing gamma-H2AX formation, and mediates pro-inflammatory characteristics in beta cells; beta-cell-specific depletion of ATM has been shown to protect against streptozotocin-induced islet inflammation.

In the context of pancreatic islets, studies on human islets have revealed a sex-dependent difference in ATM activation: following bleomycin-induced DNA damage, ATM displays a more robust and sustained activation in male human islets compared to female human islets, suggesting sex-specific variation in the DDR mediated by this kinase.

Beyond pancreatic contexts, ATM plays a role in neural stem cell biology, specifically modulating the maintenance and senescence of subventricular zone (SVZ) neural stem cells through the Notch signaling pathway, highlighting its broader regulatory influence across distinct tissue types and stem cell niches.

Collectively, ATM functions as a critical integrator of multiple stress-response pathways, influencing cellular senescence, p53 stabilization, beta cell inflammation, sex-dependent stress responses in pancreatic islets, and neural stem cell homeostasis.</data>
