<node id="ketosis-prone diabetes">
  <data key="d0">ketosis-prone diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">Disease</data>
  <data key="d2">Ketosis-prone diabetes is a distinct subtype of type 2 diabetes (T2D) characterized by acute beta cell failure and severe insulin deficiency. It is predominantly observed in males of sub-Saharan African descent, with a notably increased risk in men compared to premenopausal women, suggesting a significant influence of sex and gender on its pathogenesis.

The condition is marked by severe beta cell dysfunction, which is reminiscent of decreased cAMP-stimulated insulin secretion observed in black male islets. This biological mechanism underscores the population-specific and sex-specific nature of the disease. The acute nature of beta cell failure in ketosis-prone diabetes distinguishes it from more typical presentations of type 2 diabetes, often requiring initial insulin therapy before potential remission.

Research into ketosis-prone diabetes has focused on patients of sub-Saharan African origin, with studies identifying both gender and the transcription factor NEUROG3 as factors influencing its pathogenesis. NEUROG3 is known to play a role in pancreatic development and beta cell differentiation, suggesting that genetic and developmental factors may contribute to the susceptibility and progression of this condition in affected populations.

Overall, ketosis-prone diabetes represents a clinically and biologically distinct form of type 2 diabetes, defined by its demographic concentration in Black men of sub-Saharan African descent, its severe and acute impairment of beta cell function, and its association with specific molecular and genetic factors that differentiate it from more common forms of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ketosis-prone diabetes is a form of diabetes that occurs predominantly in Black men.&lt;SEP&gt;A form of T2D with acute beta cell failure and severe insulin deficiency predominantly observed in black men, also referred to in the context of the Louet et al and Mauvais-Jarvis et al citations.</data>
