<node id="cardiomyocyte">
  <data key="d0">cardiomyocyte</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Cardiomyocytes are post-mitotic heart muscle cells that play a central role in cardiac function and are subject to various forms of cellular senescence and stress-related changes.

A well-documented phenomenon in cardiomyocytes is their susceptibility to doxorubicin-induced senescence, a form of DNA-damage-induced senescence. Notably, testosterone has been shown to antagonize and ameliorate this senescence response, suggesting a protective hormonal role in preserving cardiomyocyte integrity under genotoxic stress conditions.

Cardiomyocytes also undergo senescence driven by length-independent telomere damage, a mechanism distinct from classical telomere shortening. During normal aging in mice, cardiomyocytes accumulate a broad array of senescence-associated markers, including telomere-associated foci (TAF), cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors CDKN1a, CDKN2B, and CDKN2a, senescence-associated β-galactosidase (SA-β-gal), senescence-associated distension of satellites (SADS), and pro-fibrotic senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) factors. This accumulation highlights cardiomyocytes as key contributors to age-related cardiac decline.

In pathological contexts, senescence-associated markers in cardiomyocytes are further elevated following myocardial infarction in mice, underscoring the relevance of cardiomyocyte senescence in both aging and acute cardiac injury.

From a developmental and sex-specific perspective, cardiomyocyte differentiation is impaired by the downregulation of KDM5D, a histone demethylase. Furthermore, KDM5D has been identified as a driver of sex differences in cardiomyocytes in males, indicating a role for epigenetic regulation in mediating sex-specific cardiac biology.

Collectively, cardiomyocytes represent a critical cell type at the intersection of cardiac aging, DNA damage responses, hormonal signaling, epigenetic regulation, and disease pathology.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart muscle cell whose differentiation is impaired by downregulation of KDM5D.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart muscle cell type whose differentiation is impaired by KDM5D down-regulation.</data>
