<node id="sex chromosome">
  <data key="d0">sex chromosome</data>
  <data key="d1">Molecule</data>
  <data key="d2">Sex chromosomes, comprising the X and Y chromosomes, are a fundamental chromosomal category that plays a central role in establishing biological sex differences across multiple physiological contexts.

In the context of pancreatic islet biology, sex chromosomes are a predominant source of sex-associated differences in gene expression and chromatin accessibility. The majority of sex-associated differentially expressed genes (DEGs) identified in non-diabetic donors are linked to sex chromosomes, while individuals with type 2 diabetes (T2D) exhibit additional autosomal differences beyond those attributable to sex chromosomes alone. This pattern suggests that sex chromosome-driven expression differences are most prominent in nondiabetic islet cells, with autosomal contributions becoming more pronounced in the context of disease.

Sex chromosomes carry specific sex-biased genes, including USP9Y, KDM5D, RPS4X, and RPS4Y, which contribute to sex-specific biological functions. Their gene dosage and expression patterns are implicated in cell-autonomous sex differences in islet function, mitochondrial activity, and broader islet biology, highlighting their direct mechanistic role in pancreatic cell biology rather than solely acting through systemic hormonal pathways.

Beyond islet function, sex chromosomes are recognized as chromosomal components that contribute to biological sex differences in senescence programs and metabolic disease risk. The gene accessibility and expression differences encoded on sex chromosomes are considered key drivers of sexually dimorphic cellular behavior, and their influence on diabetes pathogenesis underscores their relevance to understanding sex-specific susceptibility to metabolic diseases.

Overall, sex chromosomes serve as a critical genomic determinant of cell-autonomous sex differences, shaping gene expression, chromatin accessibility, mitochondrial activity, and disease risk in a sex-specific manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes (X and Y) whose encoded genes show differential expression and chromatin accessibility in islet cells between sexes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex chromosomes are the primary source of transcriptional differences observed across islet beta and alpha cells between male and female donors.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes determining sex; most sex-associated DEGs in non-diabetic donors were related to sex chromosomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sexually dimorphic chromosomes (X and Y) whose cell-autonomous gene expression contributes to sex-specific differences in islet cell function and T2D pathogenesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex chromosomes driving sex-specific genomic differences in non-diabetic individuals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8304b17dc893b8c9bfb7bfdb03703396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b67e26d82684726785f1910ba6919cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3982c3cb7b00162818de82027a853e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794cb97944fe847e61831fceed4d92ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a24341c9b63bd3b6313252fe6ed4b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238834380bfb40677324883af3e3004&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0269f34de8f0928a9614438917079ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76049b85888e8e904e7c01de9740d34f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d41db2658ec2709309c04b39b1894e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da10e6374cdbd3d6e4a07d773cd172d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cd7041acf7539c8db67413bcd98f41b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804ddf6c13fee09d00987476aa3c478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-822d03d11fbf61e2a25591e2663ef1ca</data>
