<node id="fasting glucose">
  <data key="d0">fasting glucose</data>
  <data key="d1">Molecule</data>
  <data key="d2">Fasting glucose is a metabolic trait defined as the blood glucose level measured after a period of fasting. It is a widely studied clinical and genomic marker with notable biological and genetic characteristics.

From a physiological standpoint, fasting glucose exhibits clear sex-based differences: adult premenopausal women tend to have lower fasting glucose levels than men, and impaired fasting glucose — a hallmark of prediabetes — is more commonly observed in men. In animal model research, fasting glucose levels showed no statistically significant differences between ad libitum (AL) and caloric restriction (CR) groups in mice, with values recorded at approximately 114.0 ± 19.34 mg/dL for AL and 105.4 ± 14.81 mg/dL for CR animals.

Genetically, fasting glucose is recognized as a metabolic trait with sex-dimorphic genetic effects, and genome-wide association studies (GWAS) have identified novel loci associated with it. It has been studied using sex-stratified GWAS approaches in conjunction with differentially accessible chromatin peaks, and its GWAS summary statistics — sourced from the Meta-Analyses of Glucose and Insulin-related traits Consortium (MAGIC) — have been employed in partitioned heritability analyses and linkage disequilibrium score regression (LDSC) enrichment analyses.

As a clinical trait, fasting glucose has also been used in genomic enrichment analyses focused on islet cell types, with significant genomic enrichment identified specifically in islet endocrine cells in females only, highlighting its sex-specific regulatory architecture in pancreatic tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood glucose level measured in the fasting state; does not correlate with beta cell mass in the context of clinical outcomes.</data>
