<node id="DNA damage response">
  <data key="d0">DNA damage response</data>
  <data key="d1">Pathway</data>
  <data key="d2">The DNA damage response (DDR) is a fundamental and highly coordinated cellular mechanism activated in reaction to DNA damage, serving as a central process in maintaining genomic integrity, regulating cell cycle progression, and determining cell fate across multiple tissue types and physiological contexts, including aging, disease, and cellular senescence.

At the molecular level, the DDR functions as a signaling cascade initiated through ATM/ATR-mediated p53 stabilization, which triggers downstream pathways leading to cell cycle arrest. Key molecular components include ATM, ATR, p53, p21Cip1, PCNA, RAD51, and H2A.X. Canonical markers of active DDR signaling include γH2AX (phosphorylated histone H2AX) and 53BP1, both widely observed in senescent cells as established indicators of unresolved DNA damage. Phosphorylated p38 MAPK is also associated with DDR activity, particularly in senescent cells found in the nervous system and adipose tissue.

The DDR operates within a chromatin context, involving coordinated interactions with CTCF, cohesin, Polycomb repressors, and epigenome maintenance machinery. Its initiation and efficacy are dependent upon chromatin organization, and it can be activated by programmed DNA breaks in regulatory genomic regions, making it instrumental in signal-dependent transcriptional regulation.

A notable dimension of DDR involves its activation by nucleolar stress in beta cells. Exposure to nucleolar stress agents such as CX-5461 and ActD triggers γH2AX foci formation and ATM/ATR activation, contributing to a senescent phenotype. Importantly, a pseudo-DDR can be triggered by nucleolar stress even in the absence of actual double-strand breaks, indicating that canonical DDR signaling pathways can be hijacked by non-canonical stimuli to facilitate beta cell senescence. DDR activity in this context is also represented by NME1 expression in CR state 2 beta cells.

The DDR plays a prominent role in driving the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP), wherein senescent cells secrete pro-inflammatory cytokines, chemokines, and other bioactive factors. Regulation of the SASP is not solely dependent on canonical DDR signaling, as the p38 MAPK pathway can independently modulate it. The DDR also interfaces with innate immune signaling through the cGAS-STING pathway, which detects cytosolic DNA and amplifies inflammatory responses in senescent cells. Chronic DDR activation associated with aging additionally engages regulatory pathways involving miR-29 and p53, with upregulation of miR-29 correlating with elevated γH2AX levels and sustained damage signaling.

Broadly, the DDR has been identified as a hallmark of senescence in specific disease contexts, including beta-cell senescence during the development of type 1 diabetes, and has been documented in aging tissues following telomeric DNA damage, underscoring its relevance across both physiological and pathological conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathways that detect and repair DNA breaks; activated in beta cells in both T1D and T2D.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular pathway activated by DNA damage in beta cells that promotes islet inflammation in Type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular programs activated in response to DNA damage, including repair pathways and checkpoint activation, that differ between alpha and beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular response to DNA breaks, contributing to beta-cell senescence and dysfunction in both T1D and T2D contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular process involving detection and repair of DNA damage, differentially regulated between alpha and beta cells under homeostasis and stress.&lt;SEP&gt;DDR activity is high in neonatal islets, with both alpha and beta cells exhibiting replication-associated DNA damage but employing distinct repair strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c0cbc7de7a8a1060e8f1608271a28c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7bb3e4a69884a1b79485b95f6f502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac258601b299e0c883e8e1c4a3030d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d3b1de44d6b73a10793a43351e18e6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71a6a7a3a4894dbd7d979b7e5766d5a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca3e5da7a4fe15854dde6289fd15667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b02375738a9ebacd53446fec2077ee76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6215695e8bd809fd02cb8b9b7b4123ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-732255e9544dde6a7dbc319c61cd8e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32fd907a01d621388ff41b9b47bfa34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de697a7536ea955d878a95580bf8aa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-735ea17eaeb5e3524d0871e16116e986&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e7d877b531b99ded5aba6df6f1635f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80318e0997c6182c0124775c5f01f6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58f0193e389064a60d513f13c5cad52b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db03a7d3c861658f2beac422f86b21ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f008854d459401e11c35e6bd2541318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a0590fd9be1db550e8a84c0781e8955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bb74bfaf0604b4b9f891e16fc9f0ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-787de2a35f76e08d5b7cddadbcc01954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ab370ab273e97005ced113e59cc48a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e5c8949dfa6aa24ddf3e3b92d956e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28a44d52c17c29e9741a935aa76e8afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7af94fcbbc3d167377ece712218fa36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35d7a41162ea60151fe17b4e60e06d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6204b07313b33d741c243f18ce86735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad82642ce24967122d02819b52a6f45</data>
