<node id="metabolic syndrome">
  <data key="d0">metabolic syndrome</data>
  <data key="d1">Disease</data>
  <data key="d2">Metabolic syndrome is a cluster of metabolic conditions characterized by a complex interplay of cellular and molecular mechanisms that contribute to its development and progression.

One key contributor to metabolic syndrome is the accumulation of senescent cells, including beta cells, hepatocytes, and preadipocytes. The buildup of these aged, dysfunctional cells across multiple tissue types is recognized as a driving factor in the pathogenesis of this condition.

Another central element in the pathogenesis of metabolic syndrome is the involvement of interleukin-18 (IL-18), a cytokine whose signaling has been broadly implicated in the disorder. IL-18 signaling plays a notable role in the development of metabolic syndrome, with particular relevance to conditions such as diabetes and obesity, which are commonly associated with the syndrome. Research has also highlighted the involvement of IL-18 cytokine signaling specifically in black male beta cells, suggesting potential population- or cell-type-specific dimensions to its pathogenic role.

Overall, metabolic syndrome represents a multifactorial metabolic disorder in which both cellular senescence across key metabolic tissues and dysregulated IL-18 signaling converge to promote its onset and progression, with implications spanning insulin dysregulation and adipose tissue dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic condition that can progress to type 2 diabetes with associated beta-cell dysfunction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b67e26d82684726785f1910ba6919cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3982c3cb7b00162818de82027a853e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794cb97944fe847e61831fceed4d92ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238834380bfb40677324883af3e3004&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd4ac2599dc6d5dc028ad0e83ccf82f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-822d03d11fbf61e2a25591e2663ef1ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d41db2658ec2709309c04b39b1894e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86</data>
