<node id="senolytic">
  <data key="d0">senolytic</data>
  <data key="d1">Drug</data>
  <data key="d2">Senolytics are a class of pharmacological agents designed to selectively target and eliminate senescent cells, functioning primarily by disarming the anti-apoptosis pathways that allow these cells to persist in tissues. By promoting the death of senescent cells, senolytics aim to mitigate the harmful effects of cellular senescence across a broad range of pathological conditions.

The class encompasses a diverse array of compounds, including small molecules such as Fisetin, Dasatinib, Quercetin, cardiac glycosides, and HSP90 inhibitors. Additional agents within this category include ABT-737, ABT-199, ABT-263, the Dasatinib and Quercetin combination (D+Q), and FOXO4 inhibitory peptides, all of which preferentially disrupt the survival mechanisms of senescent cells to induce their clearance.

Senolytics have demonstrated beneficial effects across multiple disease models and clinical contexts. In preclinical research, they have shown efficacy in mouse models of diabetes, as well as in Alzheimer's disease and tauopathy mouse models, where disease-related phenotypes have shown measurable responsiveness to senolytic treatment. Their clinical applications extend to conditions such as diabetic kidney disease and idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. Senolytics have also been investigated for their utility in alleviating chemotherapy-induced organ damage, specifically doxorubicin-induced and cisplatin-induced ovarian injury.

As therapeutic agents, the effective deployment of senolytics is supported by validated senescence biomarkers, which aid in monitoring treatment outcomes and guiding therapeutic strategies. Overall, senolytics represent a promising and expanding area of translational medicine focused on targeting the senescent cell burden underlying numerous age-related and chronic diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Senolytics are compounds that trigger apoptosis preferentially in senescent cells and can selectively eliminate senescent beta cells via BCL-2 inhibition.&lt;SEP&gt;Compounds that deplete senescent cells; include ABT-199 and ABT-737, used preclinically in NOD mice for T1D.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b861cee193cad9e0f142051047e83aae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d3b1de44d6b73a10793a43351e18e6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ff588c6efe73ecb07c6b95acb392e92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-682da4208f0eb800bc5523bd70a84eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf5aeca434044079720fd4ae2d32c9b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1628eba511291c52eab3e72ef0fd0239&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32fd907a01d621388ff41b9b47bfa34</data>
