<node id="ABT-737">
  <data key="d0">ABT-737</data>
  <data key="d1">Drug</data>
  <data key="d2">ABT-737 is a small molecule BH3 mimetic compound originally developed for use in cancer therapy that has demonstrated significant senolytic activity. It functions by inhibiting anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family proteins, thereby preferentially inducing apoptosis in senescent cells rather than healthy ones.

As a BH3-only mimetic senolytic agent, ABT-737 specifically targets the BCL-2 family of proteins and has been employed both in vitro and in vivo in preclinical research settings. One of its primary applications in this context involves the clearance of senescent beta cells in NOD (Non-Obese Diabetic) mice, a model relevant to Type 1 Diabetes (T1D). NOD mice are noted to preferentially upregulate Bcl-2, making them particularly susceptible to ABT-737's mechanism of action.

In studies conducted using NOD mouse islets, ABT-737 treatment resulted in the near-complete elimination of p21Cip1 expression and approximately 50% reduction in p16Ink4a expression — both established biomarkers of cellular senescence — confirming its efficacy in depleting senescent beta cells. Importantly, this selective depletion of senescent beta cells in NOD mice has been shown to confer protection against the development of T1D, highlighting the compound's potential therapeutic relevance beyond oncology.

Overall, ABT-737 represents a well-characterized senolytic tool compound with demonstrated preclinical utility in both cancer biology and the study of senescence-driven pathologies such as Type 1 Diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-add59972a262992e4bde6910520c0cdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b861cee193cad9e0f142051047e83aae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c0cbc7de7a8a1060e8f1608271a28c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-184abd4f45a78238743890eb9d4f8c56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1628eba511291c52eab3e72ef0fd0239</data>
