<node id="ABT-263">
  <data key="d0">ABT-263</data>
  <data key="d1">Drug</data>
  <data key="d2">ABT-263, also known by its clinical name Navitoclax, is a small molecule BH3 mimetic compound originally developed for use in cancer therapy that has since demonstrated significant senolytic activity.

At its mechanistic core, ABT-263 functions by inhibiting anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family proteins, thereby preferentially inducing apoptosis in senescent cells. By targeting BCL-2 family members, the compound selectively clears senescent cells that rely on these pro-survival proteins to evade programmed cell death, making it a potent tool in senolytic research and therapeutic development.

In the context of pancreatic biology, ABT-263 has been studied for its ability to clear senescent beta cells and improve glycemic responses. Notably, it has been shown to be sensitive to SA-βgal-positive (SA-βgal+) cells derived from C57BL/6J male mice, and its application in this model leads to improved beta-cell function and identity, highlighting its potential relevance to metabolic disease and diabetes research.

At the molecular level, ABT-263 has been found to act preferentially on specific senescence markers. In aged human donor islets, ABT-263 functions primarily as a senolytic compound that depletes CDKN2A (p16Ink4a mRNA) rather than CDKN1A, suggesting a degree of selectivity in its mechanism of action that distinguishes its effects across different senescence-associated gene targets.

Overall, ABT-263/Navitoclax represents a well-characterized senolytic agent with dual relevance in oncology and aging biology, capable of selectively eliminating senescent cells through BCL-2 family inhibition, with demonstrated functional improvements in beta-cell populations across both murine and human tissue models.&lt;SEP&gt;A senolytic compound targeting the BCL-2 family, tested in vitro and in vivo for eliminating senescent cells.</data>
