<node id="adipose tissue">
  <data key="d0">adipose tissue</data>
  <data key="d1">Tissue</data>
  <data key="d2">Adipose tissue, commonly known as fat tissue, is a metabolically active organ that plays a central role in energy storage and systemic metabolic regulation. It is increasingly recognized as a site where cellular and immune dysregulation converge to drive metabolic dysfunction, particularly in the contexts of obesity and aging.

A prominent feature of adipose tissue pathology involves the accumulation of senescent cells. These cells, characterized by high expression of p21Cip1 (also referred to as p21High cells), are strongly implicated in the development of insulin resistance in obesity. The tumor suppressor protein p53 plays a crucial regulatory role in this process, modulating insulin resistance through the induction of cellular senescence within adipose tissue. Senescent cell populations in adipose tissue exhibit the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) and display considerable heterogeneity in their subpopulations, accumulating progressively in both obesity and aging. Their presence is broadly linked to tissue dysfunction and the development of age-related metabolic diseases.

Experimental evidence from high-fat diet (HFD) mouse models demonstrates that selective deletion of p21High senescent cells in adipose tissue leads to meaningful improvements in metabolic health, underscoring the causal role these cells play in metabolic impairment rather than merely serving as correlative markers of disease.

Beyond senescence, adipose tissue is also subject to immune dysregulation during aging. Specifically, dysfunctional type 2 innate lymphoid cells (ILC2s) have been observed to expand within adipose tissue in response to IL-33 signaling, leading to thermogenic failure — a decline in the tissue's ability to generate heat — further contributing to age-associated metabolic deterioration.

Collectively, adipose tissue serves as a critical nexus for senescence-driven metabolic dysfunction, immune cell dysregulation, and the systemic consequences of both obesity and aging.&lt;SEP&gt;Tissue implicated in sex differences in adiposity influenced by X chromosome dosage.&lt;SEP&gt;Tissue in which ER stress is decreased by caloric restriction in ob/ob mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Adipose tissue dissected and processed for immunohistochemistry from AL, CR, and HFD mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Tissue that indirectly impacts beta cell function via mediators including miR-27a-5p-containing extracellular vesicles.&lt;SEP&gt;Fat tissue whose function is improved following bariatric surgery.</data>
