<node id="high fat diet">
  <data key="d0">high fat diet</data>
  <data key="d1">Drug</data>
  <data key="d2">High fat diet (HFD) is a dietary model and intervention widely used in mouse studies to investigate the effects of metabolic stress on pancreatic beta cells, insulin resistance, and obesity. It is commonly provided ad libitum and is abbreviated as HFD in experimental contexts.

In the context of beta cell biology, HFD is associated with a range of molecular and cellular changes. It induces stress- and diabetes-associated gene expression changes in beta cells, and is linked to increased activity of glucose-responsive transcription factors (TFs) as well as broad reprogramming of beta cell gene regulatory networks. These transcriptional changes reflect the adaptive and pathological responses of beta cells to chronic metabolic overload.

HFD is also used as a dietary model to induce beta cell senescence and to test senolytic interventions aimed at clearing senescent cells. At the cellular level, HFD feeding is associated with increased beta cell turnover, as evidenced by lower nuclear 15N levels in beta cells compared to those in ad libitum (AL) control mice — a finding derived from studies using 15N-labeled mice with the Rodent Diet 58Y1 formulation. Furthermore, a 60% high-fat dietary intervention has been shown to reduce the proportion of long-lived beta cells while promoting adaptive beta cell proliferation in response to metabolic stress.

From a methodological standpoint, HFD is applied to mice for defined durations — for example, two months of feeding prior to islet nuclei isolation for single nucleus multiomics experiments — enabling researchers to capture diet-induced epigenomic and transcriptomic changes at high resolution. Collectively, HFD serves as a well-established experimental tool for modeling type 2 diabetes-related beta cell dysfunction, senescence, and compensatory adaptation in vivo.&lt;SEP&gt;Dietary intervention fed to C57BL/6J mice for 8 weeks to induce metabolic stress; associated with increased DNA damage selectively in beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Dietary intervention used to induce metabolic stress and obesity-related T2D in mouse models; abbreviated HFD.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b861cee193cad9e0f142051047e83aae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-364a12c675ebfd67c468ab6fc47e6696&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dcd3bdc472cb74376c18c72cf9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08e345f1e7ff9529c2ab94760dcb50f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce68225f2288adf7d07962282496e8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d5c817dce40d1df02e7717bb9598632&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c85c9569fee140bfd52fcc0bbfbcc9c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7af94fcbbc3d167377ece712218fa36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad82642ce24967122d02819b52a6f45</data>
  <data key="d4">Pancreatic β-cell senescence in diabetes: mechanisms, markers and therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571466</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">14</data>
  <data key="d8">caloric restriction / autophagy / beta cell state 2</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00089051</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00144755</data>
  <data key="d11">0.04571497</data>
</node>
