<node id="CCL2">
  <data key="d0">CCL2</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">CCL2, also known as C-C motif chemokine ligand 2, is a chemokine-encoding gene prominently associated with cellular senescence and the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) across multiple tissue types and biological contexts.

CCL2 is consistently identified as a SASP component upregulated in senescent cells. In the context of pancreatic biology, CCL2 expression is elevated in senescent mouse beta cells. Similarly, it functions as a senescence marker in CD3+ T cells derived from both aged and ERCC1-deficient mice, the latter representing a model of accelerated aging. CCL2 also serves as a SASP factor expressed in senescent cells of the central nervous system and adipose tissue, and is further upregulated in senescent-like cell populations from aged mouse adipose tissue, as well as in senescent podocytes found in the kidneys of old mice.

In human contexts, CCL2 is upregulated in bone biopsies obtained from older women, establishing it as a senescence-associated chemokine relevant to human aging and skeletal biology. Additionally, CCL2 is induced as part of the SASP response in primary human melanocytes exposed to UVB radiation, highlighting its role in stress-induced senescence in skin cells. CCL2 expression is also elicited in ovarian tissue treated with doxorubicin, a chemotherapeutic agent known to induce senescence, further underscoring its involvement in therapy-induced senescence.

Collectively, CCL2 represents a broadly expressed SASP chemokine that is consistently upregulated across diverse species, tissues, and senescence-inducing stimuli, including aging, genotoxic stress, UV radiation, and chemotherapy exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;CCL2 is a chemokine component of the SASP secreted by senescent beta cells.</data>
