<node id="CCL5">
  <data key="d0">CCL5</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">CCL5, also known as C-C motif chemokine ligand 5, is a chemokine gene with multiple biological roles documented across various tissues and cellular contexts.

CCL5 is prominently recognized as a component of the Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype (SASP), a hallmark of cellular senescence in which aging or stressed cells secrete a variety of inflammatory mediators. In this capacity, CCL5 has been identified as a SASP factor upregulated in senescent human beta cells of the pancreas, as well as in senescent podocytes found in the kidneys of old mice. It is also expressed as a SASP factor in mouse models of insulin resistance and has been found elevated in the mammary glands of aged mice and in aged ovaries, underscoring its broad role in age-related inflammatory signaling across multiple organ systems.

Beyond its association with general cellular senescence, CCL5 has been specifically implicated in reproductive biology. It is secreted by senescent theca-interstitial cells and has been shown to inhibit preantral follicular development through the induction of granulosa cell apoptosis, suggesting a functional role in ovarian aging and fertility decline.

In addition to its role as a SASP factor, CCL5 serves as a marker gene for lymphocytes, as identified through single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis of human pancreatic islets and scRNA-seq cluster analysis more broadly. This highlights CCL5's utility in the transcriptomic identification and classification of immune cell populations within complex tissues.

Collectively, CCL5 represents a multifunctional chemokine involved in senescence-driven inflammation, immune cell identity, and tissue-specific physiological and pathological processes across aging organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;CCL5 is a marker gene specifically upregulated in lymphocytes, expressed independently of sex and race/ethnicity.</data>
