<node id="beta cell senescence">
  <data key="d0">beta cell senescence</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Beta cell senescence is a cellular senescence program occurring in pancreatic beta cells, representing a complex biological process with significant implications for metabolic health and disease. It is broadly recognized as an aging-associated process that can also be triggered by chronic metabolic stress, nucleolar stress, DNA damage, and other cellular insults.

At the mechanistic level, beta cell senescence is characterized by several hallmark features, including cell cycle arrest, increased lysosomal activity (reflected by senescence-associated beta-galactosidase, SA-β-gal, activity), prosurvival signaling, and the development of the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP). Key molecular markers include p16 (encoded by Cdkn2a), p21 (encoded by Cdkn1a), p53, γ-H2AX foci, ATM activation, 53BP1 accumulation, and reduced Lamin B1 (LMNB1) expression. Additional features include endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress, defective autophagy, and DNA damage accumulation. The process exhibits temporal dynamics: p21 is expressed early as an initial marker of senescence induction, while p16 persists over time as a marker of established senescence.

Beta cell senescence has multiple triggers and contexts. It can be induced by nucleolar stress (e.g., TIF-IA knockdown in beta-TC-6 cells), chronic metabolic stress, and aging. The MafaS64F/+ mutation in males on a mixed background has been associated with accelerated beta cell senescence, contributing to impaired insulin secretion and diabetes. Caloric restriction (CR) has been shown to reduce senescence markers, including 53BP1 accumulation, reduced LMNB1, and elevated CDKN2A/CDKN1A expression, suggesting a modulatory role for metabolic interventions.

The functional consequences of beta cell senescence include impaired insulin secretion, alterations to the islet microenvironment, and disruption of glucose homeostasis. The SASP contributes to paracrine signaling that can further deteriorate beta cell mass and function. Beta cell senescence also alters extracellular vesicle (EV) production, as well as their protein and miRNA cargo, which may mediate intercellular communication and accelerate disease progression.

From a pathophysiological standpoint, beta cell senescence has been implicated in the development and progression of both type 1 diabetes (T1D) and type 2 diabetes (T2D), contributing to beta cell failure and loss of beta cell mass. These characteristics make beta cell senescence a relevant and actively investigated target for therapeutic intervention in diabetes research.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular senescence program in pancreatic beta cells associated with diabetic beta-cell failure and aging.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular senescence process in beta cells associated with persistent DNA damage and failure in T1D and T2D.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac258601b299e0c883e8e1c4a3030d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bf309775cbb3bf74509531fe53568a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdd484417981e099a8f308ba0994bcaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1628eba511291c52eab3e72ef0fd0239&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68a87a3a1f3eb5d8b0e53b41df492512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f39153d9f864070e17a74724a6c8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-363f3a19905f833d29a7d1242acc6641&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0386e6a8316aca2a67c3466442e7e1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f09e5d1418f8ae04b43a24b97c1d52d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f008854d459401e11c35e6bd2541318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a0590fd9be1db550e8a84c0781e8955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bb74bfaf0604b4b9f891e16fc9f0ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b377c464e48f2efecc4b62133585c2f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f082082b0871749ee637b1571aa95c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e33cd1d99c96a7f6ffdd0fbea902670&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad82642ce24967122d02819b52a6f45</data>
