<node id="Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype">
  <data key="d0">Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype</data>
  <data key="d1">Phenotype</data>
  <data key="d2">A secretory phenotype (SASP) developed by senescent beta cells involving upregulation and secretion of inflammatory cytokines, chemokines, and extracellular matrix factors.&lt;SEP&gt;A secretory phenotype (SASP) developed by senescent beta cells involving upregulation and secretion of inflammatory cytokines, chemokines, and extracellular matrix factors.&lt;SEP&gt;The senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) is a well-characterized biological phenomenon exhibited by senescent cells, defined by a distinct secretory program involving the release of a broad array of bioactive molecules into the surrounding environment. These secreted factors include cytokines, chemokines, proteases, growth factors, and other tissue-remodeling molecules that collectively and significantly influence the tissue microenvironment.

SASP is closely associated with inflammation, as the secreted inflammatory factors can drive local and systemic age-related dysfunction. It is recognized as a well-established senescence-associated feature that shares similarities with other senescence markers. The phenotype has been linked to aging, disease progression, and tissue dysfunction, functioning in a cell-nonautonomous manner — meaning its effects extend beyond the senescent cell itself to neighboring and distant cells and tissues. Notably, SASP has been connected to oncogenic pathways involving RAS and p53, underscoring its relevance in cancer biology and tumor microenvironment regulation.

In specific tissue contexts, SASP plays important functional roles. For instance, senescent beta cells within the pancreatic islets exhibit SASP, whereby the secretory profile influences the islet microenvironment and integrated islet function, with implications for metabolic diseases such as diabetes. More broadly, the SASP-driven changes in the tissue microenvironment can contribute to chronic inflammation, impaired tissue repair, and the progression of multiple age-related pathologies.

Overall, SASP represents a critical mechanism through which senescent cells communicate with and alter their surrounding tissue, making it a key area of interest in research on aging, cancer, metabolic disease, and regenerative medicine.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenotype of senescent cells characterized by increased EV biogenesis, altered EV RNA and protein cargoes, and secretion of soluble inflammatory factors.&lt;SEP&gt;SASP; the secretory profile of senescent beta cells that exacerbates the inflammatory response within islets and contributes to T1D progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenotype of senescent cells involving altered secretion including EV release, potentially suppressed by senomorphic compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;SASP is a phenotype adopted by senescent beta cells under chronic metabolic stress, characterized by cytokines and ECM remodelling factors that promote beta cell failure.&lt;SEP&gt;SASP (senescence-associated secretory phenotype) involves secretion of SASP factors observed following nucleolar stress-induced beta cell senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;The senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) involves secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-1 beta and IL-6, measured in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c7bb3e4a69884a1b79485b95f6f502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71a6a7a3a4894dbd7d979b7e5766d5a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b773584b36e5b4845bedffea9a1b6add&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea13fe99fbc01cb6036ecddcda0aae8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c5a8121875c37f2659ae345688f6afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ff588c6efe73ecb07c6b95acb392e92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-682da4208f0eb800bc5523bd70a84eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf5aeca434044079720fd4ae2d32c9b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-184abd4f45a78238743890eb9d4f8c56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-732255e9544dde6a7dbc319c61cd8e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f3e602fed4b4ebfff436b75194d507&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f09e5d1418f8ae04b43a24b97c1d52d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bb74bfaf0604b4b9f891e16fc9f0ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e5c8949dfa6aa24ddf3e3b92d956e4</data>
