<node id="metabolic stress">
  <data key="d0">metabolic stress</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Metabolic stress is a form of physiological and cellular stress arising from metabolic challenges, particularly well-characterized in the context of pancreatic beta cells and type 2 diabetes.

As a physiological stressor, metabolic stress can trigger beta-cell senescence, contributing to beta cell vulnerability and dysfunction. In the context of type 2 diabetes, nutrient-induced metabolic stress in pancreatic beta cells is associated with a spectrum of outcomes, including cellular adaptation, detoxification, toxicity, and both persistent and transient dysfunction, underscoring its central role in disease progression.

At the molecular level, PAHSAs (Palmitic Acid Hydroxy Stearic Acids) have been identified as protective agents against metabolic stress in pancreatic beta cells. This protection is mediated through regulation of the MDM2/P53 (Mdm2/p53) axis, a pathway involved in cellular stress responses and survival signaling. Additionally, GIGYF1 has been suggested to play a role in beta cell stress responses and survival under conditions of metabolic stress, further highlighting the complex molecular network involved in managing this type of cellular challenge.

Overall, metabolic stress represents a critical convergence point between nutrient overload, cellular dysfunction, and molecular stress-response pathways in pancreatic beta cells, with significant implications for the pathophysiology of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress stimulus implicated in promoting beta cell senescence through DDR activation, including nucleolar disruption in senescent beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic stress is an unfavorable stimulus proposed to trigger nucleolar stress and facilitate the senescence program in pancreatic beta cells during diabetic progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress condition induced by increased beta-cell workload and replication under overnutrition, leading to DNA damage preferentially in beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Condition that beta cells in 3aPKO mice show impaired adaptability to, alongside functional defects and DNA damage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-58ad905d1d148d4bbfe7ce4c29566fc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea13fe99fbc01cb6036ecddcda0aae8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43761b34ecc024bd90bfa8711c45a233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-044fb2d3ececa99a53eb87d2ee6bf18f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e181574d19e6f17c0fd64b0feecf8f9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bb74bfaf0604b4b9f891e16fc9f0ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c63b5ad461844f8e5d9a90f5dfc52f1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7af94fcbbc3d167377ece712218fa36b</data>
