<node id="H2AX">
  <data key="d0">H2AX</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">H2AX is a histone variant protein whose phosphorylated form, commonly referred to as γH2AX (also written as gamma-H2AX or gH2AX), serves as a widely recognized marker of DNA damage and the DNA damage response (DDR). Phosphorylation occurs primarily at serine 139 (Ser139), and γH2AX is considered the most extensively utilized marker for assessing the DDR across various tissues, including pancreatic islet cells such as beta and alpha cells.

H2AX is canonically phosphorylated in response to DNA double-strand breaks (DSBs), where it marks damage sites and facilitates repair signaling. It functions as a substrate of the ATM kinase, and γH2AX foci formation has been observed in beta cells upon ATM activation triggered by nitric oxide. H2AX phosphorylation has also been reported following ionotropic glutamate receptor activation, and cohesin has been identified as a protective factor against γH2AX induction at gene loci. Notably, punctate H2AX has been observed to increase in 3aPKO beta cells, indicating DNA double-strand breaks in that specific context.

Beyond its classical role in DSB signaling, γH2AX foci formation has also been observed in the context of nucleolar stress in beta cells, occurring independently of DNA double-strand breaks. γH2AX foci are upregulated in primary islets treated with CX-5461, a nucleolar stress-inducing agent, highlighting a broader role for H2AX phosphorylation in cellular stress responses beyond canonical DSB repair.

In experimental settings, H2AX staining patterns provide important quantitative information about DNA damage severity. Punctate or nuclear foci patterns of H2AX indicate modest DNA damage, while pan-nuclear staining indicates severe, pre-apoptotic (terminal) damage. This distinction is applied to assess both punctate and pan-nuclear DNA damage in islet cells, including neonatal alpha and beta cells, under various diabetic conditions.

In the context of cellular senescence, γH2AX is implicated as a driver of senescence and has been detected in senescent beta and alpha cells in NOD mice. From a technical standpoint, commercially available antibodies targeting H2AX have been noted to lack specificity in islet studies, raising concerns about the reliability of certain experimental findings in pancreatic islet research that rely on these reagents.</data>
