<node id="paracrine senescence">
  <data key="d0">paracrine senescence</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Paracrine senescence is a biological process in which senescence spreads from one cell to neighboring cells through the action of secreted factors, rather than arising independently within each cell. In this process, primary senescent cells release signaling molecules that induce a senescent state in previously non-senescent ("naive") neighboring cells, effectively propagating cellular senescence across a tissue in a cell-to-cell manner.

The regulation of paracrine senescence involves multiple molecular mechanisms. Key regulatory factors include MACROH2A1, a histone variant, and ATM (Ataxia Telangiectasia Mutated), a DNA damage response kinase, both of which have been identified as modulators of this spread. Additionally, the inflammasome secretory program plays a controlling role in governing how senescence is transmitted between cells. The secreted mediators responsible for this transmission are collectively known as SASP (Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype) factors, which are released by primary senescent cells and act upon surrounding cells to trigger secondary senescence.

Experimental evidence for paracrine senescence has been demonstrated using conditioned media derived from islets containing senescent beta cells. When this conditioned media — enriched with factors secreted by senescent beta cells — was applied to naive cells, it was sufficient to induce senescence in those recipient cells, confirming the paracrine nature of the process.

Overall, paracrine senescence represents a mechanism by which cellular senescence can propagate through tissues, with implications for aging, tissue dysfunction, and disease, and is governed by a combination of chromatin-regulatory proteins, DNA damage signaling pathways, and inflammasome-driven secretory programs.</data>
