<node id="fibroblast">
  <data key="d0">fibroblast</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Fibroblast is a connective tissue cell type that holds a central and foundational role in cellular senescence research. Fibroblasts have been extensively employed in key early studies that established p21Cip1 (P21CIP1) and p16Ink4a (P16INK4A) as major drivers of the senescence program, and human fibroblast senescence models have contributed critical knowledge about the underlying mechanisms of cellular aging. They have been particularly valuable in investigating the sequential activation of p21Cip1 and p16Ink4a during senescence progression, as well as in elucidating the differential roles of p21 and p16 in senescence versus differentiation. A well-characterized fibroblast line, the human foreskin fibroblast Hs68 cell line, has been employed to validate senescence-associated beta-galactosidase (SA-β-gal) activity as a reliable biomarker of cellular aging.

Fibroblasts are also used as models for studying specific forms of stress-induced senescence, including H2O2-induced premature senescence and UV-induced senescence, particularly in dermal contexts. Senescent fibroblasts exhibit phosphorylation of RAD17 as part of the DNA damage response, and they express classical senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) components. Dermal fibroblasts additionally produce skin aging-associated secreted proteins, with chemerin found at high levels in aged skin.

In pathological and therapeutic contexts, activated fibroblasts are targeted alongside senescent cholangiocytes using BCL-XL (BCL-extra large) inhibitors to ameliorate fibrosis, as demonstrated in Mdr2-knockout mouse models. This targeting strategy highlights the clinical relevance of fibroblasts in fibrotic disease. Furthermore, targeting senescence-like fibroblasts in the tumor microenvironment has been shown to radiosensitize non-small cell lung cancer cells while simultaneously reducing radiation-induced pulmonary fibrosis.

Fibroblasts also serve as a non-trophoblast cell lineage in which syncytiotrophoblast-specific genes are induced during both replicative and stress-induced senescence, as well as during physiological aging, indicating a broader transcriptional reprogramming that occurs in these cells during senescence. Collectively, fibroblasts represent both essential research tools for understanding basic senescence biology and clinically significant cellular targets in cancer treatment and tissue damage mitigation.</data>
