<node id="obesity">
  <data key="d0">obesity</data>
  <data key="d1">Disease</data>
  <data key="d2">Obesity is a complex, multifactorial metabolic disease characterized by excessive body fat accumulation, with wide-ranging systemic effects involving multiple tissues, cellular mechanisms, and signaling pathways.

A prominent area of research concerns the role of cellular senescence in obesity-related pathology. Obesity is associated with the increased accumulation of senescent cells — particularly Cdkn1a-high (p21-highly expressing) cells — in adipose tissue and kidneys. These senescent cells are implicated in obesity-associated insulin resistance and contribute to elevated secretion of the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP). Obesity-related senescence also extends to the brain, where features of senescence have been recapitulated in mouse models, and to T cells, where T cell senescence has been observed as part of broader metabolic dysfunction. Senolytics — therapeutics designed to selectively eliminate senescent cells — have been shown to alleviate obesity-induced metabolic dysfunction, underscoring the causal role of senescent cells in disease progression.

Obesity exhibits important sex-specific dimensions and is recognized as a metabolic disease with notable sex differences. Estrogen and androgen receptors have emerged as promising therapeutic targets in this context, reflecting the influence of sex hormones on metabolic regulation, fat distribution, and metabolic homeostasis.

Obesity is also linked to immune and inflammatory signaling pathways, particularly IL-18 cytokine signaling. IL-18 signaling in pancreatic beta cells — especially in black male individuals — has been associated with obesity-related metabolic conditions, suggesting population-specific and cell-type-specific mechanisms influencing disease susceptibility. More broadly, inflammatory signaling in beta cells is implicated alongside diabetes and metabolic syndrome as co-occurring conditions.

Additional metabolic features of obesity include beta cell dysfunction, insulin resistance, and impaired insulin clearance. Obesity has been studied extensively in experimental contexts, including diet-induced obese mouse models used to investigate the effects of acute exercise on insulin clearance, as well as caloric restriction (CR) and intermittent fasting as potential interventions. Obesity has also been observed in CR mice exposed to a high-fat diet (HFD), highlighting the interplay between dietary patterns and fat accumulation.

Overall, obesity represents a condition with diverse and interacting biological underpinnings, encompassing senescent cell accumulation across multiple tissues, sex hormone receptor signaling, immune-inflammatory pathways, beta cell dysfunction, and significant metabolic dysregulation, with an elevated risk for diabetes and metabolic syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;Condition studied in causal relationship with hyperinsulinaemia, insulin resistance, and dysglycaemia in type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Condition associated with obese type 2 diabetes and beta cell deficit, studied in relation to weight management and bariatric surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;One of five genetic clusters in the type 2 diabetes polygenic score analysis, associated with BMI-related type 2 diabetes risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Condition in which serum and islet levels of miR-27a-5p are increased, inversely correlated with beta cell function.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic condition associated with increased beta-cell DNA damage vulnerability, modeled by HFD and db/db mice.&lt;SEP&gt;MafB deficiency accelerates the development of obesity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Obesity is a metabolic condition associated with hyperinsulinemia, β-cell stress, and impaired adaptive unfolded protein response in islet cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Obesity is a pathophysiologic condition that suppresses MAFA expression in islet β cells and is associated with aberrant non–β cell hormone production.&lt;SEP&gt;Obesity is a metabolic condition associated with loss of MafA in mouse models and is an earlier inducer of β cell dysfunction than mutations in other islet-enriched transcription factors.</data>
