<node id="proteomics">
  <data key="d0">proteomics</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">Proteomics is a broad analytical discipline focused on the large-scale study and characterization of proteins, and it is applied across a range of research contexts. In the field of aging and cellular senescence research, proteomics is employed as a proteomic atlas approach for the characterization of the senescence-associated secretome and the development of aging biomarkers. This includes protein-level analysis to monitor senescence-associated secretory phenotypes (SASP), enabling researchers to track molecular changes associated with cellular aging.

In the context of pancreatic biology, proteomics has been used to confirm the secretion of SASP protein factors from both senescent and non-senescent mouse and human pancreatic islet cells. It has also been applied to characterize the protein content of beta cell extracellular vesicles (EVs) derived from both mouse and human sources. Notably, while proteomic studies have been carried out to identify beta cell SASP factors, EVs were not specifically isolated in those particular investigations, representing a methodological distinction within this body of research.

Proteomics has additionally been proposed as an analytical approach to identify new potential surface markers of beta cell senescence on EVs, highlighting its prospective utility in biomarker discovery. Furthermore, proteomics demonstrates high concordance with pseudo-bulk single-cell accessible chromatin profiles, underscoring its integrative value when combined with other omics-level methodologies. Collectively, proteomics serves as a versatile and powerful tool in understanding cellular senescence, secretory phenotypes, and the molecular landscape of beta cells in both normal and pathological states.</data>
