<node id="CDK4">
  <data key="d0">CDK4</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">CDK4 (Cyclin-Dependent Kinase 4) is a cyclin-dependent kinase that plays a critical role in cell cycle regulation and cellular proliferation across multiple biological contexts.

One of CDK4's most well-characterized functions involves the regulation of pancreatic beta cells. Loss of CDK4 activity leads to insulin-deficient diabetes, while its activation results in beta-islet cell hyperplasia. CDK4 is particularly critical for beta cell proliferation in mice, where it is assembled into an active complex with cyclin D by the protein Sei1. This activity is subject to negative regulation by p16Ink4a, which targets CDK4 to modulate beta cell replication.

Beyond its role in metabolic regulation, CDK4 is also implicated in the senescence pathway. In senescent cells, CDK4 is inhibited by CDKN2A (also known as p16Ink4a), contributing to cell cycle arrest, a hallmark of cellular senescence.

Additionally, CDK4 has been observed to exhibit age-related expression changes; its mRNA levels increase in the kidney cortex with ageing, suggesting a potential role in age-associated renal biology.

From a molecular standpoint, CDK4 is encoded by the CDK4 gene and functions as a serine/threonine kinase whose activity is tightly regulated by cyclin partners and endogenous inhibitors. Its dual involvement in promoting cell proliferation (in contexts such as beta cell expansion) and its suppression in senescence highlight CDK4 as a key node in balancing cellular growth and arrest across different tissues and physiological states.</data>
