<node id="beta cell proliferation">
  <data key="d0">beta cell proliferation</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Beta cell proliferation refers to the process of cell division and replication of pancreatic beta cells, serving as the primary mechanism of beta cell renewal and mass expansion in response to physiological and metabolic demands.

At the molecular level, beta cell proliferation is regulated by a network of cell cycle factors, including p21Cip1, p16Ink4a, Sei1, CDK4, and CDK6. The tumor suppressor p16Ink4a plays a particularly notable regulatory role, and PTEN status has also been identified as an influencing factor. In the context of high-fat diet (HFD) conditions, proliferation is further linked to the activity of MLXIPL/ChREBP, and involves downstream targets of MAFA, specifically CCND1 and CDC42. Glucose itself can stimulate beta cell proliferation via ChREBP, underscoring the metabolic sensitivity of this process.

Beta cell proliferation is a key adaptive response to increased metabolic demand, especially under HFD conditions, where expansion of beta cell mass is required to maintain glucose homeostasis. However, this response is not uniform across all beta cell populations; a small subpopulation of HFD beta cells appears resistant to HFD-induced proliferation.

Caloric restriction (CR) has a significant suppressive effect on beta cell proliferation. CR beta cells exhibit limited cell division, resulting in a compromised adaptive response to HFD exposure. Notably, while reduced proliferation under CR maintains beta cells in a largely post-mitotic state, increased proliferation is associated with a more immature and less functional beta cell phenotype, suggesting a trade-off between proliferative capacity and functional maturity.

Additionally, treatment with S961, an insulin receptor antagonist, has been shown to affect beta cell replication, further highlighting the sensitivity of beta cell proliferation to both endogenous hormonal signals and experimental perturbations. Together, these findings position beta cell proliferation as a tightly regulated, context-dependent process critical to pancreatic beta cell homeostasis and adaptive capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;Process regulated by Notch signaling dynamically in adult beta cells, related to maturity.&lt;SEP&gt;Process of beta cell division and expansion, studied in identification of proliferative and mature beta-cell subpopulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta cell replication process reduced by pathophysiological levels of pancreatic elastase.&lt;SEP&gt;Process of beta cell replication; investigated as a potential direct effect of incretin therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta cell replication capacity impaired by beta cell senescence in high-fat diet-induced diabetic models.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4ef7d4c1829099c6037d8b8b7a83c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac258601b299e0c883e8e1c4a3030d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-364a12c675ebfd67c468ab6fc47e6696&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08e345f1e7ff9529c2ab94760dcb50f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002f23b1d29df711e839459b4d6f0711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-363f3a19905f833d29a7d1242acc6641&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c85c9569fee140bfd52fcc0bbfbcc9c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b61717ec6764bc92fa7c3c5067d345&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3384cef9855a1b408c68f7d1ea3153c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddac43d21e0426c8d61d02d10c0cb772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5596e00050d9f0135f87252a0093e9cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e181574d19e6f17c0fd64b0feecf8f9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298</data>
