<node id="DNA methylation">
  <data key="d0">DNA methylation</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">DNA methylation is an epigenetic modification involving the addition of a methyl group to cytosine residues (5-methylcytosine) in the genome. It plays a broad and multifaceted role in gene regulation, cellular differentiation, genomic stability, and aging across diverse biological contexts.

In the context of aging and cellular senescence, DNA methylation undergoes significant changes at specific genomic loci. Decreased methylation of the Cdkn2a locus has been observed with age in mice, correlating with increased expression of p16Ink4a, a key regulator of cellular senescence. More broadly, alterations in DNA methylation during senescence contribute to a global loss of heterochromatin alongside focal gains, reflecting widespread epigenetic landscape reorganization. Modulation of DNA methylation during cellular differentiation can also introduce DNA lesions and genomic vulnerability, highlighting the delicate balance required during this process.

In human pancreatic islets, DNA methylation exhibits notable sex differences. X-chromosome methylation is consistently higher in female than male pancreatic islets, and genome-wide patterns differ significantly between sexes. These sex-specific epigenetic differences have functional consequences, influencing gene expression, microRNA levels, and insulin secretion, underscoring DNA methylation's role in mediating sex-based physiological variation in pancreatic function. Additionally, widespread changes in DNA methylation at regulatory regions associated with beta-cell function have been observed in islets from type 2 diabetes donors, implicating this modification in disease-related epigenetic dysregulation.

Within pancreatic beta-cell biology, DNA methylation serves as a central epigenetic module across multiple stages of development and function. Patterns established during early pancreas development, particularly through the activity of DNMT3A in pancreatic progenitor cells, shape postnatal islet homeostasis and beta-cell identity, function, and survival. DNA methylation directs functional maturation of pancreatic beta cells, regulates beta-cell homeostasis, and plays critical roles at several differentiation steps, including pancreatic progenitor cell survival and neonatal beta-cell maturation. It also shapes differential DNA damage vulnerability between beta and alpha cells, maintaining genomic stability during pancreatic development. Disruption of DNA methylation during progenitor differentiation compromises beta-cell genomic integrity, reinforcing its essential role in both developmental programming and long-term cellular identity in the pancreas.</data>
