<node id="senescent cell">
  <data key="d0">senescent cell</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Senescent cells are cells that have undergone cellular senescence, a biological process defined primarily by stable cell cycle arrest. These cells are characterized by a senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP), through which they secrete a range of bioactive molecules that can influence the surrounding tissue microenvironment. In addition to SASP, senescent cells exhibit a broad spectrum of changes, including nuclear alterations, morphological modifications, and significant epigenetic and metabolic adaptations.

Senescent cells play a notable role in aging and age-related pathology. Naturally occurring p16Ink4a-positive variants of these cells have been shown to shorten healthy lifespan, and their accumulation has been linked to dysfunction across multiple organ systems, including the heart, vasculature, brain, and bone. Their presence contributes to the progression of various age-related diseases, making them a significant focus of biomedical research.

To better understand the distribution and behavior of senescent cells across the human lifespan, the NIH SenNet Consortium has undertaken systematic efforts to map these cells throughout the body. This initiative aims to characterize where and when senescent cells arise during normal human aging and disease.

Given their pathological contributions, senescent cells have become a therapeutic target. A class of drugs known as senolytics is specifically designed to selectively eliminate senescent cells, with the goal of restoring tissue homeostasis and potentially mitigating age-related decline. Research into senolytics represents a growing area of interest in the broader field of aging biology and regenerative medicine.</data>
