<node id="YY1">
  <data key="d0">YY1</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">YY1 (Yin-Yang 1) is a multifunctional transcription factor and chromatin modulator that plays critical roles in the biology of pancreatic beta cells in both humans and mice.

As a transcription factor, YY1 is essential for maintaining normal DNA repair and cell cycle progression in beta cells. Its deletion leads to a profound accumulation of beta cell DNA damage, resulting in extreme loss of beta cell mass through apoptosis, notably occurring without the involvement of cellular senescence. YY1 also functions as a key regulator of the beta-cell DNA damage response (DDR) and is known to be depleted in beta cells from individuals with type 2 diabetes (T2D), suggesting a potential link between YY1 insufficiency and the pathogenesis of T2D.

Beyond its role in DNA repair, YY1 acts as a modulator of three-dimensional chromatin architecture. Loss of YY1 in developing beta cells leads to impaired functional maturation and reduced beta cell viability, highlighting its importance during pancreatic development. Additionally, YY1 deficiency in beta cells has been associated with mitochondrial dysfunction and the onset of diabetes in mouse models.

From a clinical and genetic perspective, YY1 harbors recurrent T372R mutations identified in insulinomas, and mutations in YY1 more broadly have been linked to insulinoma development, underscoring its relevance as a potential oncogenic or tumor-suppressive factor in pancreatic endocrine tumors.

Collectively, YY1 serves as a central regulator of beta cell genomic integrity, chromatin organization, mitochondrial function, and developmental maturation, with its dysregulation implicated in both diabetes and pancreatic tumor formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor essential for normal DNA repair and cell cycle in human and mouse beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Epigenetic regulator critical for postnatal beta cell genomic stability, dysregulation of which is associated with diabetes.</data>
