<node id="DNA repair">
  <data key="d0">DNA repair</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">DNA repair is a fundamental cellular process responsible for identifying and correcting damage to the DNA molecule, thereby maintaining genomic integrity. It encompasses multiple distinct molecular mechanisms, including nucleotide excision repair, nonhomologous end joining, and homologous recombination, and is regulated by key signaling pathways involving ATM/ATR kinases and p53. Certain agents, such as PAHSAs, have been shown to enhance DNA repair activity, while estrogen signaling has been identified as an inhibitory regulator through its effects on ATR.

In the context of pancreatic beta cell biology, DNA repair plays a particularly critical role. It is essential for normal beta cell function, and its deficiency has been directly linked to diabetes phenotypes and beta cell dysfunction. Impaired DNA repair can predispose beta cells to defects that contribute to the development of diabetes, while deficiency in repairing endogenous DNA damage specifically promotes beta cell senescence and apoptosis. The transcription factor YY1 has been identified as a positive regulator of DNA repair in this cellular context, further underscoring the importance of this process for beta cell homeostasis.

Beyond metabolic disease, DNA repair infidelity has been implicated in neurological contexts as well. Chronic alcohol metabolism can lead to DNA repair infidelity, which in turn triggers cell cycle-induced senescence in neurons, highlighting the broad physiological relevance of accurate DNA repair across multiple tissue types.

Taken together, DNA repair is a multifaceted and indispensable cellular process whose dysfunction has wide-ranging consequences, from beta cell senescence and diabetic pathology to neuronal aging, making it a critical target of study in both metabolic and neurodegenerative disease research.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular process by which DNA breaks are resolved; beta cells favor error-free repair while neonatal alpha cells compensate through increased turnover.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of resolving DNA breaks during replication; beta cells repair DNA more efficiently than alpha cells before mitosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular process for resolving DNA damage, impaired in beta cells under metabolic stress and in T2D donors.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular process for resolving DNA damage; impaired selectively in beta cells under diabetogenic stress and Dnmt3a depletion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c8c7bb3e4a69884a1b79485b95f6f502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdd484417981e099a8f308ba0994bcaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71a6a7a3a4894dbd7d979b7e5766d5a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b0125512b4fa44926ba8a80a62b0a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58f0193e389064a60d513f13c5cad52b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28a44d52c17c29e9741a935aa76e8afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7af94fcbbc3d167377ece712218fa36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35d7a41162ea60151fe17b4e60e06d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6204b07313b33d741c243f18ce86735</data>
