<node id="oncogene-induced senescence">
  <data key="d0">oncogene-induced senescence</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Oncogene-induced senescence (OIS) is a form of cellular senescence triggered by the activation of oncogenes, serving as a critical tumor-suppressive mechanism. It is one of three well-established senescence-inducing stimuli studied in primary human fibroblasts, where it involves the sequential activation of the cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors p21Cip1 and p16Ink4a, establishing a stable cell cycle arrest.

OIS is characterized by several distinct molecular and cellular features. At the chromatin level, it is associated with the formation of senescence-associated heterochromatic foci (SAHF), which contribute to the stable silencing of proliferative genes. Cells undergoing OIS also display elevated expression of SA-β-galactosidase (SA-β-gal) and high levels of CDKN2A, hallmarks observable in non-malignant contexts such as human nevi harboring the BRAF-V600E mutation — a well-documented in vivo example of OIS occurring in benign tissue before malignant transformation.

OIS is further associated with significant metabolic and bioenergetic alterations, reflecting the broad reprogramming that accompanies the senescent state. A key feature of OIS is the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP), through which oncogenic RAS and other oncoproteins exert cell-nonautonomous functions, partly dependent on the p53 tumor suppressor. This secretory program involves an interleukin-dependent inflammatory network that relays senescence signals to the surrounding tissue microenvironment.

At the systemic level, OIS has been linked to myeloid-restricted hematopoiesis, chronic inflammation, and histiocytosis, indicating broader organismal consequences beyond the cell-autonomous arrest. OIS also contributes to the accumulation of senescent cells in the bone marrow, with implications for hematopoietic dysfunction and age-related pathology. Together, these features position oncogene-induced senescence as both a protective barrier against tumorigenesis and a contributor to inflammation and tissue dysfunction when senescent cells accumulate over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fac258601b299e0c883e8e1c4a3030d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d3b1de44d6b73a10793a43351e18e6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca844018665036149579ef01b8f9ec3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea13fe99fbc01cb6036ecddcda0aae8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5bb73f6c7980a9f906f42f6e0e9ce27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a471400d67fa8643230222fb3576486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c5a8121875c37f2659ae345688f6afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b02375738a9ebacd53446fec2077ee76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ff588c6efe73ecb07c6b95acb392e92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf5aeca434044079720fd4ae2d32c9b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f25c34949789593e96b7a57ead73f11</data>
