<node id="glucagon secretion">
  <data key="d0">glucagon secretion</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucagon secretion refers to the release of glucagon from alpha cells within pancreatic islets, a process central to glucose homeostasis and a key measure of alpha cell function.

In experimental settings, glucagon secretion is assessed through dynamic assays, including perifusion experiments conducted in TUID (Tulane University Islet Distribution) islets obtained from non-diabetic donors. These perifusion-based measurements capture glucagon release in response to various stimuli, such as changes in glucose concentration or potassium chloride (KCl) stimulation, providing a dynamic readout of alpha cell responsiveness over time.

Analyses of glucagon secretion across demographic variables have shown no statistically significant differences based on race or sex, though a notable trend toward higher glucagon secretion has been observed in female donors, warranting further investigation.

Glucagon secretion is also studied in the context of islet pathology. It is known to be impaired in both Type 1 Diabetes (T1D) and Type 2 Diabetes (T2D), highlighting its relevance to diabetic disease mechanisms. Furthermore, glucagon secretion from alpha cells can be altered by factors associated with the Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype (SASP). Specifically, SASP factors derived from senescent beta cells have been shown to influence alpha cell function, as demonstrated in cultured human islet models. This finding suggests a paracrine relationship between beta cell senescence and alpha cell dysfunction, implying that beta cell aging or stress-related senescence may have broader consequences for overall islet hormone secretion beyond insulin alone.

Collectively, glucagon secretion serves as a critical functional readout in islet biology research, reflecting both intrinsic alpha cell activity and the broader cellular environment of the islet, including the influence of neighboring senescent beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Biological process examined in human islets with no significant difference across race or sex, though females showed a trend toward higher secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;Process of glucagon release from alpha cells; females showed a trend toward higher secretion; no difference observed in T2D vs. non-diabetic donors under hypoglycemic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Release of glucagon from islet alpha cells in response to stimulation, measured in perifusion experiments.</data>
