<node id="ATR">
  <data key="d0">ATR</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">ATR (Ataxia Telangiectasia and Rad3-Related protein) is a serine/threonine kinase that plays a central role in the DNA damage response (DDR) and cell cycle checkpoint signaling. It is primarily known for its responsiveness to single-strand breaks (SSBs) in DNA, distinguishing it functionally from its related kinase ATM, which preferentially responds to double-strand breaks.

As a key DDR kinase, ATR mediates the stabilization of p53 in response to DNA damage, a process that can trigger cellular senescence. ATR operates within the ATM/ATR-p53 signaling axis, which has been shown to be activated during nucleolar stress-induced senescence, including in smooth muscle cells upon ablation of TIF-IA. Despite this shared axis, ATR and ATM exhibit mechanistic specificity: under nucleolar stress conditions, ATR phosphorylation is observed only minimally compared to ATM, and ATR has been reported to remain largely inactive during nucleolar stress-induced beta cell senescence, suggesting that ATM plays the dominant role in that particular context.

ATR is also implicated in the replication stress response and is coupled to nucleolar stress-mediated G2 cell cycle arrest, as well as being classified as a DDR-related signaling protein activated by nucleolar stress more broadly.

The activity of ATR is subject to regulation by various biological factors. Its DNA damage signaling is inhibited by estrogen, and its dependent DNA damage response is regulated by nitric oxide — specifically, prolonged nitric oxide exposure in beta cells has been shown to inhibit ATR signaling. Notably, primary islets exhibit lower baseline ATR protein levels compared to beta-cell lines, which may contribute to differential sensitivity in those cell types.

In experimental research contexts, ATR (phospho S428) is detectable using a rabbit monoclonal antibody, serving as a phosphorylation marker for its activation status in DDR studies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca3e5da7a4fe15854dde6289fd15667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58f0193e389064a60d513f13c5cad52b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db03a7d3c861658f2beac422f86b21ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a0590fd9be1db550e8a84c0781e8955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bb74bfaf0604b4b9f891e16fc9f0ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-787de2a35f76e08d5b7cddadbcc01954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ab370ab273e97005ced113e59cc48a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e5c8949dfa6aa24ddf3e3b92d956e4</data>
