<node id="NKX6-1">
  <data key="d0">NKX6-1</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">NKX6-1 is a transcription factor that plays a central and well-established role in pancreatic biology, particularly in the establishment and maintenance of beta cell identity and function within the islets of Langerhans. It is recognized as a key regulator of beta cell-specific gene expression programs and is considered essential for the proper functioning of insulin-producing beta cells. NKX6-1 has been identified among 242 transcription factors enriched in beta cells through SCENIC analysis, underscoring its prominence in beta cell transcriptional networks.

NKX6-1 is closely associated with mature beta cell identity and has been found to be inactivated in the context of type 2 diabetes, highlighting its pathological relevance. Its activity is also decreased in aging beta cells, a reduction that is associated with beta cell dysfunction and a progressive loss of beta cell identity over time. This age-related decline is considered a contributing factor to the deterioration of beta cell function observed during aging.

In addition to its role in disease and aging, NKX6-1 shows increased activation and upregulation in caloric restriction (CR) beta cells, functioning as a transcriptional modulator that promotes beta cell identity under these conditions. The transcription factor also cooperates with MAFA and MAFB to stimulate Insulin enhancer-driven transcriptional activity in beta cells, illustrating its collaborative role within broader regulatory complexes.

Beyond the beta cell compartment, the NKX6-1 transcription factor motif has been found to be highly enriched in delta cells of pancreatic islets, suggesting a regulatory function that extends to other pancreatic cell types. NKX6-1 has also been utilized in combinatorial profiling studies of human islet cells, further affirming its relevance as a marker of islet cell identity.

Sex-specific differences in NKX6-1's regulatory influence have also been documented. Gene regulatory network analyses reveal that NKX6-1 exhibits higher centrality in beta cell networks in males compared to females, suggesting it occupies a more prominent regulatory hub position in the male beta cell transcriptional landscape and that its orchestration of gene regulation may differ between sexes at the level of pancreatic islets.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor gene expressed in pancreatic beta cells, relevant to islet cell type-specific gene expression.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor involved in pancreatic beta cell identity and function.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcription factor involved in pancreatic beta-cell specification and maturation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b67e26d82684726785f1910ba6919cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe5b7d8c678cb311e300af3b74f19ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7873cb15c71b20e5fc369a6f0ab52a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51601485f0924871a988aae2e5ce30bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238834380bfb40677324883af3e3004&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0269f34de8f0928a9614438917079ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08e345f1e7ff9529c2ab94760dcb50f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002f23b1d29df711e839459b4d6f0711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3384cef9855a1b408c68f7d1ea3153c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c41ef398df926f1c888f284aff2a98e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd4f6cfb9180aade52c8cb84446ced85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e42c3b77d3170e854edbbc691159609d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e181574d19e6f17c0fd64b0feecf8f9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ab370ab273e97005ced113e59cc48a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86</data>
  <data key="d4">Pancreatic β-cell senescence in diabetes: mechanisms, markers and therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Defining unique structural features in the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors that control Insulin gene activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;The role of the beta cell in type 2 diabetes: new findings from the last 5 years.&lt;SEP&gt;Nucleolar stress facilitates islet β cell senescence via hijacking the DNA damage response pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571457</data>
