<node id="proinsulin">
  <data key="d0">proinsulin</data>
  <data key="d1">Molecule</data>
  <data key="d2">Proinsulin is a precursor molecule to insulin, produced and released by pancreatic beta cells. It plays a central role in insulin biosynthesis and is implicated in several mechanisms related to both type 1 and type 2 diabetes.

One well-characterized function of proinsulin involves its susceptibility to misfolding. Estrogens have been shown to actively promote the degradation of misfolded proinsulin, thereby protecting insulin production and delaying the onset of diabetes. This estrogenic protective mechanism underscores the hormonal regulation of beta cell health and insulin biosynthetic fidelity.

In the context of type 1 diabetes (T1D), proinsulin is recognized as a putative autoantigen. It has been identified in primary mouse and human islet beta cell extracellular vesicles (EVs), where it is released at elevated rates during exposure to proinflammatory cytokines. Additionally, proinsulin is released by beta cells within exosomes and functions as an autoantigen in the autoimmune processes underlying T1D, suggesting that its extracellular release may contribute to the initiation or amplification of the autoimmune response targeting beta cells.

In the context of type 2 diabetes (T2D), circulating proinsulin levels have been identified as a defining feature of one of the five genetic clusters derived from polygenic score analyses of T2D. This highlights proinsulin's relevance not only as a biosynthetic intermediate but also as a biomarker reflecting distinct pathophysiological subtypes of T2D.

Overall, proinsulin occupies a multifaceted role in diabetes biology, serving as a biosynthetic precursor, a target of hormonal regulation, an autoimmune antigen in T1D, and a genetically stratified biomarker in T2D.</data>
