<node id="NEUROG3">
  <data key="d0">NEUROG3</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">NEUROG3 (Neurogenin3) is a transcription factor and gene with critical roles in both developmental biology and metabolic disease. It is essential for the development of the brain and pancreatic islets, underscoring its broad biological significance.

In the context of metabolic disease, NEUROG3 has been implicated in the pathogenesis of ketosis-prone diabetes, with its influence operating in a sex-biased or sex-influenced manner, suggesting that gender modulates how variants in this gene contribute to disease risk.

NEUROG3 is also associated with type 2 diabetes (T2D) risk loci. Notably, specific variants — including rs61850200 and rs41277236 — at the NEUROG3 locus overlap with differentially accessible chromatin regions in male endothelial cells, but not in female endothelial cells. This sex-specific pattern of chromatin accessibility in endothelial cells highlights a potential regulatory mechanism through which NEUROG3 variants may confer T2D risk predominantly in males. These findings collectively suggest that regulatory variants in differentially accessible regions of male endothelial cells are a key feature of the NEUROG3 T2D risk locus.

Additionally, NEUROG3-independent DNA methylation has been identified as the earliest detectable epigenetic mark that distinguishes pancreatic progenitor cell identity, indicating that while NEUROG3 plays an important role in pancreatic development, certain upstream epigenetic events defining progenitor identity occur independently of NEUROG3 activity.</data>
