<node id="KDM6A">
  <data key="d0">KDM6A</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">KDM6A (Lysine Demethylase 6A) is an X-linked histone demethylase gene that plays a significant role in chromatin remodeling and epigenetic regulation. It encodes a histone demethylase enzyme and is notable for its ability to escape X chromosome inactivation, a property that contributes to its differential expression and function between biological sexes.

In the context of pancreatic islet biology, KDM6A exhibits marked sex-associated differences in chromatin accessibility and gene expression. Specifically, KDM6A is more accessible and upregulated in female beta (β) and alpha (α) cells compared to their male counterparts, as revealed through islet transcriptome analyses. These differences in chromatin accessibility and expression contribute to broader sex differences observed in islet cell biology.

Beyond its role in pancreatic islets, KDM6A is also expressed in CD4+ T lymphocytes, where it plays a role in modulating autoimmunity. Its expression in these immune cells further promotes sex differences in T cell biology, suggesting that KDM6A serves as a molecular contributor to sexually dimorphic immune responses. This immune modulatory function positions KDM6A as a potentially important factor in understanding sex-based differences in autoimmune susceptibility and disease progression.

Overall, KDM6A functions as a key epigenetic regulator whose escape from X inactivation underlies sex-biased differences in both pancreatic islet cell transcriptomics and immune cell function, making it a notable candidate in the study of sex differences in metabolic and autoimmune conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;X-linked histone demethylase expressed in CD4+ T lymphocytes that modulates autoimmunity.&lt;SEP&gt;Lysine demethylase 6A gene upregulated in female beta and alpha cells.&lt;SEP&gt;KDM6A is an X-linked gene showing sex differences in chromatin accessibility in islet beta and alpha cells.&lt;SEP&gt;X-linked histone demethylase gene more accessible in female islet cells and proximal to sex-associated cCREs.&lt;SEP&gt;Histone demethylase that escapes X inactivation; more accessible and expressed in female beta and alpha cells, and promotes sex differences in T cell biology.&lt;SEP&gt;A chromatin remodeler expressed from the X chromosome that promotes sex differences in T cell biology and may influence sex-specific chromatin access to transcription factors in islet function.</data>
