<node id="chromVAR">
  <data key="d0">chromVAR</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">chromVAR is a computational method and software tool designed for inferring transcription-factor-associated accessibility from single-cell epigenomic data. Developed by the Greenleaf Lab, it serves as a specialized instrument for analyzing chromatin accessibility at single-cell resolution.

The primary function of chromVAR is to identify transcription factor (TF) motifs that are enriched within accessible chromatin profiles across individual cell types. It accomplishes this by estimating transcription factor motif enrichment z-scores across single cells, a process that includes correction for GC content bias to ensure analytical accuracy. This capability makes it particularly well-suited for use with single-nucleus ATAC-seq (snATAC-seq) data, where understanding the regulatory landscape of individual cells is critical.

By aggregating chromatin accessibility signals associated with specific TF binding motifs, chromVAR enables researchers to infer which transcription factors are likely active or relevant in a given cell type or state, even when working with the sparse data typical of single-cell epigenomic experiments. The version referenced in multiple descriptions is v1.22.1, indicating an actively maintained and versioned software package. Overall, chromVAR represents a key analytical tool in the single-cell epigenomics field, bridging raw chromatin accessibility data and meaningful biological inference about transcriptional regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Method used to identify differentially accessible motifs in female and male beta and alpha cells.</data>
