<node id="chromatin accessibility">
  <data key="d0">chromatin accessibility</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Chromatin accessibility refers to the degree to which chromatin regions are open and physically accessible, reflecting the regulatory activity of the genome. In open chromatin regions, DNA is less tightly compacted, allowing transcription factors and other regulatory proteins to bind and influence gene expression. These open regions are preferentially fragmented and tagged during transposition-based assays, making them detectable through techniques such as single-nucleus ATAC-seq (snATAC-seq).

In the context of pancreatic islet research, chromatin accessibility is a key genomic and epigenomic feature profiled at the single-cell level to identify cell type-specific and cell state-specific regulatory programs. Using snATAC-seq, open chromatin regions are mapped across distinct islet cell types, enabling the inference of transcription factor activity and the characterization of regulatory elements that govern islet cell identity and function. In single-cell multiomics frameworks, chromatin accessibility is measured via ATAC peaks and processed using tools such as Signac.

A central focus of chromatin accessibility studies in human islets is its relationship to type 2 diabetes (T2D) risk. Differentially accessible chromatin regions have been shown to overlap with T2D-associated genetic variants in a cell-type-specific manner, helping to pinpoint which islet cell populations are most relevant to disease susceptibility. Notably, chromatin accessibility also exhibits sex-specific differences, with distinct open chromatin profiles identified between male and female islet samples from both non-diabetic and T2D donors. These sex-specific differences are assessed through differential peak analysis and are considered in the context of T2D risk loci, highlighting that the regulatory landscape of islet cells varies not only by cell type but also by biological sex. Together, these analyses provide insight into the epigenomic underpinnings of islet cell regulation and diabetes pathophysiology.</data>
