<node id="glucose">
  <data key="d0">glucose</data>
  <data key="d1">Molecule</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucose is a fundamental metabolic substrate and classical insulin secretagogue that plays a central role in pancreatic islet biology and related functional assays.

In the context of islet research, glucose serves as the primary secretagogue used at a wide range of concentrations to stimulate both insulin and glucagon secretion from islets. Specific concentrations employed in perifusion and bioenergetics experiments include 2, 5, 7, 11, 16.7, and 20 mM, reflecting its use across ascending concentration ramps and glucose challenge protocols. In dynamic insulin secretion assays, high glucose conditions are a standard stimulus, and when combined with IBMX, a decreased insulin response has been observed in black male islets compared to white male islets, highlighting its relevance in studies of donor-specific islet function.

As the primary metabolic substrate for pancreatic beta cells, glucose is integral to islet functional assays, including perifusion experiments and bioenergetics studies. Elevated glucose levels have also been associated with increased genomic instability, specifically through the inhibition of nucleotide excision repair, suggesting broader cellular consequences beyond secretory stimulation.

Beyond in vitro applications, glucose is measured as a physiological variable in vivo, with blood glucose levels recorded at multiple time points during meal tolerance tests and insulin tolerance tests, underscoring its importance as a clinical and experimental biomarker.

Overall, glucose functions both as a key driver of hormone secretion in islet biology and as a metabolic and genomic regulatory molecule, with its effects studied across a broad spectrum of experimental and physiological contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary insulin secretagogue used in dynamic secretion assays and bioenergetic challenges at concentrations including 1.7 mM, 5.6 mM, and 16.7 mM.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecule used at low (5.6 mM) and high (16.7 mM) concentrations to stimulate insulin secretion in perifusion experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary secretagogue used at various concentrations (2, 5, 7, 11, 20 mM) to stimulate islet insulin and glucagon secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is a sugar whose concentration influences the stability of MAFA in β-cells; high glucose enhances MAFA stability, while low glucose leads to instability. The p.Ser64Phe mutant is stable regardless of glucose levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is used at concentrations of 1.1 mM and 15.5 mM in cycloheximide chase experiments to study the effect of glycemic conditions on MAFA-Myc protein stability in EndoC-βH1 cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary nutrient that stimulates insulin secretion and regulates MafA stability in pancreatic β cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64f42efe8d71d6b6b69c377d2a7c44b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe5b7d8c678cb311e300af3b74f19ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a7d380dafd31120f8a1af317cfb0ab1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3f8e2c47cb96d65689fa504202cd0f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0269f34de8f0928a9614438917079ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76049b85888e8e904e7c01de9740d34f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cadc21c6ca916dd06510987c1b7dbf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce68225f2288adf7d07962282496e8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d0bf8cd8c8cb69199fb8f9bf6c37e70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cd7041acf7539c8db67413bcd98f41b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804ddf6c13fee09d00987476aa3c478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61ea436af2c14f0df238edb6a5db2bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b65a0501c576270f0560dc1e4695ef2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f64af938dff7c5f186bd7024d92cbc0</data>
