<node id="snATAC-seq">
  <data key="d0">snATAC-seq</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">snATAC-seq (Single Nucleus Assay for Transposase-Accessible Chromatin Sequencing, also referred to as Single Nuclear Assay for Transposase-Accessible Chromatin Indexing and Sequencing) is a genomic method designed to profile chromatin accessibility at single-nucleus resolution. The technique leverages a transposase enzyme to tag and sequence open chromatin regions within isolated nuclei, enabling high-resolution mapping of the regulatory landscape of individual cells.

snATAC-seq is prominently applied in the study of human pancreatic islets, where it is used to examine and map chromatin accessibility across distinct islet cell types, including data derived from non-diabetic donors. By identifying open chromatin regions at the single-nucleus level, the method provides detailed chromatin accessibility profiles for each cell type present within the islets. This includes the examination of open chromatin enhancers located near genes in islet cells, further elucidating the regulatory architecture governing islet cell biology.

Beyond basic chromatin mapping, snATAC-seq is employed to assess gene regulatory networks and to conduct genomic enrichment analyses, notably including Type 2 Diabetes (T2D) genome-wide association study (GWAS) enrichment analyses. A key application of the method involves identifying open chromatin regions and determining their overlap with T2D GWAS variants, thereby linking accessible chromatin in specific islet cell types to disease-associated genetic loci. These applications collectively advance the understanding of the regulatory mechanisms underlying pancreatic islet biology and metabolic disease, making snATAC-seq a powerful tool for connecting epigenomic landscapes to complex disease genetics.</data>
