<node id="Homo sapiens">
  <data key="d0">Homo sapiens</data>
  <data key="d1">Organism</data>
  <data key="d2">Homo sapiens is the human species that serves as the central biological subject across a range of experimental and computational studies focused on pancreatic islet biology, gene expression, and diabetes research.

In the experimental context, Homo sapiens is the organism from which de-identified pancreatic islets were obtained from human donors for multiple types of analyses. These include perifusion and bioenergetics experiments, single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq), and single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq). The islets sourced from human donors were used to investigate sex differences in islet biology, gene expression patterns, and their relevance to diabetes.

On the computational and genomic side, the Homo sapiens reference genome was employed extensively for read alignment and mapping. Specifically, the GRCh38 genome reference (also referred to as GRCh38-2020-A and hg38) was used for read alignment in single-cell gene expression mapping as well as for snATAC-seq read mapping. In addition to the genome reference, the human transcriptome assembly was utilized for snATAC-seq analyses. The JASPAR vertebrate motif database, referenced alongside the hg38 genome, was also applied in the broader analytical framework.

Overall, Homo sapiens represents both the biological source of experimental material and the genomic reference organism underpinning the computational analyses conducted in these studies on pancreatic islet function, chromatin accessibility, and diabetes-related biology.</data>
