<node id="Benjamini-Hochberg FDR">
  <data key="d0">Benjamini-Hochberg FDR</data>
  <data key="d1">Method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Benjamini-Hochberg FDR (False Discovery Rate) adjustment is a widely applied statistical correction method designed to control the false discovery rate when conducting multiple simultaneous hypothesis tests. Its primary function is to adjust raw p-values to account for the increased probability of obtaining false positives when performing large numbers of comparisons, a common challenge in high-throughput biological and genomic analyses.

In the context of differential gene expression analysis, the Benjamini-Hochberg FDR method is used to define differentially expressed genes, with adjusted p-value thresholds that vary depending on the specific analysis being performed. Common thresholds include α = 0.01 (or 1%) for more stringent applications such as identifying cell type markers, and α = 0.1 (or 10%) for comparisons involving sex, race, or disease status. In other analytical contexts, thresholds of 0.05 or 0.2 may also be applied, reflecting varying levels of stringency appropriate to the study design.

Beyond gene expression, the Benjamini-Hochberg FDR adjustment is broadly employed across genomic and epigenomic analyses. These include the identification of differentially accessible chromatin regions, the analysis of transcription factor binding motifs, pathway analyses, and the correction of peak p-values in assays such as ATAC-seq or ChIP-seq. In all these applications, the method serves to adjust raw p-values so that the overall proportion of false discoveries among the reported significant results is kept within an acceptable, pre-defined threshold.

Overall, the Benjamini-Hochberg FDR adjustment is a standard and versatile multiple testing correction tool used across a broad range of statistical analyses in genomics and molecular biology, offering a balance between sensitivity and specificity by controlling false discovery rates rather than applying more conservative family-wise error rate corrections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-78c6090d91c84168c42d7ba3a2808633&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee38120968aa57daae758d9f758edfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6615f49a664b0e6bfe3dc5ebc25a278f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7913917fca6b789f10e2948623ce222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a50ca1b65c7c406b024588b964cb7d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5e348a0adb909ac9a245bd8fb1b7b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68cd62913e543123e8319a7a70949910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c6115fe960d3cc141101692d3cf027c</data>
