<node id="gene regulatory network">
  <data key="d0">gene regulatory network</data>
  <data key="d1">Pathway</data>
  <data key="d2">A gene regulatory network (GRN) is a structured representation of the regulatory interactions governing gene expression, typically encompassing relationships among cis-regulatory elements (cCREs), transcription factors (TFs), and their downstream target genes. GRNs serve as a foundational framework for understanding how gene expression patterns are established, maintained, and altered in specific cell types and conditions.

In the context of single-cell genomics and chromatin accessibility studies, GRNs are inferred using computational tools such as Pando and SCENIC (implemented via pySCENIC and GRNBoost2). When inferred by Pando, a GRN takes the form of a regulatory graph that captures cCRE–TF pair interactions and their downstream target gene relationships, derived from chromatin accessibility data. This approach links cCREs, TF binding, TF activation states, and target gene expression into a coherent network structure. Such networks have been specifically reconstructed for male and female beta and alpha cells, defining the regulatory logic governing these pancreatic cell types in a sex-stratified manner.

Alternatively, GRNs reconstructed using SCENIC focus on co-expression relationships between transcription factors and their target genes, inferred from single-cell transcriptomic data using pySCENIC and GRNBoost2. These networks are particularly informative in metabolic research contexts, where dietary interventions such as caloric restriction (CR) and high-fat diet (HFD) have been shown to reprogram beta cell GRNs. GRNs are recognized as central controllers of beta cell gene expression patterns and cellular heterogeneity, and CR in particular has been demonstrated to substantially reorganize these regulatory networks.

Collectively, gene regulatory networks—whether derived from chromatin accessibility or single-cell co-expression data—provide a multi-layered view of transcriptional regulation, capturing the interplay between epigenomic accessibility, transcription factor activity, and target gene expression across diverse biological conditions and cell types.</data>
