</node>
<node id="delta cell">
  <data key="d0">delta cell</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Delta cells are a specialized type of pancreatic endocrine cell located within the islets of Langerhans. They are consistently identified and annotated across multiple profiling methodologies — including single-cell multiome sequencing, single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq) clustering, and somatostatin immunostaining — through the expression of key marker genes, most notably Somatostatin (SST) and HHEX, which serve as reliable molecular signatures for this cell type.

From a regulatory standpoint, delta cells exhibit specific transcription factor (TF) motif enrichments, including NKX6-2, NKX6-1, PDX1, and MEOX1, indicating distinct gene regulatory programs governing their identity and function. Notably, despite being a separate cell type, the regulatory programs of delta cells have been observed to bear closer resemblance to those of beta cells than to other islet cell populations, highlighting a degree of shared regulatory architecture within the pancreatic islet.

Delta cells are also of significant interest in metabolic and diabetes research. In studies of caloric restriction (CR), pancreatic islet delta cells have been observed to show higher 15N levels in CR islets, and their relative mass and proportion per islet have been quantified using somatostatin immunostaining.

In the context of type 2 diabetes (T2D), delta cells have been implicated through their chromatin accessibility profiles. Differentially accessible chromatin regions in delta cells overlap with T2D-associated genetic variants at loci including GCK, KCNQ1, and PIK3R1. Critically, this overlap demonstrates a notable sex-specific pattern: differentially accessible regions overlapping these classical T2D variants are observed in male delta cells but are absent in female delta cells. This sexually dimorphic chromatin accessibility at disease-relevant genomic loci implicates delta cells as potentially playing a sex-dependent role in T2D susceptibility or pathophysiology, underscoring the importance of accounting for biological sex in the study of pancreatic islet cell function and diabetes genetics.</data>
