<node id="ductal cell">
  <data key="d0">ductal cell</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Ductal cells are a non-endocrine pancreatic cell type with well-characterized molecular and functional properties. They are classified as part of the exocrine pancreatic compartment and are primarily identified by the expression of key marker genes, including CFTR (Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane conductance Regulator) and TFF1 (Trefoil Factor 1). These markers have been used to annotate ductal cells in various transcriptomic and epigenomic profiling studies, including single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq) cluster analyses.

Functionally, ductal cells are involved in organic anion transport and branching morphogenesis, processes critical to the structural and physiological organization of the pancreas. At the transcription factor level, ductal cells exhibit enriched SOX9 transcription factor motifs, implicating SOX9 as an important regulator of ductal cell identity and function.

Ductal cells also display notable sex-specific epigenomic differences. Specifically, GLI2 overlapping differentially accessible chromatin regions have been identified in female ductal cells but are absent in their male counterparts, suggesting sex-dependent regulatory mechanisms governing ductal cell biology.

In the context of disease, pancreatic ductal cells have been associated with neoplastic progression. Ductal cells harboring KRAS mutations display senescence markers in lesions linked to oncogenic KRAS activity, connecting ductal cell biology to the early stages of pancreatic neoplasia and tumor development.

Taken together, ductal cells represent a molecularly distinct, non-endocrine pancreatic population defined by CFTR and TFF1 expression, SOX9 regulatory activity, roles in exocrine function, sex-specific chromatin accessibility patterns, and relevance to KRAS-driven neoplastic transformation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic ductal cells identified in snATAC-seq clustering by CFTR gene activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Ductal cells are non-endocrine pancreatic islet cells with marker genes CFTR and TFF1, enriched in organic anion transport and branching morphogenesis pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Ductal cells are pancreatic exocrine cells characterized by SOX9 motif enrichment and CFTR marker gene expression.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cell type where differentially accessible regions overlap with GLI2 in females but not males.</data>
