<node id="acinar cell">
  <data key="d0">acinar cell</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Acinar cells are a pancreatic exocrine cell type with a well-defined molecular identity and a broad range of functional, regulatory, and genomic characteristics. Classified as non-endocrine cells, they are primarily involved in digestion and alcohol metabolism, as reflected by their enrichment in related biological pathways. Their cellular identity is defined by several key marker genes, including PNLIP, AMY2A, and PRSS1; the latter has been detected through both single-cell multiome sequencing of islets and single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq) clustering based on PRSS1 gene activity, reinforcing their characterization at both the transcriptional and chromatin levels.

From a transcriptional regulatory standpoint, acinar cells exhibit enriched SOX9 transcription factor motifs, suggesting that SOX9-driven regulatory networks play an important role in this cell type. Acinar cells are also distinguished by their high degree of cellular plasticity; they are capable of dedifferentiation or transdifferentiation and can acquire a senescent state during acinar-to-ductal metaplasia (ADM), a process of significant relevance to pancreatic pathology and disease progression.

Acinar cells have also been examined in the context of metabolic research. They have been used as a compartment reference in metabolite enrichment analyses and have been studied for nuclear ¹⁵N levels, where heterogeneity was observed among individual acinar cells; notably, no effects of high-fat diet (HFD) or caloric restriction (CR) were detected on these levels.

From a genomic and disease-association perspective, acinar cells have been identified as a cell type in which differentially accessible chromatin regions overlap with variants at the SLC30A8 locus, a gene associated with type 2 diabetes (T2D). This overlap has been observed specifically in males, suggesting a potential sex-specific regulatory dimension to the genetic risk architecture of T2D within this cell type. Together, these features highlight acinar cells as a multifunctional and genomically relevant cell population within the pancreas.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic exocrine cell involved in communication with beta cells, disrupted by excess pancreatic elastase.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic exocrine cells in which CELA3B is upregulated in type 2 diabetes, releasing pancreatic elastase into the islet microenvironment.</data>
