<node id="lymphocyte">
  <data key="d0">lymphocyte</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Lymphocyte is a multifunctional immune cell type with well-characterized roles in both pancreatic islet biology and the broader adaptive immune response.

Within pancreatic tissue, lymphocytes are classified as non-endocrine pancreatic cells found within the islet microenvironment. They are identified by characteristic marker genes including CCL5, CD7, and CD3D, with CD3D being particularly used for annotation in single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq) cluster analyses. Functionally, islet lymphocytes are involved in immune receptor signaling and T-cell selection processes. Notably, lymphocytes have been identified in the TUID dataset but not in HPAP data within the integrated islet cell atlas, reflecting variability across data sources.

From an epigenomic and genomic perspective, lymphocytes exhibit significant sex-specific differences in chromatin accessibility at T2D-associated loci. A differentially accessible chromatin region overlapping the TM6SF2 risk locus — encompassing up to four Type 2 diabetes (T2D)-associated genetic variants, including rs188247550 — is detectable in female lymphocytes but is absent in male lymphocytes. Consistent with this sexually dimorphic epigenomic landscape, enrichment of islet lymphocyte signals in T2D genome-wide association study (GWAS) data has been observed exclusively in males. Together, these findings highlight a complex, sex-specific regulatory architecture at this locus, where chromatin accessibility patterns and GWAS enrichment diverge between females and males.

Beyond their role in pancreatic islets, lymphocytes are also well characterized for core molecular mechanisms underlying adaptive immunity. They employ base deamination to generate uracil within DNA, which introduces single-strand breaks (SSBs) through the base excision repair (BER) pathway. This mechanism supports antibody class switching and somatic hypermutation, processes essential for diversification of antibody repertoires and the overall adaptive immune response.

In summary, lymphocytes represent a multifunctional immune cell type with roles spanning pancreatic islet immune surveillance, sex-specific epigenomic regulation at T2D-associated loci, and fundamental molecular processes central to adaptive immunity.</data>
