<node id="CFTR">
  <data key="d0">CFTR</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">CFTR, the Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance Regulator gene, is a well-established molecular marker used to identify and annotate specific cell populations within the pancreas, particularly in the context of single-cell and single-nucleus genomic analyses.

In studies of human pancreatic islets, CFTR serves as a key marker gene for ductal cells, as well as acinar/ductal cells more broadly. It has been identified through both single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) and single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq) approaches, where it is used to annotate and validate ductal cell cluster identities based on gene expression and chromatin accessibility profiles, respectively.

Notably, CFTR expression in ductal cells is specifically upregulated and is expressed independently of sex and race/ethnicity, suggesting its utility as a robust and broadly applicable marker across diverse human populations. Its consistent identification across multiple sequencing modalities and analytical frameworks underscores its reliability as a canonical marker for pancreatic ductal cell identification and cluster validation in high-resolution transcriptomic and epigenomic studies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9e607d2cca86a6257f1db73bacae2c96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c0d05211cf3080af14ec050e5cc97ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7873cb15c71b20e5fc369a6f0ab52a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7bd4bfe81f828dceb60b520d85bcf2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a24341c9b63bd3b6313252fe6ed4b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87d48756321bdf8ef533359c0655a3d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da10e6374cdbd3d6e4a07d773cd172d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e42c3b77d3170e854edbbc691159609d</data>
