<node id="PRSS1">
  <data key="d0">PRSS1</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">PRSS1, also known as Protease Serine 1, is a gene encoding a pancreatic serine protease that plays a prominent role in pancreatic biology and genomic research contexts.

PRSS1 is characterized by its high expression levels in pancreatic cells, particularly in acinar cells. As a result, it serves as a well-established marker gene for the identification and annotation of acinar cell clusters in pancreatic studies. Specifically, PRSS1 is widely used in single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq) analyses to annotate and validate acinar cell cluster identity within pancreatic islet chromatin accessibility studies. Its consistent and robust expression in acinar cells makes it a reliable tool for confirming the cellular identity of these clusters during data analysis and interpretation.

In addition to its utility as a cell-type marker, PRSS1's notably high expression in pancreatic cells has also been flagged as a relevant consideration in the context of ambient RNA contamination. In sequencing studies, genes with particularly high expression levels, such as PRSS1, can contribute to ambient RNA contamination concerns, where RNA from highly expressing cell types leaks into the sequencing environment and potentially confounds data quality and interpretation. This dual relevance — both as a definitive acinar cell marker and as a source of potential technical artifacts in sequencing workflows — underscores the importance of accounting for PRSS1 expression when designing and analyzing pancreatic single-cell or single-nucleus sequencing experiments.</data>
