<node id="SDS">
  <data key="d0">SDS</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">SDS (Serine Dehydratase) is a gene that serves as a well-established marker for macrophage cells, particularly in the context of pancreatic islet research. It has been identified and utilized across multiple single-cell and single-nucleus sequencing methodologies, including scRNA-seq (single-cell RNA sequencing) and snATAC-seq (single-nucleus assay for transposase-accessible chromatin sequencing).

In scRNA-seq analyses of human pancreatic islets, SDS has been identified as a marker gene through cluster analysis, where it is used to annotate and validate macrophage cell clusters. Similarly, in snATAC-seq chromatin accessibility analyses, SDS functions as a marker gene for macrophage cluster identification and annotation, underscoring its utility across both transcriptomic and epigenomic profiling approaches.

A notable characteristic of SDS as a macrophage marker is that its expression is independent of sex and race/ethnicity, suggesting it represents a broadly applicable and robust marker for macrophage identification across diverse human populations. Its specific upregulation in macrophages makes it a reliable tool for distinguishing this cell type within the complex cellular landscape of pancreatic islets.

Overall, SDS plays a consistent and validated role as a macrophage-specific marker gene in pancreatic islet studies, contributing to the accurate identification and annotation of macrophage populations in both transcriptomic and chromatin accessibility analyses.</data>
