<node id="cell proliferation">
  <data key="d0">cell proliferation</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Cell proliferation is a fundamental biological process that has been studied across multiple contexts, particularly in relation to pancreatic biology, stellate cell activation, and beta cell dynamics.

In the context of pancreatic stellate cells, cell proliferation is consistently identified as a pathway that is upregulated in activated stellate cells, including those found specifically within human pancreatic islets. This upregulation is associated with the activated state of stellate cells in the pancreatic islet environment, suggesting a role for enhanced proliferative signaling in stellate cell activation and potentially in islet remodeling or pathology.

Regarding pancreatic beta cells, cell proliferation is a critical process for maintaining beta cell mass. Research indicates that caloric restriction (CR) may arrest cell proliferation, resulting in smaller pancreases and lower islet beta cell mass in CR mice. Additionally, cell proliferation has been utilized as a mechanistic marker to determine the relative age of beta cells; specifically, proliferating cells lose approximately 50% of nuclear nitrogen-15 (¹⁵N) after each cell division, enabling researchers to estimate beta cell age through isotopic dilution.

Cell proliferation is also closely examined in the context of beta cell senescence and stress responses. Studies using beta-TC-6 cells have demonstrated that cell proliferation, assessed via the EdU (5-ethynyl-2′-deoxyuridine) incorporation assay, is irreversibly ceased in senescent beta cells upon induction of nucleolar stress. Furthermore, treatment with CX-5461 has been shown to reduce cell proliferation, while co-treatment with KU60019 can restore it, indicating a modulatory relationship between these compounds and proliferative capacity.

Methodologically, cell proliferation in these studies is commonly assessed using the EdU incorporation assay and, more specifically, the BeyoClick EdU Cell Proliferation Kit, both of which serve as standard tools for quantifying actively dividing cells in experimental settings.</data>
