<node id="TNF">
  <data key="d0">TNF</data>
  <data key="d1">Gene</data>
  <data key="d2">TNF (Tumor Necrosis Factor) is a pro-inflammatory cytokine and a well-established component of the Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype (SASP), with broad expression across numerous biological contexts and tissue types.

As a SASP factor, TNF has been reported in more than ten distinct tissues, cementing its role as a widely recognized senescence marker. It is notably found elevated in CD3+ T cells from both aged mice and ERCC1-deficient mice, highlighting its relevance as a biomarker of cellular aging and DNA repair deficiency. TNF is produced predominantly by immune cells, including monocytes and macrophages, though its expression as part of the SASP extends to a diverse range of cell types, including fibroblasts, keratinocytes, melanocytes, and trophoblasts.

TNF expression has been documented across multiple organ systems and disease models. In the central nervous system and adipose tissue, TNF is expressed in senescent cells, implicating it in neuroinflammation and metabolic dysfunction. It is overexpressed in animal models of kidney disease, and it is robustly upregulated in injury-induced senescence mouse models in the lung. Additionally, TNF functions as a SASP factor in mouse models of insulin resistance, connecting it to metabolic disease pathways.

In skin biology, TNF is part of the classical SASP in fibroblasts, keratinocytes, and melanocytes, and is associated with age-related disease outcomes as well as the cellular response to UV radiation. In reproductive biology, TNF is expressed in senescent trophoblasts, where it plays a role in mediating immune surveillance and tissue remodeling.

Collectively, TNF represents a central, multi-tissue SASP mediator involved in aging, inflammation, tissue remodeling, and a wide spectrum of disease-associated senescence contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor necrosis factor; a gene involved in senescence/SASP and a target of beta cell EV miRNAs; induced by miR-29 in splenocytes of NOD mice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d3b1de44d6b73a10793a43351e18e6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca3e5da7a4fe15854dde6289fd15667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b773584b36e5b4845bedffea9a1b6add&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce1a7b385e0f22ca823c850de9bf8a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5bb73f6c7980a9f906f42f6e0e9ce27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a471400d67fa8643230222fb3576486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f25c34949789593e96b7a57ead73f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6215695e8bd809fd02cb8b9b7b4123ad</data>
