<node id="neuron">
  <data key="d0">neuron</data>
  <data key="d1">Cell Type</data>
  <data key="d2">Neurons are specialized cells of the nervous system, particularly the central nervous system (CNS), that exhibit a wide range of distinct biological characteristics relevant to aging, neurodegeneration, and cellular stress responses.

As postmitotic cells, neurons are incapable of re-entering the cell cycle, yet they can develop senescence-like phenotypes under pathological conditions. Specifically, neurons have been identified as senescent cells expressing CDKN2D/p19 and exhibiting tau neuropathology. Additionally, they can develop a p21-dependent senescence-like phenotype driven by the DNA damage response. More broadly, neurons overexpress senescence markers during aging and neurodegeneration, placing them at the intersection of cellular aging and neurodegenerative disease mechanisms.

Neurons also engage in intercellular communication within the CNS, notably interacting with astroglia via exosomes, a finding demonstrated through reporter mouse models. This highlights the role of neurons not only as isolated computational units but as active participants in a broader glial-neuronal signaling network.

A notable feature of neurons is their molecular and developmental similarity to pancreatic beta-cells. Both cell types share gene expression toolboxes, developmental transcriptional regulatory programs, and high vulnerability to DNA damage. Neurons efficiently repair oxidative DNA damage and exhibit neuronal activity-dependent gene expression as an emerging regulatory theme.

Neurons are further distinguished by their uniquely high vulnerability to DNA breaks, a high frequency of programmed DNA breaks at specific genomic loci, and the highest levels of 5-hydroxymethylcytosine (5hmC) compared to any other known cell type. These programmed DNA breaks are understood to play a physiological role in modulating transcriptional control, suggesting that DNA strand breaks in neurons are not solely deleterious events but serve regulated functional purposes in gene expression.&lt;SEP&gt;Brain neurons whose age was quantified using MIMS-EM 15N/14N ratios alongside islet beta cells.</data>
