<node id="ATP">
  <data key="d0">ATP</data>
  <data key="d1">Molecule</data>
  <data key="d2">Adenosine triphosphate (ATP) is the primary energy molecule of the cell, playing a central role in a wide range of cellular processes, including metabolic signaling, organelle function, and stress responses.

In the context of cellular senescence, ATP depletion is a key consequence of mitochondrial dysfunction. When mitochondrial function is compromised, the resulting reduction in ATP levels contributes to the activation of AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK), which in turn promotes cell cycle arrest — a hallmark of the senescent state. This relationship between ATP depletion and senescence has also been observed in senescent syncytiotrophoblasts, where mitochondrial ATP production is specifically diminished.

ATP levels also play an important role in beta cell biology. In beta cells, nitric oxide-mediated inhibition of mitochondrial oxidative metabolism leads to reduced ATP levels, which indirectly inhibits ATM and ATR signaling pathways. Conversely, under caloric restriction (CR), beta cells demonstrate a significantly enhanced capacity for ATP generation. CR beta cell mitochondria are estimated to produce approximately 59,000 ATP molecules per second per unit of mitochondrial volume — roughly 14% higher than ad libitum (AL) fed beta cells. This increased production potential is attributed to greater mitochondrial mass and higher mitochondrial cristae density in CR beta cells. Notably, the elevated ATP production in CR beta cells is thought to primarily support endoplasmic reticulum (ER) calcium homeostasis, protein folding, and proteasome function, rather than directly enhancing insulin release. The potential ATP production rate by mitochondria in these cells can be estimated using biophysical modeling approaches.

Sex-based differences in ATP-mediated respiration have also been observed, with female islets exhibiting greater ATP-mediated respiration compared to male islets, suggesting sex-specific regulation of mitochondrial energy metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenosine triphosphate, produced via mitochondrial respiration; ATP-mediated respiration was greater in female islets than male islets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ca3e5da7a4fe15854dde6289fd15667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca844018665036149579ef01b8f9ec3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5bb73f6c7980a9f906f42f6e0e9ce27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76049b85888e8e904e7c01de9740d34f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db03a7d3c861658f2beac422f86b21ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56d5ffe02105b1bd6d77faab9c812433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-364a12c675ebfd67c468ab6fc47e6696&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002f23b1d29df711e839459b4d6f0711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa01ef8f1657a9890660f864f52123c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b61717ec6764bc92fa7c3c5067d345&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cd7041acf7539c8db67413bcd98f41b</data>
  <data key="d4">SenNet recommendations for detecting senescent cells in different tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Safeguarding genomic integrity in beta-cells: implications for beta-cell differentiation, growth, and dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778570308</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
