<node id="base excision repair">
  <data key="d0">base excision repair</data>
  <data key="d1">Pathway</data>
  <data key="d2">BER, the DNA repair pathway targeting 5fC and 5caC during DNA demethylation, resulting in conversion of 5mC to cytosine.&lt;SEP&gt;BER (base excision repair) introduces programmed SSBs or DSBs towards highly targeted genomic modification and contributes to epigenetic modulation via DNA demethylation.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA repair pathway enriched in Ki67+ proliferating cells in neonatal islets.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA repair pathway downregulated in alpha cells during G1 phase under HFD conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-de697a7536ea955d878a95580bf8aa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e7d877b531b99ded5aba6df6f1635f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7af94fcbbc3d167377ece712218fa36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6204b07313b33d741c243f18ce86735</data>
  <data key="d4">Safeguarding genomic integrity in beta-cells: implications for beta-cell differentiation, growth, and dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571465</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">76</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell senescence / DNA Damage / DNA damage response</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00054141</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00191093</data>
  <data key="d11">0.00229665</data>
</node>
<node id="single-strand break">
  <data key="d0">single-strand break</data>
  <data key="d1">Molecule</data>
  <data key="d2">Common form of DNA lesion (SSB) that can arise due to spontaneous DNA instability or as intermediates of DNA repair; can halt transcription.&lt;SEP&gt;Type of DNA damage involving breakage of one DNA strand with causes and consequences relevant to genome integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;SSBs (single-strand breaks) are part of topoisomerase cleavage complexes and occur at specific genomic sites including neuronal enhancers near CpG dinucleotides.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-de697a7536ea955d878a95580bf8aa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80318e0997c6182c0124775c5f01f6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408</data>
  <data key="d4">Safeguarding genomic integrity in beta-cells: implications for beta-cell differentiation, growth, and dysfunction.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778568722</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">76</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell senescence / DNA Damage / DNA damage response</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00069511</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00157275</data>
  <data key="d11">0.0041679</data>
</node>
<node id="double-strand break">
  <data key="d0">double-strand break</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Severe form of DNA lesion (DSB) that can be induced by unrepaired single-strand breaks upon replication.&lt;SEP&gt;Type of DNA damage involving breakage of both DNA strands; produced endogenously, associated with cancer induction and found in beta cells in type 2 diabetes and congenital hyperinsulinism.&lt;SEP&gt;DSBs (double-strand breaks) are programmed DNA breaks observed at specific loci including promoters of early response genes upon neuronal activation, and are involved in long-term memory processes.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA double-strand breaks found in diabetic beta cells, associated with P21 response limiting apoptosis.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA double-strand breaks (DSBs) are a form of DNA damage typically detected by γ-H2AX foci and comet assay, but found absent in nucleolar stress-induced senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA double-strand breaks (DSBs) that are absent during nucleolar stress-induced pseudo-DDR, distinguishing it from canonical DNA damage response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-de697a7536ea955d878a95580bf8aa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80318e0997c6182c0124775c5f01f6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72bf1fbaf7a329c364247108f8b30408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a0590fd9be1db550e8a84c0781e8955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-787de2a35f76e08d5b7cddadbcc01954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ab370ab273e97005ced113e59cc48a</data>
  <data key="d4">Safeguarding genomic integrity in beta-cells: implications for beta-cell differentiation, growth, and dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Nucleolar stress facilitates islet β cell senescence via hijacking the DNA damage response pathways.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571149</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">76</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell senescence / DNA Damage / DNA damage response</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00086222</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00436849</data>
  <data key="d11">0.03471631</data>
</node>
<node id="topoisomerase">
  <data key="d0">topoisomerase</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">Enzyme that facilitates transcription of long genes linked to autism.&lt;SEP&gt;Topoisomerase introduces programmed DNA breaks through resolution of topological stress during transcription and modulates neural transmission and plasticity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-de697a7536ea955d878a95580bf8aa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80318e0997c6182c0124775c5f01f6a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Safeguarding genomic integrity in beta-cells: implications for beta-cell differentiation, growth, and dysfunction.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778568723</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">76</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell senescence / DNA Damage / DNA damage response</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00033884</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00083019</data>
  <data key="d11">0.00134788</data>
</node>
<node id="cohesin">
  <data key="d0">cohesin</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">Cohesin protects genes against gammaH2AX induced by DNA double-strand breaks and is involved in the DNA damage response.&lt;SEP&gt;Cohesin mediates loop extrusion involved in the introduction of programmed DNA breaks.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein complex including SMC3 involved in epigenetic regulation of functional beta-cell mass.&lt;SEP&gt;Epigenetic regulator critical for postnatal beta cell genomic stability, dysregulation of which is associated with diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-de697a7536ea955d878a95580bf8aa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-735ea17eaeb5e3524d0871e16116e986&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28a44d52c17c29e9741a935aa76e8afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c63b5ad461844f8e5d9a90f5dfc52f1e</data>
  <data key="d4">Safeguarding genomic integrity in beta-cells: implications for beta-cell differentiation, growth, and dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571446</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">76</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell senescence / DNA Damage / DNA damage response</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00033694</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00051189</data>
  <data key="d11">0.02709178</data>
</node>
<node id="TLR9">
  <data key="d0">TLR9</data>
  <data key="d1">Protein</data>
  <data key="d2">TLR9 is a DNA-sensing receptor involved in the formation of memory assemblies.&lt;SEP&gt;TLR9 mediates repair of learning-induced DSBs involved in the recruitment of specific hippocampal neuronal clusters to memory circuits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-de697a7536ea955d878a95580bf8aa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-735ea17eaeb5e3524d0871e16116e986</data>
  <data key="d4">Safeguarding genomic integrity in beta-cells: implications for beta-cell differentiation, growth, and dysfunction.</data>
