<node id="caloric restriction">
  <data key="d0">caloric restriction</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Caloric restriction (CR) is a well-established dietary intervention characterized by a deliberate reduction in daily caloric intake, typically implemented as a 20% reduction relative to ad libitum feeding (i.e., mice are fed approximately 80% of the daily consumption of the ad libitum group). It has been widely studied across experimental models, particularly in mice, to investigate its broad metabolic, cellular, and molecular effects.

At the systemic level, caloric restriction improves glucose homeostasis, enhances peripheral insulin sensitivity, and improves glucose tolerance without impairing beta cell-stimulated insulin secretion mechanisms in vivo. These effects collectively contribute to an insulin-sensitive physiological state, extending both life-span and health-span in organisms.

CR exerts particularly notable effects on pancreatic beta cells. It promotes beta cell longevity by slowing beta cell turnover rates, reducing aging markers, and delaying cellular senescence. CR reduces age-dependent beta cell senescence markers and enhances autophagy, while simultaneously reducing mitophagy and increasing mitochondrial mass, density, cristae surface area, cristae density, and ATP production. These mitochondrial adaptations suggest a remodeling of beta cell energy metabolism that supports long-term cellular function and homeostasis.

At the transcriptional and epigenetic levels, CR alters beta cell transcriptional programs and chromatin accessibility, upregulating genes associated with beta cell maturation, identity, homeostasis, and nutrient metabolism. However, CR also induces epigenetic heterogeneity and has been shown to impair the adaptive replicative response of beta cells to high-fat diet (HFD) feeding, indicating context-dependent trade-offs in its effects.

CR has also been studied using advanced methodological approaches, including isotopic labeling with 15N-labeled mice fed normal chow with 14N to track beta cell turnover, as well as single nucleus multiomics following islet nuclei isolation from CR-conditioned mice. Additionally, CR is associated with enrichment of specific metabolites in islet regions compared to ad libitum feeding conditions, further highlighting its role in modulating the metabolic microenvironment of pancreatic tissue.

Overall, caloric restriction represents a geroprotective dietary strategy with multifaceted effects on beta cell structure, function, metabolism, and molecular identity, while broadly improving systemic metabolic health and promoting organismal longevity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56d5ffe02105b1bd6d77faab9c812433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68a87a3a1f3eb5d8b0e53b41df492512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2e4a544176811f5b43952b185e1ed3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0c13c5a18e2c5037815818d22c48a64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-364a12c675ebfd67c468ab6fc47e6696&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dcd3bdc472cb74376c18c72cf9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08e345f1e7ff9529c2ab94760dcb50f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cadc21c6ca916dd06510987c1b7dbf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f39153d9f864070e17a74724a6c8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce68225f2288adf7d07962282496e8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-363f3a19905f833d29a7d1242acc6641&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa01ef8f1657a9890660f864f52123c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d5c817dce40d1df02e7717bb9598632&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d286ee457150be65228790e800b5207b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c85c9569fee140bfd52fcc0bbfbcc9c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b61717ec6764bc92fa7c3c5067d345</data>
