<node id="nucleolar stress">
  <data key="d0">nucleolar stress</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Nucleolar stress is a cellular stress condition originating in the nucleolus, characterized by disruption of ribosomal RNA (rRNA) transcription, impaired rRNA synthesis, and aberrant nucleolar morphology. It is marked by nucleolar aberration, fragmentation, and the translocation of nucleophosmin (NPM) from the nucleolus to the nucleoplasm, along with a reduction in rRNA species.

This stress condition can be experimentally induced by agents such as CX-5461 and Actinomycin D (ActD), which interfere with rRNA transcription and nucleolar architecture. These disruptions serve as key tools for studying the downstream consequences of nucleolar stress in cellular models, particularly in pancreatic beta cells.

A central consequence of nucleolar stress is the induction of cellular senescence, specifically beta cell senescence, which is relevant to diabetic progression. The stress triggers senescence-like phenotypes and activates DNA damage response (DDR) pathways, including ATM (Ataxia Telangiectasia Mutated) pathway activation. Notably, this process can initiate a pseudo-DDR — a senescence-associated DNA damage response that mimics classical DNA damage signaling without necessarily involving direct genomic lesions.

Nucleolar stress is thus implicated as a general upstream stress event capable of driving beta cell senescence during the progression of diabetes. By coupling disrupted nucleolar function with ATM activation, pseudo-DDR induction, and nucleoplasmic translocation of NPM, nucleolar stress represents a critical mechanistic link between ribosomal biogenesis failure and the acquisition of senescent phenotypes in pancreatic beta cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f008854d459401e11c35e6bd2541318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a0590fd9be1db550e8a84c0781e8955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bb74bfaf0604b4b9f891e16fc9f0ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce4f6e2d90d0c06e4549d7d45037298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-787de2a35f76e08d5b7cddadbcc01954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ab370ab273e97005ced113e59cc48a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af17b436ff0aa3dd562bbc7e986cc909&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f082082b0871749ee637b1571aa95c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e33cd1d99c96a7f6ffdd0fbea902670</data>
