<node id="genomic integrity">
  <data key="d0">genomic integrity</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Genomic integrity refers to the maintenance of intact DNA in neonatal islet cells, which is affected by high replication rates during neonatal growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance of intact and accurate DNA; beta cells enforce robust genomic quality-control while alpha cells adopt more permissive repair.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance of DNA stability in islet cells, preferentially impaired in beta cells under metabolic stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7af94fcbbc3d167377ece712218fa36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c461d96db4bed849ef22472fc2b0cd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad82642ce24967122d02819b52a6f45</data>
  <data key="d4">Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d5">1778571466</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">76</data>
  <data key="d8">beta cell senescence / DNA Damage / DNA damage response</data>
  <data key="d9">0.00017171</data>
  <data key="d10">0.00015624</data>
  <data key="d11">0.02050553</data>
</node>
<node id="G2 phase">
  <data key="d0">G2 phase</data>
  <data key="d1">Biological Process</data>
  <data key="d2">Cell cycle phase preceding mitosis where DNA repair occurs; beta cells show stronger induction of multiple repair pathways during G2 compared to alpha cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7af94fcbbc3d167377ece712218fa36b</data>
  <data key="d4">Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
