<edge source="Insulin" target="MAFA">
  <data key="d12">1.0</data>
  <data key="d13">MAFA is a key regulator of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion and insulin gene transcription.&lt;SEP&gt;The relation (Insulin, MafA) describes the regulatory and transcriptional role that MafA plays in controlling insulin gene expression and secretion. MafA functions as a key transcription factor and dedicated activator of the insulin gene, responsible for driving tissue-specific insulin expression in vivo. When ectopically expressed, MafA induces expression from insulin reporter constructs, confirming its direct role as an insulin gene transcription factor. MafA is capable of selectively and independently activating insulin transcription, as demonstrated through both cell line-based assays and chick in ovo electroporation assays, as well as in vitro experimental systems.

Beyond transcriptional activation, MafA is also a critical regulator of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion in pancreatic β-cells, highlighting its functional importance not only at the gene expression level but also in the physiological secretory response to glucose. MafA activates insulin gene expression broadly in endocrine cells, with its coexpression alongside insulin increasing progressively during embryonic development and becoming the predominant regulatory factor in adult islet β-cells by approximately 4 weeks postnatal.

In the context of developmental regulation, MafA shares overlapping responsibilities with MafB, functioning as mutual regulators of the insulin gene. During embryonic development, MafA's contribution to insulin transcription is relatively limited and becomes significant primarily in the absence of MafB, as evidenced by more pronounced transcriptional effects observed in MafAΔPanc;MafB−/− double mutants. Together, these findings establish MafA as an essential, dedicated activator of insulin gene expression and a central regulator of β-cell function, with its dominant role becoming fully established in the postnatal and adult pancreas.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA is essential for insulin expression in beta cells of the pancreas.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA regulates insulin production in pancreatic islet beta cells.</data>
  <data key="d14">gene expression,transcriptional regulation&lt;SEP&gt;coexpression,embryonic,gene activation,gene expression,gene regulation,reporter construct,transcription activation,transcription regulation,transcriptional activation,transcriptional regulation,β-cell marker&lt;SEP&gt;regulates</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-b13f08985b48653da8be9b63098bbb2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d5e51c0312e54ad3757625ff1041f76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bec3f5c6cf93d2d763da970e02d0fc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f82db25373e25b34bbbcfebc1c0f861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f7f8610c162035d52f2edbe846e8a97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9ed09558012a9cb8eba4b1efe943943&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41e9c4875e9ee2b378c620275d81fe7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb7aaa2476f3163b5c3de684504f42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ecc1e3f157d04d78962c82537c6b7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-222f634a871d4f5fd5cffac2fce0d698&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4fecd9e508da1daa4a0d2d27a7e759</data>
  <data key="d16">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA is a dedicated activator of the insulin gene in vivo.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;A switch from MafB to MafA expression accompanies differentiation to pancreatic beta-cells.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778562968</data>
  <data key="d18"></data>
</edge>
