<edge source="Insulin" target="beta cell">
  <data key="d12">2.0</data>
  <data key="d13">β-cells synthesize and secrete insulin to regulate blood glucose levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin is produced by β-cells and serves as a marker for identifying β-cells in islet studies.&lt;SEP&gt;The relation (beta cell, insulin) describes the fundamental biological connection between beta cells and insulin, wherein beta cells are the insulin-producing cells located within the pancreatic islets of the pancreas. Beta cells both produce and secrete insulin as their primary function, making insulin a defining marker of this cell type.

Beta cells secrete insulin in response to increases in blood glucose levels, thereby sustaining normal glucose homeostasis and meeting the body's broader metabolic demands. This continuous secretory activity contributes to their overall secretory burden and can render them susceptible to DNA damage over time. Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion, a key functional process, can be impaired by factors such as the overexpression of the miR-21 precursor within beta cells, highlighting the sensitivity of this mechanism to molecular perturbations.

In the context of disease, a specific beta cell subset characterized by enhanced insulin secretion has been found to be reduced in type 2 diabetes, suggesting that the functional heterogeneity of beta cells plays a role in disease progression. Conversely, early insulin therapy has been shown to reduce the secretory demand placed on beta cells, thereby preserving their function and enabling improved glycaemic control.

From a methodological standpoint, insulin is widely used as an immunostaining marker to identify and localize beta cells within pancreatic tissue sections, underscoring its role not only as a biological product of beta cells but also as a key identifier in histological and research contexts.</data>
  <data key="d14">biosynthesis,cell identity,hormone production,secretion&lt;SEP&gt;marker_of,preserves,produces,reduces_stress,secreted_by,secretes</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-7a87d40ff620e7f930f155b5dea286c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ee443fb4c37f27d6895254d3f95c708&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f6e43723dd8061d1a0532981e4a43b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6215695e8bd809fd02cb8b9b7b4123ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b0125512b4fa44926ba8a80a62b0a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80318e0997c6182c0124775c5f01f6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68a87a3a1f3eb5d8b0e53b41df492512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-363f3a19905f833d29a7d1242acc6641&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f09e5d1418f8ae04b43a24b97c1d52d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28a44d52c17c29e9741a935aa76e8afe</data>
  <data key="d16">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778561557</data>
  <data key="d18"></data>
</edge>
