<edge source="Insulin Secretion" target="beta cell">
  <data key="d12">16.0</data>
  <data key="d13">Beta cells and insulin secretion share a fundamental and well-established biological relationship. Beta cells are the primary islet cell type responsible for insulin secretion in the pancreas, and this secretory function represents their defining physiological role. Under normal conditions, beta cells regulate insulin secretion in response to metabolic cues — most notably glucose — to maintain glucose homeostasis. Glucose-responsive insulin secretion is thus considered a key functional hallmark of healthy beta cells.

Research conducted using dynamic assay systems, including those employing TUID islets from non-diabetic donors, has demonstrated the robust secretory capacity of healthy beta cells across both sexes and racial groups. In response to stimuli such as high glucose, incretin hormones, and KCl, non-diabetic beta cells exhibit measurable and reliable insulin secretion, confirming their intact secretory function under experimental conditions.

Studies have also identified significant disparities in insulin secretion based on sex and racial background. Specifically, cAMP-stimulated insulin secretion is decreased in non-diabetic black male islets compared to white male islets. Similarly, the response to high glucose combined with IBMX — a phosphodiesterase inhibitor used to elevate intracellular cAMP — is diminished in black male islets relative to white male islets. These findings highlight racial disparities in beta cell secretory function even in the absence of diabetes.

In the context of type 2 diabetes (T2D), insulin secretion is substantially impaired. Compared to non-diabetic donors, T2D donors show decreased insulin secretion in response to high glucose, incretin stimulation, and KCl. The insulin secretion pathway is notably suppressed in male beta cells during the transition to T2D, with male beta cells in T2D exhibiting broader reductions in hormone and insulin secretion pathways. This underscores the sex-specific deterioration of beta cell function as the disease progresses. Collectively, these findings illustrate that while beta cells are the definitive source of pancreatic insulin secretion, their secretory capacity is subject to significant modulation by factors including sex, racial background, and pathological progression toward type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d14">expressed_in,occurs_in,performs,produced_by,produces,reduced_in,regulates,suppressed_in</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-cd3982c3cb7b00162818de82027a853e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7873cb15c71b20e5fc369a6f0ab52a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794cb97944fe847e61831fceed4d92ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51601485f0924871a988aae2e5ce30bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238834380bfb40677324883af3e3004&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0269f34de8f0928a9614438917079ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76049b85888e8e904e7c01de9740d34f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de697a7536ea955d878a95580bf8aa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d0bf8cd8c8cb69199fb8f9bf6c37e70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d41db2658ec2709309c04b39b1894e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd4f6cfb9180aade52c8cb84446ced85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cd7041acf7539c8db67413bcd98f41b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efc110efa119e343b95df1abbd0a327&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43761b34ecc024bd90bfa8711c45a233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28a44d52c17c29e9741a935aa76e8afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86</data>
  <data key="d16">Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Safeguarding genomic integrity in beta-cells: implications for beta-cell differentiation, growth, and dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;The role of the beta cell in type 2 diabetes: new findings from the last 5 years.&lt;SEP&gt;Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778571492</data>
  <data key="d18"></data>
</edge>
