<edge source="Insulin Secretion" target="glucose">
  <data key="d12">9.0</data>
  <data key="d13">Glucose is a well-established physiological stimulus for insulin secretion, playing a central role in pancreatic islet function as characterized across multiple experimental contexts.

In islet assay settings, glucose is widely employed as a secretagogue to stimulate insulin secretion. Across perifusion experiments, glucose is applied at a range of concentrations — from as low as 2 mM up to 20 mM — to elicit graded insulin secretory responses from pancreatic islets. Specific concentrations commonly referenced include low glucose at 5.6 mM, which typically serves as a baseline condition, and high glucose at 16.7 mM, which is frequently used as the primary stimulatory concentration in dynamic secretion assays.

The relationship between glucose concentration and insulin secretion is dose-dependent, with graded concentrations (2–20 mM) producing corresponding changes in secretory output from islets in perifusion systems. High glucose at 16.7 mM represents a standard stimulatory benchmark in these experimental paradigms.

Additionally, glucose has been used in combination with other agents, such as IBMX (3-isobutyl-1-methylxanthine), in dynamic secretion assays. In one such context, a decreased insulin secretory response to high glucose combined with IBMX was observed in black male donor islets compared to white male donor islets, highlighting potential donor-related variability in glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.

Overall, glucose serves as the primary physiological and experimental stimulus for insulin secretion, and its application across a spectrum of concentrations in perifusion and dynamic secretion assays makes it a foundational tool in the study of pancreatic islet biology and β-cell function.</data>
