<edge source="Type 2 Diabetes" target="beta cell">
  <data key="d12">1.0</data>
  <data key="d13">T2D is associated with loss of β cell identity and activity postnatally, including inappropriate SST+ and GAST+ cell production in β cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic beta cell dysfunction and failure are involved in the pathogenesis of both type 1 and type 2 diabetes mellitus.&lt;SEP&gt;The relation between beta cells and type 2 diabetes (T2D) is complex and multifaceted, encompassing dysfunction, cellular senescence, DNA damage, transcriptomic dysregulation, sex-specific differences, and epigenomic alterations, all of which collectively contribute to the pathogenesis of the disease.

Beta cell dysfunction and failure are broadly recognized as central to the development of T2D. Beta cells, the insulin-producing cells of the pancreatic islets, become impaired in T2D, resulting in insufficient insulin secretion and progressive disease deterioration. At the cellular level, this dysfunction is associated with DNA double-strand breaks, activation of the DNA damage response (DDR), and elevated p53 activity, indicating that genotoxic stress plays a significant role in beta cell decline. T2D is also associated with inactivation of beta cell transcription factors and beta cell dedifferentiation, further undermining normal beta cell identity and function.

Cellular senescence is another key feature of beta cell pathology in T2D. In several mouse models, beta cells exhibit high cytoplasmic staining for senescence markers, and the acceleration of beta cell aging has been identified as a determining factor in disease development. Senolysis — the targeted elimination of senescent cells — has been shown to improve outcomes in T2D, highlighting the therapeutic relevance of targeting beta cell senescence.

Importantly, the T2D metabolic state compromises the capacity of beta cells to recover function following caloric restriction. However, interventions such as caloric restriction (CR), time-restricted feeding (TRF), or dieting have been shown to recover beta cell function in both mice and humans, suggesting that functional recovery remains possible under appropriate conditions.

At the molecular level, beta cell transcriptomes are significantly dysregulated in T2D, with notable sex-specific differences in gene regulation, insulin secretion, and regulatory architecture. Female beta cells from T2D donors demonstrate a greater number of dysregulated autosomal differentially expressed genes (DEGs) compared to male beta cells. Additionally, female beta cells in T2D show reduced activation of pathways associated with mitochondrial function relative to non-diabetic donors, suggesting that mitochondrial impairment is a more pronounced feature of beta cell dysfunction in affected females.

At the genomic and epigenomic level, T2D is associated with genomic differences across islet cell types, including beta cells, with distal enhancers implicated in beta cell function. Notably, no differentially accessible chromatin regions in beta cells were found to overlap with known T2D genetic variants, representing a specific finding within the broader landscape of beta cell chromatin regulation in the disease context. These collective findings underscore the importance of considering biological sex, metabolic state, and epigenomic architecture in the study and treatment of T2D.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta cell dysfunction is a key early pathogenic event that contributes to type 2 diabetes onset and progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta cell dysfunction is a key mechanism underlying type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 2 diabetes progression is associated with declining beta cell function and senescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic beta cell failure is a prerequisite for diabetic onset and progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta-cell dysfunction and impaired repair capacity are central features of type 2 diabetes pathogenesis.</data>
  <data key="d14">dysfunction,identity loss&lt;SEP&gt;dysfunction,pathogenesis&lt;SEP&gt;affected_in,affects,aging determines outcomes,associated_with,causes,causes_dysfunction,causes_dysfunction_in,central role,contributes to,disease mechanism,dysfunction,dysregulated_in,dysregulates,impairs,impairs function,involved_in,involves_dysfunction_of,no_overlap,pathogenesis,transcriptional differences</data>
