<edge source="Type 1 Diabetes" target="beta cell">
  <data key="d12">1.0</data>
  <data key="d13">Beta cell ER stress, dysfunction, and senescence are central to the development and progression of type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;The relation between beta cells and type 1 diabetes (T1D) is multifaceted, encompassing autoimmune destruction, cellular dysfunction, and senescence-related mechanisms.

Beta cells are central to type 1 diabetes pathogenesis, functioning both as victims of autoimmune destruction and as potential instigators of disease. The hallmark of T1D is the autoimmune-mediated loss and destruction of beta cells, which leads to beta cell failure. This destruction is accompanied by varying degrees of residual beta cell function at diagnosis, with notable differences observed by gender—lower residual beta cell function has been documented in males—as well as variation associated with season of diagnosis.

Beyond direct immune-mediated destruction, beta cell dysfunction plays a significant role in T1D pathogenesis. Dysfunctional states include stressed cellular conditions such as the unfolded protein response (UPR), type I interferon (IFN) response, and cellular senescence, all of which contribute to disease development. Senescent beta cells are known to accumulate during T1D development, and DNA damage to beta cells has been shown to recapitulate features of senescent beta cells observed in the context of T1D. Beta cells from T1D donors specifically exhibit nuclear localization of senescence markers, while MAFA protein expression remains unchanged in these cells.

The accumulation of senescent beta cells contributes to immune activation and islet dysfunction, further exacerbating disease progression. Additionally, T1D involves autoimmune beta cell loss that may be promoted by senescent beta cell extracellular vesicle (EV) miRNAs operating within the islet microenvironment. Critically, targeted elimination of senescent beta cells has been shown to prevent type 1 diabetes, highlighting the functional importance of beta cell senescence in disease onset and suggesting it as a potential therapeutic target.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta cell DNA damage response and senescence promote islet inflammation and pathogenesis of type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta cells exhibit DNA damage, DDR activation, and attempted replication in type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta cells in islets proximal to immune cell infiltrate display a more prominent DNA damage response in T1D.&lt;SEP&gt;Residual beta cell function at diagnosis of type 1 diabetes varies with gender and season.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta-cell failure, senescence, and death impair insulin secretion and drive hyperglycemia in type 1 diabetes.</data>
