<edge source="Type 1 Diabetes" target="alpha cell">
  <data key="d12">9.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relationship between alpha cells and type 1 diabetes (T1D) is characterized by significant dysfunction affecting both alpha cell function and gene expression. Alpha cells become dysfunctional in the context of type 1 diabetes, with their function and gene expression being demonstrably compromised. This dysfunction manifests as an impaired cellular stress response that distinguishes alpha cells from beta cells in the diabetic environment.

A key and consistently noted feature of alpha cell dysfunction in type 1 diabetes is that it occurs independently of a senescence program. Unlike beta cells, whose stress response and pathological trajectory may involve senescence-related mechanisms, alpha cells in T1D undergo dysfunction through pathways that do not implicate cellular senescence. This distinction underscores a fundamental difference in how the two pancreatic cell types respond to the diabetic milieu.

Another notable characteristic of alpha cells in type 1 diabetes is their relative resistance to destruction compared to beta cells. While beta cells are the primary targets of autoimmune destruction in T1D — leading to insulin deficiency — alpha cells remain largely intact in terms of cell survival, yet they are nonetheless functionally compromised. This survival without proper function further distinguishes the alpha cell stress response from that of beta cells, suggesting that alpha cell dysfunction represents a separate but important pathological component of type 1 diabetes beyond the well-characterized loss of beta cell mass.

Overall, the relationship between alpha cells and type 1 diabetes reflects a pattern of functional and transcriptional impairment that is independent of senescence, with alpha cells showing a unique stress response profile that differs substantially from the autoimmune destruction and senescence-associated changes observed in beta cells.</data>
  <data key="d14">affected_by,affected_in,associated_with,becomes_dysfunctional,dysfunction independent of senescence,dysfunction_in,resistant_in</data>
