<edge source="MAFA" target="MAFB">
  <data key="d12">20.0</data>
  <data key="d13">MAFA and MAFB are two closely related members of the large Maf basic leucine-zipper transcription factor family, both functioning as islet-enriched transcription factors with essential, complementary, and temporally distinct roles in pancreatic β-cell biology. Both factors share oncogenic activity, are capable of associating with other MAF family members to form heterodimeric complexes, and coordinate activity on targets such as the glucagon gene G1 element to induce β-cell generation and differentiation.

Structurally, MAFA and MAFB share significant similarity, though AlphaFold 2 analyses have revealed differences in their non-DNA binding and dimerization sequences. During embryonic development, MafB expression precedes that of MafA, and developing insulin-producing cells pass through a transitional MafB⁺MafA⁻Ins⁺ intermediate state before becoming MafB⁻MafA⁺Ins⁺ cells in the adult pancreas. This MafB-to-MafA developmental switch is considered crucial for functional β-cell maturation. In rodents, MafB expression declines postnatally, whereas in adult humans and nonhuman primates, both MAFA and MAFB are coexpressed in islet β-cells throughout adulthood — a distinction considered a potential contributor to differences in islet architecture and β-cell composition across species.

Functionally, MAFA and MAFB regulate overlapping yet distinct sets of target genes in a temporally unique manner. Both jointly regulate critical β-cell genes such as Slc30a8 and G6pc2, negatively regulate Rbp4 production, and co-regulate exocytosis-related genes in human β-cells. The formation of MAFA:MAFB heterodimeric activator complexes is thought to confer unique functional characteristics specifically to human islet β-cells. Adult MafA controls many genes previously activated by MafB during embryonic development, indicating functional continuity across developmental stages.

Despite their relatedness, MAFA and MAFB exhibit non-redundant roles. MAFB cannot rescue β-cell defects in MafA-deficient mice, whereas MAFA can compensate for the absence of MafB in MafBΔpanc mice. MafB is reexpressed in insulin-positive β-cells under MafA-deficient conditions, suggesting partial compensatory capacity. However, combined deficiency of both MafA and MafB leads to more severe islet structural destruction and glucose intolerance than MafA deficiency alone, confirming their cooperative and non-redundant importance. Genetically, the compound MafaΔpanc;Mafb+/− mutant shows more dramatic effects on β-cell function than MafaΔpanc alone, further underscoring cooperative regulation. Additionally, MAFB, but not MAFA, directly regulates GAST misexpression observed in type 2 diabetes, highlighting divergent roles in human islet pathophysiology.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA and MAFB act as homodimers or heterodimers under physiological conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA and MAFB are related bZip transcription factors with overlapping and distinct regulatory roles in pancreatic islet cells and other tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA and MAFB can form heterodimers via their basic-leucine-zipper domains.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA and MAFB act as homodimers or heterodimers with each other under physiological conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA and MAFB form heterodimers with comparable transactivation activity to their respective homodimers in beta cell culture systems.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA and MAFB are expressed in a distinct manner: only MAFA is produced in mouse beta cells, while both MAFA and MAFB are produced in human beta cells.</data>
  <data key="d14">adulthood,beta cell impact,cell fate determination,cell maturation,co-expression,co-regulation,coexpression,combined deficiency,comparison,compensation,compensatory expression,compensatory function,compensatory relationship,compound regulation,cooperative regulation,coordinated regulation,deficiency response,developmental switch,developmental transition,differential regulation,differentiation,dimerization,dimerizes,distinct_expression,downstream transcription factors,dynamic expression,expression pattern,family relationship,functional comparison,functional compensation,functional interaction,functional interrelationship,functional overlap,functional relationship,functional similarity,gene co-regulation,gene family,gene regulation,genetic interaction,heterodimerization,human β cells,interacts_with,islet architecture,islet cell generation,islet cells,knock-in analysis,mechanistic comparison,mouse development,mutually beneficial relationship,oncogenic activity,postnatal switch,protein family,regulates,regulatory differences,regulatory targets,related_to,sequential regulation,shared expression,spatial-temporal expression,species-specific expression,species-specific roles,structural comparison,temporal activity,temporal complementarity,temporal expression,temporal gene regulation,transcription factor family,transcription factor interrelationship,transcription factor regulation,transcription factors,transcriptional regulation,β-cell differentiation,β-cell regulation</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-75261aabfd0eeb269665c62f36dfeb2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69365499ad956be649b962c79d46f483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06849490a7a3c085d30a2a392584bd67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6854465a5ab40a3dbe223209e2c5cd66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78596a38854a9e8f2c4ef9c3dfcbbfbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d01061347ba219119efa4109d89e71f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74fc4f55c8c6d83ebd1d8dba07d90692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce67306159c306b2c966577061cee6bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea3320816339f9f109dec0e1621420bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b13f08985b48653da8be9b63098bbb2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b5b330edc0a8ae22148338c6d94f154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02381256ec5732a712b97ced237c2acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1a171c046b7e074fd282a07d58cc39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb32288d5dedac1b23370508bf2f7b2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84ba2041af7b71a5fc2faad1de1c95dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3384cef9855a1b408c68f7d1ea3153c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4fecd9e508da1daa4a0d2d27a7e759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19fff7a7048ab7112926e25c2e46450b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae638e9cabe91a68fc062c9981b0e86</data>
  <data key="d16">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Defining unique structural features in the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors that control Insulin gene activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA is a dedicated activator of the insulin gene in vivo.&lt;SEP&gt;MafB Is Important for Pancreatic β-Cell Maintenance under a MafA-Deficient Condition.&lt;SEP&gt;The MAFB transcription factor impacts islet α-cell function in rodents and represents a unique signature of primate islet β-cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Examining How the MAFB Transcription Factor Affects Islet β-Cell Function Postnatally.&lt;SEP&gt;A switch from MafB to MafA expression accompanies differentiation to pancreatic beta-cells.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA missense mutation causes familial insulinomatosis and diabetes mellitus.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploring Large MAF Transcription Factors: Functions, Pathology, and Mouse Models with Point Mutations.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778569834</data>
