<edge source="MAFA" target="INS">
  <data key="d12">10.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relation (INS, MAFA) describes the regulatory relationship between the transcription factor MAFA and the insulin gene (INS), wherein MAFA functions as a key positive regulator of INS expression.

MAFA is characterized as a glucose-regulated, pancreatic beta-cell-specific transcriptional activator of the insulin gene. It acts as a dedicated activator of INS in vivo, regulating insulin gene transcription specifically within islet beta cells. This regulatory role has been demonstrated to be conserved across species, highlighting the fundamental importance of MAFA in pancreatic islet biology.

At the molecular level, MAFA directly binds to specific DNA regulatory elements associated with the insulin gene. Experimental evidence from electrophoretic mobility shift assays (EMSA) confirms that MAFA binds to the human INS MAFA/B binding site, as well as to the insulin control element DNA. Additionally, MAFA binds to INS enhancer sequences, as assessed through gel shift assays and transactivation experiments. MAFA stimulates insulin enhancer-driven reporter activity, a finding consistently demonstrated across transfection assays and independent reporter activity studies.

Functional depletion studies further support MAFA's positive regulatory role: MAFA depletion in EndoC-βH1 cells leads to a measurable decrease in INS mRNA production, directly confirming that MAFA positively regulates INS expression at the transcriptional level. Beyond acting independently, MAFA also cooperates with other islet-enriched transcription factors to regulate the insulin gene, suggesting its activity is integrated within a broader transcriptional network governing INS expression in pancreatic beta cells.</data>
