<edge source="MAFA" target="AlphaFold 2">
  <data key="d12">8.0</data>
  <data key="d13">AlphaFold 2 was employed as a primary computational tool for the structural characterization of MAFA, a transcription factor, through de novo predictive modeling approaches.

AlphaFold 2 was used extensively to predict and determine the three-dimensional organization and architecture of MAFA in multiple forms. This included modeling of the full-length MAFA protein, with particular attention to regions that were previously underdefined or structurally disordered. Through these predictions, novel structural features were revealed, including the C-terminal tail and various disordered regions of the protein that had not been well characterized by prior experimental methods.

A key application of AlphaFold 2 in this context was the de novo structural modeling of full-length MAFA homodimers. These models encompassed multiple states of the protein, including unphosphorylated forms, fully phosphorylated forms, and phosphomimetic variants. The modeling of phosphomimetic variants specifically enabled examination of conformational effects resulting from phosphorylation, providing insight into how post-translational modifications influence the structural behavior of MAFA.

AlphaFold 2 was also applied to predict the structures of wildtype MAFA alongside pathogenic mutant forms. This comparative structural analysis allowed researchers to identify structurally unique regions and to assess how disease-associated mutations alter the three-dimensional conformation of the protein. The tool thus served both to generate baseline structural models and to characterize the conformational consequences of specific mutations relevant to pathogenic conditions.

Overall, AlphaFold 2 provided critical structural insights into the full conformational landscape of MAFA, bridging gaps in experimental structural data and enabling a more comprehensive understanding of both its normal and disease-associated structural states.</data>
  <data key="d14">analysis,analyzes,models,predicts,predicts_structure,protein analysis,structural prediction</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-8b5b330edc0a8ae22148338c6d94f154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb32288d5dedac1b23370508bf2f7b2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84ba2041af7b71a5fc2faad1de1c95dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3384cef9855a1b408c68f7d1ea3153c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4fecd9e508da1daa4a0d2d27a7e759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19fff7a7048ab7112926e25c2e46450b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d07a2e48a0133bedad1b6e79e652b89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae638e9cabe91a68fc062c9981b0e86</data>
  <data key="d16">Defining unique structural features in the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors that control Insulin gene activity.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778569843</data>
