<edge source="MAFA" target="PDX1">
  <data key="d12">11.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relation between MAFA and PDX1 represents a deeply interconnected regulatory and functional partnership within pancreatic beta cells, characterized by mutual regulation, co-expression, and cooperative roles in both normal beta cell biology and cellular reprogramming.

MAFA and PDX1 are co-expressed as part of a critical subset of transcription factors involved in pancreatic β-cell development and function. Together with Ngn3, both MAFA and PDX1 can reprogram adult pancreatic acinar cells into β-like cells when misexpressed, highlighting their combinatorial power in cell fate determination. This reprogramming capacity underscores their shared and complementary roles in establishing and maintaining beta cell identity.

The regulatory relationship between MAFA and PDX1 is bidirectional. PDX1, in cooperation with FoxA2 and Nkx2.2, regulates islet beta-cell-specific MAFA expression through conserved upstream sequences, positioning PDX1 as a transcriptional activator of MAFA. Consistently, PDX1 deficiency is associated with compromised MAFA expression in pancreatic islets, further confirming PDX1's role in driving MAFA transcription.

Conversely, MAFA also regulates PDX1: MafA deletion in adult mice leads to reduced PDX1 transcription, impairing β-cell function. Additionally, gene expression analysis in Mafa-knockout ICR mice identified PDX1 as a target gene of MAFA, reinforcing the notion that MAFA directly or indirectly sustains PDX1 expression.

At the systems level, PDX1 and MAFA display a positive correlation in transcription factor activation within beta cell gene regulatory networks (GRNs), as identified through SCENIC analysis. This positive co-regulation confirms that MAFA and PDX1 function as tightly coupled, co-regulated transcription factors that collectively orchestrate beta cell gene expression programs, maintain functional beta cell identity, and enable beta cell reprogramming from alternative cell types.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA cooperates with PDX1 to activate the Insulin -238 WT reporter gene.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA does not provide enhanced activation of Insulin-reporter in the presence of PDX1, unlike MAFB.&lt;SEP&gt;PDX1 is an islet-enriched transcription factor that directly regulates MAFA expression; increased PDX1 expression is associated with increased MAFA protein levels in C57 MafaS64F/+ males.</data>
  <data key="d14">activates,beta-cell specificity,co-regulated,combinatorial reprogramming,cooperates_with,correlated,expression dependency,gene expression,no cooperative effect,regulates,regulatory interaction,reprogramming,transcription factor cooperation,transcriptional regulation,β-cell engineering</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-9693157a2d458ecc8255c49ec77d95f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a87d40ff620e7f930f155b5dea286c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ee443fb4c37f27d6895254d3f95c708&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ecc1e3f157d04d78962c82537c6b7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7568462935971fd17472cf21ed7b8a2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1a171c046b7e074fd282a07d58cc39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08e345f1e7ff9529c2ab94760dcb50f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecb3c400fe73c10974d8a6b3b2160b23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d07a2e48a0133bedad1b6e79e652b89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0386e6a8316aca2a67c3466442e7e1fa</data>
  <data key="d16">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;MafB Is Important for Pancreatic β-Cell Maintenance under a MafA-Deficient Condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploring Large MAF Transcription Factors: Functions, Pathology, and Mouse Models with Point Mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Defining unique structural features in the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors that control Insulin gene activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic background influences the phenotypic penetrance by MAFA (S64F) MODY in male mice.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778570512</data>
