<edge source="MAFA" target="MAFA_S64F">
  <data key="d12">38.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relation (MAFA, MAFA_S64F) describes the connection between the wildtype MAFA transcription factor gene and its pathogenic point mutation variant, MAFA_S64F, also known as p.Ser64Phe (c.191C&gt;T). MAFA_S64F is a missense mutation involving a single amino acid substitution at position 64 of the MAFA protein, resulting in a serine-to-phenylalanine change encoded by the single nucleotide substitution c.191C&gt;T within the endogenous MAFA locus.

At the molecular level, MAFA_S64F is located within the transactivation (transcriptional activation) domain of the MAFA protein. The mutation disrupts phosphorylation at Ser64/Ser65, impairing the normal GSK3-mediated phosphorylation cascade and rendering the mutant protein underphosphorylated relative to wildtype MAFA. As a result, the mutant protein escapes phosphorylation-dependent degradation, leading to enhanced protein stability. Structural analysis using AlphaFold 2 has confirmed distinct conformational changes introduced by this substitution, supporting the functional divergence of MAFA_S64F from its wildtype counterpart. Notably, while MAFA_S64F retains similar DNA binding capacity to the wildtype protein, it exhibits altered functional interactions and reduced transactivation activity, indicating that increased protein abundance does not necessarily translate to enhanced transcriptional function. The variant has also been employed in chimeric protein and reporter assays, as well as in partial phosphorylation mimicry modeling studies, underscoring its utility as a research tool.

The functional consequences of MAFA_S64F result in dysregulation of MAFA's normal role in pancreatic β cell biology. The mutation is associated with β cell senescence and causes sex-dependent β cell dysfunction with a notable bias in males, in whom it induces premature aging and cellular senescence of β cells. Mouse models carrying the MAFA_S64F variant have been developed to simulate these human disease phenotypes, enabling mechanistic investigation of the mutation's pathological role in β cell dysfunction.

Clinically, MAFA_S64F is classified as a pathogenic variant associated with maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY), a form of monogenic diabetes, and is specifically linked to insulinomatosis or diabetes mellitus. It represents a well-characterized example of how a single phosphosite-disrupting mutation within a critical transcription factor can lead to adult-onset beta cell dysfunction and disease.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA_S64F is a missense mutation in the MAFA transcription factor gene.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA S64F is a missense mutation in the MAFA transcription factor gene.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA S64F is a naturally occurring mutation in the MAFA gene.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA S64F is a point mutation in the MAFA gene.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA S64F is a MODY-associated mutation of the MAFA transcription factor.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA S64F is a MODY-associated mutation of the MAFA gene.</data>
  <data key="d14">disease mutation,dysfunction,mutant_of,mutation,pathogenic mutation,phenotype association,promotes_stability,protein stability,reduced_phosphorylation,structural difference,transactivation disruption,variant</data>
