<edge source="MAFA" target="beta cell">
  <data key="d12">31.0</data>
  <data key="d13">MAFA (also referred to as MafA) is a beta cell-enriched transcription factor that functions as a master transcriptional regulator of pancreatic islet β-cell identity, maturation, and function. It is expressed in both mouse and human pancreatic islet β-cells, though its expression pattern differs across species and developmental stages. In the adult pancreas, MAFA expression is found predominantly or exclusively in β-cells, becoming the dominant MAF factor as MAFB expression diminishes postnatally. MAFA is also recognized as a well-established marker gene for β-cells, commonly used to identify this cell cluster in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) studies of human pancreatic islets. Notably, MAFA is expressed heterogeneously in human pancreatic islet β-cells, and its coexpression with MAFB correlates with greater transcriptional and functional activity.

MAFA is essential for regulating insulin gene expression and glucose-stimulated insulin secretion in adult β-cells. Its functional responsibilities span multiple aspects of β-cell biology, including glucose metabolism and sensing, insulin production, insulin granule docking, calcium signaling, and glucose signaling. It sustains the expression of MafB-dependent target genes in adult islets, effectively compensating for the postnatal decline in MAFB activity. Its role in β-cell differentiation has been compared to that of myogenin in myotube differentiation, underscoring its importance as a terminal differentiation factor. MAFA is also vital in the development and maturation of pancreatic islet insulin-producing β-cells, regulating β-cell replication, proliferation, and maintenance of normal islet architecture from the weaning period onwards.

MAFA maintains monohormonal β-cell identity by actively repressing non-β-cell transcriptional programs, including gastrin-associated signatures and non-β-cell hormone production. Loss of MAFA leads to aberrant hormone expression, impaired β-cell activity, and overall β-cell dysfunction, while also revealing a compensatory importance of MAFB in β-cell maintenance. In gene regulatory network analyses, MAFA is upregulated in β-cells irrespective of sex and exhibits high centrality, highlighting its pivotal role in coordinating β-cell transcriptional programs. MAFA is further described as a transcriptional modulator activated in calorie-restricted (CR) β-cells, and its inactivation has been observed in type 2 diabetes.

MAFA transcription factor activity is known to decrease in aging β-cells, contributing to age-associated β-cell dysfunction. A pathological missense variant, the MODY-associated MAFA S64F mutation, has been identified as causing adult-onset, sex-biased islet β-cell dysfunction by inducing premature aging and cellular senescence preferentially in males. Collectively, MAFA's loss or dysfunction is directly linked to impaired insulin secretion and compromised β-cell integrity across development and postnatal life.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA is a beta cell-enriched transcription factor regulating beta cell function.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA is a top upregulated marker gene expressed in beta cells independently of sex and race/ethnicity.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA is upregulated in beta cells irrespective of sex and exhibits high centrality in beta cell gene regulatory networks.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA is a transcription factor expressed in pancreatic beta cells required for coordinated insulin secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA is a transcription factor involved in beta cell function, with mutations causing adult-onset beta cell dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA is a transcription factor expressed in pancreatic beta cells involved in their function and identity.</data>
