<edge source="MAFA" target="phosphorylation">
  <data key="d12">7.0</data>
  <data key="d13">MAFA has conserved phosphorylation residues in its transcriptional activation domain associated with human disease mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorylation within the N-terminal transactivation domain regulates MAFA DNA-binding ability but not that of MAFB.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA activity and stability are regulated by phosphorylation as a posttranslational modification.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorylation within the MAFA N terminus regulates C-terminal dimerization, DNA binding, and stability/transactivation potential at serine 65.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorylation state of MAFA influences its predicted three-dimensional structural conformation as modeled by AlphaFold 2.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA contains multiple serine and threonine phosphorylation sites that are modeled using phosphomimetic substitutions in AlphaFold 2 structural analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFAWT is heavily modified by phosphorylation in mammalian cells.</data>
