</edge>
<edge source="MAFB" target="beta cell">
  <data key="d12">14.0</data>
  <data key="d13">MAFB (v-maf musculoaponeurotic fibrosarcoma oncogene homolog B) is a transcription factor that plays a critical and multifaceted role in pancreatic beta cell development, maturation, and function across multiple stages of life and across species.

During embryonic and fetal development, MAFB is expressed in both alpha and beta cells of the mouse fetal pancreas, contributing to beta cell generation and differentiation. It is co-expressed with MAFA in insulin-positive (insulin+) β-cells during embryogenesis and is considered a key β-cell regulator at this stage. MAFB is produced in a larger fraction of β-cells than MAFA during development and regulates potential effectors of glucose sensing, hormone processing, vesicle formation, and insulin secretion, underscoring its broad regulatory importance in establishing functional beta cell identity.

Postnatally, MAFB is transiently expressed in β-cells and remains significant to islet β-cell activity before disappearing approximately two weeks after birth in rodents. This transient postnatal expression reflects an important embryonic and early postnatal role in β-cell maturation. Evidence from compound MafaΔpanc;Mafb+/− mutant mice demonstrates that MAFB is beneficial to β-cells prior to its postnatal loss, as these mice exhibit earlier and more severe islet morphology defects, highlighting MAFB's role in postnatal β-cell maturation and its importance for pancreatic beta-cell maintenance under MAFA-deficient conditions.

A notable species distinction exists regarding MAFB expression in adult islets. While MAFB disappears from rodent β-cells postnatally, it is co-expressed with MAFA in adult human and nonhuman primate islet β-cells. This co-expression represents a unique molecular signature and positions MAFB as a likely important regulator of primate islet β-cell identity and function.

Furthermore, MAFB is implicated in maintaining β-cell identity in the context of disease. Its loss in early type 2 diabetes (T2D) may promote β-cell reprogramming toward alternative cell states, suggesting that MAFB serves as a guardian of β-cell identity and that its dysregulation could contribute to β-cell dysfunction in metabolic disease.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFB is expressed in and regulates function and identity of pancreatic islet beta cells, including in primate islets.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFB plays crucial roles in beta cells and various other cell types across development and postnatal life.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFB is expressed heterogeneously in human pancreatic islet beta cells; coexpression with MAFA correlates with greater transcriptional and functional activity.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFB is expressed in human pancreatic islet beta cells but not in mouse beta cells.</data>
  <data key="d14">beneficial role,cell maturation,co-expression,developmental regulation,developmental role,embryonic development,embryonic regulation,expressed_in,functions_in,gene expression,identity maintenance,postnatal loss,postnatal maturation,primate biology,regulates,regulation,required_for,transcriptional regulation,transient expression,unique signature,β-cell function</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-9693157a2d458ecc8255c49ec77d95f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b158cd1c233246631a3b8f719a275f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a87d40ff620e7f930f155b5dea286c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207f97a443acec0faaf8f9f2d208e61f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75261aabfd0eeb269665c62f36dfeb2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69365499ad956be649b962c79d46f483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b13f08985b48653da8be9b63098bbb2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bec3f5c6cf93d2d763da970e02d0fc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1c90235486a0fe738831a720e1f94ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02381256ec5732a712b97ced237c2acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-222f634a871d4f5fd5cffac2fce0d698&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed6515ae53fc7e07b1c44a83258c3224&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1a171c046b7e074fd282a07d58cc39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe670890a826e2e1970f382c46b77260&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3384cef9855a1b408c68f7d1ea3153c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19fff7a7048ab7112926e25c2e46450b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d07a2e48a0133bedad1b6e79e652b89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae638e9cabe91a68fc062c9981b0e86</data>
  <data key="d16">The MafA transcription factor becomes essential to islet β-cells soon after birth.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA and MafB regulate genes critical to beta-cells in a unique temporal manner.&lt;SEP&gt;MafA is a dedicated activator of the insulin gene in vivo.&lt;SEP&gt;Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;The MAFB transcription factor impacts islet α-cell function in rodents and represents a unique signature of primate islet β-cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploring Large MAF Transcription Factors: Functions, Pathology, and Mouse Models with Point Mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Defining unique structural features in the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors that control Insulin gene activity.</data>
