<edge source="MAFB" target="alpha cell">
  <data key="d12">13.0</data>
  <data key="d13">MAFB (v-maf musculoaponeurotic fibrosarcoma oncogene homolog B) exhibits a well-established and critical relationship with pancreatic alpha (α) cells across both developmental and adult stages.

During embryonic and fetal development, MAFB is expressed in all glucagon-positive (glucagon+) α-cells within the mouse fetal pancreas. It plays an essential role in early developmental stages of islet alpha cells, where it activates glucagon gene expression. Loss of MAFB during this period leads to defective terminal α-cell maturation, underscoring its requirement for proper α-cell development during the embryonic stage. Collectively, MAFB is considered essential for the overall development of alpha cells in the pancreas.

In the adult pancreas, MAFB expression becomes more restricted, being exclusively found in glucagon+ α-cells. It is robustly expressed in islet α-cells and is critical for glucagon production and secretion in mouse pancreatic alpha cells in vivo. The maintenance of adequate MAFB levels appears important for normal α-cell function, as compromised MAFB expression in pathogenic settings — such as type 2 diabetes (T2D) — may negatively affect α-cell function.

In summary, MAFB serves dual roles in relation to alpha cells: it is indispensable during embryonic pancreatic development for α-cell maturation and differentiation, and it continues to be robustly expressed and functionally significant in adult α-cells, particularly in regulating glucagon gene expression and secretion. Its dysregulation in disease states like T2D highlights its broader relevance to metabolic disorders involving glucagon dysregulation.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFB transcription factor motif is highly enriched in alpha cells with fold enrichment &gt;1.5 and -log10 FDR &gt;50.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFB TF motif is highly enriched in alpha cells.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFB is vital in the development and maturation of pancreatic islet glucagon-producing alpha cells.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFB impacts islet alpha cell function in rodents and represents a unique signature of primate islet beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFB affects islet alpha cell function and gene expression, which are compromised in type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFB motif is highly enriched in alpha cells with fold enrichment &gt; 1.5 and −log10 FDR &gt; 50.</data>
  <data key="d14">cell maturation,cell type specificity,embryonic expression,enriched_in,expressed_in,expression,motif_enriched,regulates,regulatory role</data>
