<edge source="MODY" target="MAFA_S64F">
  <data key="d12">15.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The MAFA S64F variant (also referred to as MAFA_S64F, p.Ser64Phe, or MafAS64F) is a pathogenic mutation in the MAFA gene that is causally associated with Maturity-Onset Diabetes of the Young (MODY). This variant has been identified in affected heterozygous human carriers and has been studied mechanistically using the MafAS64F/+ mouse model, which serves as a representative system for understanding its pathophysiology.

At the molecular and cellular level, the MAFA S64F variant primarily impairs glucose-stimulated insulin secretion, resulting in a noninsulin-dependent diabetes phenotype that closely resembles the clinical presentation of MODY. A central mechanism underlying this dysfunction is the induction of premature aging and cellular senescence in pancreatic islet beta cells, which compromises their normal function and survival. This beta cell dysfunction is characterized as adult-onset, consistent with the progressive nature of MODY.

A defining characteristic of this variant is that its pathological effects exhibit a pronounced sex-biased pattern, with male beta cells being particularly susceptible to premature senescence and dysfunction. Consequently, MAFA S64F predisposes carriers to MODY in a sex-dependent manner, with males being disproportionately affected. This sex-specific manifestation highlights the complex interplay between the MAFA S64F mutation and biological sex in determining disease severity and beta cell health.

More broadly, the MAFA S64F variant exemplifies how gene dosage and critical regulatory gene variants can affect islet cell health in a species-specific manner, consistent with the behavior of known MODY-associated transcription factors in mouse models. Collectively, the evidence positions MAFA S64F as a MODY-causing mutation that drives beta cell dysfunction through a mechanism centered on premature senescence, with clinically relevant sex-biased effects predominantly manifesting in male pancreatic islets.&lt;SEP&gt;The MAFA S64F variant is a MODY-associated mutation causing sex-biased beta cell dysfunction.</data>
  <data key="d14">associated with,associated_with,causes,diabetes phenotype,disease causation,gene dosage,pathophysiological outcome,predisposes,species-specific phenotype</data>
