<edge source="SASP" target="cellular senescence">
  <data key="d12">8.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype (SASP) is a defining hallmark and characteristic feature of cellular senescence, representing the process by which senescent cells actively secrete a complex mixture of bioactive molecules into their surrounding environment. This secretory profile includes a broad range of inflammatory mediators such as cytokines, interleukins, chemokines, and matrix metalloproteinases, collectively constituting the SASP effector proteins.

SASP is intrinsically linked to cellular senescence across multiple tissue types and is observed as a consistent feature during the ageing process. Senescent cells producing SASP are recognized for their proinflammatory nature, and this secretory activity has significant consequences both locally and systemically. One key function of SASP is its ability to act in a cell non-autonomous manner, meaning that SASP factors secreted by senescent cells can reprogram and induce senescence in neighboring, previously non-senescent cells. This paracrine signaling capacity allows senescence to propagate beyond the initially affected cells, amplifying its impact on surrounding tissue.

Beyond propagating senescence, SASP also plays a role in immune modulation. Specifically, the secretion of SASP factors has been shown to trigger immune-mediated clearance of senescent cells and to influence macrophage polarization, highlighting a complex interplay between senescent cells and the immune system. In specific pathological contexts, such as in MAFAS64F/+ males, SASP effector proteins released by senescent cells have been implicated in contributing to β cell dysfunction, underscoring the tissue-level consequences of SASP activity.

Given the proinflammatory and potentially pathological nature of SASP-expressing senescent cells, they represent therapeutic targets for both senolytics, which eliminate senescent cells, and senomorphics, which suppress or modulate the SASP without necessarily clearing the cells themselves.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular senescence activates the release of SASP, a proinflammatory secretome comprising cytokines, chemokines, MMPs, and growth factors.</data>
  <data key="d14">activates,associated_with,cell non-autonomous reprogramming,characterized_by,component_of,immune signaling,induces,paracrine signaling,produced_by,secretion,senescence phenotype</data>
