<edge source="SASP" target="IL-6">
  <data key="d12">11.0</data>
  <data key="d13">IL-6 (Interleukin-6) is a well-established component of the Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype (SASP), the complex secretory program activated in senescent cells.

As a pro-inflammatory cytokine, IL-6 is actively secreted as part of the SASP and has been identified as a SASP factor across more than ten distinct tissue types, underscoring its broad utility as a senescence marker. IL-6 is part of the classical SASP profile observed in multiple cell types, including fibroblasts, keratinocytes, melanocytes, and UVB-exposed melanocytes. Additionally, IL-6 is notably upregulated in senescent beta cells, highlighting its relevance in metabolic and endocrine contexts of cellular senescence.

Beyond its role in senescent somatic cells, IL-6 is also produced by immune cells, including monocytes and macrophages, which introduces an important layer of complexity in its interpretation as a senescence biomarker. Because IL-6 is also secreted by immune cells, careful distinction between senescent cell-derived and immune cell-derived IL-6 is necessary in experimental models to accurately attribute its source and avoid confounding results.

In summary, IL-6 occupies a central role in SASP biology as a broadly expressed pro-inflammatory cytokine across diverse tissues and cell types. However, its dual origin from both senescent cells and immune cells necessitates methodological rigor when using IL-6 as a marker of cellular senescence in research settings.&lt;SEP&gt;IL-6 is a cytokine component of the SASP secreted by senescent beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;IL-6 is a hallmark component of the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP).</data>
  <data key="d14">component of,component_of,comprises,contains,includes</data>
