<edge source="SASP" target="beta cell">
  <data key="d12">8.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relation between SASP (Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype) and beta cells encompasses multiple dimensions of cellular biology, pathology, and therapeutic relevance.

Senescent beta cells produce a distinct SASP repertoire that includes cytokines, chemokines, matrix metalloproteases, and growth factors. This SASP profile has been confirmed through proteomics analysis, which identified a unique set of secreted protein factors originating from senescent pancreatic islet cells. Notably, the SASP repertoire differs considerably between mouse and human beta cells, highlighting important species-specific variations that must be considered in research and therapeutic contexts. While the signaling pathways underlying beta cell SASP are largely conserved, the specific factors secreted remain heterogeneous.

The functional consequences of beta cell SASP are predominantly detrimental. One significant mechanism involves paracrine signaling, whereby SASP released from senescent beta cells can trigger senescence in adjacent, otherwise healthy, non-senescent beta cells. This paracrine propagation of senescence may contribute to the progressive deterioration of beta cell mass and function. Additionally, SASP may interfere with the natural secretory machinery of the beta cell, although the precise mechanism underlying this disruption remains unclear.

From a disease perspective, senescent beta cells and their associated SASP accumulate in the context of diabetes, particularly Type 1 Diabetes (T1D). SASP production by senescent beta cells has been implicated in driving T1D progression, establishing it as both a pathological contributor and a potential therapeutic target. Accordingly, senomorphic therapies — approaches aimed at modulating rather than eliminating senescent cells — have been identified as a promising strategy to mitigate the harmful effects of beta cell SASP and potentially slow disease advancement.</data>
  <data key="d14">associated_with,causes_paracrine_senescence,expressed_in,impairs_secretory_function_of,produced_by,secreted_by,unique_in</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-58c0cbc7de7a8a1060e8f1608271a28c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcadefd5324392c8a202934109c8c242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f6e43723dd8061d1a0532981e4a43b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6215695e8bd809fd02cb8b9b7b4123ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1628eba511291c52eab3e72ef0fd0239&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a778c4bc437c223ff92d776f7ab7b6a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32fd907a01d621388ff41b9b47bfa34</data>
  <data key="d16">Pancreatic β-cell senescence in diabetes: mechanisms, markers and therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;SenNet recommendations for detecting senescent cells in different tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploring senescence as a modifier of β cell extracellular vesicles in type 1 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778568490</data>
  <data key="d18"></data>
</edge>
