<edge source="PDX1" target="beta cell">
  <data key="d12">11.0</data>
  <data key="d13">PDX1 (Pancreatic and Duodenal Homeobox 1) is a critical transcription factor (TF) intrinsically associated with beta cell identity and function. It is consistently identified as one of the key regulators enriched in beta cells, with its transcription factor motifs being highly enriched in this cell type. Studies using SCENIC analysis have identified PDX1 among 242 transcription factors enriched in beta cells, and its upregulation in beta cells has been observed irrespective of sex.

A central role of PDX1 in beta cells is the maintenance of their cellular identity, which it achieves in part by repressing an α-cell transcriptional program, thereby preventing inappropriate cell fate transitions. PDX1 has also been confirmed as a beta cell identity marker at the protein level, with immunohistochemistry demonstrating higher PDX1 levels in approximately 80% of caloric restriction (CR) beta cells.

The activity and expression of PDX1 are subject to modulation under various physiological and pathological conditions. In aging beta cells, PDX1 activity is notably decreased, suggesting a role in age-associated decline of beta cell function. Furthermore, PDX1 is inactivated in the context of type 2 diabetes, highlighting its importance in disease pathogenesis and the maintenance of normal beta cell physiology. Network analyses have also revealed that PDX1 exhibits higher centrality in beta cells compared to female-specific contexts, indicating potential sex-related differences in its regulatory network dynamics.

Overall, PDX1 represents a master regulator of beta cell identity whose dysregulation — whether through aging, metabolic disease, or altered network centrality — has significant implications for beta cell health and function.&lt;SEP&gt;PDX1 is a key transcription factor regulating beta cell identity and function in pancreatic islets.&lt;SEP&gt;PDX1 transcription factor motifs are enriched in the accessible chromatin profiles of beta cells with high fold enrichment.&lt;SEP&gt;PDX1 is upregulated in beta cells irrespective of sex and exhibits higher centrality in male beta cell gene regulatory networks.</data>
  <data key="d14">enriched_in,expressed_in,higher_centrality,identity maintenance,identity_marker,motif_enriched,regulates,upregulated,α-cell program repression</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-b13f08985b48653da8be9b63098bbb2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe5b7d8c678cb311e300af3b74f19ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7873cb15c71b20e5fc369a6f0ab52a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f87338453daad7a9a4624ce798bcf6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0269f34de8f0928a9614438917079ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02465fe113dd488219949222d1430e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f63ca40b092870f725a75e7a36045d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0178968ab91760930c812723c60d342b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c41ef398df926f1c888f284aff2a98e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd4f6cfb9180aade52c8cb84446ced85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e42c3b77d3170e854edbbc691159609d</data>
  <data key="d16">Species-specific roles for the MAFA and MAFB transcription factors in regulating islet β cell identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic β-cell senescence in diabetes: mechanisms, markers and therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778570343</data>
  <data key="d18"></data>
</edge>
