<edge source="INS" target="beta cell">
  <data key="d12">15.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relation between INS and beta cells represents a fundamental and multifaceted biological and methodological association. INS, the gene encoding insulin, is a defining marker of pancreatic beta cells and plays a central role in both their functional identity and their identification across a wide range of experimental approaches.

At the functional level, beta cells are the primary source of insulin secretion, releasing it in response to glucose stimulation and meal intake. This secretory activity is a core feature of beta cell physiology, though it can vary under different physiological conditions — for instance, caloric restriction (CR) beta cells have been shown to secrete less insulin than ad libitum (AL) fed beta cells in vivo. Notably, even in senescent states, beta cells continue to maintain insulin expression and do not acquire expression of other islet hormones, underscoring the robustness and specificity of INS expression as a hallmark of beta cell identity.

From a methodological standpoint, INS and the insulin protein serve as critical markers for beta cell identification across multiple platforms. In transcriptomic studies, INS is recognized as a highly expressed marker gene used to identify and validate beta cell clusters in both single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) and single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq) of human pancreatic islets. The gene INS-IGF2, a readthrough transcript, is specifically used as a cell type-specific marker to validate beta cell clusters in snATAC-seq. However, the high expression level of INS in beta cells also contributes to ambient RNA contamination, a known technical artifact in sequencing studies.

At the protein level, insulin immunostaining is a standard approach for identifying beta cells in pancreatic tissue sections. Insulin protein channel pixel intensity is further utilized in automated image analysis pipelines, including confocal imaging and RNAscope, to computationally identify and quantify beta cells with precision.&lt;SEP&gt;INS is used as a cell type marker to identify beta cell clusters in snATAC-seq.&lt;SEP&gt;INS is a top upregulated marker gene expressed in beta cells independently of sex and race/ethnicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta cells are identified by insulin (INS) expression in immunostaining experiments&lt;SEP&gt;Adult insulin-producing beta cells express the insulin gene and differentiate from an independent cell lineage.</data>
  <data key="d14">expressed_in,expresses,identified_by,identifies,marker,marker_gene,marker_of,secreted_by,secretes,used_to_identify</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e607d2cca86a6257f1db73bacae2c96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c0d05211cf3080af14ec050e5cc97ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794cb97944fe847e61831fceed4d92ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a24341c9b63bd3b6313252fe6ed4b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56d5ffe02105b1bd6d77faab9c812433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68a87a3a1f3eb5d8b0e53b41df492512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2e4a544176811f5b43952b185e1ed3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dcd3bdc472cb74376c18c72cf9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f39153d9f864070e17a74724a6c8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d286ee457150be65228790e800b5207b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87d48756321bdf8ef533359c0655a3d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da10e6374cdbd3d6e4a07d773cd172d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce72a72ed1aaea72aac97c28374f8f86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e953ac093714735fa9b5b27463bf5dff</data>
  <data key="d16">Pancreatic β-cell senescence in diabetes: mechanisms, markers and therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Calorie restriction increases insulin sensitivity to promote beta cell homeostasis and longevity in mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Developmental control of DNA damage responses in α- and β-cells shapes the selective beta-cell susceptibility in diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778571510</data>
  <data key="d18"></data>
</edge>
