<edge source="MAFA_S64F" target="beta cell">
  <data key="d12">14.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relation between MAFA_S64F and beta cells describes the functional and molecular consequences of the MAFA S64F variant on pancreatic islet beta cell biology, with a notable sex-dependent dimension.

The MAFA S64F variant (also referred to as MAFA_S64F or MafAS64F) is a point mutation that reflects the critical role of the MAFA transcription factor in regulating pancreatic beta cell activity. At the molecular level, this mutation increases MAFA protein stability in beta cell lines, suggesting that the S64F substitution alters normal protein turnover or degradation mechanisms that are essential for proper beta cell function.

Functionally, the MAFA S64F variant causes islet beta cell dysfunction in a sex-biased manner, with effects observed principally in males. Studies conducted in mouse models harboring this point mutation — including models relevant to Maturity-Onset Diabetes of the Young (MODY) — demonstrate divergent outcomes between male and female MafAS64F/+ islets. The dysfunction in male beta cells is mechanistically linked to the induction of premature aging and cellular senescence, with male beta cells being preferentially affected by this senescent phenotype compared to their female counterparts.

Overall, the MAFA S64F point mutation is associated with islet beta cell dysfunction driven by MAFA protein stabilization, premature senescence, and sex-dependent regulatory differences, underscoring the importance of both the MAFA transcription factor and biological sex as determinants of beta cell health and disease susceptibility in the context of MODY-related diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;The MAFA S64F variant causes sex-biased islet beta cell dysfunction by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;The MAFA S64F variant causes sex-biased islet beta cell dysfunction in males, leading to diabetes and senescent cell accumulation.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA_S64F mutation is found in subjects with adult-onset beta cell dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA S64F variant induces premature aging and senescence in male beta cells, causing sex-biased beta cell dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;The MAFA S64F variant causes sex-biased beta cell dysfunction by inducing premature aging and senescence specifically in males.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA S64F variant induces premature aging and senescence in male beta cells.</data>
  <data key="d14">affects,causes,causes dysfunction,causes dysfunction in,causes_dysfunction_in,causes_senescence_in,dysfunction,induces,sex-biased,sex-dependent effects,sex-specific</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-fa2caaffb8c37fe000f2af35e115fc27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02381256ec5732a712b97ced237c2acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1a171c046b7e074fd282a07d58cc39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bdc00ebaa404f286432a974cec96702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8304b17dc893b8c9bfb7bfdb03703396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c72c144fce7d5c94557af956a559639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b861cee193cad9e0f142051047e83aae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b67e26d82684726785f1910ba6919cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea13fe99fbc01cb6036ecddcda0aae8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ef738c0840825512b2cb7af5b7c3f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238834380bfb40677324883af3e3004&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d41db2658ec2709309c04b39b1894e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10bdc47e4d027606476b48a401061a27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ab370ab273e97005ced113e59cc48a</data>
  <data key="d16">Sex-biased islet β cell dysfunction is caused by the MODY MAFA S64F variant by inducing premature aging and senescence in males.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploring Large MAF Transcription Factors: Functions, Pathology, and Mouse Models with Point Mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic β-cell senescence in diabetes: mechanisms, markers and therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;SenNet recommendations for detecting senescent cells in different tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;The role of the beta cell in type 2 diabetes: new findings from the last 5 years.&lt;SEP&gt;Nucleolar stress facilitates islet β cell senescence via hijacking the DNA damage response pathways.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778571236</data>
