</edge>
<edge source="MAFA_S64F" target="cellular senescence">
  <data key="d12">8.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relation between MAFA_S64F and cellular senescence describes a well-characterized mechanistic link in which the MAFA S64F variant drives premature cellular senescence, particularly in male pancreatic β cells.

MAFA S64F, a MODY-associated variant, acts as a dominant activator in male MafAS64F/+ islets, upregulating metabolic pathway genes associated with cellular senescence and aging. This variant induces premature aging and senescence signatures specifically in male β cells across multiple model systems, including male murine β cells and human EndoC-βH2 cells. The senescence-promoting effect is sex-biased, contributing to glucose intolerance and β-cell dysfunction in a sex-dependent manner, with male β cells being disproportionately affected compared to female counterparts.

In human β cells, MAFA S64F expression promotes cellular senescence as evidenced by several molecular markers, including increased levels of p21 and BCL2, as well as decreased LMNB1 expression. Notably, MAFA S64F-producing male human β cells exhibit accelerated cellular senescence relative to cells expressing the wild-type MAFAWT, underscoring the pathological gain-of-function nature of the variant. This ultimately leads to an accumulation of senescent cells in male individuals, further implicating the MAFA S64F variant in sex-biased β-cell dysfunction and the broader pathophysiology of MODY-associated diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;The MAFA S64F variant induces premature aging and senescence in male beta cells causing MODY.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA S64F variant induces senescence in male beta cells, contributing to MODY pathogenesis.&lt;SEP&gt;MAFA S64F variant induces premature aging and senescence specifically in male beta cells.</data>
  <data key="d14">accelerated senescence,associated_with,causes,dominant activation,induces,induction,pathological induction,pathophysiology,premature aging,premature induction,promotes,sex-dependent,transcriptional regulation</data>
