<edge source="beta cell" target="cellular senescence">
  <data key="d12">14.0</data>
  <data key="d13">Beta cells and cellular senescence share a well-documented and multifaceted relationship, particularly in the context of diabetes pathogenesis. Cellular senescence is a stressed biological state that occurs in pancreatic beta cells and has been observed to accumulate during the development and progression of multiple forms of diabetes, including type 1 diabetes (T1D), type 2 diabetes (T2D), and monogenic diabetes.

Senescent beta cells exhibit a range of characteristic features, including loss of nuclear integrity markers, DNA damage markers, a senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP), and a prosurvival phenotype. These features have been identified across various disease contexts and experimental models. Subpopulations of beta cells undergo senescence during the development and onset of diabetes, as demonstrated in multiple mouse models—including NOD mouse models—as well as in human pancreas tissue from donors.

The accumulation of senescent beta cells is implicated in disease progression, contributing to deleterious effects in diabetes. Beta cell senescence has been shown to play a pathogenic role across type 1 diabetes, type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance, and conditions involving specific genetic mutations such as MAFA S64F. The senescent state of beta cells is considered a key determinant of diabetes outcomes.

Interestingly, the relationship between beta cell senescence and disease is not entirely harmful. Stress-induced early beta cell senescence has been shown to confer protection against type 1 diabetes, suggesting a context-dependent, dual role for this cellular state.

Given its involvement in diabetes pathogenesis, beta cell senescence has emerged as a therapeutic target. Senolytics—agents designed to selectively eliminate senescent cells—are being explored as a means to intervene in diabetes progression by targeting the accumulation of senescent beta cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Senescence represents a double-edged sword in beta cell health and failure, affecting function and identity.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular senescence is studied in beta cells via p16 and p21 expression analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular senescence plays an integral role in beta cell dysfunction and loss during diabetic progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellular senescence is studied in pancreatic beta cells in the context of diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d14">accumulates_during,affects,associated_with,causes dysfunction in,contributes_to,occurs_in,protection,stress-induced senescence,stressed_state,undergoes</data>
