<edge source="beta cell" target="BCL-2">
  <data key="d12">9.0</data>
  <data key="d13">BCL-2 and beta cells share a relationship centered on the anti-apoptotic, prosurvival role that BCL-2 plays specifically within senescent beta cells, particularly in the context of diabetes mouse models.

BCL-2, a member of the anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family, is preferentially and notably upregulated in senescent beta cells in mouse models of diabetes, including non-obese diabetic (NOD) mice. This upregulation activates a prosurvival pathway that confers resistance to apoptosis in these senescent beta cells, effectively allowing them to persist despite the cellular stress and damage associated with senescence and diabetic conditions.

Critically, BCL-2 is described as required for the maintenance of this prosurvival state in senescent beta cells of NOD mice, suggesting that its expression is not merely correlative but functionally essential. The activation of the broader anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family prosurvival pathway underscores that BCL-2's role is part of a coordinated molecular program that protects senescent beta cells from programmed cell death. This mechanism contributes to a prosurvival phenotype in senescent beta cells, which may have significant implications for understanding beta cell persistence, immune evasion, and the pathophysiology of type 1 diabetes in these experimental models.&lt;SEP&gt;BCL-2 is upregulated in senescent beta cells in NOD mice, rendering them resistant to apoptosis and sensitive to BCL-2 inhibitor senolytics.</data>
  <data key="d14">activated_in,confers_resistance,prosurvival_in,upregulated_in</data>
