<edge source="beta cell" target="islet">
  <data key="d12">9.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relation (beta cell, islet) describes the structural and functional association between beta cells and pancreatic islets of Langerhans. Beta cells are a core cellular component residing within pancreatic islets, where they function as the primary insulin-producing cell type. Within this microenvironment, beta cells cooperate with neighboring endocrine cells to maintain islet homeostasis and regulate insulin secretion.

Pancreatic islets serve as the critical microenvironment for beta cell activity and have been widely used as the basis for various experimental and clinical investigations. Beta cells located within these islets are central to studies involving glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (GSIS) assays conducted in vitro, as well as extracellular vesicle (EV) research that leverages their insulin-producing capacity. The islet microenvironment is also particularly relevant to studies on beta cell senescence and autoimmune processes, as senescent beta cells residing within islets have been shown to exacerbate local inflammatory responses.

Beyond functional and pathological contexts, beta cells and their relationship to islet composition have been examined under varying dietary conditions, where beta cells have been found to modulate overall islet cellular composition. Morphological studies have further quantified beta cell mass and proportion within islets to better understand how dietary and metabolic factors influence islet architecture. Collectively, the (beta cell, islet) relation underscores beta cells as both a structural constituent and a key functional unit within pancreatic islets, with implications spanning metabolic regulation, disease pathology, and experimental research.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets contain beta cells and are used as models to study beta cell dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic islets contain β cells among other endocrine cell types, and islet β cells are the primary subject of study regarding MafA/MAFB function and GAST+ alternative cell states.</data>
