<edge source="beta cell" target="NKX6-1">
  <data key="d12">10.0</data>
  <data key="d13">NKX6-1 is a transcription factor with a well-established role in beta cell identity and function. It is recognized as a key beta cell identity gene, identified among 242 transcription factors enriched in beta cells through SCENIC analysis, underscoring its importance in defining the mature beta cell transcriptional landscape.

In terms of its functional role, NKX6-1 acts as a transcriptional modulator that supports and maintains mature beta cell identity. Its activity is critical for proper beta cell function, and reductions in its activity have notable consequences. Specifically, NKX6-1 transcription factor activity is decreased in aging beta cells, a change that contributes to the loss of beta cell identity over time.

NKX6-1 has also been observed to exhibit context-dependent changes in activation. In caloric restriction (CR) beta cells, NKX6-1 is upregulated and shows increased activation, suggesting that it serves as a transcriptional modulator responsive to metabolic or dietary conditions. This upregulation in CR beta cells implies a potential role in maintaining or restoring beta cell identity under specific physiological conditions.

Additionally, NKX6-1 displays sex-dependent differences in its regulatory network centrality. It exhibits higher centrality in male beta cell gene regulatory networks compared to females, indicating that its role within the broader transcriptional network of beta cells may differ between sexes. This sex-specific variation in network centrality suggests potential differences in how NKX6-1 coordinates gene regulation in male versus female beta cells.

Collectively, NKX6-1 is a central transcription factor in beta cell biology, influencing mature beta cell identity, responding to metabolic cues such as caloric restriction, declining with aging, and exhibiting sex-dependent regulatory network properties.&lt;SEP&gt;NKX6-1 is a transcription factor important for beta cell identity studied in islet regulatory programs.&lt;SEP&gt;NKX6-1 exhibits higher centrality in male beta cell gene regulatory networks compared to females.</data>
  <data key="d14">expressed_in,higher_centrality,identity_marker,regulates,regulates_identity,sex-differentiated centrality</data>
