<edge source="beta cell" target="KDM5D">
  <data key="d12">11.0</data>
  <data key="d13">KDM5D is a Y-linked gene encoding a chromatin remodeler that demonstrates notable sex-specific differences in expression and chromatin accessibility within pancreatic beta cells. Because KDM5D is encoded exclusively on the Y chromosome, its expression is confined to males, and multiple lines of evidence consistently confirm that it is overexpressed in male beta cells compared to female beta cells.

Islet transcriptome analyses have identified KDM5D as upregulated in male beta cells, a finding corroborated by chromatin accessibility studies showing that the KDM5D locus is more accessible in male islet cells, including beta cells, than in their female counterparts. This increased chromatin accessibility in males is thought to contribute directly to the elevated transcriptional activity of KDM5D in the male beta cell context.

The functional implications of KDM5D overexpression in male beta cells are significant, as its role as a chromatin remodeler suggests it may broadly influence the epigenetic landscape of these cells. Specifically, KDM5D is proposed to drive sex-specific differences in chromatin accessibility within beta cells, potentially shaping gene expression programs unique to male islet function. These observations position KDM5D as a key contributor to the molecular basis of sex differences in islet biology, with possible relevance to understanding sex-specific susceptibility or responses in pancreatic function and related metabolic conditions.</data>
