<edge source="beta cell" target="YY1">
  <data key="d12">8.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relation between YY1 and beta cells describes a critical and multifaceted regulatory role that YY1 plays in the development, maintenance, and function of beta cells in both humans and mice.

YY1 is essential for normal DNA repair and cell cycle function in beta cells, a role that has been demonstrated across both human and mouse beta cell models. Specifically, YY1 serves as a key regulator of the DNA Damage Response (DDR) in beta cells, and its presence is necessary for maintaining genomic stability in these cells postnatally. Its dysregulation is associated with the onset and progression of diabetes, underscoring its physiological importance.

YY1 has also been found to be depleted in beta cells from individuals with type 2 diabetes (T2D), suggesting that loss of YY1 expression may be a contributing factor to beta cell dysfunction in the context of this disease. Furthermore, loss of YY1 during beta cell development leads to impaired functional maturation of beta cells and a reduction in beta cell viability, highlighting its indispensable role not only in mature beta cell maintenance but also during the developmental stages of beta cell differentiation.

In summary, YY1 functions as a critical regulator of beta cell genomic integrity, DNA damage response, cell cycle control, and functional maturation. Its depletion or dysregulation — whether during development or in disease states such as T2D — results in compromised beta cell health, reduced viability, and impaired function, making YY1 a significant molecular factor in beta cell biology and diabetes pathophysiology.</data>
