<edge source="beta cell" target="caloric restriction">
  <data key="d12">11.0</data>
  <data key="d13">Caloric restriction (CR) exerts a multifaceted and beneficial influence on pancreatic beta cells, affecting their structure, function, identity, and long-term longevity through several interconnected mechanisms.

At the functional level, CR reduces the overall demand placed on beta cells by inducing a systemic insulin-sensitive state in peripheral tissues. As a result, CR beta cells secrete approximately 50% less insulin than those in ad libitum (AL)-fed subjects in vivo, yet euglycemia is still maintained due to enhanced peripheral insulin sensitivity. This reduction in insulin release demand represents a significant lowering of beta cell workload, with functional beta cell adaptation emerging after at least six weeks of CR.

With respect to cellular aging and longevity, CR delays the expression of aging hallmarks and senescence markers in beta cells. It promotes beta cell longevity by slowing turnover rates, maintaining a post-mitotic state, and reducing overall cell cycling. These effects collectively contribute to a longer-lived, more stable beta cell population.

CR also drives substantial transcriptional and epigenomic remodeling in beta cells. It triggers significant changes in transcriptional programs and chromatin accessibility, leading to the upregulation of genes associated with beta cell maturation, homeostasis, and function. These molecular changes enhance beta cell identity, nutrient metabolism, and circadian rhythm regulation, further supporting cellular homeostasis.

Conversely, CR suppresses transcriptional programs and proliferation pathways that would otherwise be necessary for adaptive beta cell mass expansion in response to metabolic stressors such as a high-fat diet (HFD). This indicates that while CR optimizes beta cell quality and longevity, it may reduce the capacity for compensatory proliferative responses under conditions of metabolic overload.

In summary, caloric restriction improves beta cell structure-function relationships, reinforces cell identity, delays aging and senescence, and promotes long-term beta cell longevity primarily by reducing metabolic demand and slowing cellular turnover.</data>
  <data key="d14">activates,associated_with,improves,modulates,preserves,promotes,promotes longevity,protects,reduces,reduces turnover,reduces_demand,regulates,reprograms,suppresses</data>
