<edge source="alpha cell" target="KDM6A">
  <data key="d12">8.0</data>
  <data key="d13">KDM6A is an X-linked gene that exhibits notable sex-specific differences in its expression and chromatin accessibility within pancreatic alpha cells. Across multiple analyses of islet transcriptomes and chromatin accessibility profiling, KDM6A is consistently found to be upregulated and more accessible in female alpha cells compared to their male counterparts.

Specifically, KDM6A demonstrates greater chromatin accessibility and higher expression levels in female alpha cells, a pattern that distinguishes it from the corresponding male alpha cell population. This sex-biased upregulation has been identified through islet transcriptome analysis as well as through assessments of open chromatin regions, indicating that the differential expression occurs at both the transcriptional and epigenomic levels.

Given its identity as an X-linked gene, the observed sex differences in KDM6A expression and chromatin accessibility in alpha cells are consistent with mechanisms related to X-chromosome dosage or escape from X-inactivation. Furthermore, the increased chromatin accessibility associated with KDM6A in female alpha cells suggests that it may play a role in influencing sex-specific chromatin access to transcription factors, potentially contributing to broader sex-dependent regulatory differences within islet alpha cell biology.</data>
  <data key="d14">accessible,accessible_in,associated_with,expressed_in,regulates,sex_differences,upregulated</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-fe5b7d8c678cb311e300af3b74f19ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e607d2cca86a6257f1db73bacae2c96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794cb97944fe847e61831fceed4d92ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7bd4bfe81f828dceb60b520d85bcf2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd4ac2599dc6d5dc028ad0e83ccf82f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87d48756321bdf8ef533359c0655a3d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ea44d17d2377f3dd0f15239a73e15e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e42c3b77d3170e854edbbc691159609d</data>
  <data key="d16">Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778570384</data>
  <data key="d18"></data>
</edge>
