<edge source="macrophage" target="SDS">
  <data key="d12">8.0</data>
  <data key="d13">SDS (Serine Dehydratase) serves as a well-established marker gene associated with macrophages, particularly in the context of pancreatic islet research. Across multiple lines of evidence, SDS has been consistently identified as a reliable indicator for macrophage cell populations using various single-cell genomic technologies.

In single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) studies of human pancreatic islets, SDS is expressed in macrophages and is used as a defining marker gene to identify and annotate macrophage clusters. Its expression is specifically noted in pancreatic macrophages, making it a valuable tool for distinguishing this cell population from others within the islet microenvironment.

Beyond transcriptomic approaches, SDS also functions as a gene accessibility marker in single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq), where it is used to validate macrophage cluster identity and annotate macrophage populations based on chromatin accessibility profiles. This dual utility across both RNA expression and chromatin accessibility platforms underscores its robustness as a cell type-specific marker.

Notably, SDS has been identified as a top upregulated marker gene in macrophages in a manner that is independent of sex and race/ethnicity, suggesting that its expression in macrophages is a consistent and broadly applicable biological feature rather than a population-specific observation. Overall, the relation between SDS and macrophages is characterized by SDS functioning as a reliable, cross-platform, and demographically robust marker gene for the identification, validation, and annotation of macrophage clusters in human pancreatic islet studies.</data>
  <data key="d14">annotated_by,expressed_in,marker,marker_gene,marker_of</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-9e607d2cca86a6257f1db73bacae2c96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c0d05211cf3080af14ec050e5cc97ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7873cb15c71b20e5fc369a6f0ab52a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7bd4bfe81f828dceb60b520d85bcf2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a24341c9b63bd3b6313252fe6ed4b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87d48756321bdf8ef533359c0655a3d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da10e6374cdbd3d6e4a07d773cd172d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e42c3b77d3170e854edbbc691159609d</data>
  <data key="d16">Single cell regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islets suggests sex differences in β cell function and the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sex-specific regulatory architecture of pancreatic islets from subjects with and without type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d17">1778570408</data>
  <data key="d18"></data>
</edge>
