<edge source="islet" target="snATAC-seq">
  <data key="d12">17.0</data>
  <data key="d13">The relation (islet, snATAC-seq) describes the application of single-nucleus Assay for Transposase-Accessible Chromatin sequencing (snATAC-seq) to pancreatic islet cells as a primary experimental approach for profiling chromatin accessibility at high resolution.

snATAC-seq was employed to map accessible and open chromatin regions across human pancreatic islet cell types at single-cell resolution. This technique was applied to both non-diabetic and diabetic donors, enabling comprehensive examination of the epigenomic chromatin landscape across distinct islet cell populations. Studies involving non-diabetic donors specifically aimed to characterize the baseline chromatin accessibility landscape across islet cell types, while broader comparative analyses investigated chromatin accessibility differences across sex, race, and disease states.

In terms of experimental protocol, snATAC-seq was performed on human islets using a standardized quantity of approximately 500 islet equivalents (IEQ) per condition. Islets were typically cultured overnight prior to cell dispersal, after which isolated nuclei were used as input for chromatin accessibility profiling of open chromatin regions.

The application of snATAC-seq to islets served multiple research objectives, including: mapping the chromatin accessibility landscape across individual islet cell types, profiling open chromatin regions to support Type 2 Diabetes (T2D) genome-wide association study (GWAS) enrichment analyses, and assessing epigenomic variation linked to biological and demographic variables such as sex and race. Collectively, these studies highlight snATAC-seq as a central genomic tool for understanding the regulatory architecture of human pancreatic islet cells in both healthy and disease contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;snATAC-seq was used to assess chromatin accessibility in human pancreatic islets across sex and race.&lt;SEP&gt;snATAC-seq was used to profile chromatin accessibility in human pancreatic islet cells from donors with and without T2D.&lt;SEP&gt;snATAC-seq was performed on non-diabetic islet donors to examine chromatin accessibility landscape.&lt;SEP&gt;snATAC-seq open chromatin regions from islet cells were analyzed for genomic enrichment of T2D GWAS variants.&lt;SEP&gt;snATAC-seq is applied to human islet cells to profile chromatin accessibility after overnight culture and cell dispersal.</data>
  <data key="d14">applied_to,characterization,chromatin profiling,method_applied,profiles,used_in,used_to_study</data>
  <data key="d15">chunk-78c6090d91c84168c42d7ba3a2808633&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e607d2cca86a6257f1db73bacae2c96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c0d05211cf3080af14ec050e5cc97ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3982c3cb7b00162818de82027a853e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bcdcae8f65f7440848eed5a2448750b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794cb97944fe847e61831fceed4d92ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6615f49a664b0e6bfe3dc5ebc25a278f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7bd4bfe81f828dceb60b520d85bcf2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a24341c9b63bd3b6313252fe6ed4b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7913917fca6b789f10e2948623ce222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238834380bfb40677324883af3e3004&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd4ac2599dc6d5dc028ad0e83ccf82f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d41db2658ec2709309c04b39b1894e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87d48756321bdf8ef533359c0655a3d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2496631e25b79859522b657da346b58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5e348a0adb909ac9a245bd8fb1b7b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-822d03d11fbf61e2a25591e2663ef1ca</data>
