<edge source="ductal cell" target="CFTR">
  <data key="d12">8.0</data>
  <data key="d13">CFTR (Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance Regulator) serves as a well-established marker gene for ductal cells, particularly in the context of pancreatic tissue analysis. Its relationship with ductal cells has been consistently documented across multiple genomic and transcriptomic profiling methodologies.

In single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) studies of human pancreatic islets, CFTR is identified as a marker gene expressed in ductal cells, where it is used to identify and annotate ductal cell clusters. Its expression in pancreatic ductal cells allows researchers to reliably distinguish this cell population from other cell types present in islet tissue.

Beyond transcriptomic approaches, CFTR also functions as a gene accessibility marker in single-nucleus ATAC sequencing (snATAC-seq), where chromatin accessibility at the CFTR locus is used to validate ductal cell cluster identity. In this context, CFTR serves as a marker for acinar/ductal cells, demonstrating its utility across both gene expression and chromatin accessibility-based cell type annotation frameworks.

Notably, CFTR has been identified as a top upregulated marker gene in ductal cells in a manner that is independent of sex and race/ethnicity, highlighting its robustness and consistency as a ductal cell marker across diverse human populations. This characteristic reinforces its value as a cell type-specific marker gene for annotating ductal cell clusters in large-scale, heterogeneous datasets.

Overall, CFTR represents a reliable and broadly applicable molecular marker for ductal cell identification and validation across multiple single-cell and single-nucleus profiling platforms in pancreatic research.</data>
  <data key="d14">annotated_by,expressed_in,marker,marker_gene,marker_of</data>
